\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Long Covid is keeping millions out of work \u8211\'20\'13 and worsening labor shortage in the US\b0\par\par\uc2 Fiona Lowenstein and Ryan Prior \par \uc2 1,141 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 September 2022\par 10:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Recent analyses estimate that long Covid could be driving up to one-third of the US labor shortage\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve all seen the headlines about labor shortages, worker attrition, or \u8211\'20\'13 as many mainstream media outlets refer to it \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Great Resignation\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s true: since 2020, a record number of people have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/opub/mlr/2022/article/the-great-resignation-in-perspective.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quit their jobs}}}\uc2 . The trend is ongoing, and some argue {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/01/21/business/quitting-contagious.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quitting is contagious}}}\uc2 . But, there\u8217\'20\'19s another contagion that\u8217\'20\'19s probably causing people to leave the workforce in droves.\par \par \uc2 Since 2020, there have been more than 95m recorded US Covid-19 cases, 1 million deaths and ongoing reports of Covid-induced chronic illness and disability, known as long Covid. A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/pressroom/nchs_press_releases/2022/20220622.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent study}}}\uc2  by the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  estimated that long Covid affects one in five people infected with Sars-CoV-2. A recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/research/new-data-shows-long-covid-is-keeping-as-many-as-4-million-people-out-of-work/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brookings Institution}}}\uc2  analysis found that as many as 2 to 4 million people may be out of work as a result. With more than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/LMJVTTUVUSQ647S"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 11m US jobs }}}\uc2  vacant, it\u8217\'20\'19s plausible that up to one-third of current labor shortages are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/08/26/brookings-long-covid-keeps-millions-of-americans-out-of-workforce.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 due to long Covid}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In other words, the Great Resignation may be a symptom of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Imani_Barbarin/status/1526967824434049024?s=20&t=9ggcOQNqe5XIaTxzYe3wrA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mass disabling event}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So, why aren\u8217\'20\'19t we talking more about quitting and long Covid? Instead of investigating the impact of continuing pandemic harms on the workforce, many have been quick to frame the Great Resignation through stories of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/22/style/quitting-personal-finances.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 white-collar workers}}}\uc2  seeking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/08/15/style/quitting-work-life-balance-career.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 better work-life balance}}}\uc2 . For a society {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenation.com/article/society/covid-pandemic-cdc-fatigue/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supposedly eager to move on}}}\uc2  from the pandemic, long Covid is an inconvenient truth. Its potential impact on the workforce is even more inconvenient, since governments frequently cite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/blog/future-development/2020/04/20/a-policy-framework-for-mitigating-the-economic-impact-of-covid-19/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 economic trouble}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/19/nyregion/adams-mask-mandate-covid-new-york.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as justification}}}\uc2  for dropping Covid-19 mitigation efforts.\par \par \uc2 Despite a widespread media focus on white-collar workers who have quit, pandemic worker attrition is most obvious in \u8220\'20\'1Cessential\u8221\'20\'1D industries that require in-person work. Many states face {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kcur.org/news/2022-07-05/burned-out-as-more-educators-leave-kansas-faces-its-worst-ever-teacher-shortage"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 drastic teacher shortages}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/health/archive/2021/11/the-mass-exodus-of-americas-health-care-workers/620713/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 healthcare workers}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.healthcaredive.com/news/nurse-burnout-covid-quit-travel-incredible-health/620488/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continue to quit}}}\uc2 . The restaurant and food service industry {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.businessinsider.com/restaurant-worker-shortage-continuing-through-2022-survey-2022-2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 still experiences}}}\uc2  severe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://restaurant.org/research-and-media/research/research-reports/state-of-the-industry/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pandemic-related labor shortages}}}\uc2  today. These workers faced higher rates of infection than those working remotely, and probably experience higher rates of long Covid \u8211\'20\'13 both because {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LongCOVIDNatl/status/1549156235793969152?s=20&t=KWSDmlPseFfY4NmednaGRA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 preventing infection}}}\uc2  is the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://time.com/6211659/long-covid-after-vaccination-booster/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 only way}}}\uc2  to prevent long Covid and because reinfection {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2022-08-01/coronavirus-reinfection-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 may increase risk}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 It stands to reason that long Covid may be driving shortages in these industries. A 2021 study by scientists at the \uc2 University of California San Francisco\uc2  indicated that line cooks faced the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/02/02/jobs-where-workers-have-the-highest-risk-of-dying-from-covid-study.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 highest risk of mortality}}}\uc2  from Covid-19. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.edweek.org/leadership/1-in-5-educators-say-theyve-experienced-long-covid/2022/04#:~:text=New%20data%20from%20the%20EdWeek,they%20have%20contracted%20long%20COVID"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 One in five educators}}}\uc2  are long-haulers, and healthcare workers with long Covid {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/health/archive/2021/11/health-care-workers-long-covid-are-being-dismissed/620801/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 say workplace pressures}}}\uc2  make it difficult to retain employment.\par \par \uc2 As two people {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2020/04/13/opinion/coronavirus-recovery.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 personally affected}}}\uc2  by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2018/08/08/health/iyw-ryan-me-cfs-story"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 post-viral illnesses}}}\uc2 , we understand the difficult choices facing long-haulers who can\u8217\'20\'19t survive financially without working. We both rely on remote work and flexible schedules to manage our health \u8211\'20\'13 privileges many with long Covid lack. Our symptoms are also milder than many, allowing us to work at all. More severely sick long-haulers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2022/03/08/long-covid-disability-benefits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 face major barriers}}}\uc2  accessing social security disability benefits. Those who do qualify will receive a mere {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.disabilitysecrets.com/how-much-in-ssd.html#:~:text=In%202022%2C%20the%20average%20SSDI,spouse%20and%20children%20is%20%242%2C383."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $1,358}}}\uc2  on average each month.\par \par \uc2 When Tracey Thompson became too sick to stand, it was clear she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t return to her former job as a chef. More than two years later, Thompson is unemployed and disabled, with cognitive dysfunction and \u8220\'20\'1Coverwhelming fatigue\u8221\'20\'1D. She recently regained enough strength to lift a single frying pan, but remains housebound and mostly bed-bound. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of the normal avenues of work \u8211\'20\'13 like physical labor or mental labor \u8211\'20\'13 are cut off for me,\u8221\'20\'1D she explained. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can\u8217\'20\'19t cook remotely.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Since getting sick, Thompson has connected with other long-haulers who are similarly out-of-work. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of people that are just barely hanging on \u8230\'20\'26 by a thread,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd there are people who have definitely been going to work unwell.\u8221\'20\'1D Unfortunately, pushing through long Covid symptoms {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/california/story/2022-07-07/working-through-covid-sleep-rest-infection-test-positive"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to continue working}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2021/jun/21/long-covid-recovery-coronavirus"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 can result in worsened health}}}\uc2 . But, as Thompson explains, \u8220\'20\'1Cpeople can\u8217\'20\'19t afford to take time off\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Leigh, a physical therapist in Ohio who prefers to be identified by just her first name, is one such long-hauler. She sometimes finds herself forgetting interactions with patients and rarely has time to care for herself. If she could retain healthcare benefits while working less, she\u8217\'20\'19d do it. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m so sick of being tired all the time,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to let anyone down, but I am \u8230\'20\'26 struggling. And I\u8217\'20\'19m not sure I want people to know how much.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 While long-haulers fight to maintain employment, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@zaidleppelin/video/7124414185282391342?is_copy_url=1&is_from_webapp=v1&item_id=7124414185282391342"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TikTok video}}}\uc2  about \u8220\'20\'1Cquiet quitting\u8221\'20\'1D set off a flurry of news coverage describing workers who are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/if-your-gen-z-co-workers-are-quiet-quitting-heres-what-that-means-11660260608"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 saying no to hustle culture}}}\uc2 . As with the Great Resignation, most discussions of \u8220\'20\'1Cquiet quitting\u8221\'20\'1D fail to address the impact of pandemic harms, focusing instead on perceived generational divides. To {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://freakonomics.com/2011/04/quotes-uncovered-how-lies-travel/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 borrow a phrase}}}\uc2  often attributed to Mark Twain, it\u8217\'20\'19s as though a TikTok meme went halfway around the world before the truth had time to put its shoes on.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CQuiet quitting\u8221\'20\'1D and resignations may also be driven by recent removals of remote work options, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/04/05/1090992383/biden-is-pressured-to-end-mask-mandates-on-public-transportation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mask mandates}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theatlantic.com/health/archive/2022/08/cdc-weakened-covid-guidelines-pandemic-preparedness/671147/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quarantine requirements}}}\uc2 . Some long-haulers who can work part- or full-time are now sidelined due to increased risk of reinfection. These people are joined by millions of other high-risk individuals who have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BeingCharisBlog/status/1567865243682086912?s=20&t=HGhOY4m60jmZoUAd2avQRg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increasingly marginalized}}}\uc2  from society, and now must {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2022/03/01/1083775449/immunocompromised-americans-feel-left-behind-by-the-loosening-of-covid-safety-ru"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fend for themselves}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The connection between workplace safety, chronic illness and labor is not new. For decades, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2020/07/26/opinion/Americans-with-disabilities-act.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 health justice activists}}}\uc2  have urged policymakers to respond {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://tcf.org/content/report/economic-justice-disability-justice/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to rising rates}}}\uc2  of disabled poverty, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://beta.ada.gov/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 demanding}}}\uc2  better workplace accommodations and disability benefits. However, policymakers continually fail to adequately address these issues. Erasing disease is easier in a world where chronically ill people often seem to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://millionsmissing.meaction.net/history-of-millionsmissing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 vanish from society altogether}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Since we started covering long Covid in spring 2020, the patients we have spoken to have consistently detailed drastic financial issues. Yet little has been done to help. Instead, long-haulers burn through {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.marketwatch.com/story/this-isnt-just-gonna-go-away-long-covid-is-crashing-the-retirement-hopes-of-many-americans-11652961935"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 savings accounts}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rollingstone.com/culture/culture-features/long-covid-homeless-chronic-illness-gig-economy-1312460/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lose their homes}}}\uc2  and trudge back and forth to jobs they can no longer safely perform. Some might call this a Great Resignation, or \u8220\'20\'1Cquiet quitting\u8221\'20\'1D. To us, it\u8217\'20\'19s an example of government negligence in the face of a public health crisis, and the impossible choices facing chronically ill and high-risk workers today.\par \par \uc2 Fixing the labor shortage means treating, accommodating and mitigating long Covid. It also requires building a society in which disabled people can participate.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Fiona Lowenstein is an independent journalist, the founder of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wearebodypolitic.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Body Politic}}}\uc2 , and the editor of the forthcoming book {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theexperimentpublishing.com/catalogs/fall-2022/long-covid-survival-guide/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Long Covid Survival Guide.}}}\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ryan Prior covered the Covid-19 pandemic as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/profiles/ryan-prior"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNN features writer}}}\uc2  and is the author of the forthcoming book {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.simonandschuster.com/books/The-Long-Haul/Ryan-Prior/9781637581414"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Long Haul}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220915ei9f001gz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
