\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US railroad workers prepare for strike as rail companies see record profits\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,314 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 September 2022\par 23:54 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As Biden\u8217\'20\'19s recommendations fall flat, negotiations between management and unions are at an impasse \u8211\'20\'13 and workers are prepared to walk\par \par \uc2 US freight railroad workers are close to striking over claims that grueling schedules and poor working conditions have been driving employees out of the industry over the past several years.\par \par \uc2 Heated negotiations over a new union contract between railroad corporations and 150,000-member-strong labor unions have been ongoing for nearly three years. A \u8220\'20\'1Ccooling off\u8221\'20\'1D period imposed by the Biden administration after it issued recommendations to settle the dispute ends on Friday. If no deal is reached, unions are threatening industrial action \u8211\'20\'13 the first since 1992 \u8211\'20\'13 and workers say they will quit an industry already facing staff shortages.\par \par \uc2 The consequences of a strike would be severe. Rail moves close to 40% of the US\u8217\'20\'19s long-distance trade and a strike could cost the US economy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aar.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/09/AAR-Rail-Shutdown-Report-September-2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $2bn a day}}}\uc2 , according to a recently issued \uc2 Association of American Railroads\uc2  report, disrupting travel, commutes and the shipment of commodities and other goods across the country.\par \par \uc2 But workers argue the industry is in crisis. Between November 2018 and December 2020, the railroad industry {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/opub/mlr/2021/article/employment-in-rail-transportation-heads-downhill-between-november-2018-and-december-2020.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost 40,000 jobs}}}\uc2  in the US, according to data from the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . The US railroad industry\u8217\'20\'19s workforce dropped from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.railserve.com/employment.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 1 million workers}}}\uc2  in the 1950s to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/iag/tgs/iag482.htm#workforce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fewer than 150,000}}}\uc2  in 2022.\par \par \uc2 The cost-cutting has contributed to big windfalls at \uc2 BNSF\uc2  and Union Pacific, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://soundingmaps.com/the-largest-railroads-in-us/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two largest railroad corporations}}}\uc2  in North America, which reported record profits in 2021. \uc2 BNSF\uc2  is owned by billionaire investor \uc2 Warren Buffett\u8217\'20\'19s Berkshire Hathaway\uc2  conglomerate. US railroads have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prospect.org/economy/how-americas-supply-chains-got-railroaded/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  paid out $196bn in stock buybacks and dividends}}}\uc2  to shareholders since 2010.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe job is just really becoming fewer people doing more work faster,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ross Grooters, a locomotive engineer for \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  in Iowa and co-chair of Railroad Workers United. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in this country all workers getting more and more squeezed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese railroads are making billions of dollars. In the past, we\u8217\'20\'19ve been well compensated for being on call 24/7, 365 days a year. That\u8217\'20\'19s been eroded over the course of my career in the last two decades to where it\u8217\'20\'19s just not appealing enough to attract people into the workplace,\u8221\'20\'1D said Grooters.\par \par \uc2 Labor cuts, lack of paid days off, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/the-perils-of-precision-scheduled-railroading"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 precision scheduling systems}}}\uc2  to reduce headcounts, disciplinary attendance policies that issue points against workers for any {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/5dgezn/the-worst-and-most-egregious-attendance-policy-is-pushing-railroad-workers-to-the-brink"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 time taken off}}}\uc2  and unfair and punishing on-call schedules have made it more difficult to continue working in the railroad industry, said Grooters, and workers claim these issues aren\u8217\'20\'19t being addressed in proposed new union contract agreements.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen I first was hired out on the railroad my paychecks seemed to stretch a lot further than it does today. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think that\u8217\'20\'19s a unique experience for railroad workers. We really need to stop that trend. And hopefully, we railroad workers can help fight back against profiteering from the richest people in this country,\u8221\'20\'1D said Grooters.\par \par \uc2 With talks stalling, the Biden administration convened a presidential emergency board (PEB) earlier this year that issued recommendations for a settlement on 16 August. Biden and his economic team are pushing unions and companies to reach an agreement before Friday\u8217\'20\'19s deadline, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/09/14/us/politics/rail-strike-biden.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the New York Times reported}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2022-09-12/supply-chain-latest-us-railroad-strike-threatens-supply-chain-recovery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ten of the 12 labor unions}}}\uc2  currently negotiating new contracts have reached tentative agreements for workers to vote on, but the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen (BLET) \uc2 and the International Association of Sheet Metal, Air, Rail and Transportation Workers (Smart), which represent half of railroad union workers, have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/09/12/large-rail-labor-unions-move-closer-to-a-strike.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said}}}\uc2  workers will strike if attendance and scheduling issues aren\u8217\'20\'19t resolved.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 5,000 members of the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers (IAM) \uc2 voted on Wednesday to reject the tentative agreement, the union said. Members of the Transportation Communications Union and the Brotherhood of Railway Carmen voted to ratify their contracts Wednesday. \uc2 IAM\uc2  did agree to delay any strike until 29 September, the union said.\par \par \uc2 Workers at several other unions still have to vote on the tentative agreements, and some have expressed criticisms that the recommendations don\u8217\'20\'19t adequately address staffing and scheduling issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe PEB punted on just about every single issue, and left people feeling kind of betrayed and kind of vacant, because of the discontent on the railroad right now,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ron Kaminkow, general secretary of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.railroadworkersunited.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Railroad Workers United}}}\uc2  who currently works as an \uc2 Amtrak\uc2  engineer in Reno, Nevada, and serves as vice-president of BLET Local 51.\par \par \uc2 Kaminkow said many railroad workers don\u8217\'20\'19t have any paid time off at all, with the PEB recommending just one added day of paid time off. Workers are currently on call consistently throughout the year, making a life-work balance near impossible and contributing to fatigue issues, illnesses, job safety and discontent among the workforce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s our speculation that if this contract is approved and the PEB recommendations form the basis for a tentative agreement, and this is what we end up with, you will probably see thousands of workers in train engine service who will wait to get the best paid lump sum settlement and then they\u8217\'20\'19ll quit. So it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t solve any of the problems that the industry is facing,\u8221\'20\'1D Kaminkow added. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve worked in the industry 26 years, and it\u8217\'20\'19s \u8211\'20\'13 I never thought I\u8217\'20\'19d see it like this.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 BLET members {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ble-t.org/news/blet-strike-vote-the-rest-of-the-story/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  99.5% in favor of authorizing a strike in July 2022, representing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/supply-chain-us-railroads-labor-idAFL1N2YT2GP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 around 23,000 workers}}}\uc2  under the new contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 BLET and Smart issued a joint statement on 11 September {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://smart-union.org/smart-td-blet-joint-statement-on-rail-embargo/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criticizing}}}\uc2  railroad corporations for warning shippers of embargoes on certain shipments ahead of the end of the federally mandated cooling off period, claiming the railroad industry is using supply chain and economic concerns to try to impose a bad contract on the unions that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t address the attendance and scheduling concerns of workers.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://myemail.constantcontact.com/Survey-Results--Rail-Workers-and-the-PEB.html?soid=1116509035139&aid=Xy_EoF8yZ90"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey}}}\uc2  conducted by Railroad Workers United on the PEB recommendations received responses from 3,162 railroad workers, with over 90% of respondents saying they would vote against tentative agreements based on the recommendations and would approve of a strike after the federally mandated cooling off period.\par \par \uc2 Railroad workers have emphasized their grueling schedules, a disciplinary attendance system and lack of paid days off, and workers constantly having to be on call to report to work within two hours or less as major points of criticism of the PEB recommendations that failed to address these issues.\par \par \uc2 Under the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.pennfedbmwe.org/Docs/reference/RLA_Simplified.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Railway Labor Act}}}\uc2 , railroad corporations and labor unions have to adhere to federally mandated cooling off periods to try to resolve labor disputes. Once the cooling off period ends, on 16 September, a variety of scenarios could result, either through strikes or lockouts of workers by railroad corporations \u8211\'20\'13 or Congress could act to impose its own settlement or extend the cooling off period to continue negotiations and avoid any disruptions to interstate commerce.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Congress imposes the results of the PEB, or imposes a bad contract down our throat without addressing the attendance policy, or quality of life issues, or our taxable meal issues, without addressing any of that, then people are just going to leave. They already are, but people are just going to continue leaving the industry,\u8221\'20\'1D said Michael Paul Lindsey, a locomotive engineer for \uc2 Union Pacific\uc2  in Idaho for 17 years.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey can try to force us out of a strike, but they can\u8217\'20\'19t force us to not quit, and that could result in an even bigger effect on the economy. Congress needs to think long and hard before they force it within the strike, because if they do, it will have a much bigger strike as people resign and leave the industry,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220914ei9e000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
