\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Mass firings, wage cuts and open hostility: workers are still unionizing despite obstacles\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,172 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 September 2022\par 14:35 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Companies are hiring anti-union consultants and deploying hardball tactics when workers threaten to form a union\par \par \uc2 Brandi McNease worked at a Chipotle in Augusta, Maine, where she and her co-workers had filed a union election petition in June 2022, the first location of the fast-food retail chain to do so.\par \par \uc2 Chipotle closed the store permanently on 19 July, right before a hearing with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) on the union election, leaving workers saying they were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newscentermaine.com/article/money/business/chipotle-blacklists-augusta-maine-employees-who-filed-for-union-food-business/97-ed587a53-0828-425a-8922-7585a579b341"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 blacklisted}}}\uc2  from being hired at other locations in the area. Chipotle {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/07/20/business/economy/chipotle-union-maine.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  the store was shut down due to staffing problems. Workers have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/01-CA-299617"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/01-CA-299533"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unfair labor practice charges}}}\uc2  with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  over the store closure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEssentially, they waited until the morning, then when they realized that we weren\u8217\'20\'19t going to quit, they flipped the checkerboard,\u8221\'20\'1D said McNease.\par \par \uc2 Such events have become commonplace in the US amid a widespread unionization drive that has taken on some of the biggest names in corporate America, from \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s to Amazon and Chipotle. As the drive has moved forward \u8211\'20\'13 amid signs of increased public support for unions \u8211\'20\'13 corporations have strongly opposed unionization and attempted to dissuade efforts by hiring expensive law firms and consultants while deploying a suite of other hardball tactics, from firings to branch closings.\par \par \uc2 A recent Gallup {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/398303/approval-labor-unions-highest-point-1965.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 poll}}}\uc2  found 71% of Americans approve of labor unions, the highest approval rate since 1965. The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has seen a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/correction-first-three-quarters-union-election-petitions-up-58-exceeding"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 58% increase}}}\uc2  in union election petitions in the first three-quarters of fiscal year 2022. The number of strikes {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vox.com/recode/2022/8/30/23326654/2022-union-charts-elections-wins-strikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased 76%}}}\uc2  in the first half of 2022 compared with 2021, with nearly three times the number of workers on strike.\par \par \uc2 The US labor movement has seen a resurgence with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/02/joe-biden-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden administration}}}\uc2  and the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  more supportive of worker organizing than previous administrations. Despite this, US employers vehemently oppose unionization efforts and investigations of labor law violations have been delayed due to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/17/us-labor-agency-union-activity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 underfunding}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.morningstar.com/news/marketwatch/20220903368/biden-marks-labor-day-with-call-for-congress-to-pass-pro-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stagnated}}}\uc2  labor law reforms in Congress.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A lot of us were getting burnt out\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Workers in the midst of union organizing campaigns have reported mass firings of union-supporting employees, and accused corporations of shutting down stores in response to union campaigns. The number of charges filed for unfair labor practices increased {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/correction-first-three-quarters-union-election-petitions-up-58-exceeding"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 16%}}}\uc2  in the first three-quarters of 2022 compared with 2021.\par \par \uc2 At a \uc2 Petco\uc2  store in Aurora Village, Washington, workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/19-RC-300867"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed}}}\uc2  a petition for a union election in August 2022 in an effort to become the first unionized store at the corporate pet retail chain. But they recently had to pull their petition after \uc2 Petco\uc2  attempted to break up the bargaining unit in their store, they claim.\par \par \uc2 Olivia O\u8217\'20\'19Neill, a dog groomer at the \uc2 Petco\uc2  store for more than two years, explained that when the union campaign went public, management first tried to feel out the sentiments of workers and began to fix some underlying issues, such as the air conditioning in the store.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were having our hours cut, we weren\u8217\'20\'19t being paid according to the cost of living here and a lot of us were getting burnt out and didn\u8217\'20\'19t have time off,\u8221\'20\'1D said O\u8217\'20\'19Neill.\par \par \uc2 Mason Cross, a merchandising associate at the \uc2 Petco\uc2  store, said many full-time workers have seen their hours cut from 40 hours a week to 30, with part-time workers experiencing cuts from 20 hours to fewer than 10 hours a week.\par \par \uc2 Cross said \uc2 Petco\uc2  challenged their union election withdrawal and argued to the regional \uc2 NLRB\uc2  that the store should be barred from filing another election petition for six months. He also claimed the store manager \u8211\'20\'13 who he says has been standoffish toward workers supporting the union \u8211\'20\'13 said pro-union flyers violated \uc2 Petco\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s anti-solicitation policy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur general manager has, especially, been pretty hostile toward union supporters. He\u8217\'20\'19s been going as far as, you know, doing illegal things such as removing our union flyers from our break room repeatedly,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cross. \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like they know exactly who is very vocal and unionizing and they are trying to discourage us from moving forward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Petco\uc2  did not respond to multiple requests for comment.\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We still prevailed\u8217\'20\'19: workers gain new energy to organize\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , pro-union workers at more than 225 stores around the US have won elections so far, but they have also filed numerous unfair labor practice charges over the company\u8217\'20\'19s opposition to union organizing campaigns, including claims of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/business/retail-consumer/starbucks-workers-union-claims-retaliation-closing-two-cafes-2022-08-23/#:~:text=NEW%20YORK%2C%20Aug%2023%20(Reuters,safety%20reasons%20for%20the%20closures."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 store closures}}}\uc2  in response to union organizing. They also claim that nearly 100 workers have been fired in retaliation for union organizing.\par \par \uc2 Brett Taborelli worked at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for eight years in the Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, area until August 2022, when his manager disputed his scheduling availability, which he had maintained for the past four years. He was locked out of the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  system and removed from the schedule after he tried to invoke his right to union representation during a meeting with the manager.\par \par \uc2 Taborelli was a vocal union supporter at his store, which workers voted to unionize in June 2022. He claimed he never received termination papers from \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and that the company was disputing his unemployment benefits claim. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  did not comment on these specific allegations.\par \par \uc2 In early August 2022, Taborelli said, managers followed him into the store when he arrived at 5.30am to start his scheduled shift. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never felt more harassed in my entire life, or more creeped out,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Tori Tambellini, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  barista in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania, also claimed she was fired in retaliation for union organizing after she and her co-workers won their union election in May 2022 despite the company\u8217\'20\'19s aggressive anti-union campaign.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was, overall, a nightmare. But somehow, we still prevailed and we won our election,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tambellini.\par \par \uc2 She claimed that she was ultimately fired for time and attendance issues, but that some of the violations cited on her termination papers didn\u8217\'20\'19t align with the hours she worked.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey have just been on this scorched-earth method of union busting,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tambellini. \u8220\'20\'1CThey are just doing literally anything in their power, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s illegal or not, to at least just slow down our momentum. But we don\u8217\'20\'19t plan on stopping any time soon. In fact, it\u8217\'20\'19s helped us organize even harder and made us more motivated.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has denied all claims of retaliation and union-busting, and instead characterized all firings as linked to policy violations. The company, however, lost an appeal to an \uc2 NLRB\uc2  order to reinstate seven workers in Memphis, Tennessee, who were fired in January 2022. Other allegations remain under review.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesperson said in an email: \u8220\'20\'1CTori and Brett are no longer with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for violations of our time and attendance policies. Our partners receive training on our policies and are aware that failing to uphold them can result in termination.\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has also denied that policies are enforced inconsistently or arbitrarily.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220913ei9d001bf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
