\par\par \uc2 Environment\par \b \uc2 Grapes, berries and robots: is Silicon Valley coming for farm workers jobs?\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,121 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 September 2022\par 14:14 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The global ag-tech revolution has sped up in recent years, spurring a debate on how it will affect the workforce\par \par \uc2 The robots have arrived in California\u8217\'20\'19s fields. This summer, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sfchronicle.com/food/wine/article/Self-driving-tractors-could-be-widespread-in-17238880.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 self-driving tractor}}}\uc2  was spotted working rows of vines in Napa valley. Described as resembling a \u8220\'20\'1Csouped-up golf cart\u8221\'20\'1D, the tractor runs on an electric battery and can be operated remotely with an app.\par \par \uc2 Farther south, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/business/story/2022-07-26/strawberry-picking-robots-california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strawberry harvesting robots}}}\uc2  have been picking fruit. Complete with wheels, clipper-tipped arms and a catchment tray, its maker claims the machine can pick almost as many berries as a human with 95% accuracy.\par \par \uc2 Did you go berry picking this summer? We bet you didn't pick as much as this robot \u8212\'20\'14 called the "R2D2 of the strawberry industry" \u8212\'20\'14 which can pick up to 800 berries every hour! ?? (Robot and video by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TortugaAgTech?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @TortugaAgTech}}}\uc2  ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/1AC97KOj9a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/1AC97KOj9a}}}\uc2  \u8212\'20\'14 Code.org (@codeorg) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/codeorg/status/1562140752318046209?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The global ag-tech revolution has accelerated in recent years as the climate crisis puts a strain on farmers and crops, and the pandemic continues to disrupt the workforce on which the industry depends. In California, where much of this technology is being developed and tested, that\u8217\'20\'19s raised complex questions for the state\u8217\'20\'19s farm workers.\par \par \uc2 Not all workers view automation as a bad thing, advocates say, because it has the potential to alleviate difficult aspects of the job. But they also fear the rush to automate is being done without their input, and in a way that privileges farm owners, tech developers and investors without considering the consequences for workers.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a debate that comes as California farm workers are already fighting for more rights and protections. In August, the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2 , the largest farm workers\u8217\'20\'19 union in the US, completed a 24-day, 335-mile {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ufw.org/momentum-builds-as-5000-to-join-final-leg-of-24-day-335-mile-ufw-march-for-the-governors-signature/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 march to the capital}}}\uc2  in Sacramento, demanding the governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , sign a bill that would make it easier to unionize without fear and intimidation from employers. (Newsom, who owns a vineyard, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://calmatters.org/commentary/2022/09/newsom-feels-the-heat-on-farm-labor-measure/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has yet to sign}}}\uc2  the bill despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/administration/3629225-biden-backs-farmworkers-union-bill-as-pressure-on-newsom-grows/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pressure}}}\uc2  from the White House this week.)\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the same issue with automation in any industry, is it going to replace jobs? And, if so, is it replacing jobs with higher paid wages?\u8221\'20\'1D said Maria Cadenas, the executive director of the nonprofit Santa Cruz Community Ventures, an organisation that provides financial support and programs to low-income families in the Monterey Bay area.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re looking at systems that were not designed to have shared wealth distribution, we\u8217\'20\'19re looking at systems that were designed to continue to extract and build wealth toward the owners.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She cites the example of how mechanisation brought into {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://boomcalifornia.org/2013/06/24/thinking-through-the-tomato-harvester/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tomato harvesting}}}\uc2  in the 1960s resulted in an estimated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenation.com/article/archive/farm-workers-next-battle/#:~:text=By%201972%2C%20after%20implementation%20of%20the%20U.C%2DBlackwelder%20tomato%20harvester%2C%2032%2C000%20workers%20had%20lost%20their%20the%20jobs%20and%20the%20work%20force%20shrank%20to%2018%2C000."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 32,000 farm workers}}}\uc2  losing their jobs and pushing hundreds of small farms out of business. Writing on the impact of automation of tomato processing in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thenation.com/article/archive/farm-workers-next-battle/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 1978 article}}}\uc2  for the Nation, the farm labor leader Cesar Chavez highlighted the human cost of this \u8220\'20\'1Cwonderful technology\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CResearch should benefit everyone, workers as well as growers,\u8221\'20\'1D he wrote.\par \par \uc2 Using crisis as a selling point\par \par \uc2 Silicon Valley sits alongside the vast Central Valley region known as America\u8217\'20\'19s bread bowl, where an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.ucsusa.org/marcia-delonge/in-californias-central-valley-drought-is-a-growing-threat-to-farms-food-and-people/#:~:text=The%20Central%20Valley%20alone%20produces,percent%20of%20the%20nation%27s%20food."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 estimated 25%}}}\uc2  of the country\u8217\'20\'19s food is grown and harvested by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://latinocaucus.legislature.ca.gov/sites/latinocaucus.legislature.ca.gov/files/CRB%20Report%20on%20Farmworkers%20in%20CA%20S-13-017.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tens of thousands of workers}}}\uc2 . In 2020, ag-tech startups in California {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://business.ca.gov/industries/agriculture-and-ag-tech/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 received $5.6bn}}}\uc2  in venture capital funding, more than the next four states combined, composing 20% of the world\u8217\'20\'19s total funding in the industry.\par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 pandemic has presented new opportunities for Silicon Valley to pitch its technology, says Emily Reisman, an assistant professor of environment and sustainability at the University of Buffalo. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/abs/10.1080/03066150.2021.1934674?journalCode=fjps20"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 2021 paper}}}\uc2 , Reisman examined how webinars and live presentations across 45 ag-tech events from March 2020 to September 2020 discussed the untapped potential.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be a critical error to go into a pitch and not mention coronavirus or the global pandemic,\u8221\'20\'1D one ag-tech startup CEO told his peers in a webinar on startup funding during the pandemic. Another ag-tech founder echoed the sentiment, while executives also claimed fears of labour shortages are poised to accelerate automation.\par \par \uc2 Reisman argues that using crises as a selling point risks characterising automation as altruistic, and obscuring the impact on the farm workers who could be displaced.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany people who are existing farm workers aren\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily resistant to automation, many of them are getting older, many of them see these tools as potentially relieving some of the physical burden of farm work,\u8221\'20\'1D said Reisman. \u8220\'20\'1CBut, if we think of the pandemic as justifying them, there are some risks.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reisman said automation could also create immigration risks for farm workers, many of whom come from Latin America. Farm groups have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/article/usa-agriculture-immigration/biggest-us-farm-group-calls-for-immigration-reform-idUSL2N0AK7JX20130115"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  leading proponents }}}\uc2  for immigration reforms that support guest workers and undocumented immigrants, she explained, but that support could wane or disappear as automation decreases the industry\u8217\'20\'19s dependency on foreign workers.\par \par \uc2 Reckoning with history\par \par \uc2 Some say that while automation is on the rise, it\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely to make farm workers entirely redundant. There are some jobs that robots may simply not be able to do, argues Giev Kashkooli, the political and legislative director for the \uc2 United Farm Workers of America\uc2 , such as discerning which crops are ready to be picked off a plant that can be packed and sold aside from immature or rotten crops.\par \par \uc2 Armando Elenes\uc2 , a farm worker organiser and secretary treasurer of the \uc2 United Farm Workers\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://civileats.com/2020/09/29/automated-harvest-is-coming-what-will-it-mean-for-farmworkers-and-rural-communities/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told }}}\uc2  Civil Eats in 2020 that he was sceptical of that robots would replace farm workers, which he said had been touted for more than a decade. He said the union is more focused on protecting workers, their rights and expanding the union\u8217\'20\'19s base.\par \par \uc2 Farm workers have historically been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nps.gov/articles/000/new-era-of-farm-worker-organizing.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 treated poorly}}}\uc2  by the agriculture industry and have had to organize and fight for any gains to their working conditions and wages. Ricardo Salvador, a senior scientist and director of the Food and Environment Program at \uc2 Union of Concerned Scientists\uc2 , argued this history needs to be addressed by those advocating for new technologies if they are going to live up to the promised benefits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers are [the ones] contributing to the bottom line and making profit possible for farmers, ranchers and industry,\u8221\'20\'1D said Salvador, and suggests this knowledge should be harnessed by technology companies looking to innovate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorking together with farm laborers, who are the people that are most familiar with the way that the operations work, and enlisting them as allies and co-beneficiaries of technological improvement,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Ccould actually accelerate and maybe even improve its application.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220908ei98001jp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
