\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US added 315,000 jobs in August as strong market defies signs of worsening economy\b0\par\par\uc2 Dominic Rushe in New York \par \uc2 577 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 September 2022\par 15:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Numbers come as \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  says \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  intends to continue interest rate hikes to try to tamp down inflation\par \par \uc2 The US added another 315,000 jobs in August as the jobs market remained strong amid signs of a worsening economy.\par \par \uc2 The US jobs market lost 22m jobs in early 2020 at the start of the pandemic but roared back after the Covid lockdowns ended. It has remained strong despite four-decade-high rates of inflation and slowing economic growth. In July, the US unexpectedly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/aug/05/us-jobs-report-july-2022-unemployment-rate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 added 526,000 new jobs}}}\uc2 , restoring employment to pre-pandemic levels.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate ticked up to 3.7% in August from 3.5% in July but is still close to a 50-year low. The report also showed more people came off the sidelines in August and started looking for work.\par \par \uc2 The remarkable strength of the jobs market has the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  worried that as employers compete for workers, wage inflation will keep driving prices higher. Average hourly earnings have increased by 5.2% in the last 12 months \u8211\'20\'13 faster than the 3% annual growth pre-pandemic but less than the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/10/us-inflation-rate-july-2022"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 8.5% annual rate of inflation}}}\uc2  recorded in July.\par \par \uc2 The central bank has sharply increased interest rates in the hope of cooling the economy and bringing down prices.\par \par \uc2 Last week the Fed chair, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 , made clear the Fed intends to keep raising rates sharply as the central bank struggles to tamp down inflation. His speech triggered a meltdown on Wall Street, with the Dow Jones index {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/aug/26/fed-inflation-jerome-powell-job-losses-jackson-hole"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 losing 1,000 points.}}}\uc2  The latest jobs report is the last to be released before the Fed meets again in September.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Vanden Houten\uc2 , lead US economist at Oxford Economics, said there was \u8220\'20\'1Cmuch to like in August\u8217\'20\'19s job report\u8221\'20\'1D but added that it was unlikely to change Fed policy. \u8220\'20\'1CThe modest slowdown in employment growth in August may be welcome by the Fed, but it won\u8217\'20\'19t prevent further sizable rate hikes in the months ahead,\u8221\'20\'1D she wrote in a note to investors.\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week the White House press secretary, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , told reporters that the White House was \u8220\'20\'1Cexpecting job numbers to cool off a bit\u8221\'20\'1D as the economy transitions from the \u8220\'20\'1Chistoric economic growth that we saw last year to a more stable and steady growth\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There are mixed signals about the health of the job market. Large employers including \uc2 Ford\uc2  and \uc2 Walmart\uc2  have announced plans for widespread layoffs, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pwc.com/us/en/library/pulse-survey/managing-business-risks.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 50% of businesses }}}\uc2  surveyed by \uc2 PriceWaterhouseCoopers\uc2  last month said they were reducing their headcount or planning to.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, the government reported this week there were 11.2m open job positions in July \u8211\'20\'13 two openings for every unemployed person. New claims for jobless benefits \u8211\'20\'13 seen as a proxy for layoffs \u8211\'20\'13 fell last week to a two-month low.\par \par \uc2 The government\u8217\'20\'19s latest job report follows the monthly survey of private employers from ADP, the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest payroll supplier. Private employers added only 132,000 jobs in August \u8211\'20\'13 less than half the number ADP calculates were added in July.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur data suggests a shift toward a more conservative pace of hiring, possibly as companies try to decipher the economy\u8217\'20\'19s conflicting signals,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , chief economist at ADP. \u8220\'20\'1CWe could be at an inflection point, from supercharged job gains to something more normal.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220902ei9200232\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
