\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Americans\u8217\'20\'19 support for labor unions at highest in nearly 60 years\b0\par\par\uc2 Erum Salam \par \uc2 330 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2022\par 18:24 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gallup survey finds 71% of US residents approve of unions amid a wave of successful organizing efforts\par \par \uc2 A new {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/398303/approval-labor-unions-highest-point-1965.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Gallup annual Work and Education survey}}}\uc2  found that Americans\u8217\'20\'19 approval of labor unions is at its highest in nearly 60 years.\par \par \uc2 Seventy-one per cent of US residents approve of unions, as high as in 1965. The 1950s saw the highest support for unions, at three in four Americans, according to the polling firm.\par \par \uc2 Labor unions\u8217\'20\'19 approval rate has been steadily climbing in recent years, from 64% before the pandemic to last year, when it was at 68%.\par \par \uc2 The survey also found about one in six Americans live in a household where at least one resident belongs to a union.\par \par \uc2 Of the union members surveyed, 65% cited better pay and benefits as the top reason for joining a union. The second-most-chosen reason was employee rights and representation.\par \par \uc2 The news comes after a wave of successful union organizing efforts in industries across the country, such as food service, education, transportation, tech, media and others. Corporations such as Amazon, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Chipotle are seeing their workers form unions despite {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/25/starbucks-union-employees-fired"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 efforts}}}\uc2  to prevent such organizing.\par \par \uc2 Among non-union members interviewed by Gallup, 58% said they had no interest in joining at all, while 11% said they were extremely interested. The survey also found that while engagement at work was higher among non-union members, they were more likely to be actively looking for a new job than union members.\par \par \uc2 The survey concluded: \u8220\'20\'1CThe low unemployment rate that developed during the pandemic altered the balance of power between employers and employees, creating an environment fostering union membership that has resulted in the formation of unions at several high-profile companies. While already on an upswing, public approval of unions has only increased further during the pandemic and is now at a level not seen in nearly six decades.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220830ei8u002jr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
