\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18People are tired of being ignored while the rich get richer\u8217\'20\'19: Bernie Sanders on anger and hope in the US and UK\b0\par\par\uc2 Owen Jones \par \uc2 2,596 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 August 2022\par 09:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 4\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The Vermont senator rose from the political margins to become hugely influential within the American left. As he prepares to speak at a London rally, he explains why unions on both sides of the Atlantic must reassert their power\par \par \uc2 Both are unlikely political sensations who were long consigned to the fringes: Bernie Sanders, an octogenarian US senator who inspired an army of voters far younger than himself; and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/aug/23/mick-lynch-rmt-work-dignity-union-power"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mick Lynch}}}\uc2 , a former blacklisted construction worker and child of Irish immigrants who, as the leader of the \uc2 Rail, Maritime and Transport Workers union\uc2  (RMT), shot to national prominence when he humbled {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jul/31/praise-be-to-mick-lynch-the-brexit-arse-made-good"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hostile but underinformed broadcast journalists}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CI think Lynch is touching a nerve,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders says.\par \par \uc2 The de facto leader of the US left has swung his considerable political heft behind a new campaign \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wesayenough.co.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Enough Is Enough}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 launched to fight Britain\u8217\'20\'19s mounting cost of living crisis, which was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/aug/24/enough-is-enough-movement-gathers-pace-with-andy-burnham-latest-backer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 founded in part by Lynch and the RMT}}}\uc2 . It has certainly touched a nerve: at a recent rally in Clapham, south London, many of those who had queued around the block were turned away for lack of space. \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18Enough is enough\u8217\'20\'19, funnily enough, is an expression we use a lot here,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders says. \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are sick and tired of often working longer hours for low wages; sick and tired of their kids having a lower standard of living than them; and they\u8217\'20\'19re sick and tired of billionaires getting richer and richer while they fall behind.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhy, with all this new tech out there, are they not seeing an improved standard of living? Why not more equality, rather than less equality? Why are living standards deteriorating, not improving? Lynch is asking that, Enough Is Enough is asking that \u8211\'20\'13 and it\u8217\'20\'19s hitting a nerve, because people are tired of being ignored while the rich get richer.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Political cut-through is something Sanders knows a lot about, but it was only something he really achieved in his 70s. Born into a working-class Jewish family in New York, he became the mayor of Burlington, Vermont, at 40, later becoming a House representative and a senator. A longstanding independent, albeit one who has frequently allied with the Democratic party, Sanders championed causes long eschewed by mainstream Democrats, such as universal healthcare, the abolition of student fees, workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights and the anti-war movement. But his dramatic rise \u8211\'20\'13 when he was transformed from a marginalised figure to a frontrunner for the Democratic presidential nomination in 2016 \u8211\'20\'13 was driven by two major factors.\par \par \uc2 One was the financial crash, which exposed inequalities and insecurities that disproportionately fell on the backs of younger Americans. The other was the expectations raised by the election of \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  in 2008, which, for millions of Americans with stagnating living standards, ultimately felt dashed. Although neither his 2016 or 2020 bids succeeded, they mobilised a movement that revitalised the US left and transformed it into a major political force in the Democratic party and beyond.\par \par \uc2 This brings his attention back to a perennial passion \u8211\'20\'13 and what he wants to talk to me about: the prospects of the US labour movement. We speak over the phone, but he hits all his rousing lines with the zest of a platform rally. The thread that runs through all his answers is class politics. This is less of a novelty in progressive politics on the British side of the Atlantic \u8211\'20\'13 to rousing cheers at a recent Enough Is Enough rally, Lynch proclaimed: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffingtonpost.co.uk/entry/mick-lynch-tells-enough-is-enough-campaign-working-class-is-back_uk_62fdf347e4b071ea958cdee7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe working class is back\u8221\'20\'1D }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 but it was long considered alien in a US that peddled a myth of classlessness. This was a politically convenient myth in a country where, Sanders notes, three rich men have more wealth than the poorest half.\par \par \uc2 But the Brooklyn-born Vermont senator has a new mission: to deploy his political weight behind efforts to unite the struggles of the US and British labour movements. On Wednesday, Sanders will bring his trademark oratory to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rmt.org.uk/news/us-senator-bernie-sanders-to-join-rmt-rally-on24822/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 an RMT rally in central London}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The labour movements in the US and Britain are significantly weaker than most of their western counterparts. In the US, trade unions had been long hobbled by \u8220\'20\'1Cred scares\u8221\'20\'1D and anti-union so-called \u8220\'20\'1Cright to work\u8221\'20\'1D laws, but they were severely weakened under \uc2 Ronald Reagan\uc2 , whose administration, in 1981, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theintercept.com/2021/08/06/middle-class-reagan-patco-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fired more than 11,000 striking air traffic controllers}}}\uc2  to send a salutory lesson to other workers. Today, little more than one in 10 US workers are unionised. British trade unionism did not suffer such a comprehensive rout, but the number of organised workers \u8211\'20\'13 about a quarter of the workforce \u8211\'20\'13 is half the level of the peak in 1979.\par \par \uc2 Does Sanders believe both labour movements are learning lessons? \u8220\'20\'1CI think what we\u8217\'20\'19re beginning to see here in the US is a significant acceleration of trade union organising,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are seeing more workers organising in unions, filing with the National Labour Relations Board [\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ] to get certification \u8211\'20\'13 more than for a very long time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What has made him particularly optimistic is workers\u8217\'20\'19 struggles in the union deserts of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Amazon. Sanders recently joined striking \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers on a picket line in Boston. After {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/25/starbucks-union-employees-fired"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 85 union organisers were fired by the coffee chain}}}\uc2  in recent months \u8211\'20\'13 the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has filed multiple complaints against the firm \u8211\'20\'13 his support has boosted the national profile of the fight. \u8220\'20\'1CIn \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and Amazon, hundreds are joining unions \u8211\'20\'13 in Amazon, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jul/12/amazon-workers-union-push-north-carolina-kentucky"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 they\u8217\'20\'19re taking on Jeff Bezos}}}\uc2 , the second wealthiest person in the world. We\u8217\'20\'19re seeing struggles in university campuses, hospitals, nurses \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing unprecedented organising compared to what we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in recent years.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But he touches on an apparent contradiction: \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the middle classes decline while the rich become richer and richer, there\u8217\'20\'19s more support for the trade union movement in the US \u8211\'20\'13 people feel much stronger about unions than previously.\u8221\'20\'1D And he is right: last year, 68% of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/354455/approval-labor-unions-highest-point-1965.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Americans told pollsters they approved of unions}}}\uc2 , the highest level since 1965, while polling in the UK has shown that most working-age Britons {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://yougov.co.uk/topics/economy/survey-results/daily/2022/08/18/fa57c/2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 back the current wave of strikes}}}\uc2 . Yet that hasn\u8217\'20\'19t translated into most joining a union. Why?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the US, corporations make it very hard for workers to exercise their constitutional rights to form a union,\u8221\'20\'1D Sanders says. \u8220\'20\'1C[Last Wednesday], the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  found \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  had fired workers and rescheduled those shop workers who were forming unions \u8211\'20\'13 which is illegal. We are seeing companies threatening workers that they\u8217\'20\'19ll go to China. There\u8217\'20\'19s massive corporate opposition to workers forming unions in the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He highlights another formidable barrier: \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve got a media in the country which is certainly not sympathetic to unions, which will very rarely discuss the benefits of unions, like better working conditions, wages, pensions, et cetera, et cetera. The media is obviously owned by a handful of large corporations who don\u8217\'20\'19t talk about class issues, economic issues. All of that contributes to making it harder for workers to become organised.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But there is a tradition of militancy among US workers, despite attempts to scrub it out, not least in the 1950s under McCarthyism. During the Great Depression of the 1930s, waves of strikes rippled across US society. Does Sanders see a parallel? \u8220\'20\'1CYes, I do. In the 1930s, there was a massive increase in organising and membership, and workers fought valiantly \u8211\'20\'13 they did sit-ins, took on powerful interests. What we are seeing now is real frustration in terms of inflation accounting for wages, with the average US worker earning less than almost 50 years ago \u8211\'20\'13 taking into account productivity gains, slightly worse than then. That\u8217\'20\'19s insane!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Given the likes of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  have so long succeeded in suppressing labour organising, why has there been a blaze of activity? \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ll tell you why, in my view: a lot of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers are younger people. Many of them have college degrees and they\u8217\'20\'19re looking around them: their wages aren\u8217\'20\'19t keeping up with inflation, they can\u8217\'20\'19t afford housing or healthcare or student debt, they\u8217\'20\'19re falling further and further behind compared to their parents, and they\u8217\'20\'19re standing up to the owner of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 saying: \u8216\'20\'18You\u8217\'20\'19re worth $4bn! What\u8217\'20\'19s your problem with allowing us to organise workers?\u8217\'20\'19 And his response is simply to try and fire workers and intimidate them. To some degree, this is a multiracial generational fight \u8211\'20\'13 primarily of younger people, but not exclusively \u8211\'20\'13 standing up to a billionaire.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has denied all allegations of retaliation. A spokesperson told the Guardian previously that \u8220\'20\'1Cthese individuals are no longer with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for store policy violations. A partner\u8217\'20\'19s interest in a union does not exempt them from the standards we have always held. We will continue enforcing our policies consistently for all partners.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the relationship between the US labour movement and younger progressives has not always been harmonious, to say the least. In the 1960s and 1970s, US labour was led by the gruff former plumber George Meany, a zealous supporter of the Vietnam war, who relished denouncing student protesters as \u8220\'20\'1Ckookies\u8221\'20\'1D. The nadir came in the form of the so-called Hard Hat Riot of 1970, when hundreds of construction and office workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/hard-hat-riot-1970-pitted-construction-workers-against-anti-war-protestors-180974831/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 physically attacked student protesters in New York}}}\uc2 . Is there hope this time for solidarity between trade unionists and the rising US younger left?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat kind of unity is something we are working on very hard,\u8221\'20\'1D says Sanders. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve now held three rallies with progressive union leaderships \u8211\'20\'13 with Sean O\u8217\'20\'19Brien, the new president of the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , and \uc2 Sarah Nelson\uc2 , the president of the \uc2 Association of Flight Attendants\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 in Chicago, Philadelphia and Boston. What we see at these rallies is unionists coming together with younger progressives \u8211\'20\'13 and the unity of those forces, young people fighting for economic and racial justice with a union movement, has incredible potential. To answer the question: it\u8217\'20\'19s absolutely imperative we bring them together \u8211\'20\'13 and we are trying to do that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 When US workers fought bosses in the 1930s, they enjoyed the advantage of the sympathy and political muscle of the president, \uc2 Franklin D Roosevelt\uc2 . \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has repeatedly vowed to be \u8220\'20\'1Cthe most pro-union president ever\u8221\'20\'1D, but his career has long been wedded to establishment and \u8220\'20\'1Ccentrist\u8221\'20\'1D factions in the Democratic party. Sanders says he knows the president \u8220\'20\'1Creasonably well\u8221\'20\'1D and points to the 110-page policy platform his team hammered out with Biden\u8217\'20\'19s campaign team in 2020, with taskforces covering areas ranging from healthcare to the environment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat the president recognised is that there was, and is, a movement of working people, of young people, who are sick and tired of the status quo, and I think, when we did the American Rescue Plan [to help the US through the pandemic], it was one of the most consequential pieces of legislation for working people in modern history. When we did the Build Back Better legislation [a huge package of measures related to social policy and the climate crisis], it had the support of the president for a multibillion-dollar transformational programme, and it was sabotaged by a couple of conservative senators, but he said: \u8216\'20\'18I will stand by the working people of the country and take on the big monied interests.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This differs from some of the more pessimistic narratives about Biden from the US left, which is still reeling from Sanders\u8217\'20\'19 two presidential-nomination defeats. But his optimism springs not so much from naivety about Biden as from a firm belief in the ability of struggles from below to bend the powerful to act in workers\u8217\'20\'19 interests. \u8220\'20\'1CYou\u8217\'20\'19re seeing a progressive movement of people in every state of this country which is beginning to go beyond incremental politics, asking: \u8216\'20\'18How does it happen that every rich country on Earth \u8211\'20\'13 including the UK \u8211\'20\'13 has universal healthcare, while we have a dysfunctional system? Why in other countries is university education free, when in this country it\u8217\'20\'19s outrageously expensive?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I put it to him that his campaigns tapped into discontent, but magnified it and gave it direction. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat my campaign did was to raise issues, and the establishment suddenly discovered millions of people not happy with the status quo who wanted transformational change,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. Sanders gives the example of the president last week committing to cancel up to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/aug/26/biden-student-debt-loans-hamilton-nolan"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $10,000 (\u163\'00\'A38,500) of student debt}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CDid it go as far as I wanted? No, but is it a significant step forward to alleviate the terrible burden that young people are suffering? Yes, it will help a lot.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another example is the recently passed Inflation Reduction Act, which, among other things, lowers prescription drug prices and promotes clean energy. \u8220\'20\'1CAgain, it didn\u8217\'20\'19t go as far as we campaigned on, but, on many of those issues, part of what we demanded has been implemented.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 What next for the US left? The youthful optimism of this 80-year-old senator appears limitless. Next, he says, they will grow the labour movement and tie it to the progressive movement. \u8220\'20\'1CYou may or may not know, but, come January, in terms of politics, there will be a stronger underlying progressive presence in the House than at any time in modern history. We are seeing accomplishments at the political level, at the organising level, so we are making progress.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yet all of this relies on forcing a president to go beyond his comfort zone. Sanders remains one of the most popular politicians in the US, and his campaigns encouraged a galvanised US left to dare to dream of achieving outright political power. What lessons would a future campaign learn from his attempts, which transformed political debate in the US, but failed to secure him the presidency?\par \par \uc2 Sanders does a laugh anyone will recognise \u8211\'20\'13 the \u8220\'20\'1CI do not want to talk about this now\u8221\'20\'1D laugh. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a long question \u8211\'20\'13 a very long question!\u8221\'20\'1D Again, he highlights his campaigns\u8217\'20\'19 signature accomplishments \u8211\'20\'13 underlining that \u8220\'20\'1Ca significant part of society is not happy with the status quo, that they\u8217\'20\'19re sick and tired of income and wealth inequality and they want fundamental changes in our economic and political system\u8221\'20\'1D. But he clearly believes he was hobbled by establishment hostility. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you take on the political establishment and the media establishment and the corporate establishment \u8230\'20\'26 it\u8217\'20\'19s not an easy thing to do. We need time and we certainly didn\u8217\'20\'19t have that luxury.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I wonder, too, if he recognises that Enough Is Enough has emerged in large part because of a vacuum left by a Labour leadership that has abandoned any pretence of transformative change. Sanders is diplomatic. \u8220\'20\'1CI think it\u8217\'20\'19s not dissimilar to what we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing in the Democratic party here \u8211\'20\'13 I\u8217\'20\'19m not commenting on the Labour party; I don\u8217\'20\'19t know enough,\u8221\'20\'1D he says. Referring to traditional left-of-centre parties struggling in the global north, he adds: \u8220\'20\'1CBecause working-class people are increasingly alienated from the political process, those parties are not delivering for them. That\u8217\'20\'19s why the Democrats have a choice to make: are they the party of the working class or the elite?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The legacy of Sanders, surely, is that he brought together otherwise fragmented and disillusioned pockets of discontent into a highly visible and articulate movement with confident demands. Maybe \u8211\'20\'13 just maybe \u8211\'20\'13 he can help pull off the same trick by helping to unite the increasingly assertive labour movements on both sides of the Atlantic.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220830ei8u0015s\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
