\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Minnesota nurses\u8217\'20\'19 strike vote puts safety and conditions in spotlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 810 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 August 2022\par 09:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 15,000-strong \uc2 Minnesota Nurses Association\uc2  authorized to call what would be one of the largest nurses\u8217\'20\'19 strikes in US history\par \par \uc2 Throughout the Covid-19 pandemic, nurses around the US have faced deteriorating working conditions and challenges, from safety concerns to increasing workloads that have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mnnurses.org/minnesota-nurses-share-why-we-left-the-bedside-in-new-report-call-for-solutions-to-staffing-and-retention-crisis/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stemmed }}}\uc2  from understaffing as nurses have quit their jobs or retired early.\par \par \uc2 Those nurses who are still on the job at many hospitals say they have been expected to do more with fewer resources, an issue that nurses say is causing retention crises and jeopardizing patient safety and care.\par \par \uc2 Now nurses at 15 hospitals in the Twin Cities area (Minneapolis-St Paul) and Duluth, Minnesota, that are negotiating new union contracts with their respective hospitals have overwhelmingly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mnnurses.org/15000-nurses-authorize-strike-as-they-fight-for-fair-contracts-that-put-patients-before-profits/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  to authorize a strike. A date for the work stoppage has not been set yet by the union, the \uc2 Minnesota Nurses Association\uc2 , which represents about 15,000 nurses who voted on the strike authorization, but a 10-day notice must be given ahead of any strike.\par \par \uc2 If a strike is carried out, it would be one of the largest nurses\u8217\'20\'19 strikes in US history.\par \par \uc2 Jayme Wicklund, a registered nurse at the Children\u8217\'20\'19s hospital in St Paul, Minnesota, and member of the negotiating committee, said short-staffing issues became an issue before the pandemic when the hospital preferred relying on nurses to take overtime shifts as needed rather than bolster staff numbers. When the pandemic hit, many nurses were furloughed and Wicklund said several never returned in part because of the lack of clarity on recalling those nurses.\par \par \uc2 In the midst of short-staffing, Wicklund argued nurse-to-patient ratios, which vary by department, had increased compared with before the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need more resources to take care of the patients,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wicklund. \u8220\'20\'1CThe hospitals are very focused on wages. We have to be comparable to other places. But that\u8217\'20\'19s all that they focus on. Once you start talking about wages, they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to talk about the other important issues around patient safety or actually, other ways to save money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She argued that during the current negotiations the hospitals had been much less receptive to progressing on a deal than in previous years.\par \par \uc2 The Minnesota strike vote was taken as the previous contracts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.startribune.com/twin-cities-duluth-nurses-overwhelmingly-authorize-strike/600198688/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expired}}}\uc2  on 31 May and negotiations have been at loggerheads over compensation and staffing.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://minnesotareformer.com/briefs/nurses-say-bad-management-not-covid-19-burnout-to-blame-for-exodus-of-workers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey }}}\uc2  conducted by the union in March 2022 of 748 nurses in Minnesota who left their jobs during the pandemic cited poor management and short-staffing as driving factors in leaving their positions. As nurses have left the bedside, adverse health events in hospitals {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.health.state.mn.us/news/pressrel/2022/adverse080322.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 increased}}}\uc2  by 33% from 2020 to 2021.\par \par \uc2 The union has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://minnesotareformer.com/2022/06/01/minnesota-nurses-attack-hospital-executive-pay-amid-union-negotiations/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criticized}}}\uc2  high hospital executive pay during the contract negotiations as hospital executives have deemed the wage increase requests by the union as \u8220\'20\'1Cunrealistic\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSix months ago, a year ago, our management would say, this is just temporary, we can get through this, we just have to do this for now and recently, the messaging from management has become, this is the new normal, and that\u8217\'20\'19s when I realized, they don\u8217\'20\'19t have any intention of fixing them. They are expecting us to continue to work in these conditions forever,\u8221\'20\'1D said Chris Rubesch, a registered nurse at Essentia in Duluth and first vice-president of the \uc2 Minnesota Nurses Association\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNurses want to be at the bedside, giving care to our patients and doing our jobs. So the fact that we are willing to take this extraordinary step is reflective of the severity of our staffing crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D added Rubesch. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve tried legislatively. We tried to address this in contract language. Our employers refused to work with us on that. And so we\u8217\'20\'19re left to take this extraordinary step, and we don\u8217\'20\'19t take it lightly. We find it\u8217\'20\'19s the only path to address the problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ali Marcanti, a nurse for seven years at \uc2 Allina Health\uc2  in St Paul, Minnesota, criticized hospitals\u8217\'20\'19 talking points throughout the pandemic of praising nurses as healthcare heroes while refusing to address staffing issues in contract negotiations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNegotiations, in our eyes have been our employers\u8217\'20\'19 opportunity to put their money where their mouth is, and instead of pithy statements, like \u8216\'20\'18you\u8217\'20\'19re a hero\u8217\'20\'19, actually give us some substantial improvements to our work environment that make us actually want to continue working for Allina,\u8221\'20\'1D said Marcanti.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for Essentia Health said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are disappointed by the vote because we believe no one wins in a strike \u8211\'20\'13 and we have a shared responsibility to provide quality care to the patients and communities we are privileged to serve. Should it become necessary, we have contingency plans to ensure we can continue to care for our patients in the event of an MNA strike.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220823ei8n0018h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
