\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 First Thing: Cheney turns on pro-Trump Republicans after defeat\b0\par\par\uc2 Mattha Busby \par \uc2 1,459 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 August 2022\par 13:48 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Outgoing congresswoman \uc2 Liz Cheney\uc2  takes aim at election result deniers within GOP. Plus, a dispute at \uc2 Apple\uc2  over remote working\par \par \uc2 Good morning.\par \par \uc2 The outgoing Republican member of Congress {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/20/liz-cheney-never-trump-republicans-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Liz Cheney}}}\uc2 , who lost her Wyoming seat last week after being beaten in a primary by a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 -backed opponent, has announced plans {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/21/liz-cheney-work-against-republicans-who-embrace-trump-election-lie"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 to set up a new political organization}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In an interview with ABC News aired on Sunday, she named Kevin McCarthy, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , Ted Cruz and \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 all of whom openly supported Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lie that electoral fraudsters stole the 2020 presidential race from him and handed it to the eventual victor \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m going to be very focused on working to ensure that we can do everything we can [to] not \u8230\'20\'26 elect election deniers,\u8221\'20\'1D the daughter of former vice-president \uc2 Dick Cheney\uc2  said. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m going to work against those people, I\u8217\'20\'19m going to work to support their opponents.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Going down punching. Cheney said that two Republican US senators \u8211\'20\'13 Ted Cruz from Texas and \uc2 Josh Hawley\uc2  from Missouri \u8211\'20\'13 had both made themselves \u8220\'20\'1Cunfit for future office\u8221\'20\'1D since they \u8220\'20\'1Cboth took steps that fundamentally threatened the constitutional order\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Others pay price for standing up to Trump. Last month, Arizona lawmaker \uc2 Rusty Bowers\uc2 , state house speaker for the last four of 18 years following his election, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/20/rusty-bowers-interview-trump-arizona-republicans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost his bid to stay in the Arizona legislature}}}\uc2  in a primary contest in which his opponent was endorsed by the former president.\par \par \uc2 Apple\uc2  workers launch petition over firm\u8217\'20\'19s return-to-office stance\par \par \uc2 A dispute over remote work is deepening at \uc2 Apple\uc2  after employees hit back at the tech company\u8217\'20\'19s return-to-office orders, and launched a petition saying the firm {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/aug/22/apple-workers-launch-petition-over-return-to-office-stance"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 risked stifling diversity and staff wellbeing}}}\uc2  by restricting their ability to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 The petition is in response to an all-employee memo from the \uc2 Apple\uc2  chief executive, \uc2 Tim Cook\uc2 , who last week said workers would have to come into the office for at least three days a week from September, including Tuesdays, Thursdays and a third day to be determined by individual teams.\par \par \uc2 The plan is looser than previous proposals that would have forced staff to return every Monday, Tuesday and Thursday, with Cook saying it would \u8220\'20\'1Cenhance\u8221\'20\'1D the company\u8217\'20\'19s ability to work flexibly, \u8220\'20\'1Cwhile preserving the in-person collaboration that is so essential to our culture\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Would remote work improve greater flexibility and thus promote diversity? That is the contention of the group of workers, \uc2 Apple\uc2  Together, who have circulated the petition pushing back against Cook\u8217\'20\'19s orders: \u8220\'20\'1CWe believe that \uc2 Apple\uc2  should encourage, not prohibit, flexible work to build a more diverse and successful company where we can feel comfortable to \u8216\'20\'18think different\u8217\'20\'19 together.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Alarm in Texas on mandatory \u8216\'20\'18In God We Trust\u8217\'20\'19 signs in schools\par \par \uc2 Efforts to distribute posters to Texas schools bearing a slogan which many argue has long been used {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/21/texas-in-god-we-trust-posters-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as a tool to forward Christian nationalism}}}\uc2  have been described as \u8220\'20\'1Ca basic violation of the separation of church and state\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A state law recently took effect requiring public campuses to display any donated items bearing the phrase \u8220\'20\'1CIn God We Trust\u8221\'20\'1D. The Southlake Anti-Racism Coalition (SARC) said they were \u8220\'20\'1Cdisturbed\u8221\'20\'1D by the precedent the posters\u8217\'20\'19 distribution could set and warned of the effect on students, particularly those who do not practise \u8220\'20\'1Cthe dominant Christian faith\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that state governments in places like Texas are codifying white Christian nationalist patriarchy into law at an alarming rate,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sophie Ellman-Golan, of Jews For Racial & Economic Justice. \u8220\'20\'1CThe most dangerous examples of this are bans on abortion and gender-affirming care, as well as efforts to censor education.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Who supports the move? The \uc2 Council on American-Islamic Relations\uc2  (\uc2 CAIR\uc2 ), a Muslim civil rights\u8217\'20\'19 organization, welcomed the initiative and said this might allow for an opportunity for students to learn about other faiths. \u8220\'20\'1CThe notion of trusting God is common across faiths,\u8221\'20\'1D a \uc2 CAIR\uc2  spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 In other news \u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Hundreds of Finnish women {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/aug/20/women-in-finland-post-party-videos-to-back-pm-sanna-marin"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have posted social media films of themselves dancing}}}\uc2  in \u173\'00\'ADsupport of the prime minister, \uc2 Sanna Marin\uc2 , after she became embroiled in a scandal when footage emerged of her partying with friends. She has \u8220\'20\'1Cfor my own legal protection and to clear up any doubts\u8221\'20\'1D taken a drug test.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 After a recent scaling down of large military drills, the US and South Korea are undertaking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/aug/22/us-and-south-korea-begin-largest-military-drills-for-years-as-north-ramps-up-tensions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their largest in recent years}}}\uc2  in a show of force expected to raise tensions with an increasingly hostile North Korea. It comes despite warnings to abandon the \u8220\'20\'1Chostile military stance\u8221\'20\'1D even as the north increases the frequency of missiles tests.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Former prime minister of Pakistan Imran Khan {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/aug/22/imran-khan-pakistan-former-pm-charged-under-anti-terror-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 has been charged under the anti-terror act}}}\uc2  following accusations of threats to police and a magistrate, after a fiery speech to supporters at the weekend. He was ousted in a vote of no confidence in April but has alleged a deeper conspiracy to remove him from power.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Israel\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 NSO Group\uc2 , which makes the globally controversial Pegasus spyware used to infiltrate mobile phones, has said its CEO\par \par \uc2 Shalev Hulio\par \par \uc2 would step down as part of a reorganisation. The privately owned company also said it would focus sales {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/aug/22/nso-group-ceo-shalev-hulio-step-down-israel-pegasus-spyware"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on countries belonging to the Nato alliance}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Stat of the day: endangered sea turtles found on Louisiana islands for first time in 75 years\par \par \uc2 For the first time in 75 years, hatchlings of the world\u8217\'20\'19s smallest sea turtle species {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/aug/21/louisiana-sea-turtles-chandeleur-islands-new-orleans"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 have been discovered on the Chandeleur Islands}}}\uc2 , a chain of barrier islands in the Gulf of Mexico off the coast of New Orleans.\par \par \uc2 Wildlife experts at the Breton national wildlife refuge last week reportedly documented more than 53 turtle crawls and two live hatchlings that were navigating towards the sea. The news was particularly uplifting for environmentalists, writes Maya Yang, because the hatchlings were Kemp\u8217\'20\'19s ridley sea turtles, an endangered species.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BP\uc2  oil spill resulting from the 2010 Deepwater Horizon explosion significantly affected the Chandeleur Islands, along with various hurricanes and other tropical weather systems in recent years.\par \par \uc2 Don\u8217\'20\'19t miss this: Brian Stelter rebukes CNN on final show: \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s not partisan to stand up to demagogues\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The host of CNN\u8217\'20\'19s media affairs show Reliable Sources, cancelled last week after 30 years on air, used his final episode to make {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2022/aug/22/brian-stelter-cnn-cancels-show-reliable-sources"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a pointed rebuke of the network\u8217\'20\'19s new bosses}}}\uc2  and their intention to pursue a more \u8220\'20\'1Cneutral voice\u8221\'20\'1D to its coverage.\par \par \uc2 CNN head Chris Licht, who took over after the February departure of \uc2 Jeff Zucker\uc2 , has indicated that he wants to tone down the opinion quotient of its shows and \u8220\'20\'1Creturn\u8221\'20\'1D to an older, straighter and in his view less overtly leftwing style of reporting. It comes amid criticism the network is broadcasting too much commentary and not enough on-the-ground reporting.\par \par \uc2 But Stelter said: \u8220\'20\'1CIt is not partisan to stand up for decency and democracy and dialogue \u8230\'20\'26 It is not partisan to stand up to demagogues \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s required, it\u8217\'20\'19s patriotic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Climate check: five plants that could help feed the world\par \par \uc2 Today, just three crops \u8211\'20\'13 rice, wheat and corn \u8211\'20\'13 provide nearly half of the world\u8217\'20\'19s calories. That reliance on a small number of crops has made agriculture vulnerable to pests, plant-borne diseases and soil erosion, which thrive on monoculture \u8211\'20\'13 the practice of growing only one crop at a time, writes Cecilia Nowell.\par \par \uc2 It has also meant losing out on the resilience other crops show in surviving drought and other natural disasters. So {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2022/aug/20/ancient-crops-climate-crisis-amaranth-fonio-cowpeas-taro-kernza"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here\u8217\'20\'19s a look at five crops}}}\uc2  : amaranth, fonio, cowpeas, taro and kernza, that farmers across the world are now growing in hopes of feeding the planet as it heats.\par \par \uc2 Last Thing: are personalised vitamins the key to good health?\par \par \uc2 As the rapid rise of the personalised vitamin industry has come under increasing scrutiny, journalist David Cox {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2022/aug/21/are-personalised-vitamins-key-good-health-supplements"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signs up for customised \u8220\'20\'1Cpersonal nourishments\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  to see whether they hold the secret to deeper sleep, weight loss and a sunny state of mind. \u8220\'20\'1CIt all looks bullshit,\u8221\'20\'1D Margaret Rayman, professor of nutritional medicine at the \uc2 University of Surrey\uc2 , tells him. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think there\u8217\'20\'19s much information about any of these things changing your sleep.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But a vitamin company boss vigorously defends their recommendations, saying that the research reports they use to vindicate the products they recommend are put through an extremely rigorous auditing process: \u8220\'20\'1CWe use a lot of patented ingredients because of the level of clinical data behind them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sign up\par \par \uc2 First Thing is delivered to thousands of inboxes every weekday. If you\u8217\'20\'19re not already signed up, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/info/2018/sep/17/guardian-us-morning-briefing-sign-up-to-stay-informed"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 subscribe now}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Get in touch\par \par \uc2 If you have any questions or comments about any of our newsletters please email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:newsletters@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 newsletters@theguardian.com}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220822ei8m001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
