\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US labor leaders say underfunding at federal agency has \u8216\'20\'18reached crisis stage\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,120 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 August 2022\par 11:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Union officials fear that the overstretched \uc2 NLRB\uc2  won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to handle the surge in union activity, giving corporations the upper hand\par \par \uc2 Labor leaders in the US are worried that the federal agency that oversees unionization efforts is approaching a \u8220\'20\'1Ctrue crisis\u8221\'20\'1D as it struggles with a decade of underfunding and a huge surge in union activity.\par \par \uc2 Last year saw {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/correction-first-three-quarters-union-election-petitions-up-58-exceeding"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a 58% jump in the number of union elections}}}\uc2  as union drives spread at companies including Amazon, \uc2 Apple\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and other companies. Some labor leaders say they are worried that the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) has become so understaffed that it will have a hard time investigating the flood of allegations that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has violated America\u8217\'20\'19s labor laws hundreds of times in its efforts to crush a historic unionization drive.\par \par \uc2 Union officials fear that the overstretched \uc2 NLRB\uc2  won\u8217\'20\'19t be able to handle all these cases in a timely manner \u8211\'20\'13 including cases seeking to win reinstatement of fired pro-union baristas \u8211\'20\'13 and that as a result, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  will get the upper hand in beating back the union campaign.\par \par \uc2 With the labor board\u8217\'20\'19s budget frozen for many years, union leaders voice dismay that congressional Democrats did not increase the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s funding as part of the Inflation Reduction Act, the landmark climate, healthcare and tax package that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  signed on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been blocked for nine straight years in seeking to increase the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s funding, and inflation has left its budget 25% behind where it should be,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bill Samuel, legislative director of the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the nation\u8217\'20\'19s main labor federation. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you take into account the explosion in the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s workload because of increased organizing, renewed enforcement and the increase in labor law violations, they\u8217\'20\'19ve reached a crisis stage.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Largely due to Republican opposition, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s budget has been frozen at $274m since fiscal year 2014, with Biden proposing an increase to $319m for fiscal 2023. As a result of budgetary strains, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s field staff has been cut by 37% since 2014 and 50% since 2002.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf there is one beast that Republicans love to starve the most, it\u8217\'20\'19s the part of the government that protects workers\u8217\'20\'19 ability to form unions and bargain collectively,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Andy Levin\uc2 , a Michigan Democrat. Levin said he urged the Senate majority leader, \uc2 Charles Schumer\uc2 , to increase \uc2 NLRB\uc2  funding as part of in the Inflation Reduction Act, but it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t included in the final, carefully negotiated package.\par \par \uc2 Had \uc2 NLRB\uc2  funding been part of the budgetary reconciliation package, it would have needed only a simple majority to pass the Senate \u8211\'20\'13 and avoided a Republican filibuster, a move that requires 60 votes to overcome, a nearly insurmountable obstacle for pro-labor legislation when the Democrats have just 50 Senate seats.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNot getting it into the reconciliation package means we\u8217\'20\'19re not likely to get [increased \uc2 NLRB\uc2  funding] at all,\u8221\'20\'1D Levin said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe situation at the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is a true crisis.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lynne Fox\uc2 , president of Workers United, the union that workers at 220 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  have voted to join, said she was worried about the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  being underfunded and understaffed. \u8220\'20\'1CObviously it hurts our campaign and every organizing campaign,\u8221\'20\'1D Fox said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has brought 19 formal complaints against \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , alleging scores of illegal actions, and it is investigating more than 100 other charges that workers have brought alleging illegalities by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , including, spying on workers, intimidation and closing stores in retaliation for unionizing. All this has helped create a backlog in \uc2 NLRB\uc2  cases, and baristas say that \uc2 NLRB\uc2  delays, for instance, on winning reinstatement of fired baristas, could discourage and slow unionization efforts. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s obvious that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is playing the system to their advantage,\u8221\'20\'1D Fox said.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesperson denied that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has gamed the system or broken the law: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have fully honored the process laid out by the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  and encouraged our partners to exercise their right to vote in the election to have their voices heard. Any claims of unfair labor practices are categorically false.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Republican lawmakers strongly oppose raising \uc2 NLRB\uc2  funding. Virginia Foxx of North Carolina, the senior Republican on the House education and labor committee, said, \u8220\'20\'1CIncreasing the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s annual budget to over $300m is the equivalent of using a spray bottle to put out a grease fire \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s an inherently stupid idea. Under this administration, the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  has become the errand boy for labor unions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Larry Cohen, former president of the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  union, lobbied hard behind the scenes to increase \uc2 NLRB\uc2  funding in the Inflation Reduction Act \u8211\'20\'13 he fears that having a badly underfunded \uc2 NLRB\uc2  will make it easier for aggressive anti-union employers to defeat unionization drives. Referring to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 CEO, Cohen said, \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  is hiring as many lawyers as he can because he knows that delay means defeat. There\u8217\'20\'19s a massive number of unfair labor practice cases involving \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , and understaffing is a real problem.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  says that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers have filed over 250 unfair labor practice charges against the company. \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is working day and night on an endless stream of cases,\u8221\'20\'1D said Cohen, who long headed the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s organizing committee. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2  knows that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is not going to win on the merits, that they will only win through delay and more delay.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite not winning a funding increase in the Inflation Reduction Act, the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Samuel expressed optimism that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  could win a budget increase through the appropriations process. But many labor supporters are not optimistic because that process could face a Republican filibuster. \u8220\'20\'1CThe real story is the Republicans are completely unyielding,\u8221\'20\'1D Samuel said.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  union protested at the failure to increase the board\u8217\'20\'19s budget in the Inflation Reduction Act. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are disappointed to have been snubbed yet again,\u8221\'20\'1D the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TheNLRBU/status/1557033942070558720?s=20&t=RZuR0kolMiAU5lh55h8XFA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 union tweeted}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 IRS\uc2  needed additional funding to enforce federal tax law, and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TheNLRBU/status/1557033951398711297?s=20&t=RZuR0kolMiAU5lh55h8XFA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NLRB needs the same}}}\uc2  to enforce federal labor law.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Inflation Reduction Act failed to include something else that unions badly wanted: a provision that for the first time would have allowed substantial fines against employers that break the law when they are fighting unions, for instance, by firing workers for supporting unionization. Under current law, employers can\u8217\'20\'19t be fined for violating the \uc2 National Labor Relations\uc2  Act.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s unfortunate that the labor board is being handcuffed just when it is turning itself around with new leadership to protect workers who want to unionize and bargain collectively,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Samuel said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re fighting with one hand tied behind their back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220817ei8h001me\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
