\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Patients are getting ripped off\u8217\'20\'19: California\u8217\'20\'19s mental health workers go on strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Dani Anguiano in Los Angeles \par \uc2 627 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2022\par 20:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 More than 2,000 workers demand that \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  expand the workforce and improve access to care for patients\par \par \uc2 Thousands of mental health workers in California are going on strike to demand that \uc2 Kaiser Permanente\uc2  , America\u8217\'20\'19s largest non-profit HMO, better provide \u8220\'20\'1Cdesperately needed\u8221\'20\'1D services.\par \par \uc2 As the US grapples with an increase in anxiety and depression brought on by the pandemic, Kaiser patients are waiting months for therapy sessions, according to the union representing the workers.\par \par \uc2 Starting Monday, more than 2,000 workers, including psychologists, therapists, social workers and counselors, are expected to hold daily rallies at picket lines across the state, from Sacramento to the Bay Area to Fresno, as part of an open-ended strike.\par \par \uc2 The move comes after a year of failed negotiations between Kaiser, which serves 9 million people in the state, and the union. Kaiser has rejected union proposals seeking to expand the workforce and \u8220\'20\'1Cimproving access to care\u8221\'20\'1D, according the union, while the company argues the central issues relate to wage increases and time spent on administrative tasks.\par \par \uc2 Despite reporting record profits last year, Kaiser has just one mental health clinician for every 2,600 patients, the union said. Patients who should receive therapy weekly instead are waiting up to eight weeks, according to the union, and overwhelming caseloads are pushing therapists to leave the company entirely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPatients are getting ripped off while Kaiser\u8217\'20\'19s coffers are bulging,\u8221\'20\'1D Sal Rosselli, the president of the \uc2 National Union of Healthcare Workers\uc2  , said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nuhw.org/more-than-2000-kaiser-permanente-mental-health-clinicians-to-start-open-ended-strike-august-15/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement  }}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t take striking lightly but it\u8217\'20\'19s time to take a stand and make Kaiser spend some of its billions on mental health care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The need for mental health services surged during the pandemic as anxiety and depression rose in the US and across the world. In the first year of the pandemic, anxiety and depression increased by 25% globally, according to the \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  . The US surgeon general has warned America\u8217\'20\'19s youth are facing a mental health pandemic.\par \par \uc2 But Kaiser patients aren\u8217\'20\'19t getting the care they need, the union says, accusing the healthcare giant of violating clinical guidelines around treatment and state laws. California previously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sfgate.com/health/article/Kaiser-fined-4-million-over-mental-health-access-4622014.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fined Kaiser $4m  }}}\uc2  for problems with its mental health services, including extended wait times. The state is investigating Kaiser\u8217\'20\'19s behavioral health services after receiving complaints from patients and providers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re serving a strike notice because our patients aren\u8217\'20\'19t receiving needed services,\u8221\'20\'1D Shay Loftus, a Kaiser psychologist participating in the strike, said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not willing to be part of a system that disrespects the work we do and prevents us from providing ethical care.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Kaiser argues it\u8217\'20\'19s facing the same shortage of workers as other US care providers because there aren\u8217\'20\'19t enough mental healthcare professionals to meet the rising demand and that the union is \u8220\'20\'1Cexploiting current challenges as a bargaining tactic\u8221\'20\'1D. The company has hired nearly 200 clinicians since 2021 and used virtual care to improve access, according to Kaiser.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDespite all that we are doing, we, like others, are challenged to meet the demand and know more must be done. We are focused on continuing to find new ways to meet our members\u8217\'20\'19 and patients\u8217\'20\'19 mental health needs,\u8221\'20\'1D Deb Catsavas, the senior vice-president of human resources at Kaiser Permanente Northern California, said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://about.kaiserpermanente.org/who-we-are/labor-relations/committed-to-reaching-a-fair-and-equitable-agreement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement  }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis strike is an unnecessary tactic to increase the union\u8217\'20\'19s leverage at the bargaining table, making it harder, not easier, to deliver mental health care,\u8221\'20\'1D Catsavas said.\par \par \uc2 Patients will continue to receive care from workers who opt not to strike, Catsavas said, adding that \u8220\'20\'1Cnonurgent\u8221\'20\'1D appointments may be rescheduled.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220815ei8f003bh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
