\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 AT&T\uc2  workers fight return to office push: \u8216\'20\'18We can do the same job from home\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,014 \uc2 words\par \uc2 15 August 2022\par 15:08 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Long commutes to and from work, exorbitant childcare costs, ongoing concerns over exposure to Covid cited\par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 pandemic sent millions of workers in the US from working in offices to working remotely. As unemployment benefits ended, vaccines rolled out, and reopenings expanded, employers and commercial real estate groups have been pushing to try to get workers back into offices.\par \par \uc2 But the pandemic further exposed the issues in returning to office, from long commutes to and from work, exorbitant childcare costs, ongoing concerns over exposure to Covid-19 variants and now monkeypox, workers are pushing to keep working from home as an option as employers force a return to the office.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 AT&T\uc2 , the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://fortune.com/company/att/#:~:text=AT%26T%20is%20the%20world's%20largest,company%20of%20WarnerMedia%20in%202018."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 world\u8217\'20\'19s largest telecommunication company}}}\uc2 , workers represented by the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  agreed to a work from home extension until the end of March 2023, but workers say the company is forcing many workers to return to the office much sooner than that, while other departments had already been forced back to the office by their managers.\par \par \uc2 James Bloch, an \uc2 AT&T\uc2  employee in Cleveland, Ohio, for 21 years, said the work from home option has been beneficial for workers\u8217\'20\'19 mental health, protection from Covid-19, productivity, attendance and carbon footprint.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese are extremely stressful times over the last few years and being at home has allowed us to have less distractions, giving us better one-on-one time with our customers and our clients,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bloch. \u8220\'20\'1CWith \uc2 AT&T\uc2  technology, they\u8217\'20\'19re a communication company. We have some of the best stuff out there. Let\u8217\'20\'19s use it. We can do the same job from home anywhere that we could do if we were all sitting there together.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Other workers agree.\par \par \uc2 Sara Fry, an \uc2 AT&T\uc2  service representative in Minneapolis, Minnesota, said her department was told they were being brought back to the office as early as 21 September 2022. Fry argued \uc2 AT&T\uc2  has not provided workers with adequate explanations and details as to why they are forcing workers to return to the office. She speculated the decision stemmed from exerting control over workers and undermining the union.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll of our production is great. Our attendance numbers have improved since folks have been working from home and this news is just crushing. It\u8217\'20\'19s heartbreaking,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think management realizes how deeply hurt and how deeply angry some employees will be about this\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 AT&T\uc2  workers have started a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.change.org/p/make-work-from-home-a-permanent-option-for-at-t-employees"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 petition}}}\uc2  demanding the company makes working from home a permanent option for workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t understand if we\u8217\'20\'19re still in a pandemic, we\u8217\'20\'19re still considered essential workers and we\u8217\'20\'19re still doing the appropriate job, then why are we coming back to the office?\u8221\'20\'1D said Chuck Elgert, communication technician at \uc2 AT&T\uc2  and executive vice-president of CWA Local 6350 in St Louis, Missouri. \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no reason to risk anybody\u8217\'20\'19s health and safety that\u8217\'20\'19s not 100% necessary to run the operations of the business.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He emphasized workers were also concerned because \uc2 AT&T\uc2  has an ageing workforce, particularly in departments where many workers are over the age of 55, and that \uc2 AT&T\uc2  has workplace surveillance tools in place so that managers can constantly monitor the work that workers are doing from home and make changes if necessary.\par \par \uc2 Val Williams, an \uc2 AT&T\uc2  worker and union steward for the \uc2 Communications Workers of America\uc2  in Houston, Texas, was forced to return to work in the office in April 2022. She criticized the push to bring workers back into the office after she said workers had been praised for productivity while working from home.\par \par \uc2 When she returned to work, it was to a new building where not all departments had been brought back, and Williams argued Covid-19 infections have continued to be an issue in the workplace and Covid-19 sick leave is currently inadequate, with five days off.\par \par \uc2 Williams criticized the pushback to return to the office given \uc2 AT&T\uc2  is a communications company with the technology and resources to make working from home a seamless option.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur revenue has increased over the last two years while we were working from home. Our job descriptions state we are capable of working with little to minimum management and that\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19ve been doing,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 She also argued it was unfair how the push to return workers to the office has been enforced, with some departments being brought back while others are still working from home.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t feel like anybody\u8217\'20\'19s health is greater than any others. Because everybody has their own health issues, or they may have family members that have health issues that they have to return home to,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 Charli Haataja, another \uc2 AT&T\uc2  worker in Minneapolis, Minnesota, compared returning to the office as enacting a pay cut given the amount of money workers would need to spend on gas, on monthly parking fees, childcare and the extra time it takes to commute.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorking from home has been a gamechanger for employees that most of us have not seen in our lifetimes working in our work careers, to have gained back any kind of work-life balance, or peace of mind in the workplace, to have your circumstances improve, rather than get worse,\u8221\'20\'1D Haataja said.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 AT&T\uc2  did not provide data on how many workers at the company are still working from home, but claimed it was never the company\u8217\'20\'19s intention to make working from home indefinite.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe health and safety of our employees continues to be our priority,\u8221\'20\'1D said the spokesperson in an email. \u8220\'20\'1CAs we have throughout the pandemic, we adhere to guidance from the medical community, including implementing safety protocols to help protect our employees\u8217\'20\'19 wellbeing. And now that we are a largely vaccinated workforce, we believe it\u8217\'20\'19s safe for employees to return to the workplace. We do our best work when we\u8217\'20\'19re together.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220815ei8f001gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
