\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Too radical or not radical enough? US\u8217\'20\'19s top labor lawyer in the spotlight\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,726 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 August 2022\par 10:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2  would like to ease the path to forming unions and curb employers\u8217\'20\'19 coercion and intimidation\par \par \uc2 As the Biden administration lays claim to being the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/may/02/joe-biden-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  most pro-union in generations,}}}\uc2  many US labor leaders have hailed \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\uc2  as the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) most vigorously pro-union general counsel in decades.\par \par \uc2 The top lawyer at the body charged with enforcing US labor law has been hailed as a champion by some and as a \u8220\'20\'1Cradical\u8221\'20\'1D by her opponents. For others involved in the white-hot world of union organizing, she has not gone far enough.\par \par \uc2 Abruzzo\u8217\'20\'19s bona fides are clear. She has repeatedly urged the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s five-person board to adopt new policies that would make it easier to unionize. She wants the board to prohibit so-called captive audience meetings in which Amazon and many other companies require employees to listen to anti-union speeches from managers and consultants. She wants the board to require employers to grant union recognition once a majority of workers sign cards saying they want a union.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NLRB\uc2  is the federal agency that oversees and referees unionization efforts and collective bargaining in America\u8217\'20\'19s private sector. Its general counsel serves essentially as a prosecutor who cracks down on labor law violations, for instance, on companies that fire workers for supporting a union or on unions that engage in illegal picketing.\par \par \uc2 With a Republican Senate filibuster blocking Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s push for legislation to make it easier to unionize, many unions and workers are looking to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s board and Abruzzo to make it happen instead \u8211\'20\'13 especially after the labor board under Donald Trump moved to undermine unions in various ways.\par \par \uc2 As general counsel, Abruzzo has hit \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  particularly hard, filing an extraordinary number of complaints over its anti-union tactics \u8211\'20\'13 a total of 19 complaints accusing \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  of 81 illegal unfair labor practices, among them spying on workers, firing more than a dozen pro-union workers and closing stores in retaliation for unionizing.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers\u8217\'20\'19 union complains that Abruzzo has not been aggressive enough toward \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . It\u8217\'20\'19s upset that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  hasn\u8217\'20\'19t filed a complaint against what many union supporters say is proving to be \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 most effective anti-union strategy. \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 CEO, has warned that the company would give raises and improved benefits to baristas at its non-union stores, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2022/05/03/business/starbucks-union-schultz/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 but not to workers at stores that have already unionized}}}\uc2  or petitioned for a union election. Not only has this caused many baristas to hesitate about backing a union, but some labor leaders fear that other companies may embrace this potent anti-union tactic.\par \par \uc2 In an interview with the Guardian, Abruzzo was asked \u8211\'20\'13 while avoiding mention of any particular company \u8211\'20\'13 whether it was illegal for an employer to tell its workers that it will give raises and better benefits to its non-union workers, but not to its workers who recently voted to unionize and are seeking to negotiate their first contract.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think saying that is an unfair labor practice,\u8221\'20\'1D Abruzzo said. \u8220\'20\'1CI would say it\u8217\'20\'19s unlawful, absent compelling business justification.\u8221\'20\'1D She added: \u8220\'20\'1CTo me that statement is inherently discriminatory conduct.\u8221\'20\'1D Federal law prohibits employers from taking discriminatory or retaliatory actions against workers for supporting a union.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Chamber of Commerce\uc2  has said Abruzzo has a \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.uschamber.com/employment-law/unions/nlrb-general-counsel-asks-the-board-to-overrule-longstanding-precedents"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 radical agenda hostile to employers\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 , while Republicans on the House labor committee accuse her of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://republicans-edlabor.house.gov/uploadedfiles/nlrb_captive_audience_letter_6.27.2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cflagrant disregard of applicable case law\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  and \u8220\'20\'1Cunconscionable efforts to further a partisan objective\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Richard Bensinger, one of the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  union\u8217\'20\'19s lead organizers, complains that Abruzzo has not been aggressive enough in going after \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . He says Abruzzo\u8217\'20\'19s failure to file a complaint over Schultz\u8217\'20\'19s threat not to give certain raises and benefits to unionized employees has hurt his union\u8217\'20\'19s historic unionization drive. Starbucks Workers United has unionized more than 200 corporate-operated \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  since the first one was unionized last December. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has repeatedly denied engaging in any illegal anti-union activities.\par \par \uc2 To the union\u8217\'20\'19s dismay, Schultz\u8217\'20\'19s threat to deny raises and benefits to baristas in unionized stores has scared many employees and slowed the union\u8217\'20\'19s momentum. Schultz asserts it would be illegal to \u8220\'20\'1Cunilaterally\u8221\'20\'1D impose those raises and benefits on unionized employees. Without discussing any specific employer, Abruzzo said that a CEO who offers benefits to non-union employees but not to unionized ones seeking their first contract should be required to offer those benefits to unionized employees to give the union the opportunity to reject them, waive them or accept them while continuing to bargain.\par \par \uc2 Abruzzo\u8217\'20\'19s biggest complaint is that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s budget has not risen for nine years. This budget freeze, she said, has often made it impossible for the board to act as quickly as she would like \u8211\'20\'13 for instance, by bringing more complaints against employers engaged in widespread anti-union illegalities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re been flat-funded at $272.4m since 2014,\u8221\'20\'1D Abruzzo said, noting that after factoring in inflation, the board\u8217\'20\'19s budget has shrunk 25% since 2014. She said the board\u8217\'20\'19s staff was being squeezed terribly because the number of petitions for \uc2 NLRB\uc2 -run union elections jumped {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/correction-first-three-quarters-union-election-petitions-up-58-exceeding"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 58% in the first nine months of the federal fiscal year}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 to 1,892 from 1,197, driven in large part by workers at more than 300 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores petitioning for union elections. The board has also brought 16% more unfair labor practice charges (a total of 12,819) than in the year-earlier period.\par \par \uc2 The budget freeze \u8220\'20\'1Cplaces great strains on our agency\u8221\'20\'1D, Abruzzo said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re dealing with much more work and much less resources.\u8221\'20\'1D She noted that more than half of the board\u8217\'20\'19s 26 regional offices are down at least two full-time \u8220\'20\'1Cprofessionals\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19d say the public is suffering from our inadequate funding and our lack of staff,\u8221\'20\'1D she added. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want this agency to be an obstacle at all for workers who are attempting to engage in concerted activity to improve their workplace conditions. With insufficient resources, it becomes very challenging, and we become not an obstacle, but potentially a delay in enabling workers to get to the place where they want to be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Abruzzo is pleased that Biden and congressional Democrats have agreed to increase the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s budget to $319.4m for fiscal 2023, but she fears that number may prove a mirage because of the myriad roadblocks to enacting legislation. Many Republicans are happy to keep the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  underfunded.\par \par \uc2 One of Abruzzo\u8217\'20\'19s goals is to overturn what she says were incorrect, anti-worker decisions made by President Trump\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 NLRB\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to remember we are a neutral, independent federal agency that enforces a pro-worker statute,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI really don\u8217\'20\'19t think the Trump board majority kept in mind the congressional mandate to protect workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights to engage in protected, concerted activity.\u8221\'20\'1D She criticized the Trump board for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://onlabor.org/to-protect-concerted-activity-the-nlrb-should-overturn-alstate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 narrowing the definition of concerted, pro-worker activity}}}\uc2  and making it easier for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/transactional/nlrb-revisiting-business-friendly-decision-workplace-rules-2022-01-06/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 corporate work rules to punish workers who speak out about workplace conditions}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Abruzzo has urged the five-person board to ban captive audience meetings, prompting loud protests from business groups that such a move would abridge employers\u8217\'20\'19 free speech rights. Abruzzo denies she is limiting corporations\u8217\'20\'19 free speech.\par \par \uc2 We should not be allowing employers to delay recognition so they can coerce workers to think differently or choose differently\par \par \uc2 Jennifer Abruzzo\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEmployers,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, \u8220\'20\'1Care free to engage with their workers and offer their opinions and arguments as to why their workers would be better off without union representation \u8211\'20\'13 if they make it voluntary for workers whether they want to listen to that anti-union rhetoric.\u8221\'20\'1D Noting that workers depend on their employers for their livelihood, Abruzzo said workers would feel \u8220\'20\'1Cvery intimidated\u8221\'20\'1D about not attending these required meetings. The National Labor Relations Act prohibits employer intimidation or coercion, she noted.\par \par \uc2 In another move that upset business, Abruzzo has proposed returning to a 1949 \uc2 NLRB\uc2  policy, known as Joy Silk, that would require employers to recognize a union once a majority of workers sign pro-union cards, unless the employer has good-faith doubts about the legitimacy of the majority. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen a union demonstrates that it enjoys majority support of workers in a workplace and an employer refuses to recognize their workers\u8217\'20\'19 chosen representative \u8230\'20\'26 solely to undermine union support, which typically happens through unlawful coercion and other unlawful acts, that to me is problematic,\u8221\'20\'1D Abruzzo said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe should not be allowing those employers to delay recognition so that they can coerce these workers to think differently or choose differently.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Business lobbyists often deride the legitimacy of card checks, asserting that thuggish union supporters bully workers into signing cards. Abruzzo scoffed at that notion. \u8220\'20\'1CMany high-road employers have agreed to a card-check process and enjoy very good labor-management relations,\u8221\'20\'1D Abruzzo said. \u8220\'20\'1CEmployees aren\u8217\'20\'19t economically dependent on their union for their livelihoods, unlike being economically dependent on their employers for their livelihoods. They\u8217\'20\'19re less likely to be swayed by \u8216\'20\'18bullying\u8217\'20\'19 by unions than they would be by swayed by \u8216\'20\'18bullying\u8217\'20\'19 by their employer.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Acknowledging that the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s normal procedures can sometimes take a long time, she has pushed the board\u8217\'20\'19s regional offices to seek more emergency injunctions in federal court, for instance, to quickly reinstate pro-union workers the board believes were fired illegally. Too often those firings aim \u8220\'20\'1Cto nip in the bud organizing drives\u8221\'20\'1D, Abruzzo said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to move quickly because every day that workers are subjected to unlawful threats, interrogations, surveillance and retaliation for engaging in organizing activities is one day too many.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Abruzzo dismisses attacks from business lobbyists that she is some pro-union radical. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve spent almost 25 years of my professional career with the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 , acting as a public servant,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CDuring that time, I enjoyed \u8211\'20\'13 and I still enjoy \u8211\'20\'13 very productive relations with management and labor practitioners. We are a neutral, independent federal agency, but we have a pro-worker congressional mandate, and I\u8217\'20\'19m going to vigorously protect the right of workers to self-organize, join a union and bargain collectively with representatives of their own choosing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220805ei85001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
