\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Independent truckers block Oakland port in protest\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agency \par \uc2 505 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 July 2022\par 17:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The drivers object to a law requiring companies to hire them as employees rather than independent contractors\par \par \uc2 Truckers protesting against a California labor law effectively shut down cargo operations at the \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2 , one of the busiest in the US.\par \par \uc2 The protest began on Monday, when hundreds of independent big-rig truckers blocked the movement of cargo in and out of terminals at the port. On Wednesday, port officials warned that the shutdown would further exacerbate the congestion of containers.\par \par \uc2 The truckers are protesting Assembly Bill 5, a gig economy law passed in 2019 that made it harder for companies to classify workers as independent contractors instead of employees, who are entitled to minimum wage and benefits such as workers compensation, overtime and sick pay.\par \par \uc2 A federal appeals court ruled last year that the law applies to approximately 70,000 truck drivers who can be classified as employees of companies that hire them instead of independent contractors.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2  called it a \u8220\'20\'1Cmassive victory\u8221\'20\'1D for exploited truckers. But the California Trucking Association, which sued over the law, had argued the law could make it harder for independent drivers who own their own trucks and operate on their own hours to make a living by forcing them to be classified as employees.\par \par \uc2 The legal battle stalled enforcement of the law but last month the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  decided it would not review the decision.\par \par \uc2 Truckers are now asking the California governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , to meet and discuss the issue. There\u8217\'20\'19s been no word on when the state might begin enforcing the law, which is still being contested in lower courts.\par \par \uc2 The truckers have said they plan to continue their protest until they hear from lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve all been out here for three days, non-stop, from early mornings to late night. You know, we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to stop the port\u8217\'20\'19s operation so that we can get our point across and cancel that AB5 law. We\u8217\'20\'19re gonna be out here until Sacramento gives us an answer about this law,\u8221\'20\'1D driver Randeep Dhillon told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ktvu.com/news/trucker-protest-shuts-down-operations-at-port-of-oakland"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 KTVU}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Messages seeking comment from the governor\u8217\'20\'19s office and the governor\u8217\'20\'19s office of business and economic development weren\u8217\'20\'19t immediately returned Wednesday evening.\par \par \uc2 The protest comes as supply chain problems have already led to cargo ship traffic jams at large ports and stockpiled goods on the dock, and ports have been struggling to handle container traffic, much of it from Asia. Meanwhile, toymakers and other industries are entering their peak season for imports as retailers stockpile goods for the fall holidays and back-to-school items.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe understand the frustration expressed by the protesters at California ports,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Port of Oakland\uc2  executive director Danny Wan said in a statement. \u8220\'20\'1CBut prolonged stoppage of port operations in California for any reason will damage all the businesses operating at the ports and cause California ports to further suffer market share losses to competing ports.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220721ei7l002mi\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
