\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We didn\u8217\'20\'19t sign up to die\u8217\'20\'19: US transit workers sound alarm over rising violence\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 966 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2022\par 09:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unions say trend of assaults and abuse on staff is intolerable \u8211\'20\'13 and crisis will worsen without federal action\par \par \uc2 Monique Rondon, a bus operator in New York City for 23 years, has been spat on and assaulted several times on the job.\par \par \uc2 Now transit workers and labor unions across America are sounding the alarm over the trend of violence, assaults, and abuse that workers in the transportation industry in the US have faced throughout the Covid-19 pandemic, a crisis they say will continue to worsen without federal action and implementation of safety protections and rules.\par \par \uc2 In New York, at least one worker a week, on average, has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://new.mta.info/safety-and-security/nyct-employee-assault-data"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported }}}\uc2  being assaulted on the job, and dozens report experiencing verbal harassment. Rondon said bus operators are asking for vehicles where operators are completely separated from the public, similar to airline pilots in their cockpits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe cannot retain people coming to work. The absentee records are ridiculous just because they\u8217\'20\'19re tired, they\u8217\'20\'19re fed up, emotionally and physically. They don\u8217\'20\'19t feel like they\u8217\'20\'19re getting any help from the transit authority whatsoever,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rondon, who is also a chief union steward for Transport Workers Union local 100.\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CNothing is giving them incentive to come to work because they don\u8217\'20\'19t feel safe, they don\u8217\'20\'19t feel protected, and they feel like even if they do anything the transit authority comes down on us first.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 More than 20 national labor unions wrote a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ttd.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/UPDATED-Joint-Labor-Letter-to-Buttigieg-Fernandez-on-transit-safety-3.10.22.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 letter}}}\uc2  in March to the \uc2 US Department of Transportation\uc2  and the Federal Transit Authority, demanding federal action to protect transit workers on the job and implement baseline safety standards that were included in the bipartisan infrastructure law that Joe Biden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2021/11/15/1055841358/biden-signs-1t-bipartisan-infrastructure-bill-into-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed}}}\uc2  in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur unions are seeing historic levels of attrition as bus and subway operators, station agents, car cleaners, and others protect themselves in the only way available to them: leaving public transportation,\u8221\'20\'1D the unions wrote.\par \par \uc2 The safety standards include the \uc2 FTA\uc2  tracking data on transit worker assaults; updating a national safety plan to include workers\u8217\'20\'19 voices; denying all waiver applications from transit agencies to exempt themselves from safety obligations required by the new law; and establishing minimum level of assault protections. But workers and unions say they have still not been implemented.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the \uc2 Federal Transit Administration\uc2  said the agency is working to implement all requirements of the infrastructure law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a crisis. What frontline workers are experiencing every day around this country is horrific, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not getting better,\u8221\'20\'1D said Greg Regan, president of the transportation trades department at the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CHistorically, workers and unions have been excluded from the safety planning process of the agency. So they think that\u8217\'20\'19s insane. They\u8217\'20\'19re the ones who are the eyes and ears on the ground, who can identify vulnerabilities and help point us in the right direction for meaningful solutions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Regan said the need for action is urgent, and that the \uc2 FTA\uc2  needs to respond to every worker assault incident to push for improvements and oversight so it does not recur. He cited an ATU survey where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.atu.org/atu-pdfs/Q3-IT2021_web.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than 75% }}}\uc2  of transit workers expressed fear of being assaulted on the job on a daily basis. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://data.bts.gov/stories/s/Transportation-Economic-Trends-Transportation-Empl/caxh-t8jd/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 43%}}}\uc2  of transit workers are eligible to retire in five to 10 years, as transit agencies around the US are still experiencing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2021/12/28/worker-shortages-public-transportation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 labor shortages}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we\u8217\'20\'19re not able to have the workforce to provide that extended service, then we\u8217\'20\'19re done. I think that the systems are going to fail,\u8221\'20\'1D added Regan.\par \par \uc2 In Utah, ridership has declined since the pandemic, but there were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox13now.com/news/fox-13-investigates/fox-13-investigates-uta-insists-its-safe-despite-more-than-150-assaults-on-drivers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 150 reported }}}\uc2  cases of assault on drivers between January 2020 and April this year. In 2021, the \uc2 New Jersey Transit{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nj1015.com/attacks-on-nj-transit-workers-tripled-new-nj-law-will-crack-down/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  183 assaults on transit workers, three times the annual average. In Phoenix, Arizona, assaults and drug use on public transportation hit a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.12news.com/article/news/crime/assaults-drug-crimes-valley-buses-light-rail-risen-last-5-years/75-5de267bd-46f8-4ec9-a3c2-bed63ef74477"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 five-year high}}}\uc2  last year.\par \par \uc2 Transit workers in Chicago have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blockclubchicago.org/2022/04/06/cta-operator-pushed-onto-red-line-tracks-highlights-increasing-violence-against-transit-workers-advocacy-group-says/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protested}}}\uc2  to demand increased safety protections amid numerous reports of assaults against operators over the past year, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fox32chicago.com/news/over-300-cta-bus-drivers-attacked-assaulted-this-year-transit-union-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  more than 300 reported}}}\uc2  incidents of assaults on transit workers reported in 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe didn\u8217\'20\'19t sign up to die on these jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D said John Costa, international president of the \uc2 Amalgamated Transit Union\uc2 , which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.atu.org/union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 represents }}}\uc2  more than 200,000 transit workers in the US and Canada. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s growing, and it\u8217\'20\'19s not going to go away until people realize that if you mess with an operator, there\u8217\'20\'19s real consequences \u8211\'20\'13 like you will not be able to take transportation any more \u8211\'20\'13 or there\u8217\'20\'19s real punishment. But we\u8217\'20\'19re not there yet.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rafaele Mastrangelo Jr, a rail specialist at Los Angeles county Metro Transit Authority and a union steward for ATU local 1277, said safety concerns for workers are the worst he\u8217\'20\'19s seen in his 19 years working in the industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI hear over and over again the problems with the lack of security in the system. Workers voice their concerns to management. They\u8217\'20\'19re told they\u8217\'20\'19re being heard, but nothing\u8217\'20\'19s being done,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mastrangelo.\par \par \uc2 He said problems such as drug use, vandalism and assaults on transit lines have worsened through the pandemic, and there remain difficulties in retaining and hiring enough bus and rail operators, which reduces services and forces workers to work longer hours to compensate for the shortages.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe company has been trying to have a PR campaign to convince the general public and people working at the \uc2 MTA\uc2  to help with recruitment to get bus operators, but they\u8217\'20\'19re really struggling to do it. But the issue is who wants to do it. To be a bus operator these days, it\u8217\'20\'19s very dangerous,\u8221\'20\'1D added Mastrangelo.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220624ei6o001bb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
