\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 UK economy \u8216\'20\'18running on empty\u8217\'20\'19 as high inflation threatens recession, and drives up borrowing costs \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 8,706 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2022\par 14:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 UK business confidence falls to levels that signal a recession, as new orders growth weakensLatest: \uc2 BA\uc2  staff at Heathrow vote for strike actionUK retail sales volumes fall as shoppers cut backGermany announces emergency plan as Russia chokes gas suppliesUK \u8216\'20\'18running on empty\u8217\'20\'19, \uc2 PMI\uc2  survey showsEurozone economy slowing this monthInflation drove up UK debt repayment costs\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up, after growing signs that the UK, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b41c418f0877fb10b60aad#block-62b41c418f0877fb10b60aad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 eurozone }}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?page=with:block-62b474e48f083a9e112c78de#block-62b474e48f083a9e112c78de"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US }}}\uc2  economies are slowing\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 enltrCopper and yields seem to be trying to say something {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/OFv5hKxUy2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/OFv5hKxUy2}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Tom Hearden (@followtheh) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/followtheh/status/1539977505641320448?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8230\'20\'26.and as rising inflation pushed up Britain\u8217\'20\'19s debt repayment costs.\par \par \uc2 With railway workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/live/2022/jun/23/train-strikes-railways-rail-disruption-britain-latest-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 continuing to strike}}}\uc2 , and \uc2 British Airways\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 Heathrow staff {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/heathrow-faces-summer-strikes-as-unions-demand-pay-cut-is-reinstated"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voting to walk out}}}\uc2 , a summer of industrial action looks increasingly likely.\par \par \uc2 And as Germany takes a step closer to rationing gas after a drastic drop in supplies from Russia, energy disruption this winter looms....\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/uk-economy-starting-to-run-on-empty-as-order-books-dry-up"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK economy starting to \u8216\'20\'18run on empty\u8217\'20\'19 as order books dry up}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowed-14bn-may-inflation-interest-debt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Soaring inflation pushes UK borrowing to \u163\'00\'A314bn in May}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jun/23/second-day-of-rail-strikes-start-after-talks-collapse-in-acrimony"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Second day of rail strikes start after talks collapse in acrimony}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/heathrow-faces-summer-strikes-as-unions-demand-pay-cut-is-reinstated"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  Heathrow faces summer of disruption as BA staff vote to strike}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jun/23/ministers-press-on-with-cynical-plan-to-let-agency-staff-replace-striking-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ministers press on with \u8216\'20\'18cynical\u8217\'20\'19 plan to let agency staff replace striking workers}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/23/german-announces-emergency-plan-as-russia-chokes-gas-supplies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Germany announces emergency plan as Russia chokes gas supplies}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/city-regulation-uk-financial-sector-post-brexit-mps-treasury-subcommittee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 MPs seek to avoid race to the bottom in post-Brexit City regulation}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/buy-now-pay-later-uk-research"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A third of UK \u8216\'20\'18buy now, pay later\u8217\'20\'19 users say they can\u8217\'20\'19t handle payments}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/post-office-to-extend-compensation-scheme-for-victims-of-it-scandal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Post Office to extend compensation scheme for IT scandal victims}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/only-100-days-left-to-use-paper-20-and-50-notes-says-bank-of-england"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Only 100 days left to use paper \u163\'00\'A320 and \u163\'00\'A350 notes, says Bank of England}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 US private sector growth hits five-month low\par \par \uc2 The US economy slowed sharply in June as companies were hit by a drop in demand, according to the latest healthcheck on American firms.\par \par \uc2 Growth across the US private sector slowed to its lowest level since January, when {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/12/us-omicron-cases-effects-schools-supply-shortage-hospitals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Omicron variant caused a massive surge in Covid-19 infections}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 S&P Global Flash US Composite PMI, which measures activity in the sector, hit a five-month low this month, while manufacturing output is now shrinking.\par \par \uc2 Companies also reported a drop in new business, for the first time in almost two years, which underlines that demand is easing.\par \par \uc2 The report says:\par \par \uc2 Although service providers continued to indicate a rise in output, it was the weakest increase for five months.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturers fared worse, with factory production slipping into decline as the respective seasonally adjusted index fell to a degree only exceeded twice in the 15-year history of the survey, at the height of the initial pandemic lockdowns in 2020 and the height of the global financial crisis in 2008.\par \par \uc2 Weaker demand conditions, often linked to the rising cost of living and falling confidence, led to the first contraction in new orders since July 2020.\par \par \uc2 enltr???? Latest flash {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PMI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PMI}}}\uc2  data indicated a slowdown in US growth in June (51.2, May: 53.6), with the manufacturing sector signalling its first contraction in two years. Read more: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ELJVdPgDrg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/ELJVdPgDrg}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ze6yc7zPcE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ze6yc7zPcE}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 S&P Global PMI\u8482\'21\'22 (@SPGlobalPMI) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SPGlobalPMI/status/1539975325555245056?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 But athough inflationary pressures \u8220\'20\'1Cremained marked in June\u8221\'20\'1D, the pace of input price inflation eased to the slowest for five months.\par \par \uc2 Traders are calculating that this means US interest rate may not rise as fast as thought -- if the economy is at greater risk of dropping into recession....\par \par \uc2 enltrImplied US interest rates plunge after weak US PMI report.\par \par \uc2 Terminal rate down to 3.38% in Q1 next year - it was over 4% last week - and Dec 2023 implied rate falls below 3%. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/aHWIO7vROW"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/aHWIO7vROW}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jamie McGeever (@ReutersJamie) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ReutersJamie/status/1539972421129711617?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 British Airways\uc2  said it was \u8216\'20\'18extremely disappointed\u8217\'20\'19 after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b46f368f0877fb10b60eab#block-62b46f368f0877fb10b60eab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 staff at London\u8217\'20\'19s Heathrow airport voted in favour of strike action in a pay row today}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re extremely disappointed with the result and that the unions have chosen to take this course of action,\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are fully committed to work together to find a solution, because to deliver for our customers and rebuild our business we have to work as a team.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.05pm BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?page=with:block-62b46f368f0877fb10b60eab#block-62b46f368f0877fb10b60eab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The dates when British Airways\u8217\'20\'19 staff at Heathrow would hold industrial action}}}\uc2  will confirmed in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 It is likely to be over the busy summer holiday period, the \uc2 GMB union\uc2  says, unless a pay deal is reached in time.\par \par \uc2 Nadine Houghton, GMB national officer, says holidaymakers face massive disruption -- but there is still time for \uc2 BA\uc2  to avoid a walkout, if it reverses pay cuts made during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur members need to be reinstated the 10% they had stolen from them last year with full back pay and the 10% bonus which other colleagues have been paid.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGMB members at Heathrow have suffered untold abuse as they deal with the travel chaos caused by staff shortages and IT failures.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt the same time, they\u8217\'20\'19ve had their pay slashed during \uc2 BA\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s callous fire and rehire policy.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s some snap reaction:\par \par \uc2 enltrBritish Airways ground staff at Heathrow have voted to strike in a pay dispute. No dates yet set. Earliest possible date would be 8 July, but it could be later. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/jVvk19Pk3h"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/jVvk19Pk3h}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Simon Calder (@SimonCalder) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SimonCalder/status/1539969761827688453?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr?? BREAKING: Hundreds of \uc2 British Airways\uc2  check-in and ground staff at Heathrow vote to strike over the summer holidays, adding to the industry's woes\par \par \uc2 Workers voted 95% to strike (81% turnout)\par \par \uc2 Workers are calling for a 10% pay cut imposed during the pandemic to be reinstated\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Tom Boadle (@TomBoadle) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TomBoadle/status/1539967567900471298?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 BA\uc2  workers at Heathrow vote in favour of strikes in pay dispute\par \par \uc2 British Airways\uc2  staff at London\u8217\'20\'19s Heathrow airport have voted in favour of a strike for better pay, trade \uc2 union GMB\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The move could bring fresh disruption to the UK\u8217\'20\'19s busiest airport, after passengers have already suffered significant disruption, delays and cancellations as airlines struggled to handle the increase in demand.\par \par \uc2 enltrBreaking: \uc2 British Airways\uc2  check-in and ground staff at Heathrow have voted 95% in favour of strike action, on an 81% turn out.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Graham Hiscott (@Grahamhiscott) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Grahamhiscott/status/1539968870198308864?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The dispute stems from \uc2 BA\uc2  using \u8220\'20\'1Cfire and rehire\u8221\'20\'1D practices to cut workers\u8217\'20\'19 pay during the pandemic when they could not fight back, the \uc2 GMB union\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 A GMB spokesperson said earlier today that:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll our members are asking for \u8211\'20\'13 and these are primarily low-paid women \u8211\'20\'13 is for \uc2 BA\uc2  to reinstate the 10% taken from them during the pandemic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/heathrow-faces-summer-strikes-as-unions-demand-pay-cut-is-reinstated"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Heathrow faces summer strikes as unions demand pandemic pay cut is reinstated}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s main indexes have opened a little higher, as investors continue to weigh up the risks of a US recession.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average has gained 121 points, or 0.4% in early trading to 30,604 points.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 (which sank into a bear market last week), is also 0.4% higher, while the tech-focused Nasdaq is up 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 Raffi Boyadjian, Lead Investment Analyst at XM syas:\par \par \uc2 Shares on Wall Street closed marginally lower on Wednesday as investors were unable to shake-off worries about the US economy contracting in the upcoming quarters. But neither was there any panic.\par \par \uc2 If anything, [Fed chair Jerome] Powell\u8217\'20\'19s acknowledgement of the real threat of a recession might have been taken as a sign by some traders that the Fed will adjust its policy if the data worsens.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe markets are open! ??\par \par \uc2 Here are some things in focus today. ??\par \par \uc2 ? Fed Chair Powell testimony (again)\par \par \uc2 ? S&P Global Manufacturing PMI\par \par \uc2 ? Initial/continuing jobless claims\par \par \uc2 ? Fed bank stress test results\par \par \uc2 ? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24FDX&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $FDX}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24RAD&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $RAD}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24SWBI&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $SWBI}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24ACN&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $ACN}}}\uc2 , & {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24BB&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $BB}}}\uc2  earnings {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/dAfPgNXOap"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/dAfPgNXOap}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Stocktwits (@Stocktwits) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Stocktwits/status/1539964344057749507?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s offshore oil industry have told chancellor \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2  that his new windfall tax will hit investment.\par \par \uc2 Sunak met oil and gas chiefs in Aberdeen today, a month after executing a tight u-turn and imposing a levy on the earnings of energy companies.\par \par \uc2 Deirdre Michie\uc2 , chief executive of Offshore Energies UK, has thanked Sunak for coming to Aberdeen but said the new tax would undermine the industry.\par \par \uc2 OEUK also says the sudden introduction of the 25% levy on oil and gas producers also threatened the UK\u8217\'20\'19s reputation for fiscal stability.\par \par \uc2 Michie said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Energy Profits Levy is an unexpected new tax that changes the basis for investments. We had a candid and constructive meeting with the chancellor to discuss these issues and our industry leaders were clear about their concerns, especially the impact on investor confidence. Both sides have committed to further discussions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will work constructively with the \uc2 UK government\uc2  and do our best to mitigate the damage this tax will cause but if energy companies reduce investment in UK waters, then they will produce less oil and gas. That means they will eventually be paying less taxes and have less money to invest in low carbon energy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/may/26/sunak-u-turns-on-energy-profits-levy-in-15bn-cost-of-living-package"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sunak U-turns on \u8216\'20\'18energy profits levy\u8217\'20\'19 in \u163\'00\'A315bn cost of living package}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in the City, shares in online drinks retailer Naked Wine have plunged over 40% today after it predicted it will only breakeven this year.\par \par \uc2 Naked, which had seen a surge in sales in the pandemic lockdown, posted a \u163\'00\'A32m adjusted profit for the last financial year, up from a \u163\'00\'A31.5m loss a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 But sales growth slowed, up just 3% in the year to the end of March, or 73% higher than two year ago before {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/jun/11/naked-wines-benefits-from-covid-online-boom-as-sales-soar"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the pandemic sparked a rush of interest}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Naked predicted that total sales this year would be between 4% lower and 4% higher, pointing to the uncertain macroeconomic background. Its customers pay a monthly fee, which is then spent on wine from independent winemakers.\par \par \uc2 Nick Devlin, chief executive, says:\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead \uc2 Naked Wines\uc2  is well positioned to continue to grow amidst a changing consumer environment. Our enhanced scale, attractive unit economics and healthy balance sheet allow us to continue to invest for growth.\par \par \uc2 At the same time we will not pursue growth at any cost, and our guidance is that we intend to trade the business at or around breakeven this year.\par \par \uc2 Shares in Naked, which hit a record high over 850p last year, are down almost 45% today at 162p.\par \par \uc2 Naked Wine\u8217\'20\'19s share price Photograph: Refinitiv\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 Over in the US, 229,000 people filed new unemployment claims last week, slightly more than expected.\par \par \uc2 The previous week\u8217\'20\'19s total has been revised up, to 231,000, while the four-week average of claim (which are a proxy for layoffs) nudged up to its highest since late January.\par \par \uc2 enltrInitial jobless claims were slightly above expected in the week ended June 18, when they were down 2,000 to 229,000 from an upwardly revised 231,000 the previous week. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/9GSflQCcxA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/9GSflQCcxA}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Econoday, Inc. (@Econoday) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Econoday/status/1539951196282138624?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Jobless claims have been moving up, generally, over the last few weeks, but are still around their pre-pandemic levels ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/mar/24/us-unemployment-lowest-level-since-1969"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 after hitting the lowest in over 50 years this spring}}}\uc2  )\par \par \uc2 enltrBoth initial UI claims and continuing claims remain historically low. On the initial claims, both seasonally adjusted and non-seasonally adjusted series declined, but the 4 week moving avg (nsa) has trended up over the last 2 weeks. Something to watch, but not concerning yet. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/diSV0K8bJR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/diSV0K8bJR}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 AnnElizabeth Konkel (@AE_Konkel) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AE_Konkel/status/1539951396556050434?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrInitial jobless claims came in at 229k for the w/o Jun 18, similar to the prior two weeks. Continuing claims also held steady at 1.315mil. The labor market is slightly weaker than earlier this year, but no signs of significant cooling yet. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/28Ctn7ch7R"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/28Ctn7ch7R}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Liz Young (@LizYoungStrat) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/LizYoungStrat/status/1539950841792286721?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Heathrow airport could be hit by strikes this summer, as 700 check-in and ground staff vote on whether to walk out during the peak holiday period in a dispute with \uc2 British Airways\uc2  over pay.\par \par \uc2 As a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jun/23/second-day-of-rail-strikes-start-after-talks-collapse-in-acrimony"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 second day of national rail strikes}}}\uc2  is under way, 700 workers employed by \uc2 BA\uc2  are being balloted on industrial action by unions including GMB and Unite, with the result of the vote expected on Thursday afternoon. The Unite ballot closes on Monday.\par \par \uc2 If they vote for strike action, it would be \u8220\'20\'1Clikely during school holidays\u8221\'20\'1D, a GMB spokesperson said.\par \par \uc2 The dispute stems from \uc2 BA\uc2  using \u8220\'20\'1Cfire and rehire\u8221\'20\'1D practices to cut workers\u8217\'20\'19 pay during the pandemic when they could not fight back, the \uc2 GMB union\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/heathrow-faces-summer-strikes-as-unions-demand-pay-cut-is-reinstated"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Heathrow faces summer strikes as unions demand pay cut is reinstated}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 European gas prices for delivery later this year have jumped, after Germany raised its level of alarm over disruptions to Russian gas supplies.\par \par \uc2 Berlin warned that Moscow\u8217\'20\'19s decision to weaponise its energy exports had plunged Europe\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy into a \u8220\'20\'1Cgas crisis\u8221\'20\'1D, as it announced the second of three energy emergency phases.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/profile/philip-oltermann"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Philip Oltermann}}}\uc2 , our correspondent in Berlin, reports:\par \par \uc2 The ministry said the reason for the warning was a reduction in Russian gas deliveries since 14 June amid continued high prices on the gas market. Should Russian gas deliveries via the Nord Stream 1 pipeline continue to remain at the low level of 40%, the ministry said in a statement, \u8220\'20\'1Ca storage target of 90% by December cannot be reached without additional measures\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no point pretending: the throttling of gas deliveries amounts to an economic attack on us by Putin,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Robert Habeck\uc2 , the minister for economic and energy affairs. \u8220\'20\'1CPutin\u8217\'20\'19s strategy is blatantly to stir insecurity, to drive up prices and to drive a wedge through our society.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven if it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t feel like it yet: we are in a gas crisis,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CFrom now on, gas is going to be a scarce good.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jun/23/german-announces-emergency-plan-as-russia-chokes-gas-supplies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Germany announces emergency plan as Russia chokes gas supplies}}}\par \par \uc2 The benchmark Dutch wholesale gas contract for winter 2002 has risen 7.7%, while the UK contract for delivery in the third-quarter of the year is up 5.9%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 Turkey\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  has bucked the trend of interest rate hikes, by leaving borrowing costs unchanged despite soaring prices.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Central Bank of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  (\uc2 CBRT\uc2 ) left its policy rate on hold at 14% today, even though consumer price inflation surged to 73.5% in May.\par \par \uc2 Turkish inflation has roared higher after the \uc2 CBRT\uc2  cut interest rates several times last year, under pressure from president \uc2 Recep Tayyip Erdogan\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2021/dec/16/erdogan-intervenes-after-turkish-lira-sinks-to-lowest-level-against-dollar"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 That weakened the lira dramatically}}}\uc2 , driving up import costs, before Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine drove energy and food prices higher.\par \par \uc2 The central bank said disinflation would begin due to measures already taken, a potential end to the Ukraine conflict and favourable base effects.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Committee will continue to implement the strengthened macroprudential policy set decisively and take additional measures when needed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltr???? CBRT MPC Meeting, 23 Jun'22 - The \uc2 Central Bank of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  decided to keep the policy rate (1-w repo auction rate) constant at 14%. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Turkey?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Turkey}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/CBRT?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #CBRT}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/mpc?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #mpc}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/policy?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #policy}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/rate?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #rate}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/cRD7c8PtDr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/cRD7c8PtDr}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Verimetrik (English) (@verimetrik_eng) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/verimetrik_eng/status/1539926936646041606?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 US sportswear maker \uc2 Nike\uc2  making a full exit from Russia, three months after suspending its operations there, the company said in an emailed statement to Reuters today.\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/BREAKING?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #BREAKING}}}\uc2  : U.S. sportswear maker {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Nike?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Nike}}}\uc2  is making a complete exit from Russia three months after suspending its operations there, the company said in an emailed statement. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/MGAno5DRFm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/MGAno5DRFm}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CGTN Europe (@CGTNEurope) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CGTNEurope/status/1539930767106662402?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 It might be time to have a rummage through your wallet purse or piggy bank for any old paper banknotes lurking.\par \par \uc2 People have just 100 days left to use the paper \u163\'00\'A320 and \u163\'00\'A350 banknotes remaining in circulation, before they stop being legal tender status on 30 September.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/bankofenglandgovernor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bank of England}}}\uc2  is encouraging anyone who still has them to use them or deposit them at their bank or a post office before the end of September. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/only-100-days-left-to-use-paper-20-and-50-notes-says-bank-of-england"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More here.}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/only-100-days-left-to-use-paper-20-and-50-notes-says-bank-of-england"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Only 100 days left to use paper \u163\'00\'A320 and \u163\'00\'A350 notes, says Bank of England}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.27pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.47am BST\par \par \uc2 Fuel prices climb again The entrance to the M6 northbound Burton-In-Kendal service station. Photograph: Joel Goodman/The Guardian\par \par \uc2 There is no respite from rising inflation for UK motorists.\par \par \uc2 Petrol\uc2  and diesel prices hit new records on Wednesday, despite a drop in wholesale petrol prices last week, new figures show.\par \par \uc2 The average price of petrol rose half a penny to 189.84p on Wednesday while diesel jumped almost a penny to 198p a litre, data from RAC Fuel Watch shows.\par \par \uc2 That will have stung drivers who had to take the car to work this week due to train strikes.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2022/jun/20/commuters-switching-to-cars-face-record-uk-petrol-prices-to-fill-up"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Commuters switching to cars face record UK petrol prices to fill up}}}\par \par \uc2 RAC\uc2  fuel spokesman Simon Williams says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt seems as though we are just days away from the frightening prospect of the price of diesel averaging \u163\'00\'A32 a litre across the UK taking the cost of a full tank to a staggering \u163\'00\'A3110. For drivers who still think in gallons this would be \u163\'00\'A39 a gallon.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re surprised and disappointed to see the price of unleaded continuing to rise as the cost on the wholesale market tells a very different story. Over the course of last week delivered wholesale petrol averaged 148p a litre which should lead to a price of around 186p after factoring in 7p-a-litre retailer margin and VAT at 20%.\par \par \uc2 We suspect if retailers fail to reduce their prices in the next few days they will find themselves playing into the hands of the \uc2 Competition and Markets Authority\uc2  which is currently looking into their behaviour.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The CMA is conducting a \u8220\'20\'1Cswift high-level review of competition in the fuel retail market\u8221\'20\'1D following a request by Business secretary \uc2 Kwasi Kwarteng\uc2 :.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/12/kwasi-kwarteng-orders-probe-into-uk-petrol-station-operators-amid-concerns-over-prices"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Petrol prices: Kwasi Kwarteng orders inquiry into cost of filling up}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2022/jun/17/petrol-profiteering-im-not-robbing-motorists-im-only-making-2-3p-a-litre"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Petrol retailers have denied claims of profiteering}}}\uc2 , saying they are simply passing on higher prices for fuel.\par \par \uc2 But there were hopes that price rises would have topped out, after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2022/jun/13/uk-petrol-price-rises-aa-fuel-duty"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wholesale prices finally fell earlier this month}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Williams also points out that prices vary across the sector:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe average price of a litre at a motorway services is now 203.45p for petrol and 205.88p diesel while at the cheaper end of the market the average paid at one of the big four supermarkets is 187.83p for petrol and 196.21p diesel.\par \par \uc2 The price of supermarket diesel has rocketed by 11p a litre in the last fortnight alone.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.42am BST\par \par \uc2 Full story: UK economy starting to \u8216\'20\'18run on empty\u8217\'20\'19 as order books dry up\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s economy is starting to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Crun on empty\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  as post-pandemic order books dry up and the highest inflation in 40 years affects confidence, the latest snapshot of the private sector has shown.\par \par \uc2 Flash estimates of the economy\u8217\'20\'19s performance in June showed business optimism at its lowest since the early months of the Covid-19 pandemic in the spring of 2020 and the sharpest drop in new order volumes for a year.\par \par \uc2 The monthly survey of purchasing managers produced by S&P and the \uc2 Chartered Institute of Procurement and Supply\uc2  (\uc2 CIPS\uc2 ) said overall business activity across the services and manufacturing sectors was unchanged on the 15-month low of 53.1 reached in May.\par \par \uc2 A reading above 50 suggests private sector activity is expanding while a reading below 50 points to contraction....\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/uk-economy-starting-to-run-on-empty-as-order-books-dry-up"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK economy starting to \u8216\'20\'18run on empty\u8217\'20\'19 as order books dry up}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.19am BST\par \par \uc2 UK retail sales weaken as inflation hits consumers\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s cost of living squeeze has hit spending at UK retailers this month.\par \par \uc2 British retailers have reported a year-on-year fall in sales in June, the third month in a row in which sales volumes didn\u8217\'20\'19t grow.\par \par \uc2 The CBI\u8217\'20\'19s latest \u8216\'20\'18distributive trades\u8217\'20\'19 survey, just released, found that sales were seen as poor for the time of year in June and are expected to remain below seasonal norms in July too.\par \par \uc2 enltrRetail sales volumes fell slightly in the year to June, marking the third successive month in which volumes have failed to grow. Retailers expect sales volumes to be flat over the year to July {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/DTS?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #DTS}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/LR7EqqFHAH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/LR7EqqFHAH}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CBI Economics (@CBI_Economics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CBI_Economics/status/1539911803223908354?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 With demand softening, orders placed with suppliers fell in the year to June, while wholesalers also saw a sharp drop in sales growth.\par \par \uc2 Ben Jones\uc2 , lead economist at the CBI, says inflation is eroding shoppers\u8217\'20\'19 disposable income:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRetail volumes are struggling as high inflation eats away at consumers\u8217\'20\'19 budgets. The squeeze on household incomes appears to have offset any boost to activity from the extended Platinum Jubilee bank holiday earlier this month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are also clearer signs that a downturn in consumer spending is beginning to ripple out across the wider distribution sector, with wholesalers seeing a 14-month period of robust sales growth come to a grinding halt this month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAs business sentiment weakens, government action is needed urgently to prevent a deeper and more prolonged downturn. Creating a permanent investment incentive and tackling skills shortages by introducing immediate flexibility to the apprenticeship levy would be strong first steps for boosting confidence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrRetailers also reported lower orders with suppliers\u8212\'20\'14a fourth consecutive month of flat or falling orders. Orders are expected to decline at a similar pace next month {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/DTS?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #DTS}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/hsfkLhz5jp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/hsfkLhz5jp}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 CBI Economics (@CBI_Economics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CBI_Economics/status/1539911994089869314?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.02am BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Norwegian central bank\uc2 . Photograph: Gwladys Fouche/Reuters\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b424de8f088eb951437933#block-62b424de8f088eb951437933"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rising inflation}}}\uc2  isn\u8217\'20\'19t just a UK problem of course.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s just prompted \uc2 Norway\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  to make its largest interest rate increase in two decades, as it joined the rush to raise borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy committee has raised its benchmark interest rate by 50 basis points, from 0.75% to 1.25% - more than expected, and predicted another rate hike in August.\par \par \uc2 The hike came after Norwegian inflation hit 5.4% in April, a 13-year high (but still much lower than the UK, US or eurozone).\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Britain has introduced a new tranche of trade sanctions against Russia, a notice published on the government website said, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 The \u8216\'20\'18Notice to Exporters\u8217\'20\'19 listed new measures including prohibitions on the export to Russia of a range of goods and technology, the export of jet fuel, and the export of sterling or EU denominated banknotes.\par \par \uc2 It also said there were new prohibitions on the provision of technical assistance, and financial services, funds, and brokering services relating to iron and steel imports.\par \par \uc2 The notice set out a further list of goods prohibited for export:\par \par \uc2 * internal repression goods and technology\par \par \uc2 * goods and technology relating to chemical and biological weapons\par \par \uc2 * maritime goods and technology\par \par \uc2 * additional oil refining goods and technology\par \par \uc2 * additional critical industry goods and technology\par \par \uc2 Earlier this week we learned that the UK\u8217\'20\'19s sanctions enforcement office has been trying to introduce the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost extraordinary package of sanctions ever implemented\u8221\'20\'1D in UK history with a group of just 70 staff.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/22/uk-office-charge-russia-sanctions-just-70-staff-director"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK office in charge of Russia sanctions has just 70 staff, says director}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.22am BST\par \par \uc2 UK consumer spending softened last week, as job ads fell\par \par \uc2 UK consumers\u8217\'20\'19 spending on credit and debit cards fell slightly over the past week and the number of job adverts declined, which also indicates growth is slowing.\par \par \uc2 The ;atest weekly data from the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  found that credit and debit card spending fell two percentage points last week.\par \par \uc2 Spending fell in all categories, apart from \u8220\'20\'1Cwork related\u8221\'20\'1D (including spending on road fuel) which rose by 6 percentage points last week, as petrol and diesel prices continued to climb to new heights.\par \par \uc2 enltrUK spending on debit and credit cards decreased to 100% of its February 2020 average in the week to 16 June 2022.\par \par \uc2 There were falls in:\par \par \uc2 ?? Staple spending\par \par \uc2 ?? Social spending\par \par \uc2 ?? Delayable spending\par \par \uc2 ?? Work-related spending (which includes spending on road fuel) increased. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/vagyxrmpWa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/vagyxrmpWa}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1539889818930388992?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 The number of jobs advertised by recruiters \uc2 Adzuna\uc2  fell by 5% in the week to June 17 to 123% of its pre-pandemic average.\par \par \uc2 enltrTotal online job adverts fell by 5% with decreases across all English regions and UK countries.\par \par \uc2 The categories with the largest decreases were:\par \par \uc2 ?? IT, computing and software (14%)\par \par \uc2 ?? Wholesale and retail (13%) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/tq0mLhDZDb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/tq0mLhDZDb}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1539889814698344448?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 A fifth of companies reported that their turnover decreased last month compared with April, while 14% reported a rise in turnover.\par \par \uc2 And over a third said that record energy prices had affected production, suppliers or both.\par \par \uc2 enltrOf businesses not permanently stopped trading:\par \par \uc2 ?? 35% reported their production, suppliers or both had been affected by energy prices in mid-June 2022\par \par \uc2 This is a slight increase from 33% in the second half of May 2022.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1539889811242323968?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.13am BST\par \par \uc2 Consumer-facing businesses at particularly concerned that demand is slowing, says Rhys Herbert, senior economist at \uc2 Lloyds Bank\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He also warns that small firms less able to protect themselves against inflation.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s his take on today\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b424de8f088eb951437933#block-62b424de8f088eb951437933"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey of UK purchasing managers}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven though today\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 PMI\uc2  index remained in growth territory the economic outlook remains cloudy . The one thing that is certain about the UK economy right now is the high degree of uncertainty. High inflation remains a key concern, but as its drivers are still primarily international, using interest rates to combat it creates its own pressure on an economy still shrugging off the effects of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile there are now signs that blockages and delays in supply chains are easing, these are still well above normal and affecting prices. In particular, fuel and raw material costs remain very elevated, and while bigger businesses may be able to hedge these, that may be less of an option for smaller ones, which are left to explore other operational and financial options to ease pressures on their profit margins.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMeanwhile, consumer-facing businesses, particularly, are becoming more concerned that demand is slowing. The squeeze on real spending power from high inflation on essentials, such as energy and food, leaves little room for other, more discretionary items. With further fuel cost rises to come and broader above-target inflation to remain for some time, businesses have almost certainly not yet seen the full effects of the squeeze on spending.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.06am BST\par \par \uc2 UK firms are being hit by rising inflationary pressures, warns Dr. Peter Colman, partner at global consultancy Simon-Kucher & Partner s :\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b424de8f088eb951437933#block-62b424de8f088eb951437933"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAnother disappointing flash PMI for the UK this morning }}}\uc2  with Manufacturing down to 53.4 for June from 54.6 previously, though Services steadies at 53.4. These figures follow on from the slowdown in May\u8217\'20\'19s data and yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s record CPI reading, showing that inflationary pressures on the UK economy are continuing to build.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s becoming increasingly obvious that UK businesses in the manufacturing and services sectors are caught between a rock and a hard place. With unprecedented levels of inflation, slowing volumes and recession fears, implementing price increases is the only viable option to address margin erosion \u8211\'20\'13 a solution that many businesses have been desperate to avoid but may no longer be able to do so.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith no obvious end in sight, the agenda within many UK boardrooms is turning from growth to profitability.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.02am BST\par \par \uc2 Supply chain managers are worried about the current cost of doing business, as inflation hits 40-year highs, says \uc2 Duncan Brock\uc2 , group director at \uc2 CIPS\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe economic uncertainty brought about by war disruptions, the cost of living crisis, and China\u8217\'20\'19s Zero Covid-19 policy, have all dampened [UK] business optimism to its lowest point since the start of the pandemic.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/HeY8QDLdU0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the PMI report}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 enltr???? Latest flash data revealed growth in the UK with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PMI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PMI}}}\uc2  unchanged at 53.1 in June. That said, new order growth slowed as the cost of living crisis intensified while service providers continued to outperform manufacturers. Read more: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/HeY8QDLdU0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/HeY8QDLdU0}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/9QRK2ZeFV7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/9QRK2ZeFV7}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 S&P Global PMI\u8482\'21\'22 (@SPGlobalPMI) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SPGlobalPMI/status/1539892623011446786?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.44am BST\par \par \uc2 UK economy 'running on empty' as high inflation threatens recession\par \par \uc2 UK firms have been hit by a slowdown in new orders, indicating that the economy is \u8216\'20\'18running on empty\u8217\'20\'19 as b usiness confidence falls to levels that typically signal a recession.\par \par \uc2 S&P Global\u8217\'20\'19s survey of UK purchasing managers at services firms and manufacturers found that new order growth this month was the weakest since February 2021.\par \par \uc2 Factory bosses reporting a drop in new business for the first time in two years,\par \par \uc2 UK private sector firms said demand was hit by hesitancy among clients and squeezed budgets due to rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 Overall, June\u8217\'20\'19s composite \uc2 PMI\uc2  index (which tracks activity in the economy) was unchanged at 53.1 in June, showing the slowest growth in a year, helped by orders placed in earlier months.\par \par \uc2 But the new order index fell to 50.8, the lowest since March 2021, showing near-stagnation.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe UK flash \uc2 PMI\uc2  held steady at 53.1 in June, but the forward-looking indicators from the survey deteriorated sharply, down to levels consistent with recession {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/GwCgDV0eMT"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/GwCgDV0eMT}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/uoDAF8mJ23"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/uoDAF8mJ23}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Chris Williamson (@WilliamsonChris) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WilliamsonChris/status/1539890851328974848?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Business confidence slumped to its weakest since May 2020, as firms worried about customers cutting back on spending, and the impact of rapid inflation on the longer-term economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 Job creation was the fastest for three months, led by stronger hiring in the service sector, while wage growth pushed up businesss costs.\par \par \uc2 Firms also kept raising their own prices, as they passed on higher energy, fuel and wage costs to customers.\par \par \uc2 Chris Williamson, chief business economist at S&P Global Market Intelligenc e says the data suggests the UK faces \u8216\'20\'18a troubling combination of recession and elevated inflation\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe economy is starting to look like it is running on empty. Current business growth is being supported by orders placed in prior months as companies report a near-stalling of demand. Manufacturers in particular are struggling with falling orders, especially for exports, and the service sector is already seeing signs of the recent growth spurt from pent-up pandemic demand move into reverse amid the rising cost of living.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBusiness confidence has now slumped to a level which has in the past typically signalled an imminent recession. The weakness of the broad flow of economic data so far in the second quarter points to a drop in GDP which the forward-looking \uc2 PMI\uc2  numbers suggest will gather momentum in the third quarter.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile there are some signs that the inflation could soon peak, the survey data suggest the rate of inflation will meanwhile remain historically high for some time to come, indicating that the UK looks set for a troubling combination of recession and elevated inflation as we move into the second half of the year.\par \par \uc2 enltrSome hinting at a peaking of UK {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/CPI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #CPI}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/inflation?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #inflation}}}\uc2  in the flash \uc2 PMI\uc2  data for June, but this chart illustrates how CPI inflation lags the \uc2 PMI\uc2 , so we can expect CPI to remain very elevated for some time still before moderating later in the year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/yCxB8KeEqY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/yCxB8KeEqY}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Chris Williamson (@WilliamsonChris) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WilliamsonChris/status/1539893021583577089?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.03am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.27am BST\par \par \uc2 Full story: UK borrowed \u163\'00\'A314bn in May as inflation drove up interest debt costs\par \par \uc2 Government borrowing was higher than expected in May at \u163\'00\'A314bn as soaring inflation sent interest payments on the UK\u8217\'20\'19s debt to a monthly record, my colleague Phillip Inman writes:\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk/office-for-national-statistics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Office for National Statistics}}}\uc2  (\uc2 ONS\uc2 ) said debt interest payments leapt 70% on a year ago to \u163\'00\'A37.6bn, the third highest debt interest payment made by central government in any single month and the highest payment in May on record.\par \par \uc2 Some economists said the increase in borrowing and the UK\u8217\'20\'19s slowing economy was likely to push government borrowing \u163\'00\'A320bn higher this year than the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR)\uc2 , the Treasury\u8217\'20\'19s independent forecaster, expected at its last estimate in March.\par \par \uc2 Paul Dales\uc2 , chief UK economist at the consultancy {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.capitaleconomics.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Capital Economics}}}\uc2  , said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the economy weakening and interest rates rising, the public finances will probably perform worse this year than the \uc2 OBR\uc2  forecast.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2 , chief UK economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics, said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/may/26/sunak-u-turns-on-energy-profits-levy-in-15bn-cost-of-living-package"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the extra spending}}}\uc2  by the government to protect low-income families and pensioners from inflation would also dent the public finances in the second half of the financial year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAccordingly, we think that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/apr/26/uk-government-borrowing-deficit-ons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public borrowing}}}\uc2  will total about \u163\'00\'A3130bn this year, well above the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A399bn forecast.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowed-14bn-may-inflation-interest-debt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK borrowed \u163\'00\'A314bn in May as inflation drove up interest debt costs }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.13am BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b41c418f0877fb10b60aad#block-62b41c418f0877fb10b60aad"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18flash PMI\u8217\'20\'19 report shows }}}\uc2  that the eurozone economy is slowing fast, explains Chris Williamson, chief business economist at S&P Global Market Intelligence:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEurozone economic growth is showing signs of faltering as the tailwind of pent-up demand from the pandemic is already fading, having been offset by the cost of living shock and slumping business and consumer confidence.\par \par \uc2 Excluding pandemic lockdown months, June\u8217\'20\'19s slowdown was the most abrupt recorded by the survey since the height of the global financial crisis in November 2008.\par \par \uc2 The slowdown means the latest data signal a rate of GDP growth of just 0.2% at the end of the second quarter, down sharply from 0.6% at the end of the first quarter, with worse likely to come in the second half of the year. Inflows of new business have stalled, led by a slump in demand for goods and reduced demand for services from cash-strapped consumers in particular.\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Eurozone?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Eurozone}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/manufacturing?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #manufacturing}}}\uc2  is looking set for a steep downturn, according to today's flash {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PMI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PMI}}}\uc2  data. Output is already falling and inventories of unsold stock are rising at the steepest rate for 2 years amid weak demand {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/nyycCXCb74"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/nyycCXCb74}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Chris Williamson (@WilliamsonChris) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WilliamsonChris/status/1539883292404731904?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Eurozone business growth slumps as rising prices hit firms\par \par \uc2 Euro zone business growth has slowed sharply to a 16-month low, as demand stalled and rising prices hit customer spending.\par \par \uc2 S&P Global\u8217\'20\'19s flash Composite Purchasing Managers\u8217\'20\'19 Index (\uc2 PMI\uc2 ) has slumped to 51.9 in June from 54.8 in May, weaker than expected, and the lowest level since February 2021.\par \par \uc2 Manufacturing output contracted for the first time in two years and service sector growth cooled, particularly for consumer-facing services.\par \par \uc2 This will reinforce concerns that rising prices could push the eurozone into recession, after inflation hit a record high of 8.1% in May.\par \par \uc2 enltrEurozone growth slumped to a 16-month low in June, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PMI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PMI}}}\uc2  flash estimates, with the headline index dropped to 51.9 (May: 54.8). Demand levels stalled whilst goods production fell for the first time in 2 years. Read more: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/C8Mzmh43EW"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/C8Mzmh43EW}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/3rfCVEmTSB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/3rfCVEmTSB}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 S&P Global PMI\u8482\'21\'22 (@SPGlobalPMI) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SPGlobalPMI/status/1539882138643873793?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrEurozone \uc2 PMI\uc2  drops markedly in June as recession concerns broaden {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ZkJVHpgymC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ZkJVHpgymC}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Bert Colijn\uc2  (@BertColijn) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BertColijn/status/1539881880174092289?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 An index of new business dropped to a 16-month low of 50.0 -- the dividing line between growth and contraction.\par \par \uc2 With growth slowing, companies scaled back their expectations for output over the coming year to the lowest since October 2020 - just before the first Covid-19 vaccine results.\par \par \uc2 The report says:\par \par \uc2 Both the stagnation of demand and worsening outlook were widely blamed on the rising cost of living, tighter financial conditions and concerns over energy and supply chains linked to the Ukraine war and ongoing pandemic disruptions.\par \par \uc2 Price pressures meanwhile remained elevated at levels not seen prior to the pandemic, though a moderation of cost growth for a third successive month hinted at a peaking in the rate of inflation.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.54am BST\par \par \uc2 enltrFlash \uc2 PMI\uc2  data out of Europe this morning is grim, with the French and German metrics coming in below the analyst forecast range, pretty much. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24EUR&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $EUR}}}\uc2  pressured ahead of the release of the Eurozone-wide metrics in around 30 mins.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Newsquawk (@Newsquawk) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Newsquawk/status/1539874331580399616?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Private sector growth in both Germany and France has slowed sharply this month, which will fuel concerns that Europe\u8217\'20\'19s economy is faltering.\par \par \uc2 In France, growth has slumped to its weakest since the peak Omicron disruption at start of 2022, the latest survey of purchasing managers shows.\par \par \uc2 In Germany, growth slowed for the fourth month in a row to a six-month low, signalling a \u8220\'20\'1Csustained loss of momentum in the private sector economy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrBig miss for French PMI... Flash {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/France?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #France}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PMI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PMI}}}\uc2  Composite Output Index slides to 52.8 in June (May 57.0), a 5-month low and way below expectations of 56.0. Manufacturing output index at just 45.7 (\uc2 PMI\uc2  at 51.0) with services index down to 54.4. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Qo7r5ebRf6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Qo7r5ebRf6}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/bczXQ8l55b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/bczXQ8l55b}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Chris Williamson (@WilliamsonChris) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WilliamsonChris/status/1539873193225232386?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrFlash {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Germany?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Germany}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/PMI?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #PMI}}}\uc2  Composite Output Index falls to 51.3 in June (May 53.7, consensus 53.1), a 6-month low and indicative of GDP contracting. As with France, consensus well and truly missed\par \par \uc2 More at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/uRyhgC0GC9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/uRyhgC0GC9}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wG2POaTqMg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/wG2POaTqMg}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Chris Williamson (@WilliamsonChris) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WilliamsonChris/status/1539874467693871106?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 We get the eurozone-wide \uc2 PMI\uc2  report in about 20 minutes....\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.36am BST\par \par \uc2 The copper price has dropped to a 16-month low this morning, as concerns rise over a rise in Covid-19 cases in key consumer China and aggressive US interest rate hikes.\par \par \uc2 Copper is seen as a barometer of economic health; if the global economy dropped into recession, demand for metals would be dented.\par \par \uc2 Three-month copper on the \uc2 London Metal Exchange\uc2  is down over 1% at $8,673 a tonne, Reuters reports, after dropping to its lowest since February 19, 2021, at $8,564.50.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.29am BST\par \par \uc2 The pound is also weaker this morning, dropping by half a cent against the US dollar to $1.22.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.27am BST\par \par \uc2 Recession fears weigh on markets\par \par \uc2 A recession would put fresh strain on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b3fc528f088eb951437814#block-62b3fc528f088eb951437814"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the UK public finances}}}\uc2 , knocking tax revenues and pushing up welfare spending.\par \par \uc2 And worries about an economic downturn have knocked European stock markets at the start of trading.\par \par \uc2 In London, the FTSE 100 index has shed 70 points, or 1%, to 7018 points, back towards last week\u8217\'20\'19s three-month low. Mining companies are among the fallers.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX has lost 0.5%, with France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC 0.6% lower and Italy\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE MIB down 1%.\par \par \uc2 enltr?? European Opening Bell ??\par \par \uc2 ???? FTSE 100 down 0.5%\par \par \uc2 ???? STOXX 50 down 0.7%\par \par \uc2 ???? STOXX 600 down 0.7%\par \par \uc2 ???? DAX down 0.4%\par \par \uc2 ???? CAC 40 down 0.6% {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/peNzOHy3lV"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/peNzOHy3lV}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 PiQ ? (@PriapusIQ) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/PriapusIQ/status/1539867048406392832?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Investors fear that interest rate increases to fight inflation could tip economies into recessions. Yesterday, US central bank chief \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  said the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  was fully committed to bringing prices under control, even if doing so risked an economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 Ipek Ozkardeskaya, senior analyst at \uc2 Swissquote Bank\uc2 , explains:\par \par \uc2 Market optimism couldn\u8217\'20\'19t survive to \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s testimony yesterday, as he said that a recession is possible, and that calling a soft landing is \u8216\'20\'18very challenging\u8217\'20\'19 under the current circumstances.\par \par \uc2 More worryingly, Powell mentioned another risk: the risk of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  not managing to restore price stability and allowing inflation to get entrenched in the economy.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.28am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.15am BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b3fc528f088eb951437814#block-62b3fc528f088eb951437814"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The larger-than-expected rise in public borrowing in May }}}\uc2  is an early blow for the government on a day when it is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jun/23/boris-johnson-double-verdict-wakefield-tiverton-and-honiton-byelections"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expected to lose two by-elections}}}\uc2 , says \uc2 Paul Dales\uc2  of Capital Economics:\par \par \uc2 What\u8217\'20\'19s more, the combination of a further weakening in economic activity and more interest rates rises will probably mean that borrowing overshoots the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2022/23 forecast of \u163\'00\'A399bn by at least \u163\'00\'A310bn.\par \par \uc2 That will limit the ability of the Chancellor to cut taxes and/or provide more grants to households when the cost of living crisis worsens later this year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.12am BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-06-23/uk-government-borrowed-more-than-expected-in-may-as-growth-slows?utm_campaign=socialflow-organic&utm_content=business&utm_source=twitter&cmpid=socialflow-twitter-business&utm_medium=social"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s Bloomberg\u8217\'20\'19s take}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowed more than forecast in May after a 70% surge in interest payments to service the national debt.\par \par \uc2 The budget deficit stood at \u163\'00\'A314 billion, \u163\'00\'A32 billion higher than economists had forecast. Overall government spending was higher than the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2  predicted in March, and receipts lower.\par \par \uc2 Higher interest rates and inflation boosted the money the Treasury spends to service its debt to \u163\'00\'A37.6 billion, the most for any May on record, from \u163\'00\'A34.5 billion a year earlier. The \uc2 OBR\uc2  is forecasting a surge to \u163\'00\'A319.7 billion in June.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowed more than forecast in May, highlighting risks to the public finances as the cost of living crisis threatens to push the economy into recession {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/OekfbdRm2D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/OekfbdRm2D}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bloomberg (@business) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/business/status/1539864060925280256?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.07am BST\par \par \uc2 Public finances 'off to a bad start' this year\par \par \uc2 Martin Beck\uc2 , chief economic advisor to the EY ITEM Club, says the public finances have made a bad start to the financial year, and could get worse.\par \par \uc2 Although the May data showed central government current receipts continuing to grow strongly, the rise was not as robust as the \uc2 OBR\uc2  had anticipated. Similarly, the fall in central government spending was less steep than the \uc2 OBR\uc2  expected, with the impact of very high inflation on debt interest payments a factor.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith April\u8217\'20\'19s outturn revised up significantly, fiscal year 2022-2023 has got off to a disappointing start \u8211\'20\'13 borrowing over the first two months of the fiscal year was \u163\'00\'A36.4bn above the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s forecast. The borrowing data is notoriously revision-prone, so this picture could change. But a further decline in the public finances looks likely as we move through the fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 A slowdown in economic growth could also hit tax revenues, Beck adds:\par \par \uc2 Growth in receipts is likely to come under increasing pressure from faltering activity\par \par \uc2 At the same time, government spending is set to come in well ahead of the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s March forecast given that inflation and interest rates will be much higher, and the cost of the Government\u8217\'20\'19s recent fiscal support package is yet to be incorporated into the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s forecasts.\par \par \uc2 As a result, the EY ITEM Club expects borrowing to come in some way above the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s March projection.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.54am BST\par \par \uc2 Sunak: Rising inflation and debt interest costs challenge the public finances\par \par \uc2 Chancellor of the Exchequer, \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 , has warned that rising inflation is a challenge to the public finances:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRising inflation and increasing debt interest costs pose a challenge for the public finances, as they do for family budgets.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is why we are taking a balanced approach - using our fiscal firepower to provide targeted help with the cost of living, while remaining on track to get debt down.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBeing responsible with the public finances now will mean future generations aren\u8217\'20\'19t burdened with even higher debt repayments, and we can secure our economy for the long term.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 As this chart shows, UK borrowing is running ahead of forecasts this financial year ( since April), and is higher than before the pandemic:\par \par \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowing Photograph: ONS\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.49am BST\par \par \uc2 The cost of servicing \uc2 UK government\uc2  debt has increased considerably in recent months as inflation pushes up the interest paid to holders of RPI index linked gilts, explains Fraser Munro, public sector finance statistician at the \uc2 ONS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s pulled together the key points from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2022/jun/23/uk-borrowing-inflation-debt-costs-oil-gas-recession-stock-markets-factories-services-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-62b3fc528f088eb951437814#block-62b3fc528f088eb951437814"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 May\u8217\'20\'19s borrowing figures}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 enltrIn May 2022, the public sector spent more than it received in taxes and other income, requiring it to borrow \u163\'00\'A314.0 bn, \u163\'00\'A34.0 bn less than in May last year and the third-highest May borrowing on record.\par \par \uc2 This thread provides the story behind the headlines. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4v9zRKGQE3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/4v9zRKGQE3}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/NMkVqY9bQH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/NMkVqY9bQH}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Fraser Munro (@Fraser_ONS_PSF) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Fraser_ONS_PSF/status/1539850843280769029?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrCentral government income was \u163\'00\'A366.6 bn in May 2022, \u163\'00\'A35.7 bn more than in May last year. These receipts include \u163\'00\'A348.3 bn in taxes and \u163\'00\'A314.4 bn in compulsory social contributions (largely National Insurance payments). {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/EpdUOeDz11"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/EpdUOeDz11}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Fraser Munro (@Fraser_ONS_PSF) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Fraser_ONS_PSF/status/1539852836183449601?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrCentral government current (or day-to-day) spending was \u163\'00\'A374.0 bn in May 2022, \u163\'00\'A32.2 bn less than in May last year, with a \u163\'00\'A33.1 bn rise in debt interest being offset by a \u163\'00\'A34.9 bn fall in subsidy payments. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/F36imunTZm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/F36imunTZm}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Fraser Munro (@Fraser_ONS_PSF) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Fraser_ONS_PSF/status/1539852955884699649?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe cost of servicing government debt has increased considerably in recent months as inflation pushes up the interest paid to holders of RPI index linked gilts. \uc2 OBR\uc2  expect interest payments to hit \u163\'00\'A319.7 bn in June, dwarfing the previous record of \u163\'00\'A39.1 bn recorded in June 2021. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/vDeIt7rAkn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/vDeIt7rAkn}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Fraser Munro (@Fraser_ONS_PSF) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Fraser_ONS_PSF/status/1539853124646719489?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe additional borrowing in April has pushed the financial year-to-date total up to \u163\'00\'A335.9 bn. Although \u163\'00\'A36.4 bn less than in the same period last year, borrowing is \u163\'00\'A319.8 bn more than in the same period in pre-pandemic 2019. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ijlMpSofBr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/ijlMpSofBr}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Fraser Munro (@Fraser_ONS_PSF) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Fraser_ONS_PSF/status/1539856322627264512?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: Rising debt costs add to UK borrowing in May\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the world economy and the financial markets.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowed more than expected to balance the books last month, as rising inflation pushed up the cost of servicing the national debt.\par \par \uc2 May\u8217\'20\'19s public finances, just released, show that the public sector spent more than it received in taxes and other income. This required it to borrow \u163\'00\'A314bn, \u163\'00\'A33.7bn more than the independent \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility (OBR) \uc2 forecast -- and ahead of City forecasts of a \u163\'00\'A312bn monthly deficit.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A34bn less than a year ago, due to the drop of pandemic spending such as the furlough scheme, and Test and Trace. But it\u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A38.5bn more than in May 2019, before the coronavirus (COVID-19) pandemic.\par \par \uc2 As this chart shows, it\u8217\'20\'19s the third-highest May borrowing on record (after 2020 and 2021).\par \par \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowing Photograph: ONS\par \par \uc2 Tax take increased year-on-year reflecting the reopening of the economy; Value Added Tax revenues were up 10%, and Business Rates bringing in 13% more than a year ago. That helped to lift tax receipts to \u163\'00\'A348.3bn, an annual increase of \u163\'00\'A33.4bn.\par \par \uc2 But interest payments on the UK national debt jumped by 70% compared with a year earlier. Britain spent \u163\'00\'A37.6bn on debt repayments, around \u163\'00\'A33.1bn more than a year ago when it cost \u163\'00\'A34.5bn.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s because the payments on some \uc2 UK government\uc2  debt, or gilts, are linked to the retail prices index measure of inflation (which hit 11.7% last month, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 we learned yesterday}}}\uc2  ). So as the cost of living increases, so does the interest bill on the national debt.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ONS\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 On an accrued basis, this month saw the third highest debt interest payment made by central government in any single month and the highest payment made in any May on record.\par \par \uc2 UK borrowing Photograph: ONS\par \par \uc2 May\u8217\'20\'19s borrowing lifted the national debt (excluding public sector banks) to \u163\'00\'A32.36 trillion, or around 95.8% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 enltrPublic sector net debt excluding public sector banks was \u163\'00\'A32,363.2 billion at the end of May 2022, or around 95.8% of GDP.\par \par \uc2 This is up \u163\'00\'A3170.1 billion, or 0.5 percentage points of GDP, on May 2021 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/59hUfl8kLE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/59hUfl8kLE}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/3jCpNSXgrF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/3jCpNSXgrF}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  (ONS) (@ONS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ONS/status/1539852129116082177?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 June 23, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Michal Stelmach, senior economist at \uc2 KPMG UK\uc2 , warns that \u8220\'20\'1Cdebt reduction this year remains a long shot\u8221\'20\'1D, given \uc2 Rishi Sunak\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A315bn cost of living support package will add to borrowing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe pace of deficit reduction is set to slow over the coming months, with the government\u8217\'20\'19s latest package of cost of living measures providing a net fiscal loosening worth 0.4% of GDP in 2022-23. We expect borrowing to overshoot the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s March forecast by around \u163\'00\'A320bn this year, largely on account of higher spending and weaker economic growth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe debt profile will depend on the economic outlook which faces acute downside risks in the near term, while rising demand for healthcare coupled with falling working-age participation could also impede fiscal sustainability. We now expect public sector debt to peak in 2023-24, missing the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s March forecast by two years.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up today\par \par \uc2 New surveys of purchasing managers in the UK, eurozone and US will show whether growth is slowing this month, as worries about a possible recession rise.\par \par \uc2 Millions of UK rail passengers faced another day of disruption as this week\u8217\'20\'19s second strike begins. The rail industry is asking people to only travel if necessary today, with fewer than one in five trains in Great Britain expected to run.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2022/jun/22/uk-rail-strike-second-day-of-action-to-go-ahead-after-talks-fail-again"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK rail strike: second day of action to go ahead after talks fail again}}}\par \par \uc2 With UK inflation hitting a 40-year high of 9.1% last month, industrial unrest could spread as the government faces more calls for pay rises that reflect the cost of living.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/education/2022/jun/23/english-schools-warn-of-acute-teacher-shortages-without-inflation-plus-pay-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The country\u8217\'20\'19s biggest teaching union is warning of strike action this autumn}}}\uc2  without an \u8220\'20\'1Cinflation plus\u8221\'20\'1D deal.\par \par \uc2 Norway\u8217\'20\'19s central bank\uc2  is expected to raise interest rates, from 0.75% to 1%, while the \uc2 Central Bank of the Republic of Turkey\uc2  could leave rate on hold at 14%.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 * 7am BST: UK public finances for May\par \par \uc2 * 9am BST: Eurozone \u8216\'20\'18flash\u8217\'20\'19 \uc2 PMI\uc2  survey of manufacturing and services for June\par \par \uc2 * 9am BST: \uc2 Norges Bank\uc2  interest rate decision\par \par \uc2 * 9.30am BST: UK \u8216\'20\'18flash\u8217\'20\'19 PMI survey of manufacturing and services for June\par \par \uc2 * 11am BST: CBI distributive trades survey of UK retail\par \par \uc2 * Noon BST: \uc2 Turkish central bank\uc2  interest rate decision\par \par \uc2 * 1.30pm BST: US weekly jobless claims report\par \par \uc2 * 2.45pm BST: US \u8216\'20\'18flash\u8217\'20\'19 PMI survey of manufacturing and services for June\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.48am BST\par \par \uc2 2022-06-23T06:37:06Z 2022-06-23T14:48:06Z\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.guim.co.uk/4592c0fbfd9990d7d24521785183cc820d416060/85_0_5570_3342/500.jpg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://media.guim.co.uk/4592c0fbfd9990d7d24521785183cc820d416060/85_0_5570_3342/500.jpg}}}\uc2  Commuters on London Bridge heading towards the City of London. Naked Wine\u8217\'20\'19s share price The entrance to the M6 northbound Burton-In-Kendal service station. The \uc2 Norwegian central bank\uc2 . \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowing \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowing UK borrowing\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220623ei6n000rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
