\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Exhausted US airport workers see hope in minimum wage bill as summer of travel chaos looms\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 779 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 June 2022\par 13:16 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Bill would raise the minimum wage for US airport workers at a time when the travel industry in the US is in a deep crisis\par \par \uc2 Ana Vazquez has worked for five years at Orlando international airport in Florida as a wheelchair attendant and child escort for unaccompanied minors. She makes $12 an hour, with no paid time off and the only health insurance coverage offered to her is charged out of pocket and unaffordable on her pay.\par \par \uc2 Vazquez often does the work of two or three workers because of chronic understaffing, which results in travelers waiting long periods for wheelchair service and attendants having to push two wheelchairs at once.\par \par \uc2 Vazquez lives with her daughter because she can\u8217\'20\'19t afford her own apartment, as and rent has increased significantly throughout the area, while her wages have remained the same.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re receiving our wages like we\u8217\'20\'19re in 1995,\u8221\'20\'1D said Vazquez. \u8220\'20\'1CI have to live with my daughter, sleeping on the corner of her bed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Vazquez. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m going to set up the walk-in closet like a bedroom.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On 16 June, Senator \uc2 Ed Markey\uc2 , Senator Richard Blumenthal and Representative Chuy Garcia \u8211\'20\'13 all Democrats \u8211\'20\'13 introduced the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://chuygarcia.house.gov/sites/evo-subsites/chuygarcia.house.gov/files/wysiwyg_uploaded/Updated%20Good%20Jobs%20for%20Good%20Airports%20Act-Congressman%20Jesus%20Chuy%20Garica_xml.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Good Jobs for Good Airports Act}}}\uc2 , which would raise the minimum wage for US airport workers, including those who work for contractors, to $15 an hour, provide paid time off and at least $4.60 an hour toward health insurance.\par \par \uc2 The bill is backed by large US transportation unions, including the \uc2 SEIU\uc2 , \uc2 CWA\uc2 , \uc2 Unite Here\uc2 , the Transport Workers Union (TWU), the \uc2 International Brotherhood of Teamsters\uc2 , the \uc2 International Association of Machinists and Aerospace Workers (IAM) \uc2 and the National Conference of Firemen and Oilers (NCFO).\par \par \uc2 It comes at a time when the travel industry in the US \u8211\'20\'13 especially the airlines and airports \u8211\'20\'13 is in a deep crisis, as staff shortages in the wake of the coronavirus pandemic have resulted in thousands of delayed and canceled flights that have promised a summer of chaos for millions of American travelers.\par \par \uc2 Rob Hill, executive vice-president of 32BJ \uc2 SEIU\uc2  and director of the national airports organizing campaign, said airlines and airports have relied on contractors that have driven down standards and wages for workers in a race to the bottom for the cheapest bid, which has made organizing workers by a single employer difficult.\par \par \uc2 This legislation focuses on entire airports to raise standards across the board to facilitate union organizing and stabilize the workforce at airports with livable wages and benefits amid high employee turnovers and worker shortages, especially as significant federal funds go to airports and the airline industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith this bill, the federal government will be raising the standard for potentially hundreds and thousands of airport workers, mostly workers of color,\u8221\'20\'1D said Hill. \u8220\'20\'1CIf the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s going to fund money into this, it should be a benefit for workers, it should be a benefit for taxpayers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shawn Montgomery, an airplane cabin cleaner for an \uc2 American Airlines\uc2  contractor at Charlotte Douglas international airport in North Carolina for about a year, said he is constantly being rushed while on the job, often given only 10 minutes to clean an entire plane.\par \par \uc2 Montgomery and his co-workers take out trash and work outside in hot weather, without time to get a drink of water, and they often experience heat exhaustion symptoms. Some of their break rooms don\u8217\'20\'19t provide cold water or air conditioning and they\u8217\'20\'19re not allowed to use airline break rooms.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur jobs, our positions and who we are, we\u8217\'20\'19re not valued, and that\u8217\'20\'19s the biggest reason why we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to unionize. I want conditions to be better for my current co-workers, but I also want the conditions to be better for the next set of people that are coming in,\u8221\'20\'1D said Montgomery.\par \par \uc2 Since he started last year, only one co-worker out of 10 in his training class is still on the job, because of the low pay, lack of benefits and strenuous working conditions.\par \par \uc2 He recently missed the graduations of two grandchildren because he has no paid time off and couldn\u8217\'20\'19t afford to lose the income. He works a second job to try to make ends meet, but struggles with the stress of trying to keep up with rising costs of rent, gas, food and other basic necessities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe airline industry itself has been granted billions and billions of dollars in help. None of that has trickled down to the very people who are the essence of those airplanes being able to leave the ground,\u8221\'20\'1D added Montgomery.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220623ei6n0018i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
