\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Workers are winning\u8217\'20\'19: Colorado law hailed as important victory for public sector workers\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 838 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 June 2022\par 09:51 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands of municipal workers have won the right to form unions but law stops short of allowing them to strike\par \par \uc2 Labor unions have been fighting to secure the right to unionize and collectively bargain for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.denverpost.com/2022/04/12/colorado-public-workers-union-rights-bill/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  more than 250,000 public sector workers}}}\uc2  at cities, schools, colleges and counties in Colorado.\par \par \uc2 Today, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://files.epi.org/uploads/246189.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 24 US states}}}\uc2 , including Colorado, prohibit or limit collective bargaining rights for public sector workers, resulting in significant discrepancies in union density and wages among public sector workers in these states compared with states that mandate public employers to bargain with workers.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afscme.org/share/support-psfna"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Unions }}}\uc2  have also been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamster.org/2022/03/all-public-sector-workers-deserve-the-right-to-collectively-bargain/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pushing }}}\uc2  for Congress to pass a bill to expand collective bargaining rights to all public sector workers across the US through the Public Service Freedom to Negotiate Act.\par \par \uc2 An {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://files.epi.org/uploads/246189.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 analysis}}}\uc2  conducted by the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  found local government workers receive, on average, 14.1% less weekly pay compared with similar private sector workers, and local government workers in states with no collective bargaining rights averaging 22.9% lower pay. Those local government workers with weak collective bargaining rights average a 16.6% pay gap, compared with just 10.5% gap for workers in states with strong collective bargaining rights.\par \par \uc2 Brittany Williams worked as a clinical social worker in child welfare services in El Paso county, Colorado, for about four years before leaving in July 2021 due to management\u8217\'20\'19s lack of concern for high workloads and failure to listen to feedback from frontline workers on how to improve services.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is worse than not being unionized. It was like they\u8217\'20\'19re going to pretend like you have a voice, and they\u8217\'20\'19re going to make you feel like you do, but the minute you try to use it, we\u8217\'20\'19re going to use it against you and punish you for it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Williams. \u8220\'20\'1CI stayed long enough to know what the problems were and I left because I knew that I was never going to have a voice to fix them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Williams and several other co-workers started talking about ways in which they can unionize, and shortly after her management pulled her into anti-union meetings, which halted the talk about unionizing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNone of us left those meetings feeling secure of our employment. We definitely all felt like our jobs had been threatened,\u8221\'20\'1D Williams said. She said she wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have left her position if she had collective bargaining rights to exercise her voice in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 On 27 May, Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Jared Polis\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afscme.org/blog/colorado-collective-bargaining-bill-signed-into-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 signed}}}\uc2  the Collective Bargaining for Counties bill into law, which expands collective bargaining rights to 36,000 county workers throughout Colorado \u8211\'20\'13 though that is far from the total sought by unions in the state.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afscme.org/blog/covid-19-is-not-slowing-down-foster-care-adoption-services-in-adams-county-co"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Heather Burke}}}\uc2 , president of \uc2 American Federation of State, County and Municipal Employees\uc2  (AFSCME) Local 3927, and a full-time social caseworker for Adams county division of children and family services, fought with her co-workers to win collective bargaining rights before the bill\u8217\'20\'19s passage, winning a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afscme.org/blog/adams-county-workers-unanimously-ratify-historic-contract"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first contract}}}\uc2  in August 2021, which included important safety protections for workers on the job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFrom the start, we noticed a difference,\u8221\'20\'1D said Burke. \u8220\'20\'1CWe went from this culture of fear to this culture of being able to speak up and ask for help or give suggestions without fear of retaliation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The bill, although a compromise from a previously proposed bill that would have granted the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://co.chalkbeat.org/2022/4/25/23042142/colorado-public-sector-collective-bargaining-bill-excludes-k-12-higher-ed-workers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 right to strike}}}\uc2  to about 250,000 public sector workers throughout Colorado, was hailed as one of the most significant expansions of collective bargaining rights for public sector workers in recent years. It goes into effect next year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll across the nation, workers are fighting tooth and nail to get a seat at the table, and they\u8217\'20\'19re winning. We see it in \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  coffee shops. We see it in cultural institutions, and now we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing it in Colorado, where county workers will have the freedom to negotiate to improve their lives and strengthen the public services they provide,\u8221\'20\'1D said the AFSCME president, \uc2 Lee Saunders\uc2 , in response to the bill\u8217\'20\'19s passage.\par \par \uc2 Opponents to the legislation included {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fortmorgantimes.com/2022/05/10/officials-from-37-counties-call-for-veto-of-collective-bargaining-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several county governments}}}\uc2 , which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.denverpost.com/2022/04/25/editorial-colorados-counties-shouldnt-be-left-holding-the-purse-for-new-unions/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued }}}\uc2  the costs would be too high to cover the cost of wage and benefit increases that come with unionization.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt stung a little, especially coming from my own county,\u8221\'20\'1D said Josette Jaramillo, president of the Colorado \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2  and AFSCME Local 1335, who has worked as a caseworker in Pueblo county\u8217\'20\'19s department of child welfare for 17 years. She has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.coloradopolitics.com/opinion/give-county-workers-a-seat-at-the-table/article_f9a1b258-bc54-11ec-8d35-0375d57f49e9.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  fighting}}}\uc2  for years to secure collective bargaining rights for herself, her co-workers, and other public sector workers throughout Colorado.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe services that county workers provide are vital to our community, especially our most vulnerable neighbors. This just gives us an opportunity to have a voice on the job and to talk about how we can improve the workplace of the workers, as well as the services that we provide to our communities,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jaramillo.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220612ei6c000p1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
