\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re still struggling\u8217\'20\'19: low unemployment can\u8217\'20\'19t hide impact of low wages and rising inflation\b0\par\par\uc2 Dominic Rushe in Las Vegas \par \uc2 1,618 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 June 2022\par 15:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers in Las Vegas are still feeling the effects of employers\u8217\'20\'19 pandemic cuts \u8211\'20\'13 all made worse by rising inflation and low wages\par \par \uc2 In the rotating restaurant at the top of the Strat hotel and casino, guests can once again enjoy $20 cocktails or a $90 shellfish display for two while taking in the expansive views of downtown Las Vegas from its landmark tower. After the Covid shutdown, Vegas is back in business. But not everyone seems happy, or sure how long it will last.\par \par \uc2 On a recent afternoon, just out of view of the hotel\u8217\'20\'19s 1,000-plus feet (350-metre) spike, a couple of hundred hospitality service workers were gathered in a nearby car park. In baking 90F (32C) heat, speakers told the workers that they must fight to get improved contracts and controls for soaring rents. \u8220\'20\'1C S\u237\'00\'ED, se puede \u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 yes, we can \u8211\'20\'13 they shouted outside the headquarters of Nevada\u8217\'20\'19s powerful Culinary Workers Union.\par \par \uc2 While the housekeepers, chefs and other workers sang and chanted through the meeting, there was no disguising a deep sense of anger about their working conditions and the direction of the economy.\par \par \uc2 A cursory scan of Nevada\u8217\'20\'19s economic statistics would suggest that life has got better recently for its members. Unemployment hit 30% in Las Vegas in April 2020 when Covid closed the city down \u8211\'20\'13 the highest rate in the nation. Now it\u8217\'20\'19s 5%, higher than the national average but still a huge improvement.\par \par \uc2 Las Vegas has been on a roll recently. Few cities were hit as hard financially by the pandemic. Now the tables are open again, gambling revenues are at new highs, hotel occupancies are climbing, conventioneers are back in town. But the mood is strained. Worker after worker said they were still feeling the effects of cuts made by their employers during the pandemic and were now suffering as inflation drove up prices and wages failed to keep up.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEverything is going up. Gas, food, rent, everything,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gladis Blanco, a housekeeper at the Bellagio. \u8220\'20\'1CBut not our wages. It\u8217\'20\'19s getting better for businesses but not for us: we are still struggling.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In many ways, Vegas holds a funhouse mirror to the deeply strange US economy. Nationally, the unemployment rate is 3.6%, close to a 50-year low. Consumers are spending and wages are rising. And yet supply chain problems persist, businesses complain they can\u8217\'20\'19t get staff, workers are angry about how they were treated during the pandemic and after, and tourists are unhappy with shortages and poor service.\par \par \uc2 Everyone is worried about inflation \u8211\'20\'13 rising faster than wages for many \u8211\'20\'13 and interest rates. Looming over all this is the threat of a recession \u8211\'20\'13 one likely to be felt first in a city reliant on freewheeling spending, which dries up as quickly as spilled water on the hot Las Vegas Strip in leaner times.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s becoming a more delicate situation every day,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brian Gordon, principal of Las Vegas-based economic analyst Applied Analysis. \u8220\'20\'1CLas Vegas was ground zero for what the pandemic meant: casinos had never shut down before,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CRight at the time when the recovery is taking hold, global economic factors are taking a hold. Global inflation, supply chain challenges, rising interest rates, all of that is putting downwards pressure on spending,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a very unique time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In five short months, \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s weird good/bad economy will define the US midterm elections \u8211\'20\'13 especially important in swing-state Nevada, which only narrowly backed the president in 2020. But bigger tensions will remain. The pandemic highlighted a schism between America\u8217\'20\'19s workers and their bosses, one that has created a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/16/us-union-victories-wave-labor-strategists"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wave of union organising}}}\uc2  not seen in a generation \u8211\'20\'13 and that schism appears only to have widened since the pandemic was unofficially declared \u8220\'20\'1Cover\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 This lack of trust is broadly felt across the US. Some 42% of Americans told Gallup they were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/5248/big-business.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cvery dissatisfied\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  with the size and influence of major corporations in 2022, up from 36% in 2019.\par \par \uc2 At the union rally, workers argued that their employers had taken advantage of the pandemic to lay them off and were now wanting to call them in on short shifts to save money, even as business boomed.\par \par \uc2 James Loreto, 51, has worked as a food server at the Mandalay Bay hotel for 21 years. He was laid off in March 2020 and now, while he is officially back at work, he is on call for shifts. Some weeks he works two days, sometimes five. \u8220\'20\'1CI have to sit by the phone. I can\u8217\'20\'19t do anything,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAll that time, all those years \u8211\'20\'13 blood, sweat, tears \u8211\'20\'13 and I\u8217\'20\'19m still struggling to make my hours every week,\u8221\'20\'1D he told the crowd.\par \par \uc2 Like many workers at the rally, Loreto said businesses seemed to be doing well \u8220\'20\'1Cpost-Covid\u8221\'20\'1D, but workers not so much.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe casino is packed at the weekend and business is resuming, and yet there are still so many of us struggling to make payments to cover our healthcare,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile business executives \u8220\'20\'1Care still selling, buying, expanding and using their money so they can drive around looking nice and sharp while you and I are out here in this heat\u8221\'20\'1D, he said.\par \par \uc2 The culinary union \u8211\'20\'13 which represents 60,000 members in Las Vegas and Reno \u8211\'20\'13 is pushing for legislation to cap rent rises, which have been as much as 40% in the city. It is a force to be reckoned with, knocking on 650,000 doors in the last election and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2020/11/08/932744233/culinary-workers-union-take-credit-for-bidens-win-in-nevada"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claiming credit for Biden\u8217\'20\'19s narrow victory}}}\uc2  in a swing state.\par \par \uc2 It will be out in force to back progressive candidates again in the upcoming elections, hoping for structural change. Similar battles will play out across America as the election cycle spins faster.\par \par \uc2 Over at the Las Vegas Chamber of Commerce, there are similar concerns about the economy but very different views on what has gone wrong and what can be done about it.\par \par \uc2 Its president, Mary Beth Sewald, will never forget how the pandemic closed Vegas down. \u8220\'20\'1CI have never seen the like. It was actually a very scary and sad time,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CEverything was shuttered. Locals were walking, riding their bicycles down the Strip. It was surreal,\u8221\'20\'1D not a word to be taken lightly from a woman who lives in a city with a fake volcano, a pyramid and wedding chapels filled with Elvis impersonators.\par \par \uc2 Now that the lights are back on, Vegas is still going through strange times. \u8220\'20\'1CThe demand has come back but a lot of restaurants, the larger properties, don\u8217\'20\'19t have the employees to be able to handle the amount of demand,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Tourists agree. Friends Simon Payne and Nick Wadia, over from the UK for a golf and gambling holiday, have been very disappointed with Vegas hospitality. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve not once seen a housemaid. I had no towels,\u8221\'20\'1D said Payne. When they had interacted with staff, they had been unhelpful and ill-informed, they said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey just seem really unhappy in their jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D said Wadia.\par \par \uc2 Nor are they impressed with the prices. \u8220\'20\'1CI paid $8 [\u163\'00\'A36.40] for a coffee that is \u163\'00\'A32.50 [$3.15] at home. Water was $7.90.\u8221\'20\'1D The only thing they found cheap was gas: it\u8217\'20\'19s now more than $5 a gallon in Nevada, a huge rise for American consumers and up from $3.60 a gallon a year ago, but still far cheaper than in the UK.\par \par \uc2 Sewald described Vegas\u8217\'20\'19s hiring problem as a \u8220\'20\'1Cheadscratcher\u8221\'20\'1D. The government stimulus cheques had gone but, she said, people didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to work. Childcare was a big issue and the chamber had been working on helping with that. But something bigger may be happening. Employers speculate that the pandemic has reordered priorities for many, particularly millennials.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think there is a certain portion of the population saying work and money is not our priority,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI speak to our members and they say we can\u8217\'20\'19t find anyone who will come to work and take these jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She believes there may be worse to come. The lack of staff has been compounded by inflation, which is hitting small businesses hardest.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s kind of a perfect storm,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhen you consider inflation, supply chain, lack of workforce, what else could you have? The cost of doing business has gone up dramatically and immediately; now, with inflation, those costs have gone up even more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For Sewald, the answer is less regulation, not more. The chamber successfully lobbied to kill off 85 bills it argues would have increased costs on business in the last legislative session and stands ready to do it again. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of our legislators are well-intentioned but they don\u8217\'20\'19t understand the unintended consequences of legislation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The chamber\u8217\'20\'19s position will set it at loggerheads with the Culinary Union come November, another fight between employers and employees both struggling with the same issues but fighting from opposing sides.\par \par \uc2 Whichever party wins the election in November will inherit an economic landscape as divided as the political one, and an economy that may be turning for the worse.\par \par \uc2 Lareto fears for the future of his children. \u8220\'20\'1CMan, it brings me to tears,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI have an 18-year-old and a 21-year-old. Their dreams are being destroyed, something needs to be done. I see a lot of empty houses, people being displaced, people sleeping in cars \u8230\'20\'26 We have got to do something and we have got to do something now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220608ei66002pn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
