\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I cannot survive on $260 a week\u8217\'20\'19: US retail and fast-food workers strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,026 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 May 2022\par 10:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers who bore the brunt of the Covid pandemic at billion-dollar companies such as \uc2 Dollar General\uc2 , \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  and Wendy\u8217\'20\'19s are leading a surge in action\par \par \uc2 Workers in America\u8217\'20\'19s fast-food and retail sectors who worked on the frontlines through the dangers of the Covid-19 pandemic are continuing a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/dec/21/labor-organizing-pandemic-decline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 trend }}}\uc2  of strikes and protests over low wages, safety concerns and sexual harassment issues on the job.\par \par \uc2 The Covid-19 pandemic has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/2022/4/28/labors-great-resurgence"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 incited }}}\uc2  a resurgence of interest and support for the US labor movement and for low-wage workers who bore the brunt of Covid-19 risks.\par \par \uc2 The unrest also comes as corporations have often {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/blog/the-avenue/2022/05/04/frontline-workers-were-excluded-from-companies-pandemic-windfalls-no-wonder-so-many-are-forming-unions/?preview_id=1604227"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  record profits and showered executives with pay increases, stock buybacks and bonuses, while workers received minimal pay increases. Workers at billion-dollar corporations from \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  to \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s still {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/19/how-much-retail-food-companies-pay-workers-per-hour"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 make on average}}}\uc2  less than $15 an hour while often being forced to work in unsafe, grueling conditions.\par \par \uc2 On 2 May, \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  workers at a store in Marion, North Carolina, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.tiktok.com/@fightforfairwages/video/7093257572182117678?is_copy_url=1&is_from_webapp=v1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 walked off the job}}}\uc2  over low wages.\par \par \uc2 Ashley Sierra has worked at \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  for two years and makes just $11 an hour, while only receiving part-time hours. A mother of three, she relies on family members to barely make ends meet. \u8220\'20\'1CMy weekly paycheck is no more than $200, $260 at the max. I have three children, I cannot survive on $260 a week, it\u8217\'20\'19s just not working. It needs to get upped to at least $15 an hour, the bottom is $15, because we work so hard for so little,\u8221\'20\'1D said Sierra.\par \par \uc2 Dollar General{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newscenter.dollargeneral.com/news/dollar-general-corporation-reports-fourth-quarter-2021-results.htm#:~:text=Operating%20profit%20for%20fiscal%20year,to%20the%20COVID%2D19%20pandemic."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2  a profit of $3.2bn and their CEO was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aflcio.org/paywatch/DG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paid over $16.4m}}}\uc2  in 2021, 986 times the median pay of the company\u8217\'20\'19s workers.\par \par \uc2 Sierra said the store was often understaffed and overstocked with items that block aisles, and that she feared for her safety over potential robberies and theft when she and just one other co-worker are working the entire store.\par \par \uc2 Dollar General\uc2  did not comment on the company\u8217\'20\'19s low wages. In regards to the walkout, a spokesperson said in an email: \u8220\'20\'1CWe understand a small number of employees chose to express their personal opinions about the company earlier this week by walking off the job. We respect our employees\u8217\'20\'19 right to engage in protected activities and as is our practice, we plan to listen, and are listening, to their feedback.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers at a McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s in Los Angeles began a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Fightfor15LA/status/1521572668297809921"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strike}}}\uc2  on Monday, 2 May, in response to plumbing issues at the restaurant that recently worsened, emitting odors that have made workers sick.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt gives me headaches, stomachaches and nausea, and it\u8217\'20\'19s been happening to my co-workers also,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jasmina Alfaro, one of the striking workers at the \uc2 McDonald\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s location. \u8220\'20\'1CIt has become a horrible smell all over the kitchen and store and we\u8217\'20\'19ve noticed there\u8217\'20\'19s water also leaking with disgusting debris.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She explained for the past year a foul odor had been emanating from pipes near the drive thru window, but severely worsened over the past week throughout the entire restaurant. Despite the odor, workers were still expected to conduct business as usual. Alfaro said she had missed days of work due to getting sick from the odor and had not been compensated for that time missed from work.\par \par \uc2 Alfaro said the strike will continue until the problem is fixed, and the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Fightfor15LA/status/1521572668297809921"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  issue}}}\uc2  is an example of why fast-food workers in California need local proposed law {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/01/california-fast-food-workers-bill-fast-recovery-act"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AB 257 }}}\uc2  to pass, which would establish a statewide fast-food council with worker representation to set wage and other industry standards, including safety protections.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the only way we\u8217\'20\'19re going to have a voice that represents us, so we can be listened to and not be ignored. We shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have to risk our health to make a living,\u8221\'20\'1D Alfaro said.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s\uc2  did not comment on the strike but claimed in an email: \u8220\'20\'1CThis restaurant underwent a recent health inspection and was found to be in good standing. We have already begun repairing a plumbing issue that recently arose at the restaurant and expect it to be resolved shortly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At a Jack in the Box location in Sacramento, California, workers went on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://link.mediaoutreach.meltwater.com/ls/click?upn=dOOQzUBvOXAxCaULtariHT23Tq54Ka3iqRiBNRqF-2BYbfVC7s5nwDJipZxwJQ8pE-2BrJhVYbe7Gfrdh6PB3JEuXtymIxtQn-2Bu6fJGbZnSGdX4-3D7BFJ_vDhyrByJyj9jzFVVCWkYC7U4bd54RrN1Ohtso1eFzPBknXdvGqBAS4WCheAdkJDL2lltQykJsKC0tTiyYoeEutwQoHcgHWB7Rq8A7q81eHSmW43PzSEqj1Zb5oXSntke6RYFwu4jtAOvjVzTAh-2FsgOexqgHko5z66bIyR156MmJ6ryIVjOWiCuuTubtSuzOeFvYz0utLlNcRlYnUOOPTEM-2FtqK6CXIBhxJNJmrOQyjI0WGjwpA6k6PwkksrAfDwWbsFibkrv1-2BSOwvdAS-2FhC1ZKWTd0iq6uO55-2BwWpbZuW-2BsSs8UPOqsUuwYLcetzyq32gR7dnzaBIZb-2F-2FbaF-2FIWyldlyTeTB2ztkjcpkVsL7qxmkmiCJYkuboYLyW8NCHns8-2F4dBFMODTJOWKiWvaJtag-3D-3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 strike for three days}}}\uc2  at the end of April 2022 over faulty equipment and safety concerns, and short staffing.\par \par \uc2 Workers at a Wendy\u8217\'20\'19s in Weaverville, North Carolina, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc17news.com/news/2022/04/30/wendys-employees-protest-claim-sexual-harassment-from-management/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went on strike}}}\uc2  on 23 April through 1 May over sexual harassment and abuse from the restaurant\u8217\'20\'19s general manager toward employees that upper management had not addressed.\par \par \uc2 Charity Bradley, a Wendy\u8217\'20\'19s manager at the restaurant, claimed she was retaliated against for reporting complaints about the general management to corporate, as she was taken off the schedule and locked out of the crew app used to communicate with co-workers. Bradley was reinstated by the end of the strike and returned to work on 2 May.\par \par \uc2 Bradley said the strike began the very next day after she was removed from the schedule and the app.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we reported it to upper management, they pretty much just blew it off, they really didn\u8217\'20\'19t do anything. We called the corporate hotline several times and never received any response,\u8221\'20\'1D said Bradley.\par \par \uc2 She added: \u8220\'20\'1CWendy\u8217\'20\'19s could be a good place to work. They need to get it together and start taking care of their people and start doing what\u8217\'20\'19s right, not sweeping it under the rug.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The CEO of \uc2 Tar Heel Capital Corp\uc2 , which operates several Wendy\u8217\'20\'19s franchises in North Carolina, said in a statement in regards to the strike: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are aware of the allegations being made and are taking this very seriously, following our policy of conducting a thorough investigation into the matter. As an organization, we strive to create a safe and comfortable work environment free from harassment. If, at the conclusion of our investigation, we find that any of the accusations are true, we will take swift disciplinary action.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220516ei5g001gy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
