\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The key to worker power in America? Let a thousand Chris Smalls bloom\b0\par\par\uc2 Steven Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,282 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 May 2022\par 13:41 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Labor leader and fired Amazon worker Chris Smalls says workers at 100 Amazon facilities have contacted him to set up a union. They need support\par \par \uc2 In recent days, Americans have witnessed developments on the labor front that could have scarcely been imagined just a few years ago. There was the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/31/amazon-vote-union-elections-new-york-alabama"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 landmark union victory}}}\uc2  at Amazon\u8217\'20\'19s 8,300-employee fulfillment center on Staten Island \u8211\'20\'13 at a corporation that many thought impossible to unionize. Then there\u8217\'20\'19s the flood of union wins at another iconic company, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , with the union victorious in 57 of 64 elections at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  cafes, an extraordinarily high winning percentage. What\u8217\'20\'19s more, baristas have petitioned for union votes at nearly {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://perfectunion.us/map-where-are-starbucks-workers-unionizing/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  200 more Starbucks}}}\uc2 , notwithstanding {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/mar/16/starbucks-howard-schultz-ceo-again-us-workers-unions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CEO Howard Schultz}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s fierce efforts to squelch the union drive.\par \par \uc2 At \uc2 Grinnell College\uc2  in Iowa, undergraduate workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.teenvogue.com/story/grinnell-college-student-union-election"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted overwhelmingly}}}\uc2 , 327 to 6, to form the nation\u8217\'20\'19s first wall-to-wall undergraduate workers union, representing everyone from dining hall workers to research assistants to library aides. At Dartmouth, student dining hall workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nhpr.org/nh-news/2022-04-01/dartmouth-dining-workers-form-union-in-unanimous-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted 52-0 to unionize}}}\uc2 . Workers at an REI store in Manhattan voted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/03/02/business/rei-union-new-york.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in favor of unionizing}}}\uc2  in an 88-14 landslide, becoming the first unionize REI vote. And now tech giant \uc2 Apple\uc2  is facing the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/business-61145127"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first-ever union drive}}}\uc2  at its retail stores.\par \par \uc2 In my 25 years writing about labor, I\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen so much excitement about unions. For years, America\u8217\'20\'19s labor leaders have struggled to figure out how to reverse the union movement\u8217\'20\'19s decades-long decline, but to no avail. But thanks to two important, often unappreciated trends, unions have the best opportunity in decades to halt their slide.\par \par \uc2 First, young workers are showing extraordinary enthusiasm about unions. Second, workers have embraced a long-neglected strategy, called self-organizing or bottom-up organizing that\u8217\'20\'19s proving highly successful. With self-organizing, employees at a workplace, dissatisfied with their pay and working conditions, conclude that unionizing is a smart way to improve matters (and they\u8217\'20\'19re often doing that with little or no help from large, established unions).\par \par \uc2 Among young Americans there\u8217\'20\'19s an undeniable surge of interest in unions. A study by professors at MIT and Columbia found that 74% of Americans aged 18 to 24 say they would vote to join a union if they could. This helps explain the recent landslide votes to unionize at many \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , including tallies of 20-0, 17-0, 23-1 and 33-2. This also helps explain the victory at Amazon\u8217\'20\'19s mammoth warehouse on Staten Island, where many key organizers were Amazon workers in their twenties. (The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/02/technology/amazon-union-staten-island.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 union lost}}}\uc2 , however, at a smaller Amazon sorting center on Staten Island). All this youthful excitement has fueled the unionization wave among thousands of grad student workers and adjunct professors as well as workers at tech companies, art museums, digital news sites, political campaigns and nonprofits like the Audubon Society and Friends of the Earth.\par \par \uc2 Young workers are flocking to unions for numerous reasons. Many are overwhelmed by student debt and fast-rising housing costs. Many feel they\u8217\'20\'19re being paid far less than they deserve at a time when corporate profits have soared. Many are frustrated to be told they will be the first generation in US history to do worse economically than their parents. Many have been inspired by Bernie Sanders, the Fight for $15, Black Lives Matter and/or #MeToo, and they\u8217\'20\'19re raring to fight for a better world \u8211\'20\'13 and workplace.\par \par \uc2 Unlike their parents and grandparents, today\u8217\'20\'19s young workers came of age long after the era of horrendous Teamster and Longshoremen corruption that turned off so many Americans to unions. Remember the movie On the Waterfront? Today\u8217\'20\'19s young workers also came of age after Ronald Reagan defeated the 1981 air traffic controllers\u8217\'20\'19 strike and fired more than 11,000 of them \u8211\'20\'13 moves that made many workers nervous about unions. Today\u8217\'20\'19s young workers have a more upbeat attitude about unions.\par \par \uc2 The current burst of self-organizing also makes this an unusually promising moment for labor. Christian Smalls, the fired Amazon worker who spearheaded the winning drive on Staten Island, led a classic self-organizing effort \u8211\'20\'13 he co-founded a new, independent union, used \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  to raise the $120,000 spent on the campaign, and relied on 15 Amazon workers as the main organizers.\par \par \uc2 The self-organizing we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen at Amazon and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has some big advantages over traditional organizing drives run by paid union staffers. Self-organizing excites workers because they see that it\u8217\'20\'19s led by their co-workers, people they know (rather than by union staffers they don\u8217\'20\'19t know). Self-organizing effectively neutralizes one of corporate America\u8217\'20\'19s favorite anti-union arguments \u8211\'20\'13 that unions are an outside third party. Workers who self-organize have a powerful response: \u8220\'20\'1CHow can you say we\u8217\'20\'19re outsiders? We work here. We know the problems workers face here.\u8221\'20\'1D In addition, workers who lead self-organizing efforts do not face a major obstacle to most unionization drives: unlike paid union staff organizers, they can\u8217\'20\'19t be barred from company property \u8211\'20\'13 they work there and can easily talk up the union with their co-workers.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s youth-fueled union drives are the most promising thing to come along for labor in years, but they often lack resources. These union drives badly need more money and lawyers not just to help them succeed against intense anti-union efforts, but to go to scale across the US \u8211\'20\'13 and perhaps try to spread unionization fever from \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to \uc2 McDonalds\uc2 , Chipotle and other fast-food companies and from Amazon to \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , Target and other retailers.\par \par \uc2 This is a critical opportunity \u8211\'20\'13 and moment \u8211\'20\'13 for America\u8217\'20\'19s labor leaders. For today\u8217\'20\'19s unionization surge to become a lasting, powerful movement that transforms decades of decline into increased membership and power, labor leaders will need to step up and provide money, lots of it, to help the current efforts succeed and spread.\par \par \uc2 Christian Smalls says workers at 100 other Amazon facilities have contacted him to say that they, too, want to unionize. But it will take money to fertilize those efforts and to send Smalls and his fellow organizers to dozens of other Amazon warehouses to explain how to do successful self-organizing. Those campaigns will also need lawyers to file the paperwork to request union elections and to lodge complaints with the government if and when companies fire union supporters illegally.\par \par \uc2 Many labor leaders might hesitate to back today\u8217\'20\'19s burst of self-organizing because they won\u8217\'20\'19t control those efforts and won\u8217\'20\'19t get the members and the dues money resulting from them. But if labor leaders are serious about ending decades of decline and about strengthening unions to win a better deal for workers, they need to jettison their old-style thinking and provide money and lawyers to help today\u8217\'20\'19s union surge grow and then grow some more. If they don\u8217\'20\'19t do this, it will show that all their talk about reversing labor\u8217\'20\'19s decline is empty rhetoric.\par \par \uc2 With their energy and idealism, the kids \u8211\'20\'13 the young workers \u8211\'20\'13 are all right. But with their inertia and lack of imagination, many of today\u8217\'20\'19s older unions seem inexplicably reluctant to seize \u8211\'20\'13 and build on \u8211\'20\'13 this promising moment for labor.\par \par \uc2 Labor\u8217\'20\'19s hot hand is with young workers and self-organized shops. Union leaders need to take note, adjust their model and get on board.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Steven Greenhouse is an American labor and workplace journalist and writer. A former labor reporter for \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , he has covered unions for over 25 years\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220512ei5c001ut\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
