\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Pure propaganda\u8217\'20\'19: inside \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 anti-union tactics\b0\par\par\uc2 Nick Bowlin in Denver, Colorado \par \uc2 4,126 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 May 2022\par 16:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 In a state where labor culture isn\u8217\'20\'19t strong, a Denver store\u8217\'20\'19s union drive is notable. As soon as it was announced, high-ranking company figures began visiting\par \par \uc2 When \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  management came for Sean Andrews, it didn\u8217\'20\'19t surprise him, exactly. Tensions were already running high in the Denver coffee shop where he worked. A few days prior, Andrews\u8217\'20\'19 fellow baristas had walked off the job in a six-hour strike. They were demonstrating for their unionization effort and against what they saw as a sustained pattern of anti-labor tactics by \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Workers knew the strike was an escalation and expected retaliation. Even so, Andrews was unprepared when the Denver Starbucks district manager pulled him aside before a shift and asked him to take a call. The woman on the other end would eventually identify herself as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccorporate investigator\u8221\'20\'1D who looks into losses in stores.\par \par \uc2 After some innocuous questions about company policy, she began quizzing him about leftover food, according to a recording of the interaction obtained by the Guardian. Did his co-workers ever take any for themselves? Did he?\par \par \uc2 After sticking to the company line at first, an audibly exasperated Andrews turned blunt. In the back of the store, there\u8217\'20\'19s a box for leftover food that goes to a local food pantry. If a piece of food was about to turn bad, or if a store item was expired and set to be thrown away, he or his co-workers would at times grab something on their way out, \u8220\'20\'1Clike every [barista] who works for \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D, he said.\par \par \uc2 Cost of living has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://denverrelocationguide.com/cost-of-living-in-denver/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ballooned}}}\uc2  in Denver in recent years, with housing prices {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cpr.org/2022/03/30/denver-home-prices-construction-real-estate/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 growing by 21%}}}\uc2  in the past year alone. The starting hourly salary for baristas is $16.37, according to a job posting from March (experienced employees can make more than $21 an hour).\par \par \uc2 One of Andrews\u8217\'20\'19 co-workers was functionally homeless at the time, a fact that he shot back at the company investigator when she asked for the names of people who took food. (Several \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees interviewed for this story described regularly being behind on rent.) The investigator then asked if Andrews had a home. \u8220\'20\'1CYes,\u8221\'20\'1D he replied, \u8220\'20\'1Cno thanks to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 At this point, the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  investigator began grilling him, repeatedly demanding the identities of workers who took food, and demanding to know who was homeless.\par \par \uc2 Andrews felt angry and uncomfortable. He refused to answer. \u8220\'20\'1CI know exactly why you\u8217\'20\'19re doing this,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, referring to anti-union retaliation.\par \par \uc2 Eventually, he stood up and announced that he quit.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI will let all the other [baristas] know that this is happening,\u8221\'20\'1D he said to the investigator and his district manager. The investigator told him that if he did not leave immediately, his manager would call the police.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Andrews\u8217\'20\'19 run-in with his bosses is a small part of an ongoing struggle between the world\u8217\'20\'19s largest coffee chain and thousands of its American workers. For years, unions had considered \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  notoriously difficult to organize. In December, workers at a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in Buffalo, New York, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/dec/09/starbucks-workers-unionize-buffalo-new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted to form a union}}}\uc2 , the first in the history of the Seattle coffee chain. The victory broke the dam, and the subsequent flood has spread across the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was amazed that they were able to pull it off,\u8221\'20\'1D said Trey Slopsema, a Denver barista and an early organizer at the Denver store. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd it really just stuck with me that this is something that we could actually do.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union vote results for this Denver Starbucks will be announced on 10 May. This store is one of more than 200 locations with active union campaigns. More than 50 stores have successfully voted to unionize, including, in late April, one in the small town of Superior, Colorado, the first in the state.\par \par \uc2 Taken along with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/apr/09/amazon-union-organization-staten-island"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 recent labor win}}}\uc2  at an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Amazon\uc2  was another white whale for labor organizers \u8211\'20\'13 the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  union push is part of a historic outpouring of worker activism in the US.\par \par \uc2 But even in this context, the Denver union drive is notable.\par \par \uc2 Buffalo, the site of the first \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  union win, has a strong labor culture and hundreds of local unions. By contrast, Colorado is a nominally liberal state that has trended Democratic in recent elections, but its union culture is noticeably weak. Colorado has a modified right-to-work law, which means that employees in unionized workplaces are not required to join the union. The state\u8217\'20\'19s rate of unionized workers is several points {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/regions/mountain-plains/news-release/unionmembership_colorado.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 below}}}\uc2  the national average.\par \par \uc2 At a basic level, Denver residents are far less likely to know a union member, or have been exposed to labor organizations, than in some other parts of the country. Colorado labor historians and union leaders described a pro-business, libertarian-tinged political climate that adds an extra degree of difficulty to an already hard process.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you go to Great Lakes cities, rust belt cities, there are old industries that still carry strong union identity,\u8221\'20\'1D said Nate Bernstein, healthcare director for United Food and Commercial Workers Local Number 7, which represents 23,000 grocery store and retail workers in Colorado and Wyoming.\par \par \uc2 I was amazed that they [the Buffalo Starbucks workers] were able to pull it off\par \par \uc2 Trey Slopsema\par \par \uc2 Denver\u8217\'20\'19s economic makeup, he said, is not conducive to union density. On top of that, Bernstein identified a cultural resistance to unions that stems from Denver\u8217\'20\'19s self-image as a \u8220\'20\'1Ccowboy town\u8221\'20\'1D, where myths of rugged western individualism are dearly held.\par \par \uc2 John Enyeart, a labor historian at \uc2 Bucknell University\uc2  , called this a common trope among western employers, one with little basis in historical fact.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe mountain men worked together,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to fight off a bear on one\u8217\'20\'19s own. The idea that they were doing this all individually is nonsense.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Corporations want you to think of the workplace as your family. For decades, American companies have often talked about their workplace in friendly terms. Workers are encouraged to trust their employer, to meld personal identity and professional occupation. \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is part of this lineage. A press release on the company website from 2016 begins: \u8220\'20\'1CColleagues at work can sometimes feel like family.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The company has long referred to all their employees, from high-level management down to baristas, as \u8220\'20\'1Cpartners\u8221\'20\'1D. In a video call {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://perfectunion.us/in-leaked-video-call-howard-schultz-begs-starbucks-managers-to-step-up-union-busting/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked}}}\uc2  to the pro-labor news site More Perfect Union, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 CEO, \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 , recently told company managers that \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s an American right of workers not to unionize and to embrace the values and the culture of his or her company\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Appeals of this sort to a sympathetic company culture have not always been the norm. In Colorado, the state\u8217\'20\'19s labor history recalls a time when the seesawing conflict between organized labor and corporate power was often confrontational \u8211\'20\'13 and sometimes violent. At the turn of the 20th century, the state\u8217\'20\'19s unions were strong and well organized across industries. More conservative craft unionists would go on strike with workers in other industries. And in the mines, the unions were radical, powerful and ready to fight.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s economy relied on coal, gold and silver extracted from the Rocky Mountains. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you wanted to do any kind of work in a mining town like Cripple Creek \u8211\'20\'13 bartender, musician, anything \u8211\'20\'13 you had to be a union member,\u8221\'20\'1D said Thomas Andrews, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hup.harvard.edu/catalog.php?isbn=9780674046917"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 historian}}}\uc2  of the western United States at the \uc2 University of Colorado\uc2 . He also noted that these mining towns were often racially exclusive, with animus toward Mexican Americans and Chinese and eastern European immigrants running high among the unions.\par \par \uc2 In this period, large and aggressive mining strikes were the norm at least once a decade, as miners went on strike for better working conditions, while the companies retaliated in often brutal fashion. In 1913, the \uc2 United Mine Workers of America\uc2  called a general strike in Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s coalfields, one that targeted the Colorado Fuel and Iron Company, the state\u8217\'20\'19s largest miner and owned by John D Rockefeller Jr.\par \par \uc2 To break the strike, the company hired private guards to harass the workers and their families, who had gathered in a tent encampment in the town of Ludlow. On 20 April, the private soldiers, along with troops from the Colorado national guard, attacked the camp, killing at least 21 people, including 11 children. A 10-day war followed. Well-organized militias of striking miners \u8211\'20\'13 including many veterans of older mining conflicts \u8211\'20\'13 fought a guerrilla campaign against the Colorado national guard and the mining corporations, killing more people than died at Ludlow.\par \par \uc2 The Ludlow Massacre became a pivotal moment in early 20th-century labor struggles. An investigation by Congress into mine safety conditions followed, and there was widespread outrage against the Rockefeller family. But labor\u8217\'20\'19s reputation suffered as well. Colorado newspapers turned against the unions, and business interests believed that the state\u8217\'20\'19s reputation needed to be restored.\par \par \uc2 After the second world war, Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s economic path largely tracked that of the rest of the sun belt: huge investments of federal funds into the national highway system, technology and military infrastructure brought lifestyle-oriented, upper-class professionals to the state in droves. This economy endures today, with aerospace, telecommunications, financial services and tech driving the booming economy on Colorado\u8217\'20\'19s Front Range. None of these industries have strong union membership.\par \par \uc2 This thin labor culture meant that some Denver Starbucks employees were unfamiliar with unions when the store\u8217\'20\'19s employees began organizing.\par \par \uc2 Kelly Morris took a job at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  six years ago to supplement her income as a preschool teacher, which did not cover groceries and rent. In that time, she had never considered the need for a union. Morris was born in Golden, a Denver suburb. Growing up, she didn\u8217\'20\'19t know any union members or have any personal connection to labor. She was not against unions, more that they weren\u8217\'20\'19t a part of her life.\par \par \uc2 We need to have a say. A union would give us that\par \par \uc2 Kelly Morris\par \par \uc2 So when her co-workers began to discuss unionizing, Morris was hesitant. She didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to be fired and lose the healthcare she desperately needed. She wrestled with the idea for some weeks. But talking to her co-workers eventually convinced her. Morris signed a union card, as well as the initial letter to management announcing the staff\u8217\'20\'19s intention to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Putting her name out there still makes her nervous, but Morris ultimately feels proud to stand with her co-workers. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to have a say,\u8221\'20\'1D she told me. \u8220\'20\'1CA union would give us that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Enyeart, the Bucknell historian, drew a connection between the mine wars of the past and the struggles of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas today. \u8220\'20\'1C[\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  workers] are being intimidated and their livelihoods are being destroyed,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. Back then, Rockefeller knew he was never going to jail for violating labor laws that were on the books. The \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  people are doing the same thing. They know that, worst-case scenario, they have to pay some fines.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  store where Andrews worked is on Colfax Avenue \u8211\'20\'13 the longest continuous commercial street in America. It offers a procession of taco counters, electronics stores, laundromats and several of Denver\u8217\'20\'19s famous theaters and music venues. The \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  store sits between an apartment complex and an addiction treatment center.\par \par \uc2 It looks like a barn, and that\u8217\'20\'19s what locals call it.\par \par \uc2 In the Denver Starbucks community, the Barn has something of a reputation. The pace is fast, with no space for inexperienced baristas. It\u8217\'20\'19s also notorious for being understaffed, and the clientele includes a fair share of unhoused people.\par \par \uc2 These factors, according to multiple interviews with store baristas, make it so workers from suburban Denver locations virtually never pick up shifts at the store.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8216\'20\'18Oh God, you work at the Barn,\u8217\'20\'19 that\u8217\'20\'19s not an uncommon thing to hear,\u8221\'20\'1D said Slopsema. But the store\u8217\'20\'19s isolation creates a distinctly close-knit team, he went on.\par \par \uc2 When he turned 18, Slopsema wanted to travel around the country and used \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  to fund his wandering. He worked at stores in Indiana and Florida before moving to Colorado to see the mountains. The camaraderie at the Barn stands out. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why I stick around. I\u8217\'20\'19ve never worked with a team that\u8217\'20\'19s been so close.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This cohesion helped the store\u8217\'20\'19s team weather the pandemic. Many employees have been sick at various points in the past two years. Customers were often belligerent in response to mask mandates. Some were even violent.\par \par \uc2 Last fall, Andrews was punched in the face so hard by an angry customer that his tooth chipped. Another employee, Michaela Sellaro, was pepper-sprayed in the face at the drive-thru window by a man upset that the store was out of mocha Frappuccinos. For a month after the incident, baristas would find shattered glass bottles littering the outside of their store in the morning. The man was throwing full Frappuccino bottles at the store in the night.\par \par \uc2 According to Sellaro, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  management was of little help. The bosses refused to allow workers to hang a picture of the man in the store, so baristas shared it among themselves. Sellaro noted that management did offer her a trauma counseling session in the back of the store. Overall, she said, \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think they took it very seriously.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 So when news of Buffalo\u8217\'20\'19s union win broke, employees at the Barn were immediately excited. Talk of the union benefits proposed in Buffalo consumed the group chat \u8211\'20\'13 better pay, workplace protections, a policy where, if the store is short-staffed, the other employees absorb the missing employees\u8217\'20\'19 pay for that shift rather than the company \u8211\'20\'13 as well as basic discussion of what unions are.\par \par \uc2 The horizon of possibilities for their workplace had just expanded.\par \par \uc2 After the Barn announced its union drive in January, high-ranking Denver Starbucks figures began to appear in the store, observing the baristas and nitpicking tiny infractions, like not wearing a name tag. According to several baristas, the store is chronically out of name tags, and refill requests often go ignored.\par \par \uc2 Every barista sat through a one-on-one meeting with management over the course of three days in February. They were asked what they knew about the union and how they intended to vote. Companies cannot legally tell workers that they will lose benefits because of labor organizing. Instead, according to more than a half dozen \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  employees, managers made vague insinuations, stating that they could not promise that benefits would not get worse. \u8220\'20\'1CIf we have a union, we\u8217\'20\'19ll be doing the bargaining,\u8221\'20\'1D Sellaro said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhy would we argue for worse benefits?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a reason Sellaro\u8217\'20\'19s bosses homed in on benefit loss. Benefits are why many workers seek out \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  in the first place \u8211\'20\'13 and part of what has made organizing at the company historically difficult.\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  was the first large US corporation to offer healthcare benefits to part-time workers. Several workers at the Barn mentioned this as an incentive to stick with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 . Most of them are in their 20s and 30s. Some have other service industry gigs. For many others, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  is their only job.\par \par \uc2 The company\u8217\'20\'19s college tuition aid program is \u8220\'20\'1Cthe only reason\u8221\'20\'1D why Vanessa Castro has stuck with \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for four years and three different stores, including a move to Portland and then back to her home state of Colorado. Her getting a communications degree from \uc2 Arizona State University\uc2  would not be possible otherwise, she told me in an interview at a park down the street from the Barn.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you start to empower employees by giving them benefits, options, resources to get success, you don\u8217\'20\'19t get to choose when to stop,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 The next step, for Castro, is a union.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 Shortly after the first union win in Buffalo, Schultz, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 well-known CEO, came out of retirement to lead the company again. The timing appears to be no accident. Schultz has a long history of opposing unions. In recent months, as the nationwide unionization campaign has gained momentum, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has deluged employees with anti-union messaging, fired pro-union workers and spent millions of dollars on a law firm famous for union-busting.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s unclear whether his advocacy is going well. At a recent event, Schultz promised workers that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  would soon be getting into NFTs. In November, he made a shocking {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/11/09/business/howard-schultz-starbucks/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comparison}}}\uc2  between the company\u8217\'20\'19s benefit program and Jewish concentration camp prisoners sharing blankets during the Holocaust. At the same time, Schultz has argued that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  can treat its employees well voluntarily, just without the presence of a union.\par \par \uc2 Now he plans to do just that \u8211\'20\'13 and pit unionized workers against non-unionized ones.\par \par \uc2 On 3 May, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  announced immediate nationwide pay increases, with better sick time benefits and, for the first time, the option for customers to tip with their credit cards coming this fall.\par \par \uc2 But in the fine print of the announcement, the company stated that the entire benefit expansion would apply to non-unionized stores only. On \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , the Starbucks Workers United union account {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SBWorkersUnited/status/1521624795439419392/photo/1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claimed}}}\uc2  that pressure from the union campaign led to the raises. The union also stated that it has \u8220\'20\'1Cfiled charges against \uc2 Howard Schultz\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s threats that union stores won\u8217\'20\'19t receive these benefits\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 At the Barn, loss of benefits became a concern in February and March, as workers who once had no trouble getting shifts found their hours abruptly cut \u8211\'20\'13 a strategy, most believed, meant to demoralize them or force them to quit so new employees could be hired and potentially change the union vote. Some of them relied on \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for all of their income. Requests for more shifts went unheeded. Yet several shift supervisors \u8211\'20\'13 a role reserved for experienced baristas \u8211\'20\'13 describe being left with skeleton crews during busy times.\par \par \uc2 Lillian Sanders, a shift supervisor who has worked in five \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores in three states over almost four years, called the tactics \u8220\'20\'1Cretaliatory\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cpurposefully isolating\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never had to work so hard to get my manager to answer her phone,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 We watched everything we know about \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and our culture erode\par \par \uc2 Kaylin Driscoll\par \par \uc2 Then there were the write-ups. In mid-February, Slopsema, a barista at the Barn, was given a final warning \u8211\'20\'13 the last step before being fired. The reason given was that the store safe was found unlocked after one of his shifts, as well as a failure to lock the store. According to Slopsema, the safe\u8217\'20\'19s latch was broken, an issue he raised with his bosses weeks before the incident. In a recording of a conversation about the warning obtained by the Guardian, Slopsema can be heard expressing shock at the severity of the punishment.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve never received a written warning before,\u8221\'20\'1D he said in the recording, \u8220\'20\'1Cso it\u8217\'20\'19s kind of upsetting to see that it\u8217\'20\'19s just a final right off the bat after working here for four years.\par \par \uc2 He takes responsibility for leaving the store unlocked \u8211\'20\'13 he blames it on a mental lapse \u8211\'20\'13 but pushes back regarding the safe. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve also showed that the safe itself needs to be worked on,\u8221\'20\'1D he can be heard saying to his manager. \u8220\'20\'1CIt doesn\u8217\'20\'19t shut properly. Unless you really press on to it, it will just open back up, but I\u8217\'20\'19ve already connected with you on that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Two weeks later, Sellaro received her own final warning after she left her keys in her apron, hanging in the back, locked in the store overnight. Sellaro thought so little of her oversight with the keys that she subsequently mentioned it to her boss. Nothing happened for more than two weeks, at which point she was informed that she was one step from being terminated.\par \par \uc2 Below a final warning are two less severe disciplinary levels. Both she and Slopsema had worked at \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  for years and never been written up for any infraction (Andrews now suspects that his encounter with the company investigator would have led to a final warning had he not quit on the spot). Both were known to be leading union advocates. Sellaro called it intimidation. \u8220\'20\'1CIf it was so egregious, why didn\u8217\'20\'19t you say something to me sooner?\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 After baristas at the Barn went on strike in early March, messages from the company became more direct. \u8220\'20\'1CPlease vote and vote NO,\u8221\'20\'1D read a letter distributed to employees. Another letter from around that time accused the workers and their supporters of physically intimidating and yelling obscenities at customers during the walkout and strike. The letter urged employees to vote against the union. It was signed by Andrea Streedain, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 regional vice-president for the mountain west.\par \par \uc2 In an internal company video call for store managers that recently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://mobile.twitter.com/moreperfectus/status/1519814311560069120"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked}}}\uc2 , the Denver district manager, Kaylin Driscoll, can be seen tearfully recounting these allegations against the striking workers. \u8220\'20\'1CWe watched everything we know about \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  and our culture erode,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 (Driscoll was the manager in the room when Andrews was questioned by the investigator. \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  called and left voicemails requesting comment with both Driscoll and Streedain. The company did not respond to a list of questions by press time.)\par \par \uc2 Multiple workers who participated in the strike called allegations of physical intimidation a lie, though they acknowledge booing cars that broke the picket line to use the drive through. \u8220\'20\'1CWe behaved as if we were on the clock,\u8221\'20\'1D Sellaro said. Several recalled irate customers screaming at the demonstrators. Someone threw an egg at them from a nearby building. In the staff group chat, one worker described the letter as \u8220\'20\'1Cpure propaganda\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On 20 April, \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 lawyers filed a complaint to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) against Starbucks Workers United (SBWU is an affiliate of the \uc2 Service Employees International Union\uc2 , which represents millions of workers across multiple sectors), one that specifically cites the March walkout at the Barn. It mentions the alleged yelling at customers and physical intimidation, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/amp/2022/04/20/starbucks-files-nlrb-complaints-against-baristas-union.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 according}}}\uc2  to CNBC.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 With less than a week before the results of the vote are announced, there has been serious attrition among the Barn\u8217\'20\'19s leading union voices.\par \par \uc2 Andrews had quit after his encounter with the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  investigator. Sanders gave her two weeks notice in early April, along with two other baristas. She described feeling \u8220\'20\'1Cforced out\u8221\'20\'1D by a difficult and intentionally unhelpful management. And Slopsema was finally terminated. (He declined to discuss the details of his dismissal, but several co-workers described the firing as not justified.)\par \par \uc2 At least four unfair labor practice complaints had been filed to the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  on behalf of employees at the Barn, to go with dozens from other locations around the US, alleging anti-union activities of many kinds.\par \par \uc2 Despite \u8211\'20\'13 and perhaps in response to \u8211\'20\'13 this pressure campaign, several workers said they are confident in the results of the vote. Corporate higher-ups continue to make regular appearances in the store. Sellaro said that the short-staffing persists. At least eight employees have quit or been fired in recent months, several in response to Slopsema\u8217\'20\'19s dismissal. The store has been forced to close early several times, angering some customers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re consistently being staffed as thinly as humanly possible,\u8221\'20\'1D Sellaro said in a late April phone interview. She suspects the goal is to create resentment among the staff, but said the tactic had backfired. \u8220\'20\'1CThe way we\u8217\'20\'19ve been abandoned by leadership, the way they leave us to flounder, and don\u8217\'20\'19t seem to understand how exhausting that is \u8211\'20\'13 [co-workers] keep saying that they are excited to have their own voice.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 I spoke to Sellaro by phone on the day she mailed in her ballot. It was a proud moment, almost three months to the day since she and her co-workers publicly announced their intent to unionize. But the recent months have tainted her relationship to \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 , her main source of income. She feels scrutinized, like her bosses are looking for a chance to fire her. She has had panic attacks before some shifts. Longtime work friends have quit or been fired. She can\u8217\'20\'19t help feeling sad for her co-workers who began organizing with her, but will not be there at the end.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe slogged through the, excuse my language, extremely shitty moments together, and now we\u8217\'20\'19re getting those little joyous moments,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t imagine how hard it is for Trey [Slopsema] and Sean [Andrews] and Lillian [Sanders] and everyone else to see us celebrating and not be able to fully share in it. Bittersweet is the only way to describe it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220504ei5400130\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
