\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s hopes of post-Brexit US trade deal \u8216\'20\'18depend on workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Richard Partington Economics correspondent \par \uc2 672 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 April 2022\par 05:15 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Unions from both sides of the Atlantic accuse \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s government of failing to grasp importance of labour rights\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s hopes of a favourable post-Brexit trade deal with the US risk being undermined by the government\u8217\'20\'19s lack of engagement on workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights, trade unions have warned.\par \par \uc2 As a second round of US-UK talks begins this week, union leaders from both countries said Washington would push for a \u8220\'20\'1Cworker-centred approach to trade\u8221\'20\'1D to help unlock a deal.\par \par \uc2 Accusing \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s government of failing to grasp the importance of labour rights, the TUC and the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , the biggest union federations in the UK and the US, said a change of tack was urgently required.\par \par \uc2 It comes as ministers push to build new ties around the world after leaving the EU, with a US trade deal considered a prize target for the government as it attempts to demonstrate benefits of Brexit.\par \par \uc2 After opening a dialogue on the future of Atlantic trade last month in Baltimore, talks resume this week in Aberdeen and London between the UK trade secretary Anne-Marie Trevelyan and \uc2 Katherine Tai\uc2 , the US trade representative.\par \par \uc2 At the first round of meetings Trevelyan pledged she would \u8220\'20\'1Cstrengthen the protection of labour rights and the environment\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Ctackle forced labour globally\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 However, the US and UK union leaders said the \uc2 British government\uc2  had too often hurtled into deals with unsavoury regimes that had no respect for fundamental human and labour rights.\par \par \uc2 The government had promised a role for union representatives in powerful post-Brexit trade advisory groups, which are consulted on negotiations. However, the TUC warned its nominees for the posts had not yet been confirmed by ministers, meaning unions did not have a place at the table.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, the two groups representing more than 17 million workers, called on the US and UK governments to work together to protect employment standards.\par \par \uc2 Frances O\u8217\'20\'19Grady\uc2 , the general secretary of the TUC, said the \uc2 UK government\uc2  had rushed into post-Brexit negotiations with countries that \u8220\'20\'1Creadily abuse fundamental human and labour rights\u8221\'20\'1D such as Colombia and Turkey.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrade deals can lift labour standards, promote decent work and reduce inequality around the world. But the \uc2 UK government\uc2  has agreed too many deals that leave working people worse off,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEnough is enough. It\u8217\'20\'19s time for a truly worker-centred trade approach. That means meaningfully consulting with trade unions and acting on our concerns. Only then is the \uc2 US government\uc2  likely to consider closer trade ties with the UK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Eric Gottwald, a trade policy specialist at the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , said: \u8220\'20\'1CFor too long, the voices of working people have been shut out of trade negotiations or discussions. We need the TUC and its unions at the table to shape a fair agreement that lifts wages and standards on both sides of the Atlantic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The groups said that under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s administration, the US had increasingly involved protections for workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights in trade negotiations in recent years, including in the latest US-Mexico-Canada agreement.\par \par \uc2 The leaders said as a result of trade union involvement in the USMCA negotiation, the agreement contains \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the strongest labour rights enforcement chapters ever agreed\u8221\'20\'1D, with possibilities for sanctions to be introduced against companies that are abusing workers\u8217\'20\'19 rights\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A Department for International Trade spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CTrade unions attended the first UK-US trade dialogue in Baltimore last month, and tomorrow the TUC general secretary is speaking in the plenary session of the second dialogue which we\u8217\'20\'19re hosting in Aberdeen.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe UK has a strong trading relationship with the US, worth over \u163\'00\'A3200bn per year, and through our joint trade dialogues we can deliver a broader and even more ambitious trade agenda.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are committed to listening to trade unions and a wide range of voices to ensure our trade policy delivers for the whole of the UK.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220425ei4p000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
