\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Burnt out and tired\u8217\'20\'19: nurses at leading California hospitals prepare to strike\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 973 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 April 2022\par 21:09 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Overtime and unfilled vacancies during the pandemic have left nursing staff feeling overworked and under-compensated\par \par \uc2 Five thousand nurses at Stanford and Lucile Packard children\u8217\'20\'19s hospital in Stanford, California, are preparing to strike in demand of wage increases, mental health and wellness support, better healthcare benefits, and a focus on hiring and retaining nurse staff.\par \par \uc2 The union has set a strike date for 25 April.\par \par \uc2 Stanford hospital at \uc2 Stanford University\uc2  in California has been consistently {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://med.stanford.edu/news/all-news/2021/07/stanford-health-care-honor-roll.html#:~:text=top%20hospitals%20Story-,Stanford%20Health%20Care%20ranked%20among%20top,by%20U.S.%20News%20%26%20World%20Report&text=For%20the%20seventh%20year%20running,of%20the%20nation's%20finest%20hospitals.&text=Stanford%20Health%20Care%20ranked%20No,22%20Best%20Hospitals%20Honor%20Roll."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ranked }}}\uc2  among the top hospitals in the US by US News, but nurses say high turnover rates, understaffing, and inadequate proposed wage increases and benefits have contributed to high burnout rates. In a survey of union members, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://crona.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Press-Release_Strike-Authorization-Vote_8Apr2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 45% }}}\uc2  of nurses reporting said they intend to leave their job within the next five years.\par \par \uc2 Kathy Stormberg, a nurse in the radiology department at Stanford hospital for 19 years and vice-president of the Committee for Recognition of Nursing Achievement (Crona), blamed the strike on the hospitals\u8217\'20\'19 continued reliance on contractors and its policy of pushing nurses to work overtime amid staff shortages, unfilled vacancies, and difficulties retaining enough nursing staff.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is not sustainable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Stormberg. \u8220\'20\'1CNurses have an overwhelming sense of guilt to work overtime when they are getting texts requesting nurses to come in every four hours on their days off.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She also cited inflation, the high cost of living in the Bay area, student debt and other area hospitals providing better wages and benefits as obstacles for nurses to sustain careers at Stanford.\par \par \uc2 Nurses represented by the union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.paloaltoonline.com/news/2022/04/08/thousands-of-stanford-and-packard-nurses-vote-to-authorize-strike#:~:text=A%20whopping%2093%25%20of%20nurses,on%20Friday%2C%20the%20union%20announced"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  by 93% in favor of a strike authorization as the union\u8217\'20\'19s contract expired on 31 March and 30 bargaining sessions over the past three months have yet to yield a tentative agreement. The union has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://crona.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/04/Press-Release_Strike-Authorization-Vote_8Apr2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criticized}}}\uc2  the lack of participation from nursing administrators in bargaining sessions. This would be the first time they have gone on strike {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.stanford.edu/news/2000/august9/strike-89.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  since 2000}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Leading up to the strike vote, nurses have held {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/CRONAnurses/posts/7940039669343045?__cft__[0]=AZW7aEMkhkzgHQHULX8NWrEUBbG_QfHQIGcvaRHGOQ3zreift6Ydv6iIp0uKZ1lvYD-gY2rzsKWe1puE4r1rBozEyqL1I-OPSikTfFENWKbGOLQl1Zyj7BV7mnFjhzlgxUK0auBq9H0G2EH9PE8S2k88&__tn__=%2CO%2CP-R"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rallies}}}\uc2  outside the hospitals to demand a new contract. The union must give a 10-day notice before walking out.\par \par \uc2 Kelli McNulty, a nurse at Stanford hospital, said that due to the inadequate health insurance benefits provided to nurses, she had to get a job at a local Amazon for a week working night shifts and then sign up for \uc2 Cobra\uc2  insurance in order to obtain health insurance coverage that covered fertility services.\par \par \uc2 She had a partial hysterectomy at age 27 and has been trying to bank embryos through in vitro fertilization \u8211\'20\'13 the only way she could have children of her own \u8211\'20\'13 but the health insurance coverage offered to nurses with the union doesn\u8217\'20\'19t cover it.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s mind-boggling to me that we\u8217\'20\'19re one of the most well-known institutions in the world and they don\u8217\'20\'19t even give us IVF insurance or reproductive benefits,\u8221\'20\'1D said McNulty. \u8220\'20\'1CI was a little shellshocked by the fact that I worked full-time at Stanford and then I had to go live in an Econo Lodge hotel to work overnight for a week at Amazon just to get benefits.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In January 2022, a nurse on a contract at Stanford hospital {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.medpagetoday.com/special-reports/exclusives/96920"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 walked out }}}\uc2  of their shift and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kron4.com/news/bay-area/death-of-stanford-nurse-brings-attention-to-healthcare-workers-mental-health/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 killed themself}}}\uc2 , highlighting the need for better mental health and wellness support services and for improvements to the poor working conditions that nurses have faced through the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe working conditions that we have now are just no longer sustainable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Leah McFadden, a nurse in Stanford\u8217\'20\'19s surgical trauma unit since October 2019. \u8220\'20\'1COver the last two years, we\u8217\'20\'19re starting to run on empty, we aren\u8217\'20\'19t having a chance to decompress, or even just get away from the hospital as much as we should.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 McFadden said she pays out of pocket to see a therapist to help her manage the stress of working in nursing during the pandemic. Her unit took care of Covid patients for the first 15 months of the pandemic, and has since reverted to surgical trauma, but patients\u8217\'20\'19 acuity has been intense given the delays in medical care many Americans faced due to pandemic restrictions and hospitals being overwhelmed with Covid patients.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNurses are burnt out and tired, and nurses are not only leaving the bedside nationwide, but don\u8217\'20\'19t necessarily want to live in this area because of how expensive it is,\u8221\'20\'1D said McFadden. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want the hospital to recognize that they\u8217\'20\'19re going to need to make some drastic changes in order to keep the nursing force that they\u8217\'20\'19re so proud of.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement, a spokesperson for Stanford hospital and the Lucile Packard children\u8217\'20\'19s hospital said in an email:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAt Stanford Health Care and Lucile Packard Children\u8217\'20\'19s Hospital, we believe that hard work at the negotiations table is a far better path than a strike to achieving new contracts for our nurses. We are committed, through good faith bargaining, to reach agreement on new contracts that provide nurses a highly competitive compensation package, along with proposals that further our commitment to enhancing staffing and wellness benefits for nurses. Now, as we take the necessary and precautionary steps to prepare for the possibility of a strike, we hope that Crona chooses to instead focus its efforts on working with us toward contract agreements.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 In the US, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://suicidepreventionlifeline.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 National Suicide Prevention Lifeline}}}\uc2  is at 800-273-8255 and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://suicidepreventionlifeline.org/chat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 online chat is also available}}}\uc2 . You can also text HOME to 741741 to connect with a crisis text line counselor. In the UK and Ireland, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.samaritans.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Samaritans}}}\uc2  can be contacted on 116 123 or email {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jo@samaritans.org"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jo@samaritans.org}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jo@samaritans.ie"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jo@samaritans.ie}}}\uc2 . In Australia, the crisis support service {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.lifeline.org.au/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lifeline}}}\uc2  is 13 11 14. Other international helplines can be found at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.befrienders.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 www.befrienders.org}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220413ei4d002uu\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
