\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The Guardian view on Amazon\u8217\'20\'19s first American union: inspiration from New York\b0\par\par\uc2 Editorial \par \uc2 543 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2022\par 18:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A famous victory for the tech giant\u8217\'20\'19s employees in Staten Island can be a catalyst for change\par \par \uc2 Amazon, notoriously, does not like trade unions very much. In 2021, America\u8217\'20\'19s wealthiest corporation {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.engadget.com/amazon-spent-43-million-on-anti-union-consultants-in-2021-alone-082051777.html?guccounter=1&guce_referrer=aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZ29vZ2xlLmNvbS8&guce_referrer_sig=AQAAAAII4wsawTi_yEWL-Bf0Dp9eS3_l3Xm_GJSCU5sJQtqHJw_YbSEJpMcEzuD__axaOAswQlLUUQdyI-IwQyjywMvvOciRqXjbpNLDEG2lHwoB2H_hpJV_0q61fAM3awIw4kAJuoXWUCltudfoYuAB0YnYr6odG7yPFdBvDZtpCfOa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spent}}}\uc2  around $4.3m (\u163\'00\'A33.3m) hiring consultants to help keep them out of its warehouses. For years, the tech giant has waged scandalous campaigns across the US to discourage its million-strong workforce from seeking formal representation. At mandatory \u8220\'20\'1Ccaptive audience\u8221\'20\'1D meetings, employees have been required to listen to anti-union lectures from their managers \u8211\'20\'13 and draw the right conclusions. Perceived troublemakers have allegedly been harassed and dismissed. And until this month, the bullying strategy had a 100% success rate.\par \par \uc2 All of which makes the pioneering {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/apr/01/amazon-union-groups-see-hope-workers-vote-alabama-new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 establishment}}}\uc2  of the Amazon Labor Union (ALU) in New York a minor miracle, as well as a landmark moment. Defying the usual playbook of scare tactics and dirty tricks \u8211\'20\'13 and a lavishly funded \u8220\'20\'1Cvote no\u8221\'20\'1D campaign \u8211\'20\'13 Staten Island warehouse workers voted on 1 April to form the first Amazon union outside Europe. A second New York warehouse will stage a similar vote later this month, and staff at more than 50 other Amazon sites have contacted the Staten Island organisers for advice and support. \u8220\'20\'1CThe revolution is here!\u8221\'20\'1D one ALU coordinator {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/apr/06/chris-smalls-amazon-union-warehouses"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the Guardian at the weekend.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRevolution\u8221\'20\'1D may be overstating it, but something is in the air. For decades, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2022/02/18/majorities-of-adults-see-decline-of-union-membership-as-bad-for-the-u-s-and-working-people/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 decline}}}\uc2  in union membership in America has been accompanied by a corresponding rise in social inequality. But US public support for unions has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pewresearch.org/fact-tank/2022/02/18/majorities-of-adults-see-decline-of-union-membership-as-bad-for-the-u-s-and-working-people/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 risen}}}\uc2  significantly in recent years, and there are signs that labour shortages are encouraging employees to take greater risks in demanding better pay and working conditions. Under \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s presidency, the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  has been become far more proactive in defending the right to organise and following up on cases of employee victimisation.\par \par \uc2 A tangible effect is beginning to be seen. On the same day as the Staten Island vote, baristas at a New York \uc2 Starbucks{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://theconversation.com/amazon-starbucks-and-the-sparking-of-a-new-american-union-movement-180293"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 formed}}}\uc2  the 10th branch of the Starbucks Workers United union \u8211\'20\'13 an organisation that only achieved its first breakthrough in Buffalo in December. Employees at more than 170 \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  stores across the US are now contemplating following suit. Perhaps most significantly of all, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2022/apr/09/amazon-union-organization-staten-island"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new generation}}}\uc2  may be on the move; younger workers were at the heart of the ALU campaign, opting to start an independent movement from scratch rather than relying on outside help from traditional unions. Startup energy, creative use of social media, barbecues paid for by \uc2 GoFundMe\uc2  and two years of listening to workers\u8217\'20\'19 concerns did the trick.\par \par \uc2 The postwar heyday of the great industrial unions will not come back. But that does not mean that modern workforces must be condemned to the exploitative, controlling tendencies exemplified by Amazon. The ALU\u8217\'20\'19s victory has set an inspiring precedent. Having been so unexpectedly humiliated, Amazon last week {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/apr/09/amazon-new-york-warehouse-union-victory-nlrb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  that it would seek to legally overturn the Staten Island vote on technical grounds. Its New York executives should instead resign themselves to sitting down and bargaining with a workforce that it has treated with disdain. Hopefully, there will be many more such negotiations to come.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220412ei4c002xm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
