\par\par \uc2 Money\par \b \uc2 Work remote, get paid less? The battle dividing offices will define the future of work\b0\par\par\uc2 Julia Hobsbawm \par \uc2 1,758 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 April 2022\par 09:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Mobility and freedom are the new prizes for the professional working class. But some companies fail to grasp the scale and sweep of change\par \par \uc2 The world of work was already quite sick before the coronavirus took hold, but the pandemic put rocket boosters on cultural change.\par \par \uc2 We can see the impact of this in every metric around work: during {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jan/04/great-resignation-quitting-us-unemployment-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Great Resignation}}}\uc2  of 2021, millions of American workers resigned en masse. Workers worldwide have declared that they would quit their jobs if not provided with flexibility.\par \par \uc2 The reduction in corporate property rents last year as high as 10%, with huge changes in the use of office space and co-working space. And the city has a new competitor: the suburb. The flight to suburbia during the pandemic has accounted for a rise in the property market for residences outside city centers. For downtown districts to attract and retain people as places to live and work, city centers will need to be redesigned completely.\par \par \uc2 These developments come as no surprise: the \uc2 McKinsey Global Institute\uc2  estimates that up to a quarter of workers in advanced economies will work permanently on a hybrid basis, ie partly from home, several days a week. Discussions about RTO (returning to the office) are increasingly fraught and in flux. There is no uniform model or agreement.\par \par \uc2 The case for going into an office regularly is having to be made to the workforce \u8211\'20\'13 and many are rejecting it. Meanwhile, CEOs have to grapple with employees who want more flexibility, the ability to work remotely and even the ability to choose their working hours \u8211\'20\'13 and this without a paycut.\par \par \uc2 The degree of agency workers will be given \u8211\'20\'13 to be able to choose your place and hours of work \u8211\'20\'13 might well define us far more than previous classifications in the future. Being labeled a \u8220\'20\'1Cwhite-collar\u8221\'20\'1D or a \u8220\'20\'1Cblue-collar\u8221\'20\'1D worker could be replaced by being a \u8220\'20\'1Chybrid have\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Chybrid have-not\u8221\'20\'1D worker instead.\par \par \uc2 People recognize that their work is a valuable commodity and they want to have a greater say in when and where they sell it\par \par \uc2 In addition, up to half of America\u8217\'20\'19s jobs are projected to be freelance by 2030 and two-thirds of employers now regard some form of remote work or hybrid work as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe new norm\u8221\'20\'1D. Many companies are declaring themselves \u8220\'20\'1Cfully remote\u8221\'20\'1D, giving them a competitive edge over those requiring presenteeism.\par \par \uc2 As more of us can now choose how to manage the time we spend working in a way that suits us rather than the traditional nine to five, discussions around the four-day week have reached an intensity never seen before. We are not yet anywhere near to the famous 15-hour working week John Maynard Keynes predicted in the 1930s, but his prediction seems newly relevant. People recognize that their work, and therefore their time, is a valuable commodity and they want to have a greater say in when and where they sell it.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\u8226\'20\'22\par \par \uc2 In spite of those changes, considerable ambivalence remains among some leaders. In one camp, you get the hardliners who believe working from the office is best. Many feel that those who work from home are to some extent work-shy. At the very least, they wish to penalize people who prefer to work hybrid.\par \par \uc2 Take the bombastic internal memo sent by \uc2 James Gorman\uc2 , chair and CEO of \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2 , to his staff: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you want to get paid New York rates, you work in New York. None of this \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19m in Colorado \u8230\'20\'26 and getting paid like I\u8217\'20\'19m sitting in New York City,\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D echoing an equally robust statement from \uc2 David Solomon\uc2  of \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  that working from home was \u8220\'20\'1Can aberration\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Similarly, the veteran Wall Street observer William Cohan simply said this: \u8220\'20\'1CHere\u8217\'20\'19s my advice to you, fellow Wall Street drones: Get back to the office.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In another camp are the more emollient hybrid softliners such as Kevin Ellis, London-based chair of consultancy firm PwC with 285,000 employees in 155 countries around the world, who said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe want to enshrine new working patterns so that they outlast the pandemic\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Regardless of which camp employers are in, it is obviously true that an awful lot of social capital resides in the office. I talked to Kevin Ellis, who said, \u8220\'20\'1CMy worry is that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to create a glass ceiling for people whose careers will be stunted because they\u8217\'20\'19re working from home and not realising what they\u8217\'20\'19re missing out on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, all these comments reflect a wistfulness on the part of big business, which can no longer magically attract the same kind of worker prepared to work in the same way they did before the pandemic. Hybrid working reflects the fact that mobility and freedom are the new prizes for the professional working class. The shift to a placeless and timeless dimension for work means the fixed HQ will have to work a lot harder to attract and retain talent.\par \par \uc2 Smart\uc2  leaders are thinking the unthinkable and asking whether they need an office in the same way again\par \par \uc2 Smart\uc2  leaders today are thinking the unthinkable and asking whether they need an office in the same way again, not because they are following the hybrid herd, but because they are keeping their eyes and ears open to what is going on in their own businesses.\par \par \uc2 Joanna Swash, CEO of outsourcing reception, PA and communications provider Moneypenny, was frank that her perceptions had been challenged by the pandemic when everyone had to go fully remote overnight. \u8220\'20\'1CBefore Covid-19 I thought we\u8217\'20\'19ve got amazing offices, and that they are this space that everybody loves,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat I learned was that our culture was so strong that it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t just based on the office or on the physical environment, but it was based on that whole community feel, and how people trust each other. It should have been obvious to me, but that was a really big lesson at the start of the pandemic.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A similar point was made by Chris Thurling, chair of Armadillo, a digital design firm which went fully remote during the pandemic, who expanded his business during this period:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI want to remain completely open-minded about whether we ever need to have a traditional office again. If you look at the performance of our business since March 2020, we are performing really well and our clients are not saying there\u8217\'20\'19s been a drop-off in quality. Our profitability as a business has gone up and we\u8217\'20\'19re growing. Why would we change too much?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bruce Daisley, an authority on the future of work and presenter of the podcast {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://eatsleepworkrepeat.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Eat Sleep Work Repeat}}}\uc2 , watches the hybrid work trend closely. He told me, \u8220\'20\'1CProbably the most farsighted approach I have seen was \uc2 Dropbox\uc2 , which said late in 2020 that getting people into the office for a certain number of days or specific days doesn\u8217\'20\'19t work. Because people think, why am I going in to the office on Wednesday? Just because it\u8217\'20\'19s Wednesday doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make any sense. People will come in to the office when they need to and they will come into the office for experiences.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 People will not come into the office, however, under duress. And if they do come, they will not stay loyal for long. In the summer of 2021, \uc2 Google\uc2  faced significant employee discontent when it announced that it intended to use its pay calculator to implement pay according to proximity to the office, reflecting the priority some employers still put on presenteeism.\par \par \uc2 This strategy is risky and unfair, as Sarah O\u8217\'20\'19Connor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/a37150e3-7480-4181-90e4-1ce9dc5d1d39"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 commented}}}\uc2  in the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2 : \u8220\'20\'1CIf two workers from the same head office want to switch to working from home, but one inherited a house in an expensive city while the other had been living in a commuter town, is it fair for the latter to take a pay cut?\par \par \uc2 She quoted \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg\uc2  of \uc2 Meta\uc2  telling his employees by video, \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll adjust salary to location \u8230\'20\'26 There\u8217\'20\'19ll be severe ramifications for people who are not honest about this.\u8221\'20\'1D But who is not being honest?\par \par \uc2 Some companies are clearly struggling to accept the serious shift in mindset and values of their talent\par \par \uc2 Some companies are clearly struggling to accept the serious shift in mindset and values of their talent. It remains to be seen whether management will rise to the challenges of supporting home-based working or continue to believe they can persuade and cajole workers to be present in the office when they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to be.\par \par \uc2 Anne-Elisabeth Moutet, a French broadcaster and columnist based in Paris, understands presenteeism as something else too: a feature of power politics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe French system hates hybrid,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause the French boss wants to know what his subordinates are doing, very micro managing most of the time. A strict hierarchy prevails. And the fact that this hierarchy actually doesn\u8217\'20\'19t really work with the new ways of [hybrid] working means that innovation is slower in our country; sometimes it\u8217\'20\'19s even sabotaged by people next to you who are vying with you for the boss\u8217\'20\'19s attention. If this sounds like the court of a minor Borgia or Louis XIV, minus the decor, that\u8217\'20\'19s where it comes from.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In the end the question, for leaders who want their people back in the office is: why? Is it because regulating some work from home \u8211\'20\'13 finance especially \u8211\'20\'13 is legally complex? Is it optics? That management and leaders feel emotionally invested in high-spec, hi-tech, visible offices? Or is it a failure to comprehend the scale and sweep of change?\par \par \uc2 Mobility and work is hardwired into us \u8211\'20\'13 the early 20th-century office workers came, as the great Chicago poet Carl Sandburg noted in Skyscraper, from the prairies to the city, to work a fixed day and then be \u8220\'20\'1Cpoured out again back to the streets, prairies and valleys\u8221\'20\'1D. The city became the fixed place and so too the office. But the arrival of the internet, followed now by the pandemic, has made a new centre of work: home, and a new form of mobility: the smartphone, which can work anywhere.\par \par \uc2 The future of work cannot go back in time \u8211\'20\'13 it can only go forwards.\par \par \uc2 This piece was adapted from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hachettebookgroup.com/titles/julia-hobsbawm/the-nowhere-office/9781541701946/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Nowhere Office: Reinventing Work and the Workplace of the Future}}}\uc2  by Julia Hobsbawm, out now\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220412ei4c001e3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
