\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 America is entering the great experiment of hybrid work\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 1,169 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 March 2022\par 07:06 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Companies will need to find a new model that works for them and their employees \u8211\'20\'13 many of whom prefer to work at home some days\par \par \uc2 It can be hard to remember what work at the office was like before the pandemic forced millions of Americans to start working from home. That shift was monumental and seemingly implausible, until it happened. But people soon adapted to saying \u8220\'20\'1Csorry, you\u8217\'20\'19re on mute\u8221\'20\'1D on Zoom calls and wearing sweatpants all day.\par \par \uc2 This spring, workers are finally heading back to the office en masse and into another untested and ambitious experiment in work life: hybrid working.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a brave new world \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19re doing something we\u8217\'20\'19ve never done before, which is we\u8217\'20\'19re going to go, en masse, hybrid,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Nicholas Bloom\uc2 , an economics professor at Stanford.\par \par \uc2 Many companies that are bringing their workers back to the office are doing so on this basis, meaning they are allowing employees to do a mix of in-person and virtual work during the week. Some workers will be expected to work a set number of days in the office; others will get to choose which days they want to come in.\par \par \uc2 The great experiment is already under way. During the week of 9 March, the average office {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kastle.com/safety-wellness/getting-america-back-to-work/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 occupancy rate}}}\uc2  across 10 large US metro areas was 40.5% \u8211\'20\'13 the highest percentage since December, according to \uc2 Kastle Systems\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Companies that once told employees that they would be working from home indefinitely have set dates to return to the office. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  was the first big tech company to announce that it wanted its employees {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-02-14/microsoft-returning-to-the-office-on-feb-28-as-covid-cases-decline?sref=fqqmZ8gi"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 back in the office}}}\uc2 , by the end of February. Other companies, including \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Apple\uc2 , soon followed suit. \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , which at the beginning of the pandemic {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2020/05/12/twitter-tells-employees-they-can-work-from-home-forever.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  employees they could work from home \u8220\'20\'1Cfor ever\u8221\'20\'1D, said it would {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/03/03/twitter-to-reopen-offices-march-15-though-remote-work-still-an-option.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reopen}}}\uc2  its offices in mid-March.\par \par \uc2 But these companies are telling staff that the era of the five-day office week is over. Some of employees\u8217\'20\'19 time can be spent working from home.\par \par \uc2 Whether this new structure of office work will last is unclear, largely because this is the first wide-scale adoption of hybrid work.\par \par \uc2 Bloom, who has studied remote work for nearly two decades, has been consulting with hundreds of companies and managers on their return to office plans. Managers, it seems, want their workers back in the office. A survey from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://slack.com/blog/news/the-great-executive-employee-disconnect"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Slack}}}\uc2  found that executives were three times more likely than non-executive employees to want to return to the office full-time.\par \par \uc2 Surveys show that people have liked working from home so much that many are willing to switch jobs to keep that option\par \par \uc2 Employees, on the other hand, want to keep working remotely \u8211\'20\'13 at least some of the work week. With today\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nber.org/papers/w29739#:~:text=While%20some%20indicators%20on%20the,market%20is%20already%20very%20tight."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tight labor market}}}\uc2 , Bloom said, the worst thing a company can do is expect workers to come in five days a week. Multiple {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.gallup.com/poll/355907/remote-work-persisting-trending-permanent.aspx"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 surveys}}}\uc2  have shown that workers have liked the perks of working from home so much that many are willing to switch jobs to keep that option.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat is the fastest way to destroy your company,\u8221\'20\'1D Bloom said. \u8220\'20\'1CHybrid work-from-home, it\u8217\'20\'19s become completely a standard.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bloom points out that before the pandemic, many people were already doing some type of hybrid work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost of us probably did some work on the weekend, some work in the evenings. So it isn\u8217\'20\'19t actually such a radical shift to say \u8216\'20\'18we\u8217\'20\'19re going to formalize this\u8217\'20\'19,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 The best hybrid mix right now, according to Bloom, is the \u8220\'20\'1Cboring and predictable\u8221\'20\'1D hybrid model of three days in the office and two days working from home. The number of days in this model are not random. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wfhresearch.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/02/WFHResearch_updates_Februrary2022.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Research}}}\uc2  has shown that people want to work from home on average 2.5 days a week, making the three-two model the closest compromise.\par \par \uc2 The plans of companies like \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 , \uc2 Apple\uc2  and \uc2 Google\uc2  will allow most workers to follow this model. Other companies, such as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , are leaving it up to employees to work out with their teams how often they will work in the office.\par \par \uc2 Some companies that have been testing hybrid models have noticed that employees seem to appreciate this flexibility.\par \par \uc2 Joanne Wright, vice-president of enterprise operations and services at \uc2 IBM\uc2 , said the company had been working on opening its 100 office locations scattered across the country for employees and teams to use as much as they want.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe know that there\u8217\'20\'19s going to be days when they\u8217\'20\'19re going to work from home and continue to be productive, and there\u8217\'20\'19s days where it makes sense for them to be with a team or clients, collaborating and innovating,\u8221\'20\'1D Wright said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe were flexible before the pandemic, and that\u8217\'20\'19s what we\u8217\'20\'19ve been trying to drive in this new world, is maybe we need to even think more flexibility than before.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Management experts stress that return-to-office plans should work toward accommodating balance between employees\u8217\'20\'19 work and non-work lives, and employers need to communicate to their employees that hybrid models are still experimental. Surveys and discussions with employees will be crucial in understanding how return-to-office plans are affecting their lives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve never actually been in this place before,\u8221\'20\'1D said Heidi Brooks, a lecturer at the Yale School of Management. She said employers needed to be especially aware of how the nature of productivity has changed during the pandemic, when people built their work lives outside the office. \u8220\'20\'1CThe question that\u8217\'20\'19s now up is: can we transition back and hold on to what works \u8230\'20\'26 and all of the gains that were possible when people didn\u8217\'20\'19t have to commute and transition [to the office]?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Brooks emphasizes the importance of fairness in return-to-office plans and says executives must consider the pandemic\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cdifferential impacts\u8221\'20\'1D, particularly around race and gender. For example, women with young children have to figure out childcare to go into work.\par \par \uc2 Johnny C Taylor\uc2 , president and chief executive of the \uc2 Society for Human Resource Management\uc2 , said that employers would need to see how return-to-office plans affect morale and a company\u8217\'20\'19s culture, not just worker productivity.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C[A plan] may prove to be incredibly effective, but you may not be creating an environment where people enjoy their work and are engaged,\u8221\'20\'1D Taylor said.\par \par \uc2 Employers also have to be clear about how return-to-office plans balance the needs of employees and the company.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to bring more balance into the discussion that this is good for you, but it also has to be good for us too,\u8221\'20\'1D Taylor said, adding that employers needed to be transparent about how they are still figuring out what will work best for the company.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is important for employers to be honest about the fact that we don\u8217\'20\'19t really know how this is all going to work out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220329ei3t000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
