\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 New wave of teachers\u8217\'20\'19 strikes rolls across US\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,147 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2022\par 11:55 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Thousands have walked out as school districts face staffing shortages amid Covid disruptions and historically low pay\par \par \uc2 Thousands of teachers around the United States are resuming a strike wave in education that swept the country in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/global/commentisfree/2019/nov/12/teacher-strikes-chicago-kentucky-political-impacts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2018 and 2019}}}\uc2 , before the Covid-19 pandemic.\par \par \uc2 School districts across America are facing severe staffing shortages of teachers, substitute teachers and support staff amid Covid-19 disruptions and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/solving-k-12-staffing-shortages/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 historically low pay}}}\uc2 , contributing to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://america.cgtn.com/2022/02/11/the-heat-u-s-teacher-shortage#:~:text=According%20to%20the%20National%20Education,they%20love%20earlier%20than%20planned."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 burnout }}}\uc2  and worsening working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Over the past few weeks, teachers have gone on strike in Sacramento, California, Minneapolis, Minnesota, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pressdemocrat.com/article/news/tentative-deal-reached-to-end-cotati-rohnert-park-teacher-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sonoma county, California}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abc7chicago.com/riverdale-elementary-school-village-of-teacher-strike-george-patton/11631914/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Riverdale, Illinois}}}\uc2 , and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://wgntv.com/news/proviso-district-209-teachers-reach-tentative-agreement-to-end-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Proviso, Illinois}}}\uc2 , as teachers in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ktvu.com/news/tentative-agreement-reached-with-mt-diablo-unified-school-district-teachers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other districts}}}\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://triblive.com/local/valley-news-dispatch/teacher-strike-authorized-but-not-set-at-new-ken-arnold/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  authorized strikes}}}\uc2 . Earlier this year, teachers in Chicago, Illinois, were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gizmodo.com/chicago-teachers-locked-out-of-digital-classrooms-amid-1848308598"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 locked ou}}}\uc2  t by Chicago Public Schools over their demands for pausing in-person learning during a Covid surge.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://drive.google.com/file/d/10wIncXw4EPLCA8hXWKVMuch_IP_l7796/view"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 About 200 teachers}}}\uc2  at Summit Charter Public Schools in California and Washington \u8211\'20\'13 a charter school network {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://files.eric.ed.gov/fulltext/ED607124.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supported}}}\uc2  by funds from the philanthropic foundations of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2020/06/25/new-concerns-raised-about-well-known-digital-learning-platform/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mark Zuckerberg}}}\uc2 , Bill Gates, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://summitpsnews.org/2020/07/01/breaking-news-summit-public-schools-announces-plan-for-faculty-wide-salary-freeze/#:~:text=Philanthropies%20and%20others.-,Ms.,12%20public%20educator%20in%20California."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jeff Bezos}}}\uc2  and other wealthy Silicon Valley investors \u8211\'20\'13 have been fighting for a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://summitpsnews.org/2022/03/03/summit-public-schools-continues-contract-negotiations-with-teacher-union/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 first union contract}}}\uc2  since unionizing in December 2019. The move came despite opposition from school administration, which included {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.change.org/p/summit-public-schools-leadership-overturn-wrongful-termination?recruiter=1824582&utm_source=share_petition&utm_medium=twitter&utm_campaign=psf_combo_share_abi&recruited_by_id=72ae4b10-c1c6-0130-9de1-3c764e0480c1&share_bandit_exp=abi-16028048-en-US&share_bandit_var=v2"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the firing}}}\uc2  of three union-supporting teachers.\par \par \uc2 Negotiations have been at an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unitesummitteachers.com/2021/07/15/moving-on-to-mediation/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  impasse}}}\uc2  since 2021, and now teachers are threatening to take steps {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unitesummitteachers.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  toward a strike}}}\uc2  if a new contract isn\u8217\'20\'19t reached withthe administration, which includes the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://summitpsnews.org/2020/07/01/breaking-news-summit-public-schools-announces-plan-for-faculty-wide-salary-freeze/#:~:text=Philanthropies%20and%20others.-,Ms.,12%20public%20educator%20in%20California."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  highest-paid school administrator}}}\uc2  in California, CEO \uc2 Diane Tavenner\uc2 , with a salary of more than $450,000 annually.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic, teachers at Summit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://actionnetwork.org/petitions/no-pay-freezes-no-layoffs-fair-contract-now?source=direct_link"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faced}}}\uc2  threats of pay freezes and layoffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf we don\u8217\'20\'19t come to an agreement as soon as possible, our teachers are preparing for a strike,\u8221\'20\'1D said Janine Pe\u241\'00\'F1afort, a Spanish teacher at Summit School Prep in Redwood, California, and president of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://unitesummitteachers.com/member-services/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Unite Summit Teachers}}}\uc2 , which represents teachers at the schools. \u8220\'20\'1CEver since Summit expanded their schools, teachers have had less of a say in the decisions being made in our school. I think that sense of powerlessness and our demanding workload causes a lot of teachers to leave our schools every year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://drive.google.com/file/d/10wIncXw4EPLCA8hXWKVMuch_IP_l7796/view"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued}}}\uc2  that despite millions of dollars in funding from wealthy billionaires, Summit Public Schools imposes austerity on teachers and schools, which has contributed to high turnover of teachers, lack of support for English language learning students, and an intense workload, which includes mentorships with individual students and more work days than other school districts.\par \par \uc2 Pe\u241\'00\'F1afort cited a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://drive.google.com/file/d/10wIncXw4EPLCA8hXWKVMuch_IP_l7796/view"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 retention survey}}}\uc2  conducted by the union that found about half of all teachers at Summit Public Schools did not plan to return next school year. Amid high turnover, Pe\u241\'00\'F1afort argued teachers were expected to serve as mentors for students throughout their four years of high school, but have no job security.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19re advocating for is a two-year probationary period to have that sense of security, especially since the past two years during the pandemic, teachers shifted to virtual learning within a day, showing the value and dedication we put into our jobs every single day,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 In Sacramento, California, more than 4,000 teachers and staff represented by Sacramento City Teachers Association and Service Employees International Union 1021, began a strike on 23 March over severe staffing shortages and Covid-related issues as new union contract negotiations with the city\u8217\'20\'19s school district have been at an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scusd.edu/press-release/sac-city-unified-seeks-impasse-determination-resolve-issues-impacting-continuity"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  impasse}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Workers voted overwhelmingly to authorize the strike earlier this month, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kcra.com/article/sacramento-city-teachers-staff-vote-authorize-strike/39402151"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 95% }}}\uc2  voting in favor of striking with Sacramento City Teachers Association and 97% in favor of \uc2 SEIU\uc2  1021. SCTA represents about 2,800 staff in the district, and \uc2 SEIU\uc2  1021 represents an additional 1,800 school workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just reached past a crisis point,\u8221\'20\'1D said David Fisher, president of SCTA and a second-grade teacher for over 20 years. \u8220\'20\'1CThe district has been saying for years, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sacbee.com/news/local/education/article245805965.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 falsely}}}\uc2 , that they were in some sort of economic turmoil, even {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sacteachers.org/scusds-structural-surplus-continues-ends-2020-21-with-19-million-surplus/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claiming}}}\uc2  they were on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scusd.edu/e-connections-post/facts-about-state-takeover-our-schools"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 precipice}}}\uc2  of state takeover. It turns out, it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t true then and it\u8217\'20\'19s even less true now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Fisher argued as the school district has received {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://actionnetwork.org/letters/sacramento-city-unified-needs-to-reject-budget-cuts-to-student-services"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 millions in Covid-19 relief funds and state funds}}}\uc2 , severe staff shortages are leaving students and teachers without proper support. The district has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cta.org/educator/posts/sacramento-city-educators-prepare-to-strike-tomorrow"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  about 10,000 students}}}\uc2  without a permanent teacher. An additional 600 students are on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sacteachers.org/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/Factfinding-report-31722-Sac-City-schools.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wait lists}}}\uc2  for independent study, with 14 teacher vacancies for the program. He also argued the district had continued laying off or was refusing to hire support staff such as teacher assistants.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInstead of investing these dollars on the students, they are just amassing this giant rainy day fund, but it\u8217\'20\'19s pouring out,\u8221\'20\'1D added Fisher. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery student deserves at the very least a teacher in the classroom. What we\u8217\'20\'19ve been experiencing and families have been experiencing is 10,000 students that come to school don\u8217\'20\'19t have a regular teacher, 3,000 or so don\u8217\'20\'19t even have a substitute so they get corralled into cafeterias or split up between classes.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Sacramento City Unified School District {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.scusd.edu/press-release/negotiations-update-sacramento-city-unified-negotiations-team-met-scta-and-mediators"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued}}}\uc2  the strike hurts students and argued their current proposals reflect what the district can afford, with the next successor contract providing an opportunity for the unions and district to negotiate to improve staffing issues.\par \par \uc2 In Minneapolis, Minnesota, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mprnews.org/story/2022/03/17/mpls-teachers-strike-of-1970-changed-education-across-the-state#:~:text=More%20than%204%2C000%20Minneapolis%20educators,education%20and%20public%20sector%20unions."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over 4,000 teachers}}}\uc2  represented by Minnesota Federation of Teachers Local 59 have been on strike since 8 March {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mft59.org/strike-readiness"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 over}}}\uc2  low pay, a lack of support staff and resources for students, and high turnover. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://prismreports.org/2022/03/02/educators-in-st-paul-and-minneapolis-may-be-going-on-strike-soon-heres-why/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 640 teachers and support staff }}}\uc2  have left the district in the past 18 months.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re fighting for safe and stable schools, and what that looks like is systemic changes to the way that education is run in Minneapolis,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kearney Burns, a special education teacher at Hiawatha community school in Minneapolis. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat is being done in Minneapolis is trying to run our school systems like a business. There\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of top-down decision making and not a lot of input from actual stakeholders.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Burns emphasized the need for support staff positions to be fully staffed and for these workers to be properly compensated with a living wage, such as educational support professionals who currently make as little as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.minnpost.com/community-voices/2022/02/for-two-minneapolis-educators-a-strike-for-living-wages-and-stable-staffing-has-been-years-in-the-making/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $24,000 a year.}}}\par \par \uc2 She noted teachers had received immense community support through the strike, as these improvements that teachers are fighting for will benefit students and the community.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople know that safe and stable schools create safe and stable communities, and those safe and stable communities create safe and stable cities,\u8221\'20\'1D Burns added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe all want to be back in classrooms teaching our students, but we don\u8217\'20\'19t want it to be the same as it was before.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Early on Friday the union announced a tentative agreement had been reached, following a similar tentative agreement in neighbouring St Paul announced the night before.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220325ei3p0015u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
