\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Jay-Z needs to decide which side he\u8217\'20\'19s on\u8217\'20\'19: Chateau Marmont workers to picket star\u8217\'20\'19s Oscars after-party\b0\par\par\uc2 Wilfred Chan \par \uc2 1,525 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 March 2022\par 01:23 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Jay-Z and Beyonce\u8217\'20\'19s ultra-exclusive post-Oscars bash runs afoul of a worker boycott of the famed hotel, which laid off hundreds when the pandemic hit\par \par \uc2 Nestled at the foot of mansion-studded hills, just north of Los Angeles\u8217\'20\'19 legendary Sunset Boulevard, the seven-story Chateau Marmont has been a mainstay of Hollywood socializing for nearly a century \u8211\'20\'13 including in recent years, the venue for Jay-Z and Beyonc\u233\'00\'E9\u8217\'20\'19s ultra-exclusive post-Oscars bash.\par \par \uc2 But the storied Hollywood playground has become a rallying point for a growing labor movement, and this Sunday, scores of Chateau Marmont workers, alleging longstanding rights violations and discrimination by their employer, plan to protest outside the Academy Awards afterparty. That means {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/shortcuts/2013/feb/04/beyonce-mrs-carter-show"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Carters}}}\uc2  and their star-studded guestlist will have to choose whether to cross a picket line to get in.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHopefully our presence will educate people that they need to go somewhere else,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kurt Petersen, the co-president of \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11, a union supporting the service staff, who are not unionized. \u8220\'20\'1CThis hotel should not be seen any more as the go-to spot in Hollywood until they change the way they treat workers. We\u8217\'20\'19re in a moment in our history where people need to decide which side they are on.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s the question everyone needs to ask themselves, including Jay-Z.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A pseudo-European castle, Chateau Marmont has long been a favorite retreat for some of America\u8217\'20\'19s most celebrated cultural figures. Built in 1929 by a Los Angeles attorney and originally intended as a top-flight apartment building for wealthy New Yorkers moving West, the Chateau was converted into a hotel after the Great Depression and has cultivated an air of exclusivity ever since.\par \par \uc2 Luminaries from F Scott Fitzgerald to Sofia Coppola have produced work at the building, and the hotel has made countless appearances in popular music, film and literature, including references in songs by the Grateful Dead, Miley Cyrus and Lana Del Rey. Since 1990, the hotel has been run by Andr\u233\'00\'E9 Balazs, an elite hotelier and celebrity in his own right, known for his romantic relationships with A-listers. All these associations with stars have only added to the hotel\u8217\'20\'19s premier reputation over the years.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s all changing now. Since last February, the Chateau\u8217\'20\'19s staff \u8211\'20\'13 some of whom have worked at the hotel for decades \u8211\'20\'13 have led a fierce boycott that has drawn the support of Hollywood figures including Jane Fonda, Spike Lee, Issa Rae, Gabrielle Union, Samira Wiley, Robin Thede, Ashley Nicole Black and Alfonso Cuar\u243\'00\'F3n. Director Aaron Sorkin {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/aaron-sorkin-lucy-desi-movie-chateau-marmont-shoot-scrapped-4171428/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scrapped a shoot}}}\uc2  at the hotel for Being the Ricardos; Paramount Plus series The Offer also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://deadline.com/2021/08/the-offer-paramount-plus-series-honors-chateau-marmont-boycott-hotel-responds-1234820493/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pulled out}}}\uc2  of filming there.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a dramatic fall from grace for the hotel\u8217\'20\'19s management. In an emailed statement, a spokesperson accused the union of trying to \u8220\'20\'1Cdamage the Chateau Marmont\u8221\'20\'1D by orchestrating protests using \u8220\'20\'1Cpaid agitators \u8230\'20\'26 most of whom are not former employees and have no connections to the non-union Chateau Marmont\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But the movement has been a vital boost for the workers, whose discontent runs deep over what they have called a toxic environment.\par \par \uc2 Bias appeared to be rife at the hotel. Darker-skinned employees have said they were subjected to racist remarks and passed over for promotions. According to an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/rot-at-hollywoods-playground-chateau-marmont-staff-allege-racial-discrimination-sexual-misconduct-and-neglectful-management-4060721/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 investigation}}}\uc2  by the Hollywood Reporter, the Chateau\u8217\'20\'19s managing director, Amanda Grandinetti, referred to one staff member as a \u8220\'20\'1Cblackie\u8221\'20\'1D, and told another that they should respond to her by saying \u8220\'20\'1CYessa, massa,\u8221\'20\'1D apparently in reference to a slave owner. In a lawsuit filed against the Chateau last year, April Blackwell, a black woman who worked at the Chateau, said Grandinetti fired her after she complained about a pattern of racist abuse from guests.\par \par \uc2 Grandinetti did not respond to the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s requests for comment, but she previously {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-features/rot-at-hollywoods-playground-chateau-marmont-staff-allege-racial-discrimination-sexual-misconduct-and-neglectful-management-4060721/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 acknowledged}}}\uc2  to the Hollywood Reporter that she \u8220\'20\'1Ccould have advocated more quickly for [her] team\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Chateau\u8217\'20\'19s female employees have said they were subjected to frequent sexual harassment. Workers painted a grim portrait of Balazs, alleging that the owner would get drunk on the premises and grope female workers \u8211\'20\'13 an accusation Balazs has denied. Management also failed to take action when guests touched female employees without their consent, workers alleged.\par \par \uc2 The hotel\u8217\'20\'19s spokesperson said, \u8220\'20\'1CThese meritless allegations are all unproven for one simple reason: they were manufactured in lawsuits bought and paid for by \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  Local 11 as part of their targeted efforts to unionize Chateau Marmont. Contrary to the bogus claims in these already-dismissed, union-backed sham filings, Chateau Marmont has a long and well-documented history of diversity and inclusion among both our employees and our guests.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Things came to a head in 2020. Just before the pandemic hit, Chateau staff approached \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  to discuss how they could push for better working conditions, Petersen, the union organizer, said. That effort was shattered in mid-March, as the coronavirus began to spread, when the Chateau\u8217\'20\'19s management abruptly laid off the vast majority of its staff \u8211\'20\'13 248 people \u8211\'20\'13 with no severance or extended health insurance.\par \par \uc2 One of those workers was Alejandro Roldan, a 33-year-old full-time housekeeper, who told the Guardian he was making just over $15 an hour at the Chateau before he lost his job, and with it, his health insurance. Then he caught Covid \u8211\'20\'13 and decided against a costly hospital visit. But then his symptoms became severe. \u8220\'20\'1CI was afraid I was going to die,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 It was a blow on top of a workplace accident he suffered just over a month before the layoff, when he was setting up for Jay-Z\u8217\'20\'19s last Oscars party and a glass coffee table shattered, sending shards into his eyes. He made a full recovery, but was hit with more hospital bills, which his employer didn\u8217\'20\'19t help cover. \u8220\'20\'1CI was frustrated,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI was like, I\u8217\'20\'19m losing my vision for someone that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t even support us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That July, Balazs {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.latimes.com/business/story/2020-07-28/chateau-marmont-converted-to-members-only-hotel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  he was reorganizing the property as a members-only club, and would not hire back most of the staff.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was the best union-busting campaign ever,\u8221\'20\'1D Petersen said. \u8220\'20\'1CJust fire all the workers and make sure that none of the ones who were standing up for their rights come back to work.\u8221\'20\'1D When Roldan and other workers began to protest, members of the Chateau\u8217\'20\'19s management responded by showing up to film and warn them, \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19re watching you.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the Chateau\u8217\'20\'19s staff pressed on. In May 2020, the hotel workers and \uc2 Unite Here\uc2  11 won the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://covid19.lacounty.gov/covid19-news/adopt-right-of-recall-and-worker-retention-policie/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passage of a \u8220\'20\'1Cright of recall\u8221\'20\'1D ordinance}}}\uc2  in Los Angeles, requiring employers to hire back workers laid off during the pandemic instead of replacing them with new ones. A similar statewide law was passed the following year.\par \par \uc2 In January 2022, the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  found that Chateau Marmont had illegally surveilled its laid-off workers at protests, in order to disrupt their efforts to organize. The federal labor board negotiated a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/business/business-news/chateau-marmont-union-settle-1235073049/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 settlement}}}\uc2  with the Marmont, requiring that the hotel respect workers\u8217\'20\'19 labor rights and cease its interference with worker organizing.\par \par \uc2 Petersen sees the victories as part of a broader strengthening of labor solidarity between Hollywood\u8217\'20\'19s entertainment and hospitality industries in the wake of the pandemic. \u8220\'20\'1CWe wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have this boycott without the solidarity from actors, or from Sag-Aftra, from the Iatse [International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees], from the \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , who have been extraordinary,\u8221\'20\'1D he told the Guardian. \u8220\'20\'1CBoth of our industries suffered tremendous losses during this time in terms of business. Those unions and those members have stood by us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Chateau\u8217\'20\'19s workers\u8217\'20\'19 demands are still far from met. They want their jobs back, and they want clear commitments from Chateau Marmont\u8217\'20\'19s management that it will reform its workplace environment. And they want to form a union, so that they\u8217\'20\'19ll no longer have to feel \u8220\'20\'1Calone\u8221\'20\'1D, said Roldan.\par \par \uc2 The Chateau Marmont spokesperson said that the hotel had hired more than 50 former employees under the new ordinance, and said the union\u8217\'20\'19s protests had \u8220\'20\'1Cslowed the process of rehiring former workers\u8221\'20\'1D. But Petersen believes that it\u8217\'20\'19s the hotel that\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cslowed the reopening purposely to wear down folks and their willingness to go back\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 This Sunday, Roldan will be among the workers picketing Jay-Z\u8217\'20\'19s party. The former housekeeper is still thinking about the injury he suffered at the rapper\u8217\'20\'19s last event.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI just want Jay-Z to support us,\u8221\'20\'1D said Roldan. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery time they go to the hotel, we serve them, we get whatever they want and we\u8217\'20\'19re there for them. So they have to be there for us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Representatives at \uc2 Roc Nation\uc2 , Jay-Z\u8217\'20\'19s company, did not return the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s request for comment.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This story was amended on 24 March 2022 to correct the spelling of Samira Wiley\u8217\'20\'19s name. Alejandro Roldan\u8217\'20\'19s age and pay rate were also corrected.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220324ei3o0030i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
