\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Completely demoralized\u8217\'20\'19: US railroad workers pushed to the brink\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,189 \uc2 words\par \uc2 14 March 2022\par 10:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers are under intense strain amid grueling schedules, union contract negotiations and an arcane attendance policy system\par \par \uc2 American railroad workers are criticizing a new restrictive attendance policy at one of the largest railroad freight networks in the US, as they experience grueling schedules and labor cuts in the midst of new union contract negotiations, which have hit an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.omahadailyrecord.com/content/railroad-contract-talks-head-mediation-after-impasse"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  impasse}}}\uc2 , prompting federal mediation.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  spoke with several railroad trainmen and engineers who requested to remain anonymous as they are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bnsf.com/about-bnsf/pdf/2019-Code-of-Conduct.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prohibited }}}\uc2  by their employer from speaking to the media.\par \par \uc2 On 1 February, \uc2 BNSF Railway\uc2 , one of the largest railroad freight networks in North America implemented a new point system attendance policy, criticized by workers and unions representing about 17,000 workers, for being more restrictive, incentivizing going to work fatigued or ill, and increasing threats of discipline.\par \par \uc2 One train engineer in the network\u8217\'20\'19s Great Plains Region claimed they had to push back a medical procedure for a chronic illness to the summer, when they have vacation time off scheduled, as they would have used nearly all of their attendance points required for the time off needed for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe feel stabbed in the back, completely demoralized with the work we did through the pandemic, understanding the situation, going the extra mile, and doing what we\u8217\'20\'19re asked to do, and then some without complaint,\u8221\'20\'1D said the worker.\par \par \uc2 The new attendance system appears arcane. Workers are allotted 30 career attendance points and docked several points for taking any days off, with points varying on the time of the week and days designated as high-impact days. Workers can get four points back after working uninterrupted for 14 days. Previously, workers were allotted five weekdays off a month and two weekend days off. Workers are continuously on call and have just over an hour to report to work when they are called in.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFatigue is a chronic issue with almost everyone I know and work with. We manage it so it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t get too severe,\u8221\'20\'1D they added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve been struggling through the pandemic with fatigue, exhaustion, and we have no chance to even catch our breath.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Despite the grueling work schedules, workers explained there is no room for error in the work they do given the important safety components of their job, such as transporting hazardous chemicals.\par \par \uc2 Workers are legally permitted to work a maximum of 12 hours operating a train, but the worker noted it often takes several additional hours for relief to arrive and to get to a hotel or terminal when away from home. While always on call, the work hours vary widely and often change on a moment\u8217\'20\'19s notice, making it impossible for workers to adapt to healthy sleep schedules, having to piece together naps whenever possible while waiting to be called into work.\par \par \uc2 Workers say that even though high-impact days, such as various holidays including Super Bowl Sunday, are designated as seven attendance points, workers are not paid any additional pay for working those days.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t know when they\u8217\'20\'19re calling and they can call you at any time,\u8221\'20\'1D a worker said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re still going through the pandemic, whether folks like to believe that or not, or abide by what\u8217\'20\'19s going on or not, even though it\u8217\'20\'19s lessened, you\u8217\'20\'19re forcing folks to come to work sick because they\u8217\'20\'19re scared to use points. You\u8217\'20\'19re literally having them pick between \u8216\'20\'18do you go to work sick and ill and not feeling well or do you save that time to be with your kids and your family?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Another worker criticized the attendance policy, arguing it makes it even more difficult to take time off for medical appointments or to be able to take time off to spend with family as railroad workers spent most of their time away from home, waiting anywhere from 12 to 30 hours in a hotel room before being called back into work on a return trip to their home.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere are 17,600 employees that work with this new attendance policy, and not one of them will tell you that it\u8217\'20\'19s a good program at all. It\u8217\'20\'19s completely unmanageable,\u8221\'20\'1D they said.\par \par \uc2 Workers represented by the \uc2 Brotherhood of Locomotive Engineers and Trainmen\uc2 , and the Transportation Division of the International Association of Sheet Metal, Air, Rail, and Transportation unions mobilized and pushed for the right to strike over the new attendance policy, but a federal judge {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ble-t.org/news/judge-rules-hi-viz-attendance-policy-court-case-as-a-minor-dispute/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled }}}\uc2  the dispute was \u8220\'20\'1Cminor\u8221\'20\'1D under the Railway Labor Act, meaning workers cannot legally strike over the policy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a policy that, frankly, is just blind to the moment,\u8221\'20\'1D said Greg Regan, president of the transportation trades department, \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ttd.org/about-ttd/leadership/#:~:text=In%202021%2C%20Greg%20Regan%20was,issues%20that%20impact%20transportation%20workers."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 represents }}}\uc2  36 labor unions in the transportation industry. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re turning around and punishing their employees, because they\u8217\'20\'19re faced with some of the challenges that they created themselves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He argued staffing shortages and supply chain issues have been self-made in the industry, as drastic labor cuts have been implemented in recent years, reducing the workforce and lowering overall costs, while rail transportation companies have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/politics-news/biggest-railroad-companies-profiting-supply-chain-crisis-watchdog-alle-rcna18885"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 used the supply chain crisis}}}\uc2  to increase fees and make record profits.\par \par \uc2 The attendance policy change was implemented as workers have continued working through the pandemic via {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/US/wireStory/railroad-contract-talks-head-mediation-impasse-82448291"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  contract negotiations}}}\uc2  between 10 unions and over 30 railroad companies that have been ongoing since fall 2019 amid widespread labor cuts in the industry since 2017 where over 20% of staff have been cut under a new operating model.\par \par \uc2 The industry is pushing to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fightfortwopersoncrews.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reduce}}}\uc2  two person rail crews, an engineer and a conductor, to one person crews, which unions and workers have criticized as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.vice.com/en/article/3angy3/freight-rail-train-disaster-avoidable-boeing"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 serious safety issue }}}\uc2  that will lead to an increase in train derailments, severely affecting communities around the country.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat the companies are offering at the bargaining table, you would think they\u8217\'20\'19re facing declining revenue and decreasing profits, not recording the highest profit margin in the history of railroading,\u8221\'20\'1D Regan said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s clear that the industry does not want to give an inch right now, and they want to continue to claw out as much profit as possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 BNSF Railway\uc2 , owned by billionaire \uc2 Warren Buffett\u8217\'20\'19s Berkshire Hathaway\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.trains.com/trn/news-reviews/news-wire/bnsf-railway-reports-record-financial-results-for-2021/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported }}}\uc2  record profits in 2021, with operating income increasing 13.7% to $8.8bn.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 BNSF Railway\uc2  argued the new attendance policy will provide more predictability for train crews and provide more reliable crew availability.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur program is designed to provide ample time for obligations outside work, including planned vacations, personal leave days and unplanned absences while ensuring that we have sufficient employees available to work,\u8221\'20\'1D a spokesperson said in an email. \u8220\'20\'1CBased on initial feedback, BNSF has already modified the program. We look forward to continuing to work with our employees to gather input and refine the program if needed. BNSF team members drive our success and we couldn\u8217\'20\'19t deliver the nation\u8217\'20\'19s goods without them. We understand that change can be an adjustment, but we believe we can adapt together to meet today\u8217\'20\'19s competitive freight environment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220314ei3e001e2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
