\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18It\u8217\'20\'19s not worth it\u8217\'20\'19: rising gas prices force drivers to work for less than minimum wage\b0\par\par\uc2 Dani Anguiano in Los Angeles \par \uc2 1,052 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 March 2022\par 20:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Drivers already hit by low wages and poor working conditions are spending more time driving to keep their wages the same\par \par \uc2 By Tuesday afternoon, \uc2 Lyft\uc2  driver Elida Zabaleta had earned $100 in the five hours she spent ferrying passengers across the city of San Jose. With gas prices in California surging, she\u8217\'20\'19d have to use more than half of that to cover fuel for the day, leaving her with just $45.\par \par \uc2 The rising cost of gas has made a difficult job all the more difficult, Zabaleta said, forcing her to spend more time behind the wheel to earn enough to afford living in one of the country\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kron4.com/news/real-estate/san-jose-is-2nd-most-expensive-area-to-rent-in-u-s-new-report-reveals/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 most expensive cities}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 US gas prices have reached record highs in recent weeks, surging in part by the fallout of Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine after already having been high for months. California drivers are paying the most of any in the country, at an average of $5.57 a gallon, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://gasprices.aaa.com/?state=CA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AAA}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Rising prices are hitting gig workers particularly hard as fuel makes up a large part of their daily costs. \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  drivers already struggling after the pandemic hit both wages and working conditions say paying more at the pump means they have to spend more time driving in order to achieve the same level of pay. Some are spending more than 60 hours a week working, and some say driving is simply no longer profitable.\par \par \uc2 Zabaleta, who has driven for \uc2 Lyft\uc2  for two years and also works as an organizer with Gig Workers Rising, paid $5.20 a gallon to fill up this week. Meanwhile, factoring in the cost of gas, her income came out to about $9 an hour, far below San Jose\u8217\'20\'19s $16.20 minimum wage. Zabaleta routinely spends as many as 50 hours a week behind the wheel, giving herself just one day off, and is working more to cover the increasing costs.\par \par \uc2 Even before gas prices started rising, pay was becoming increasingly unpredictable driving for \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , she said. She previously drove five days a week, but with fewer passengers during the pandemic and fewer bonuses from \uc2 Lyft\uc2 , Zabaleta had to add an extra work day to her week in order to maintain the same level of income, she said. Companies such as \uc2 Lyft\uc2  aren\u8217\'20\'19t doing enough to help drivers, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis job should be something the worker should enjoy doing \u8211\'20\'13 this is a job that\u8217\'20\'19s high risk and we\u8217\'20\'19re putting our lives on the line everyday,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Other drivers said that without increased wages or other support from ride share companies to help alleviate the burden of fuel costs, driving will no longer be worthwhile.\par \par \uc2 Rondu Gantt started working as a ride share driver in the San Francisco Bay area in 2018 to supplement his income, but began driving full-time after his teaching job left him burnt out. Since then wages have only dropped, he said, and without the bonuses the companies offer, the job wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be profitable at all.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are at the point where we need the companies to pay bonuses every week just to make the job sustainable,\u8221\'20\'1D said Gantt, who is also an organizer with Gig Workers Rising. The normal pay rate is too low, he said, so in addition to driving during the day, he routinely returns to the road at night when surge pricing is in effect.\par \par \uc2 The high price of gas has made the job more challenging, Gantt said. \u8220\'20\'1CMy costs are higher, my pay is the same, so I\u8217\'20\'19m losing money. That\u8217\'20\'19s a mathematical reality,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Benjamin Valdez, who drives part-time for \uc2 Uber\uc2  and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  in Los Angeles and works as an organizer with Rideshare Drivers United, said he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t driven for two weeks because of the high gas prices. When fuel costs go up, drivers stay out on the road for longer in order to try to make a profit, he said, which means there is more competition for rides.\par \par \uc2 The loss in income is difficult for his family, Valdez said, but with gas prices as high as they are and more drivers out on the road, he\u8217\'20\'19d be losing money driving. \u8220\'20\'1CI only drive when it\u8217\'20\'19s profitable, which is few and far between right now,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 California\u8217\'20\'19s governor, \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2 , has said he will propose a tax rebate to help the state\u8217\'20\'19s drivers grapple with the increasing costs at the pump. Drivers are also hoping to see companies such as \uc2 Lyft\uc2  and \uc2 Uber\uc2  take steps to help support them amid the high prices, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/business/2022/03/09/gas-prices-going-up-russia-ukraine/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 experts say}}}\uc2  will likely continue for months.\par \par \uc2 Organizers with Rideshare Drivers United are petitioning the rideshare companies to raise compensation and \u8220\'20\'1Cset the rates fairly and equitably because of our increased costs\u8221\'20\'1D, said Daniel Russell, a part-time \uc2 Uber\uc2  driver and organizer with RDU.\par \par \uc2 When asked if \uc2 Lyft\uc2  would offer additional support to drivers as fuel costs increase, \uc2 Lyft\uc2  spokesperson CJ Macklin said it had taken concrete steps to help, including a fuel cash-back program run {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/lyft-and-getupside-partner-to-bring-cashback-promotions-directly-in-the-lyft-driver-app-301469302.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in partnership}}}\uc2  with GetUpside. Macklin also said that US drivers were now seeing greater average hourly pay than they were a year ago, even with high fuel prices, as more riders return to the platform. \uc2 Uber\uc2  did not immediately respond to a request for comment.\par \par \uc2 Vitalii Konstantinov, who drives for \uc2 Uber\uc2  in San Diego and works with Rideshare Drivers United, said rising costs have forced him to dip into his family\u8217\'20\'19s budget, and cancel a trip to Disneyland he had planned for his young children. He\u8217\'20\'19s actively looking for other jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not worth driving for \uc2 Uber\uc2  any more,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Gantt said he was also considering leaving the industry.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt is unsustainable for me to feel comfortable in this industry right now. I feel the vulnerability. I see how reliant I am on them to give me a bonus, I see how low the rate is,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI can\u8217\'20\'19t work 16 hours every day. I\u8217\'20\'19m gonna burn out. It\u8217\'20\'19s physically demanding to be tired, drinking coffee, under-rested, and driving morning and night.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220310ei3a001jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
