\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Target directing store managers to prevent workers from unionizing\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,049 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 February 2022\par 20:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 New leaked training guidelines prompt managers to look for warning signs of worker and labor union organizing\par \par \uc2 Leaked training documents from Target, one of the largest retailers in the US, reveal how the company is directing management at stores to prevent workers from organizing unions.\par \par \uc2 At the end of January 2022, Target {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://targetworkersunite.com/2022/01/30/leak-targets-anti-worker-and-anti-union-training-for-managers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 emailed}}}\uc2  store management new training guidelines on labor relations to complete, prompting managers to look for warning signs of worker and labor union organizing within their stores and coordinate with corporate human resources to quell union organizing campaigns.\par \par \uc2 The training email and documents were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://targetworkersunite.com/2022/01/30/leak-targets-anti-worker-and-anti-union-training-for-managers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leaked }}}\uc2  anonymously to the workers\u8217\'20\'19 advocacy group, Target Workers Unite, and come as other large US chains including \uc2 Amazon\uc2  and \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  are fighting unionization plans.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis training will help you understand Target\u8217\'20\'19s philosophy on unions and how unions could impact our Brand and our team,\u8221\'20\'1D states an introduction slide to the training.\par \par \uc2 The training goes on to characterize unions as \u8220\'20\'1Ca business that makes money from the dues and fees paid by its members\u8221\'20\'1D, but then explains a union is formed when employees come together and express the desire to have a union represent them, typically through an election process.\par \par \uc2 Target warns a union could reduce flexibility and narrow job descriptions, increase operational costs and create conflict between managers and employees. The company emphasizes preferring working directly with its workers over directly negotiating with workers represented by a union.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe don\u8217\'20\'19t believe that a union or any other third-party representative would improve anything \u8211\'20\'13 not for our team members, our guests, or the company,\u8221\'20\'1D states the training.\par \par \uc2 Target also claims in the training the company isn\u8217\'20\'19t \u8220\'20\'1Canti-union\u8221\'20\'1D but rather \u8220\'20\'1Cpro team member and guest\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Managers are informed during the training that their critical role in leadership is to create an environment where workers don\u8217\'20\'19t feel the need to organize a union, and provides managers with training on how to determine and intervene in any behavior of workers that signals dissatisfaction with working conditions and flags potential warning signs of union organizing.\par \par \uc2 Some examples included small gatherings of workers, the emergence of employees as leaders in vocalizing concerns, seeing workers meet with recently terminated workers, and work-related conversations among employees regarding issues such as pay, benefits, job security or other grievances.\par \par \uc2 The training also flagged \u8220\'20\'1Cunusual activities\u8221\'20\'1D, such as union flyers distributed in store parking lots or missing corporate documents such as schedules or payroll rosters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEarly detection and response to the signs of organizing is vital in maintaining a union-free environment,\u8221\'20\'1D states the training.\par \par \uc2 The training includes a question-and-answer section on different theoretical scenarios prompting managers to take actions in response to union organizing signs, such as surveilling workers\u8217\'20\'19 participation on worker-related blogs or pages on social media, and notifying human resources for guidance on proper action.\par \par \uc2 Adam Ryan, an organizer with Target Workers Unite and an employee at Target in Christiansburg, Virginia, said the training documents were leaked from his store shortly after his flyers to promote a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/events/314877207192216"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  local event presentation}}}\uc2  on Black labor in Richmond, Virginia, for Black History Month co-hosted by Target Workers Unite, were removed from the break room.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI confronted HR about it. We still have a standing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nrworkerspower.com/2018/04/23/target-found-guilty-of-violating-worker-rights/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 settlement }}}\uc2  from the \uc2 NLRB\uc2  [\uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 ] in 2018 and taking down the flyer was in violation of that settlement. I argued with them that the no solicitation policies would be true but they didn\u8217\'20\'19t consistently enforce those policies,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ryan.\par \par \uc2 He claimed the bulletin board at the time included non-work-related content such as a memorial for Betty White and new baby pictures from employees and that managers began questioning employees one-on-one shortly after the incident on work-related issues, as the labor relations training directed.\par \par \uc2 Ryan is currently involved in a union card authorization drive at his store location in an attempt to garner enough support from his co-workers to form a union that Target must directly negotiate a contract with.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you look at the training, they try to say they\u8217\'20\'19re not anti-union, that they\u8217\'20\'19re pro guests and pro team members, but they wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be having these training forced on management at our stories if they weren\u8217\'20\'19t actually trying to union-bust and prevent us from exercising our rights,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ryan. \u8220\'20\'1CTarget is no better than any other company out there. They talk a big game, they say they\u8217\'20\'19re this very progressive company that cares about their workers, but then when workers start exercising their rights, they respond just like any other company that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want a union coming in.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Andrew Stacy, a Target employee in Indianapolis, Indiana, for over two years who is also trying to organize his coworkers to form a union, experienced similar issues, which he has since filed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/25-CA-287773"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unfair labor practice charge}}}\uc2  with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  over.\par \par \uc2 In December 2021, Stacy explained he was distributing union flyers with a co-worker and a manager confiscated and removed the flyers, then interrogated his co-worker about it.\par \par \uc2 The labor relations training, Stacy argued, undermined the company\u8217\'20\'19s claims of being pro-worker.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt just shows how little Target thinks of us, that we don\u8217\'20\'19t know what we want and they know what\u8217\'20\'19s best for us,\u8221\'20\'1D Stacy said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis union-busting training, that unions are a business is 100% untrue. We\u8217\'20\'19re the union. Unions are the workers and what I\u8217\'20\'19m organizing in my store is a rank-and-file effort.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A Target spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CAt Target, we\u8217\'20\'19ve always been committed to listening to our team and creating an environment of mutual trust where every team member\u8217\'20\'19s voice matters.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe regularly provide training to leaders on a variety of topics, and this particular training was recently used in a few stores to help leaders build and maintain Target\u8217\'20\'19s culture of care, identify and address any team member concerns and ensure our team members feel supported and their voices are heard. This training also makes it clear that Target wants our team members to make informed decisions and respects our team\u8217\'20\'19s right to discuss their varying viewpoints on representation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220210ei2a001e1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
