\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Traffic, tickets, gas: rideshare and delivery app workers fight to unionize\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 814 \uc2 words\par \uc2 7 February 2022\par 15:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Coalition in New York City is organizing to demand better work conditions, including living wages, basic benefits and safe work environment\par \par \uc2 For nearly three years, Kevin Zhou has worked as a delivery driver in New York City for \uc2 DoorDash\uc2 , UberEats, \uc2 Grubhub\uc2  and other food delivery apps, and often has to work 12- to 16-hour days just to make ends meet and support his family.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes in an order, you can make just $3 to $5 and it can take half an hour,\u8221\'20\'1D said Zhou.\par \par \uc2 He added issues such as traffic and parking tickets, car maintenance, car insurance, gas, other expenses, and being sent by the app on long delivery runs that are out of the way of areas where most orders are concentrated force him and other workers to have to work long hours to make it worthwhile to work the apps.\par \par \uc2 Just a few weeks ago, Zhou got into a car accident while working during a winter storm in New York City, as he said during bad weather is often when the app is the busiest, and he had to pay several hundred dollars in repairs out of his own pocket.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe companies can take 20 to 30% of our earnings, and by myself I don\u8217\'20\'19t have the power to change that, so we need to unite together to fight for our rights,\u8221\'20\'1D added Zhou. \u8220\'20\'1CWe need change and to organize together to fight these big companies.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Zhou is a founding member of the International Alliance of Delivery Workers, one of nine grassroots worker organizations in the New York City area that are launching a new coalition, Justice for App Workers, representing 100,000 rideshare and delivery app workers on 1 February and kicking off the new organizing effort with a rally in Foley Square in Manhattan.\par \par \uc2 The coalition is one of the largest organizing campaigns focused on the gig app economy in the US. It held the New York rally last week, where hundreds of workers held banners and protested to highlight their struggles in the gig economy.\par \par \uc2 The coalition comprises groups including the NYC Rideshare Club, United Delivery Workers Association, International Alliance of Delivery Workers, the Independent Drivers Guild, Long Island Uber & Lyft Network, Black Car Mafia, UzBER, NYC Drivers Unite, and Utany, which represents Spanish-speaking rideshare drivers in the Bronx.\par \par \uc2 The groups are organizing together in demand of living wages, a safe working environment, an end to unfair deactivation, quality healthcare benefits, reliable bathroom access, and the right to form a union for rideshare and delivery app workers, with the aim to expand these demands and organizing efforts around the US.\par \par \uc2 Rideshare and delivery app workers around the US have been pressuring elected officials to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/aug/27/gig-workers-massachusetts-lawsuit-independent-contractor-status"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rein in}}}\uc2  the industry\u8217\'20\'19s treatment of workers. The New York city council {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2021/sep/23/gig-economy-doordash-grubhub-food-delivery"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed}}}\uc2  legislation several months ago to establish minimum pay and guarantee bathroom access for food delivery workers and some regulation efforts have been made over the years, but workers affirm that significant issues remain that they hope to amend through organizing efforts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are fighting to transform this industry,\u8221\'20\'1D said Naomi Ogutu, who has worked full-time as a rideshare driver for Uber and \uc2 Lyft\uc2  since 2016. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want to take on these multibillion-dollar companies that control our lives. We are a big part of the New York economy and we are an important and integral part of New York. We deserve better.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She explained the issues she has faced as a rideshare driver and heard from other drivers over the past several years, such as the apps taking a significant chunk of revenue out of every ride, a lack of security features to protect drivers, a lack of support or fair arbitration in cases of wrongful deactivation of driver accounts, and a lack of paid leave if a driver has any job-related issues such as getting assaulted by a passenger.\par \par \uc2 Ogutu also cited a lack of access to bathrooms as a prevalent problem for rideshare drivers in New York City, as she often has to drive far out of her way to find a bathroom she is able to use while on the job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI close my app to go to the Wegmans supermarket in Brooklyn, that\u8217\'20\'19s where I go to the bathroom. So that\u8217\'20\'19s how I\u8217\'20\'19ve broken my day. That means I\u8217\'20\'19m losing income, because I\u8217\'20\'19m driving across the bridge, going all the way to Brooklyn, just to use a bathroom,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ogutu, who is also president of NYC Rideshare Club, which represents about 4,400 rideshare drivers in New York City.\par \par \uc2 Ogutu added: \u8220\'20\'1CIf you are working and generating billions of dollars for a company, and you can\u8217\'20\'19t even access a bathroom, that\u8217\'20\'19s not fair and it\u8217\'20\'19s not human at all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220207ei27001be\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
