\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Seattle\u8217\'20\'19s concrete workers strike over low wages and unfair labor practices\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 771 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 January 2022\par 19:11 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Concrete mixer drivers and plant workers allege employers refuse to negotiate and are purging unionized workers\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEvery day is different,\u8221\'20\'1D said Tim Davis, a concrete mixer truck driver in the Seattle, Washington, area.\par \par \uc2 The work Davis and his coworkers do on a daily basis provides the foundation for every big construction and infrastructure project in the region, with long hours and varying start times every workday, leaving little time to spend with family. \u8220\'20\'1CWe live in those trucks day in and day out,\u8221\'20\'1D Davis said.\par \par \uc2 He is one of 330 concrete mixer drivers and plant workers \u8211\'20\'13 represented by \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  Local 174 \u8211\'20\'13 who have been on a general strike over unfair labor practices, accusing their employers of refusing to negotiate in good faith. The striking workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamster.org/2022/01/striking-seattle-mixer-drivers-call-out-concrete-cartel-for-holding-construction-industry-hostage/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fear}}}\uc2  employers want to purge unionized workers from the area\u8217\'20\'19s construction industry and attempt to bankrupt the union through litigation.\par \par \uc2 The strike is reverberating through the construction industry in and around Seattle, which has the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seattlepi.com/local/seattlenews/article/seattle-ranks-second-for-construction-cranes-in-us-16086534.php"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 second most construction cranes}}}\uc2  of any city in the US and has been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecentersquare.com/washington/report-greater-seattle-area-leads-the-way-in-nationwide-construction-boom/article_e79cf4c0-a9cf-11eb-bb23-8314b92e3119.html#:~:text=(The%20Center%20Square)%20%E2%80%93%20Three,despite%20the%20pandemic%2Dinduced%20recession.&text=Statewide%2C%20Washington%20saw%20construction%20employment,5%25%20growth%20in%20construction%20jobs."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 leading}}}\uc2  the country\u8217\'20\'19s construction boom since the pandemic hit. Hundreds of contractors have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.seattletimes.com/business/construction-slows-workers-laid-off-as-seattle-concrete-strike-drags-on/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 laid off}}}\uc2  as construction projects have ground to a halt or been forced to reduce operations without concrete, and layoffs are expected to continue as the strike drags on.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe industry is moving forward and we\u8217\'20\'19re falling behind,\u8221\'20\'1D added Davis. \u8220\'20\'1C The employers say they are family run, but I would never treat family like that. We were out on the picket lines through the holidays in the cold and there\u8217\'20\'19s over 300 other guys out there. They all have families and their healthcare or medical is up this month. That affects 300 families and there are others now being laid off and that affects their families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Some 34 dump truck drivers began striking on 19 November at \uc2 Gary Merlino Construction\uc2 , with about 100 workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/stoneway-concrete-drivers-begin-unfair-labor-practice-strike-301435686.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joining the strike }}}\uc2  on 1 December. The remaining workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamster.org/2021/12/concrete-teamsters-begin-industry-wide-work-stoppage/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  launched}}}\uc2  an industry wide strike on 4 December at six different employers that dominate the concrete industry in the Seattle area: \uc2 Gary Merlino Construction\uc2 , Stoneway Concrete, Cadman, \uc2 CalPortland\uc2 , Salmon Bay Sand & Gravel and \uc2 Lehigh Cement\uc2 . Workers {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamsters174.net/after-unanimous-strike-vote-sand-and-gravel-companies-appear-to-want-a-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unanimously voted }}}\uc2  in favor of authorizing the strike.\par \par \uc2 The companies\u8217\'20\'19 lead negotiator, president of \uc2 Gary Merlino Construction\uc2  Charlie Oliver, did not respond to multiple requests for comment for this story.\par \par \uc2 The workers\u8217\'20\'19 contract expired on 31 July 2021, and they had worked without a contract for months before walking off the job in response to the stalled negotiations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSince the contract expired, we\u8217\'20\'19ve gotten nowhere. It\u8217\'20\'19s actually gotten worse,\u8221\'20\'1D said Todd Parker, a concrete driver at Cadman Seattle and member of the bargaining committee. \u8220\'20\'1CWhat we\u8217\'20\'19d like to see is what everybody else got this year. We just want to be treated as equally as everybody else and they\u8217\'20\'19re not right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers have argued employers\u8217\'20\'19 proposals for a new union contract included less compensation than other construction industry workers in the area receive, and would result in a decrease in compensation over the next three years, with inflation and the high cost of living in the area taken into account. The union has also claimed employers refuse to fund healthcare for retirees, even though the union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://teamster.org/2022/01/striking-seattle-mixer-drivers-call-out-concrete-cartel-for-holding-construction-industry-hostage/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  offered }}}\uc2  to cover any cost increases.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFor the last two years we have been deemed essential workers fighting through this pandemic. You think we\u8217\'20\'19d be met with just a little bit of appreciation and gratitude, but that sure hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been the case,\u8221\'20\'1D said Brett Gallagher, a concrete mix driver at \uc2 CalPortland\uc2  and bargaining committee member.\par \par \uc2 Gallagher emphasized the pride he takes in his work, along with his coworkers, who get to see and help lay the foundation the rest of the building trades build upon. But he argued that concrete workers shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be treated with any less respect, as most of the office workers at their employers have been working from home throughout most of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re fighting for not just us, but for everybody else in these trades and everybody else in our communities,\u8221\'20\'1D added Gallagher. \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of us have families, some with young kids. I have three kids myself who depend on my medical insurance. I\u8217\'20\'19ve got child support to pay and this is tough. It was tough to tell them that it was going to be a lean Christmas this year and they wonder why. It\u8217\'20\'19s because a few people decided they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to sit and talk to us like grownups.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220126ei1q001bd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
