\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 Too young to retire but at risk for Covid, older Americans struggle to find work\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato in Florida \par \uc2 1,404 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 January 2022\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Despite reports of US worker shortages, people who are less than five years from retirement are facing a lack of employment options\par \par \uc2 Elaine Simons, a 61-year-old substitute art teacher in the Seattle, Washington area, was on a 10-month contract and hoping to settle into a more permanent role at the school where she was teaching when the Covid-19 pandemic hit the US in March 2020.\par \par \uc2 Her school shut down for the remainder of the school year, with Simons having to pack up her classroom and learn to navigate the technology necessary to teach remotely. In June 2020, Simons was informed her teaching contract would not be renewed.\par \par \uc2 Some {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/older-workers-were-devastated-by-the-pandemic-downturn-and-continue-to-face-adverse-employment-outcomes-epi-testimony-for-the-senate-special-committee-on-aging/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 5.7 million workers }}}\uc2  ages 55 or older lost their jobs in the US in March and April 2020, 15% of workers in an age demographic that has also experienced the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://data.cdc.gov/widgets/9bhg-hcku?mobile_redirect=true"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  vast majority of Covid-19 deaths}}}\uc2 . The unemployment rate for workers ages 65 and older hit {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RJRetirePolicy/status/1347668495367077888"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a record rate }}}\uc2  of 7.5% in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Simons was able to find a summer teaching position but had to file for unemployment assistance before the fall 2020 school year began. Since then, she has switched back and forth between taking periods of substitute teaching jobs whenever they\u8217\'20\'19re available, and reverting to unemployment during periods where she\u8217\'20\'19s been unable to find work.\par \par \uc2 She found, despite claims of substitute teacher shortages, that longer term substitute contract positions weren\u8217\'20\'19t being offered, and older workers at higher risk for Covid-19 like herself aren\u8217\'20\'19t willing to take substitute gigs day by day, at various different schools without any compensation for quarantine if they catch or are exposed to Covid-19. Simons is fully vaccinated and boosted, but still worried about catching Covid-19 and exposing her elderly mother, whom she helps care for.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome like myself are too young to retire, so I\u8217\'20\'19m still looking for that dream job, I want my permanent job. I want to be fully set for my pension and my social security,\u8221\'20\'1D said Simons, who cannot afford to retire early because the social security benefits would be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/jul/04/social-security-expansion-reform-push"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 far too low }}}\uc2  to live on.\par \par \uc2 Even with unemployment, Simons has had to rely on food banks, food donations, and mutual aid with neighbors. She has tried to get ahead on bills in anticipation of receiving no income if her unemployment benefits expire and she\u8217\'20\'19s unable to obtain a new substitute contracting position, which has been more difficult as many employment resources offered to the unemployed, such as job counseling and training, have been limited during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m sad that at almost 62 years old, I don\u8217\'20\'19t see the likelihood of getting employment at this point,\u8221\'20\'1D added Simons. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really hard for people in my age bracket. I think that we\u8217\'20\'19re feeling undervalued. We\u8217\'20\'19re feeling missed. Nobody seems to care that we\u8217\'20\'19re also part of the high risk community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jan/07/us-jobs-report-december-latest-edited-"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tumbled dramatically}}}\uc2  since the height of the pandemic but older workers are still struggling to find work despite all the reports of worker shortages.\par \par \uc2 Jerry Jenkins, 63, of Lehigh Acres, Florida, lost his job at a manufacturing plant in 2021, where he had worked for seven years. With Florida\u8217\'20\'19s maximum unemployment benefits among the lowest in the US, at $275 a week, Jenkins was forced to cash in part of his retirement savings as he is still trying to find another job.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m losing my retirement because no one will hire me. I can\u8217\'20\'19t even get anybody to answer my resumes,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jenkins. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve even tried to go back to some of the jobs I\u8217\'20\'19ve had in my past to get rehired, but no one seems to be interested because I only have two or three years before retirement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He expressed frustrations with the touting of falling unemployment rates, given he and millions of other workers aren\u8217\'20\'19t {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brookings.edu/blog/up-front/2021/12/14/labor-market-exits-and-entrances-are-elevated-who-is-coming-back/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 classified }}}\uc2  as '\u8220\'20\'1Cin the labor force\u8221\'20\'1D in part due to expired unemployment benefits.\par \par \uc2 A June 2021 analysis by economists at the Schwartz Center for Economic Policy Analysis at The New School found {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.economicpolicyresearch.org/jobs-report/the-pandemic-retirement-surge-increased-retirement-inequality"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least 1.7 million more older workers}}}\uc2  than expected retired due to the pandemic. The analysis encouraged policy solutions such as expanding Medicare eligibility to the age of 50, expanding social security benefits, and creating a department dedicated to older workers at the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Monique Morrisey, an economist at the Economic Policy Institute, explained there are differences among affected older workers, with 55 to 64 year olds who are not eligible for Medicare and in most cases are not yet receiving social security, and workers 65 and older who left the workforce. The issues facing late middle age workers are more worrisome, given their economic demographics.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmong this younger group, the job losses are concentrated among non-college educated workers in lower paid jobs and are disproportionately also jobs that have high contact with the public,\u8221\'20\'1D said Morrissey. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a lot harder to get another job when you\u8217\'20\'19re an older worker.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A survey published by AARP in May 2021 found {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.aarp.org/research/topics/economics/info-2021/older-workers-new-skills-covid-19-pandemic.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 78%}}}\uc2  of workers ages 40 to 65 reported either seeing or experiencing age discrimination in the workplace, the highest level found by AARP since they began surveying the question in 2003. More than half of jobseekers over the age of 55 were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.aarp.org/thinking-policy/older-men-left-workforce-during-pandemic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 classified }}}\uc2  as unemployed for longer than 27 weeks in early 2021.\par \par \uc2 As of October 2021, older workers were the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.economicpolicyresearch.org/images/docs/research/nssr_working_papers/NSSR_WP_062021.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 furthest behind}}}\uc2  in employment recovery among age demographics.\par \par \uc2 Beverly Matozzo of Deptford, New Jersey, worked about 12 hours a week at a school cafeteria for seven years, but was laid off once the school year ended last summer and she hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been able to return as she is recovering from a knee injury and from Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Matozzo, 73, filed for unemployment, but has yet to receive any compensation because the office that assisted in filing her claim made an error with her working dates and she hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been able to get the state unemployment agency to fix the problem and get her claim paid out. She has also been locked out of her account and still trying to resolve that issue.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have been told a thousand different things. All I want is my 17 weeks of unemployment, which is what I am owed,\u8221\'20\'1D said Matozzo. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve had to have three shots in my knee, walked with crutches for several weeks, and now I\u8217\'20\'19ve just gotten over Covid. I just want to be with people. I enjoyed being around the kids, they make you feel alive and fun, so as soon as I can, I\u8217\'20\'19m going back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 57-year-old Dawn Leeson of Springhill, Florida, ran a nonprofit after-school program before Covid-19 forced her to shut down the program in March 2020, and she contracted Covid around the same time as her son was hospitalized in New Orleans. She receives social security disability benefits, which aren\u8217\'20\'19t enough to survive on, and limits her options for new work.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy job is still not available. I\u8217\'20\'19ve looked a little bit even though I\u8217\'20\'19m not ready to go into the workforce because of my fears of Covid. I\u8217\'20\'19m high risk and so is my partner,\u8221\'20\'1D said Leeson. \u8220\'20\'1CI know at this point I don\u8217\'20\'19t have a lot of options. Who is going to want someone who is disabled and 57?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Leeson\u8217\'20\'19s unemployment benefits were cut off in September 2021 when federal extended unemployment benefits ended without any further extensions, after it took her months to start receiving benefits due to long delays and backlogs. She has gone from having zero debt to accumulating about $10,000 in credit card debt in order to make ends meet over the past several months.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI barely leave my home, so I don\u8217\'20\'19t have gas and car expenses. We eat cheaply. I\u8217\'20\'19ve lost 5lbs mainly by eating just a sandwich for dinner most nights,\u8221\'20\'1D added Leeson. \u8220\'20\'1CI made it through the holidays, but I\u8217\'20\'19m really scared about what comes next.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This article was written with the support of a journalism fellowship from the \uc2 Gerontological Society of America\uc2 , the Journalists Network on Generations and the RRF Foundation for Aging.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220125ei1p001jo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
