\par\par \uc2 Reimagining work\par \b \uc2 How businesses can put productivity \u8211\'20\'13 and people \u8211\'20\'13 first\b0\par\par\uc2 Steve Smethurst \par \uc2 967 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 January 2022\par 16:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Companies are adopting new technology at a rapid pace \u8211\'20\'13 but many employees are jumping ship. Here\u8217\'20\'19s how to build a successful company while supporting your workforce at the same time\par \par \uc2 Right now the world is reeling from the effects of a \u8220\'20\'1Cgreat resignation\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 an economic trend in which employees decide to resign from their jobs. In the US, an estimated {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/11/12/a-record-4point4-million-people-quit-jobs-in-september-great-resignation.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 24 million}}}\uc2  people quit between April and September last year.\par \par \uc2 Companies are now seeking ways to address this issue. \uc2 Nike\uc2 , for instance, gave its head office staff a free week off in August in order to \u8220\'20\'1Cdestress\u8221\'20\'1D. \uc2 Matt Marrazzo\uc2 , senior manager of global marketing at Nike, said: \u8220\'20\'1COur senior leaders are sending a clear message: take the time to unwind, destress and spend time with your loved ones. Do not work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, \u8220\'20\'1CDo not work\u8221\'20\'1D, isn\u8217\'20\'19t expected to be a long-term strategy. So successful businesses need to know what\u8217\'20\'19s putting a strain on productivity and how to combat it.\par \par \uc2 The curse of work about work\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s somewhat ironic that people are working longer hours, spending more time communicating in meetings and yet being less productive. For the average worker, just 26% of their time is spent on the skilled work they were hired for and 14% on planning or strategy, according to research from the work management platform Asana.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the curses of the modern workplace is activities that take time away from meaningful work,\u8221\'20\'1D says Simon O\u8217\'20\'19Kane, head of international at Asana. \u8220\'20\'1CThis includes communicating about work, searching for information, switching between apps, managing shifting priorities, or following up on the status of work. None of which is the actual skilled work you were hired to do.\u8221\'20\'1D Asana\u8217\'20\'19s study found that 60% of a person\u8217\'20\'19s time at work is spent on this \u8220\'20\'1Cwork about work\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s also the duplication of work. Most work today involves more than one team or department. Yet the way many companies are structured doesn\u8217\'20\'19t account for this. Departments such as marketing, sales and finance may have shared missions and objectives, but the work they do often takes place in silos. This lack of coordination and transparency can lead to duplicated efforts. For example, both sales and marketing might create a report on the impact of advertising.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHours can be spent gathering the same data, undertaking similar analyses and building near identical presentations,\u8221\'20\'1D says O\u8217\'20\'19Kane. Indeed, Asana research suggests that employees are losing 236 hours a year to duplication of efforts, a 27-hour increase from 2019.\par \par \uc2 Why app switching drains the brain\par \par \uc2 One challenge that hampers productivity is \u8220\'20\'1Ccontext switching\u8221\'20\'1D. On average, people swap between 10 apps 25 times a day to do their work. How many times do we stop working on a project to respond to an email or to take a call? It disrupts focus and drains mental energy.\par \par \uc2 It might be described as \u8220\'20\'1Cmultitasking\u8221\'20\'1D, but Dr Sahar Yousef, a leading expert on productivity and cognitive neuroscientist at the \uc2 University of California, Berkeley\u8217\'20\'19s Haas School of Business\uc2 , argues that the concept of multitasking is, in fact, a myth. \u8220\'20\'1CIn essence, it\u8217\'20\'19s rapidly switching from one task to another, and then back again,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CUnfortunately, every time you make that switch, you pay a \u8216\'20\'18tax\u8217\'20\'19 on both your time and your energy. For that reason, it\u8217\'20\'19s almost always more efficient to focus on one thing and move on when you\u8217\'20\'19re done. Otherwise, every time we switch, it\u8217\'20\'19s draining and it takes longer to complete a task.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CApp overload and the context-switching that it fuels present real risks to businesses,\u8221\'20\'1D says O\u8217\'20\'19Kane. \u8220\'20\'1CAs digital transformations continue, business leaders need to think carefully about the apps they are investing in and how this will impact employees. Blocking out time for set tasks, taking advantage of notification controls and making it easier for people to quickly find what they need can help limit the impact of app overload and context switching.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wellbeing and the workplace\par \par \uc2 According to the survey by Asana \u8211\'20\'13 which studied more than 13,000 knowledge workers in Australia, New Zealand, France, Germany, Japan, Singapore, the UK and the US \u8211\'20\'13 the root causes of burnout are related to how work is being managed. Overworking, a lack of clarity on tasks and roles, and an inability to disconnect from work were the top factors fuelling burnout.\par \par \uc2 Asana\u8217\'20\'19s research found that workers believe that having the right technology will enable people to work efficiently. Flexibility on working hours and clarity over priorities and work processes are also essential for workers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s up to business leaders to deliver on these demands,\u8221\'20\'1D says O\u8217\'20\'19Kane. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile it can be challenging to understand where to start, leaders can begin by recognising each of these priorities is linked.\par \par \uc2 Tools like Asana allow organisations to streamline work-management processes, enabling teams to work together efficiently, rather than adding a layer of complexity. For example, by keeping all deadlines and updates in a single space, Asana can remove the need for unnecessary status meetings or multiple emails simply to update on a project\u8217\'20\'19s progress.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 However, tackling these challenges also requires a mindset shift. \u8220\'20\'1CNo app can be a silver bullet,\u8221\'20\'1D says O\u8217\'20\'19Kane. \u8220\'20\'1CManagers also need to give teams channels to provide feedback on workloads and pressure points.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Technology has a role to play, but providing clear expectations on roles and responsibilities and finding time to shout about success are also key to creating a culture in which no one is at risk of burning out.\par \par \uc2 Find out more about how companies, teams and individuals can thrive in the year ahead by taking a look at Asana\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://resources.asana.com/TheGuardian-AoW-UK-Report.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Anatomy of Work Index 2021}}}\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220113ei1d0030e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
