\par\par \uc2 World news\par \b \uc2 Record numbers admitted to US hospitals with coronavirus; Sweden to introduce stricter curbs \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Samantha Lock (now); Jedidajah Otte , Damien Gayle and Tom Ambrose (earlier) \par \uc2 15,520 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 January 2022\par 01:12 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Over 132,000 patients currently on US wards with Covid; Swedish measures include work from home mandateEmail shows \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  aide invited No 10 staff to lockdown partyNovak Djokovic wins appeal against decision to cancel Australian visaProtesters on French island pelt MP with seaweed over Covid passChina battles Omicron outbreak weeks before Winter OlympicsNew Zealand not prepared for Omicron outbreak in \u8216\'20\'18matter of weeks\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 Thanks for following along \u8211\'20\'13 this blog is now closed. You can catch up with the latest coronavirus coverage {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/coronavirus-outbreak"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 We have also launched a new live news feed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/jan/11/covid-news-live-pfizer-says-omicron-specific-vaccine-could-be-available-by-march-mexico-president-tests-positive-again"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  with all the current developments.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 Summary of key developments\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a quick rundown of all the latest headlines:\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  was accused on Monday night of an \u8220\'20\'1Cutterly outrageous\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/email-shows-boris-johnsons-official-invited-no-10-staff-to-lockdown-byob-party"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 breach of lockdown rules as a leaked email s}}}\uc2  howed one of his top officials invited more than 100 Downing Street staff to a \u8220\'20\'1Cbring your own booze\u8221\'20\'1D party during the first lockdown. The police are now investigating.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has ordered officials to examine plans to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/10/pm-orders-officials-to-look-at-cutting-covid-isolation-period-to-five-days"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cut Covid isolation to five days}}}\uc2  in England as scientists urged caution over the lack of evidence to support a change.\par \par \uc2 * {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/nhs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NHS}}}\uc2 England has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/nhs-england-left-with-no-choice-but-turn-to-private-hospitals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 struck a deal with private healthcare providers }}}\uc2  under which their hospitals will be ready to start treating \uc2 NHS\uc2  patients who cannot get the Covid or non-Covid care they need because their local \uc2 NHS\uc2  hospital is under too much pressure.\par \par \uc2 * Pfizer Inc chief executive \uc2 Albert Bourla\uc2  said on Monday that moving toward a redesigned Covid-19 vaccine that is specifically targeted to combat the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/omicron"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Omicron variant}}}\uc2  is the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost likely scenario.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * The small number of people in Italy\u8217\'20\'19s population who refuse to be vaccinated against Covid-19 are largely responsible for the continued health crisis, prime minister \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 * In the US, a new record has been set for the number of people admitted to hospital with Covid-19, with 132,646 currently on wards, according to Reuters. The latest figure, which comes as the highly contagious Omicron strain spreads across the country, surpassed the record of 132,051 set in January last year.\par \par \uc2 * Moderna has announced it expects record sales of about $18.5bn (\u163\'00\'A313.6bn) in contracts for its Covid vaccine in 2022.\par \par \uc2 * Novak Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s brother reportedly ended a press conference after questions about why the tennis ace appeared in public in Belgrade a day after he had tested positive for Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  has condemned protesters on the French overseas territory of Saint Pierre and Miquelon who pelted an MP with seaweed and dirt during a protest against vaccine passes.\par \par \uc2 * Stricter pandemic measures are to be introduced in Sweden in response to a rising number of Covid cases and pressure on hospitals, the prime minister has said.\par \par \uc2 * The prime minister of Spain, Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez, has called for European officials to consider ditching detailed pandemic tracking for Covid in favour of a flu-like monitoring system. The change would mean treating Covid-19 as an \u8220\'20\'1Cendemic illness\u8221\'20\'1D rather than a pandemic, S\u225\'00\'E1nchez said on Monday. He pointed out that deaths as a proportion of recorded cases has fallen since the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 France eases Covid rules for schools as infections soar\par \par \uc2 France has announced an easing of Covid rules for schools as record-high case numbers shut down thousands of classes and sparked concern among parents and teachers.\par \par \uc2 Prime minister \uc2 Jean Castex\uc2  told France 2 television on Monday that more than 10,000 classes - 2% of the total - had to be cancelled because of coronavirus outbreaks, but that the government would not \u8220\'20\'1Cshut down the schools or the country\u8221\'20\'1D, Agence France-Presse reports.\par \par \uc2 France on Monday reported more than 125,000 new coronavirus cases as the Omicron variant drives up daily infections to record highs.\par \par \uc2 French prime minister \uc2 Jean Castex\uc2  said that more than 10,000 classes - 2% of the total - had to be cancelled because of coronavirus outbreaks. Photograph: SYSPEO/SIPA/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 Under the first change, from Tuesday, parents will no longer be obliged to pick up their child immediately for Covid testing if he or she is a contact case of a virus sufferer.\par \par \uc2 Home-testing will be deemed sufficient in such cases rather than testing at an officially approved site, with the parents signing a certificate to confirm the result.\par \par \uc2 The test kits, available from pharmacies, will be free.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Peru reported an all-time high 70,000 Covid-19 infections in the first week of January, a health official told reporters on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Dante Cersso, a government health official, said the new weekly case count had exceeded the previous record of 67,107 cases during the second week of April of last year.\par \par \uc2 At the time, Peru was going through a brutal second wave that left the country with the world\u8217\'20\'19s worst per-capita death rate, according to \uc2 Johns Hopkins University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 About 0.5% of Peru\u8217\'20\'19s population has died of Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Death counts have not spiked with the recent surge in cases, according to data from \uc2 Peru\u8217\'20\'19s Ministry of Health\uc2 , Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 More than 65% of Peru\u8217\'20\'19s population has received two doses of a Covid-19 vaccine, according to Our World in Data. Peru is also offering booster shots to all adults three months after receiving their second shot.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Samantha Lock back with you on the blog reporting to you from Sydney.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a quick snapshot of how Covid is unfolding across Australia.\par \par \uc2 Victoria recorded 37,944 new Covid-19 cases and 13 deaths while NSW recorded 25,870 new Covid-19 cases and 11 deaths.\par \par \uc2 Novak Djokovic yesterday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2022/jan/10/novak-djokovic-appeal-federal-circuit-court-cancellation-australian-visa-covid-vaccination-live-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 won an appeal}}}\uc2  against his visa cancellation after spending four days in an immigration detention centre.\par \par \uc2 A wild 24 hours saw ecstatic Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s fans pepper-sprayed by police as they celebrated his release in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/melbourne"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Melbourne}}}\uc2 . But the victory comes with the threat that immigration minister \uc2 Alex Hawke\uc2  may revoke his entry visa for a second time, using powers granted by Australia\u8217\'20\'19s Migration Act. Djokovic said he was \u8220\'20\'1Cpleased\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cgrateful\u8221\'20\'1D for the ruling, and was still determined to stay and compete at the Australian Open.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Australia passed the grim milestone of 1m active Covid-19 cases yesterday. There have been 250,000 Covid cases since Friday alone.\par \par \uc2 default\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Brazil reported 34,788 new cases in the past 24 hours and 110 deaths from Covid-19, the health ministry said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Infections are increasing in Brazil, with 32,954 new infections reported on average each day. According to the Reuters Covid-19 Tracker that is 43% of the country\u8217\'20\'19s peak, the highest daily average reported on 23 June 2021.\par \par \uc2 The South American country has now registered 22,558,695 cases since the pandemic began, while the official death toll has risen to 620,091, according to ministry data.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Israeli foreign minister \uc2 Yair Lapid\uc2  has tested positive for Covid-19, Israel\u8217\'20\'19s Ynet news website reported on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Lapid was in quarantine at home and was feeling well, the website reported.\par \par \uc2 In a tweet, the minister said: \u8220\'20\'1CI feel great because I\u8217\'20\'19m vaccinated. Go get vaccinated, put on a mask, [and] we\u8217\'20\'19ll get through this together.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several Israeli lawmakers have been infected with the virus over the past week, Haaretz reports, as the Omicron variant drives a surge in cases in the country.\par \par \uc2 Lapid\u8217\'20\'19s deputy, Idal Rol l, is also in isolation after having tested positive last week. He was confirmed to have contracted the virus a few days after seen in footage at a New Year\u8217\'20\'19s Eve party without a mask.\par \par \uc2 Yair Lapid\uc2  at the Knesset, Israel\u8217\'20\'19s parliament, in Jerusalem on 31 May, 2021. Photograph: Reuters\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 From delayed ambulances to police shortages, Canadian public agencies hit hard by Covid-19 worker absences have cut back on service, rearranged staff or warned the public that emergency responses may be disrupted.\par \par \uc2 Reuters reports:\par \par \uc2 Over the weekend, paramedics in Toronto, Canada\u8217\'20\'19s largest city, said there were briefly no ambulances available to respond to emergencies.\par \par \uc2 The city said about 12.8% of its \u8220\'20\'1Cessential and critical services\u8221\'20\'1D staff were off due to Covid-19 as of Monday.\par \par \uc2 It is not unusual for ambulances to be tied up at any moment, city spokesperson Brad Ross said. But with workers sidelined by Covid-19 and stretched hospitals delaying ambulance offloading, it was becoming more common.\par \par \uc2 Low-priority calls may be put off and some will go to the fire department, he said.\par \par \uc2 Like much of the rest of the world, Canada is swamped by the fast-spreading Omicron variant. Earlier this month Canada broke its previous one-day record for the highest number of people hospitalised with Covid-19, at more than 4,100 nationally.\par \par \uc2 Ontario regional transit operator Metrolinx cut its service by 15% as 20-30% of its staff were off work due to Cocid-19, spokesperson \uc2 Anne Marie Aikins\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 About 9% of Winnipeg Police Service staff were on leave due to Covid-19, and the police service said it had reallocated officers from specialised units such as gang enforcement to street patrol.\par \par \uc2 The Atlantic city of Halifax cancelled bus and ferry trips due to Covid-19 absences.\par \par \uc2 A row of ambulances is seen outside a hospital in Montreal, on Monday, 10 January, 2022. Photograph: Canadian Press/REX/\uc2 Shutterstock\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.43pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Redesigned vaccine specifically targeting Omicron 'most likely scenario', \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  chief says\par \par \uc2 Pfizer Inc chief executive \uc2 Albert Bourla\uc2  said on Monday that moving toward a redesigned Covid-19 vaccine that is specifically targeted to combat the Omicron variant is the \u8220\'20\'1Cmost likely scenario.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Speaking at the J.P. Morgan Healthcare Conference, Bourla said \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  and partner \uc2 BioNTech SE\uc2  are working on both an Omicron-targeted vaccine variant as well as a shot that would include both the previous vaccine as well as vaccine targeted at the Omicron variant, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 Bourla said the company could be ready to file for approval for a redesigned vaccine and start producing it as soon as March.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 People who have recovered from Covid in Germany will in future only be able to prove their status digitally with a QR code, the health ministers of the country\u8217\'20\'19s federal states decided on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The government\u8217\'20\'19s health minister \uc2 Karl Lauterbach\uc2  has promised to implement this promptly, according to Saxony-Anhalt\u8217\'20\'19s health minister and current chairwoman of the conference of health ministers Petra Grimm-Benne.\par \par \uc2 The federal ministry of health is to create a corresponding legal basis \u8220\'20\'1Cwith which the obligation to exclusively present a digitally readable health record\u8221\'20\'1D becomes possible for access controls, for example for events or in restaurants. People would be able to store the relevant evidence in the country\u8217\'20\'19s Covid warning app, the German press agency DPA reports.\par \par \uc2 At their first regular conference this year, the health ministers also discussed a possible fourth vaccine as is currently being distributed in the Israeli population. Grimm-Benne said further scientific research was necessary before a decision can be made.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 White House spokesperson \uc2 Jen Psaki\uc2  said Americans should be able to order free, rapid Covid tests through a federal web site later this month.\par \par \uc2 The White House is working closely with test manufacturers and expects to have all procurement contracts signed in the next two weeks, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 The White House has pledged to make 500 million rapid, Covid tests available to all Americans in January.\par \par \uc2 People wait to receive a test for Covid-19 on Capitol Hill on 10 January, 2022, in Washington, DC. Photograph: Brendan Smialowski/AFP/Getty Images\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.36pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The Covid 19 Bereaved Families for Justice campaign have described \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s private secretary {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/email-shows-boris-johnsons-official-invited-no-10-staff-to-lockdown-byob-party"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 inviting over 100 Downing Street officials to a gathering in Number 10\u8217\'20\'19s back garden}}}\uc2  while the UK was still in lockdown as \u8220\'20\'1Ctruly beyond belief\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Hannah Brady, a spokesperson for the group, said:\par \par \uc2 My Dad died just four days before this email was sent out; he was only 55 and was a fit and healthy key worker. I\u8217\'20\'19ve missed him every day since. Just like the rest of the country, my family had done everything we could to keep him and others safe during the lockdown.\par \par \uc2 Those days will stay with me for the rest of my life, just like the families of the 353 people that died that day. My family couldn\u8217\'20\'19t even get a hug from our friends. To think that whilst it was happening \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  was making the \u8216\'20\'18most of the weather\u8217\'20\'19 and throwing a party for 100 people, is truly beyond belief.\par \par \uc2 Brady was one of five families involved in the campaign {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.gov.uk/government/news/pm-meeting-with-representatives-of-bereaved-families-4-justice-28-september-2021"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 who met the prime minister last September}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 She added:\par \par \uc2 At the time, everyone would have known that going to a party was wrong, so how can those running the country have thought it was OK? Could there be a more disgraceful example of \u8216\'20\'18one rule for them, and another rule for the rest of us\u8217\'20\'19?\par \par \uc2 To make matters worse, in September last year I sat in that same garden, looked the prime minister in the eyes and told him how my dad had died. He told me he had \u8216\'20\'18done everything he could\u8217\'20\'19 to protect my Dad, knowing that he had partied in that same spot the very day that Dad\u8217\'20\'19s death certificate was signed. It makes me feel sick to think about it.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The small number of people in Italy\u8217\'20\'19s population who refuse to be vaccinated against Covid-19 are largely responsible for the continued health crisis, prime minister \uc2 Mario Draghi\uc2  said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The government last week made vaccinations mandatory for everyone aged 50 and over, one of very few European countries to take such a step, in an attempt to ease pressure on hospitals as new cases surge, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 Draghi told a news conference:\par \par \uc2 We must never lose sight of the fact that most of the problems we have today are because there are non-vaccinated people.\par \par \uc2 For the umpteenth time, I invite all those Italians who are not yet vaccinated to do so, and to get the third shot.\par \par \uc2 Health minister \uc2 Roberto Speranza\uc2  said 89.4% of all those aged 12 and over had received at least one vaccine dose, adding that the unvaccinated accounted for two-thirds of all the Covid patients in intensive care units.\par \par \uc2 Latest data released on Monday showed there were 1,606 people in intensive care with the virus, while the country reported 101,762 new cases and 227 additional deaths over the past 24 hours.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, unvaccinated people were banned from entering bars and restaurants and from using public transport, in a further tightening of restrictions for this part of the population.\par \par \uc2 Only those who have recently recovered from Covid will be exempted from the new rule.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.08pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Hello, I\u8217\'20\'19m Jedidajah Otte and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be taking over for the next few hours. If there\u8217\'20\'19s anything you\u8217\'20\'19d like to flag, feel free to get in touch, I\u8217\'20\'19m on Twitter {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JedySays"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @JedySays}}}\uc2  or you can {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:jedidajah.otte.casual@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 email me}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Summary\par \par \uc2 Latest coronavirus-related headlines and developments from around the world include:\par \par \uc2 * One of the UK prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s top officials invited staff to \u8220\'20\'1Csocially distanced drinks in the No 10 garden\u8221\'20\'1D during May of the first lockdown. The email, from Martin Reynolds, the principal private secretary to \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , invited just over 100 employees in No 10 at a time when social mixing was banned.\par \par \uc2 * A new record has been set for the number of people admitted to hospital with Covid-19 in the US, with 132,646 currently on wards, according to Reuters. The latest figure, which comes as the highly contagious Omicron strain spreads across the country, surpassed the record of 132,051 set in January last year.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is already manufacturing a Covid vaccine targeting the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/omicron"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Omicron variant}}}\uc2 , which it expects to be ready to distribute by June, its chief executive, \uc2 Albert Bourla\uc2 , has said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis vaccine will be ready in March,\u8221\'20\'1D Bourla told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/01/10/covid-vaccine-pfizer-ceo-says-omicron-vaccine-will-be-ready-in-march.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNBC}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe [are] already starting manufacturing some of these quantities at risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 * Moderna has announced it expects record sales of about $18.5bn (\u163\'00\'A313.6bn) in contracts for its Covid vaccine in 2022. The US vaccine maker said it could also make about $3.5bn from potential additional purchases, including booster candidates updated for variants. The company in November said its sales could be in the range of $17bn to $22bn in 2022, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 * Novak Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s brother reportedly ended a press conference after questions about why the tennis ace appeared in public in Belgrade a day after he had tested positive for Covid-19. Djokovic has said he had Covid in December to justify an exemption allowing him to visit Australia for the Australian Open without taking a Covid vaccine.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  has condemned protesters on the French overseas territory of Saint Pierre and Miquelon who pelted an MP with seaweed and dirt during a protest against vaccine passes. Stephane Claireaux, an MP from from Macron\u8217\'20\'19s ruling party, was confronted on Sunday at his home on the windswept territory off the Canadian island of Newfoundland.\par \par \uc2 * Stricter pandemic measures are to be introduced in Sweden in response to a rising number of Covid cases and pressure on hospitals, the prime minister has said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe situation has deteriorated, without doubt. The level of infections in Sweden is at a historically high level,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Magdalena Andersson\uc2  told a news conference, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 * The prime minister of Spain, Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez, has called for European officials to consider ditching detailed pandemic tracking for Covid in favour of a flu-like monitoring system. The change would mean treating Covid-19 as an \u8220\'20\'1Cendemic illness\u8221\'20\'1D rather than a pandemic, S\u225\'00\'E1nchez said on Monday. He pointed out that deaths as a proportion of recorded cases has fallen since the pandemic began.\par \par \uc2 Interested to read about what happened earlier? Take a look at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2022/jan/10/covid-live-news-40-of-israel-could-be-infected-in-current-wave-germany-to-study-rapid-antigen-test-reliability-for-omicron?page=with:block-61dc0e4a8f0813d917539253#block-61dc0e4a8f0813d917539253"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 our last summary of headlines}}}\uc2 , posted eight hours ago.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from me, Damien Gayle, for today. Ciao!\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 One of the UK prime minister\u8217\'20\'19s top officials invited staff to \u8220\'20\'1Csocially distanced drinks in the No 10 garden\u8221\'20\'1D during May of the first lockdown, a leaked email shows, writes Rowena Mason, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s deputy political editor.\par \par \uc2 The email, from Martin Reynolds, the principal private secretary to \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2 , invited just over 100 employees in No 10 at a time when social mixing was banned apart from with one other person from another household outdoors.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.itv.com/news/2022-01-10/email-proves-downing-street-staff-held-drinks-party-at-height-of-lockdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to ITV News}}}\uc2 , it said: \u8220\'20\'1CHi all, after what has been an incredibly busy period it would be nice to make the most of the lovely weather and have some socially distanced drinks in the No 10 garden this evening. Please join us from 6pm and bring your own booze!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/email-shows-boris-johnsons-official-invited-no-10-staff-to-lockdown-byob-party"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Email shows Boris Johnson aide invited No 10 staff to lockdown \u8216\'20\'18BYOB\u8217\'20\'19 party}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Omicron has become the dominant strain of coronavirus in the Czech Republic, the country\u8217\'20\'19s public health institute has said.\par \par \uc2 According to the institute, Omicron had accounted for more than 50% of positive tests as of 8 January, with samples from mainly big cities on 9 January showing 79% of Covid-19 cases were the Omicron variant, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 So far the pick-up in Omicron cases has not raised daily infection numbers significantly and the number of hospitalised people continues to decline as the previous wave of infections recedes. There were 2,229 people in hospital on Sunday, down form more than 7,000 in early December.\par \par \uc2 In an effort to reduce the impact of rising numbers having to self-isolate, the government has been planning to allow people in critical professions, including the emergency services and workers in the health and energy sectors, to continue to work work even after a positive lateral flow test.\par \par \uc2 It has also cut the length of quarantine and isolation to five days for people without symptoms.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.16pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Record numbers of people admitted to US hospitals with Covid\par \par \uc2 A new record has been set for the number of people admitted to hospital with Covid-19 in the US, with 132,646 currently on wards, according to a Reuters tally on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The latest figure, which comes as the highly contagious Omicron strain spreads across the country, surpassed the record of 132,051 set in January last year.\par \par \uc2 Hospital admissions have increased steadily since late-December, doubling in the last three weeks, as Omicron overtook Delta as the dominant variant in the US.\par \par \uc2 The analysis found that Delaware, Illinois, Maine, Maryland, Missouri, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Puerto Rico, the U.S. Virgin Islands, Vermont, Virginia, Washington DC, and Wisconsin have reported record levels of Covid-19 patients recently.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.13pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A major UK broadcaster has been warned to take \u8220\'20\'1Cgreater care\u8221\'20\'1D after a medical expert falsely claimed on air that 90% of Covid patients in hospital had not been vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 The claim by Dr Hilary Jones during a discussion on the \uc2 ITV\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s morning magazine programme Lorraine about the number of unvaccinated Covid-19 patients in hospital prompted 3,833 complaints from viewers.\par \par \uc2 During the segment, broadcast on 6 December, Jones and the programme\u8217\'20\'19s eponymous presenter, Lorraine Kelly, had been encouraging viewers to accept the offer of a coronavirus vaccination. \u8220\'20\'1CThose people who haven\u8217\'20\'19t been vaccinated, we\u8217\'20\'19d really love you to think again and be vaccinated because 90% of people in hospital are unvaccinated right now with Covid,\u8221\'20\'1D Jones said.\par \par \uc2 A clarification was broadcast on the show two days later, explaining the statistic related to patients in intensive care units rather than the proportion of unvaccinated Covid-19 patients in hospital.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, \uc2 Ofcom\uc2 , the media watchdog, said it would not be launching a formal investigation but have issued \uc2 ITV\uc2  with guidance.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, \uc2 Ofcom\uc2  said: \u8220\'20\'1CThis programme incorrectly referenced the proportion of unvaccinated Covid-19 patients in hospital. We have told \uc2 ITV\uc2  that greater care should be taken by trusted medical experts when presenting facts and figures on public health issues.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHowever, given official statistics and research have consistently shown that vaccination against Covid-19 offers greater protection against serious health consequences, we do not consider that the error was sufficient to materially mislead viewers on this main point of discussion.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.49pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Seventy-seven more people have died within 28 days catching Covid in the UK, as 142,224 more people tested positive for the virus, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://coronavirus.data.gov.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the latest statistics}}}\uc2  from the government.\par \par \uc2 The seven-day figure for the number of deaths (1,330) was up 49.8%, but for infections (1,201,563) it was up 1%.\par \par \uc2 The number of patients admitted to hospital was also on the rise, with the 15,890 over a seven-day period announced on Monday, up 43.3% on previous week.\par \par \uc2 Just more than nine in 10 people in the country had received at least one dose of a coronavirus vaccine, according to the government\u8217\'20\'19s data, with 83% having had a second dose and 62% having a third or booster dose.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 This post was amended on 11 January 2022. The seven-day figure for infections was up 1%, not down 1% as an earlier version said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Covid-19 spurred wealthy motorists to buy more Rolls-Royces than ever before because it made them realise life was short, the luxury carmaker has said, writes Rob Davies for \uc2 the Guardian\uc2  business desk.\par \par \uc2 As global cases escalated in 2021, \uc2 Rolls-Royce Motor Cars\uc2 , based in Goodwood, West Sussex, booked the highest annual sales in its 117-year history, selling 5,586 vehicles.\par \par \uc2 The company\u8217\'20\'19s chief executive, Torsten M\u252\'00\'FCller-\u214\'00\'D6tv\u246\'00\'F6s, said the pandemic had led to customers, whose average age was 43, responding to the reminder of their own mortality by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2020/jan/12/bling-up-my-roller-inside-the-new-breed-of-bespoke-rolls-royce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 splashing out on luxury cars}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMany people witnessed people in their community dying from Covid and that made them think life can be short and you\u8217\'20\'19d better live now rather than postpone until a later date,\u8221\'20\'1D said M\u252\'00\'FCller-\u214\'00\'D6tv\u246\'00\'F6s.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat has helped \uc2 Rolls-Royce\uc2 .\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jan/10/rolls-royce-covid-has-spurred-record-sales-of-our-cars"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rolls-Royce: Covid has spurred record sales of our cars}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.59pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A million people in India received a third dose of coronavirus vaccine on Monday, as the country rolled out boosters.\par \par \uc2 Amid an eightfold rise in infections in 10 days, the government sent booster reminders to more than 10 million people who took a second dose of Covaxin or Covishield nine months ago.\par \par \uc2 A nurse prepares to inject a dose of Covid vaccine in Srinagar, the summer capital of Indian Kashmir. Photograph: Farooq Khan/EPA\par \par \uc2 Only healthcare personnel, other frontline workers and people over 60 and suffering from other health conditions were eligible for what the government called a \u8220\'20\'1Cprecaution dose\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 India has administered more than 1.5bn vaccine doses in total, among a population of 1.4bn. About 68% of the country\u8217\'20\'19s 939 million adults have had two doses.\par \par \uc2 There were 146 deaths reported on Monday, bringing the toll to 483,936 since the pandemic began in early 2020. Only the US and Brazil have recorded more deaths.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.22pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Healthcare is a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral obligation\u8221\'20\'1D, the pope has said, as he denounced the \u8220\'20\'1Cbaseless information\u8221\'20\'1D on which people are deciding to decline covid vaccines.\par \par \uc2 In what the Associated Press described as his strongest words yet calling for people to take vaccines, \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  said individuals had a responsibility to care for themselves and that this translated into \u8220\'20\'1Crespect for the health of those around us\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHealth care is a moral obligation,\u8221\'20\'1D the agency quoted him as saying.\par \par \uc2 Francis\u8217\'20\'19s comments came during a speech to ambassadors accredited to the Holy See, an annual event in which he sets out the Vatican\u8217\'20\'19s foreign policy goals for the year.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  meets diplomats accredited to the Holy See. Photograph: Vatican Media Handout/EPA\par \par \uc2 Ideological divides were discouraging people from getting vaccinated, the pope said. \u8220\'20\'1CFrequently people let themselves be influenced by the ideology of the moment, often bolstered by baseless information or poorly documented facts,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, calling for the adoption of a \u8220\'20\'1Creality therapy\u8221\'20\'1D to correct this distortion.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CVaccines are not a magical means of healing, yet surely they represent, in addition to other treatments that need to be developed, the most reasonable solution for the prevention of the disease,\u8221\'20\'1D he added.\par \par \uc2 Some Catholics, including some conservative US bishops and cardinals, have claimed vaccines based on research that used cells derived from aborted fetuses were immoral, and have refused to take them.\par \par \uc2 The Vatican\u8217\'20\'19s doctrine office, however, has said it is \u8220\'20\'1Cmorally acceptable\u8221\'20\'1D for Catholics to receive Covid-19 vaccines based on research that used cells derived from aborted fetuses.\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  and Emeritus Pope \uc2 Benedict XVI\uc2  have been fully vaccinated with \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 -BioNTech shots, according to the Vatican.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  already manufacturing Omicron vaccine, says CEO\par \par \uc2 Pfizer\uc2  is already manufacturing a Covid vaccine targeting the Omicron variant, which it expects to be ready to distribute by June, its chief executive, \uc2 Albert Bourla\uc2 , has said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis vaccine will be ready in March,\u8221\'20\'1D Bourla told {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2022/01/10/covid-vaccine-pfizer-ceo-says-omicron-vaccine-will-be-ready-in-march.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CNBC}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWe [are] already starting manufacturing some of these quantities at risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The need for a Omicron-specific coronavirus vaccine has been questioned, not least by \uc2 Anthony Fauci\uc2 , the White House medical adviser, who has said booster doses of existing vaccines are sufficient to counter the variant.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Bourla said there were governments who wanted an updated vaccine as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 Bourla said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe hope is that we will achieve something that will have way, way better protection particularly against infections, because the protection against the hospitalisations and the severe disease \u8211\'20\'13 it is reasonable right now, with the current vaccines as long as you are having let\u8217\'20\'19s say the third dose.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.28pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.39pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The president of Mexico has told anyone in the country who has to symptoms to just assume they have Covid, as the number of confirmed cases jumped by 186% last week amid a scarcity of tests.\par \par \uc2 Noting hospitalisations and deaths had not increased at the same rate, \uc2 Andr\u233\'00\'E9s Manuel L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\uc2  claimed the Omicron variant was \u8220\'20\'1Ca little Covid\u8221\'20\'1D. He advised Mexicans with symptoms to stay at home, take paracetamol and isolate, rather than going out and trying to find tests.\par \par \uc2 L\u243\'00\'F3pez Obrador\u8217\'20\'19s administration has not implemented mass testing, saying it a waste of money. He called on companies not to require Covid tests for employees.\par \par \uc2 Mexico passed 300,000 test-confirmed coronavirus deaths last week, but so little testing is done in the country of 126 million that a government review of death certificates puts the real toll at almost 460,000.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Kim Willsher, the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s Paris correspondent, has now filed on the pelting of a French MP with seaweed.\par \par \uc2 Protesters opposed to Covid regulations pelted a French MP with seaweed and stones outside his home on the overseas territory of St-Pierre-et-Miquelon at the weekend, Willsher writes.\par \par \uc2 St\u233\'00\'E9phane Claireaux, a member of the governing La R\u233\'00\'E9publique en Marche (LREM), said he had made an official police complaint after the attack, which fellow MPs described as a \u8220\'20\'1Clynching\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Claireaux said he had gone outside to speak to the protesters who were angry about the decision by local authorities to impose the \u8220\'20\'1Chealth pass\u8221\'20\'1D. When his wife joined him she was also attacked, he said.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/10/protesters-pelt-french-island-mp-covid-health-pass-saint-pierre-and-miquelon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Protesters pelt French island MP over Covid health pass}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 5.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 2.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Novak Djokovic \u8217\'20\'19s brother has reportedly ended a press conference after questions about why the tennis ace appeared in public in Belgrade a day after he had tested positive for Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Djokovic has said he had Covid in December to justify an exemption allowing him to visit Australia for the Australian Open without taking a Covid vaccine. He spent several days in immigration detention after his visa was cancelled at the Australian border.\par \par \uc2 According to documents released on Saturday, as part of a legal challenge, Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s Covid-19 infection was recorded by the Institute of Public Health of Serbia on 16 December.\par \par \uc2 It subsequently emerged that also on 16 December, Djokovic attended an event {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/p/CXlyLRpAPsu/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 commemorating his own personal stamp}}}\uc2  in the Serbian capital. Pictures were also posted of Djokovic a day later at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/teniskisavezbeograda/photos/a.4866662756730218/4866655583397602/?type=3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennis Association of Belgrade for an award ceremony}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 On Monday, a judge quashed the visa cancellation and ordered the Australian government to pay legal costs and release the Serbian from detention within half an hour.\par \par \uc2 When asked whether Novak was COVID-19 positive while at events in Belgrade, his brother ended the press conference.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Sally Gyte (@SallyGyte) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SallyGyte/status/1480534131393978369?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 10, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.50pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The UK prime minister has said the government is \u8220\'20\'1Clooking at the science\u8221\'20\'1D of moving to an isolation period of five days instead of seven for a positive Covid case, after calls to ease staffing shortages across the economy.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/10/ministers-considering-cutting-covid-isolation-to-five-days-says-boris-johnson"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ministers considering cutting Covid isolation to five days, says Boris Johnson}}}\par \par \uc2 1.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Guardian Sport has obtained a fascinating transcript of Novak Djokovi c\u8217\'20\'19s interview with an official when he was stopped while entering Australia for the Australian Open last week.\par \par \uc2 The exchanges at the airport when Djokovic landed in Melbourne on 6 January show how the world men\u8217\'20\'19s tennis No 1 contended with the cancellation of his visa.\par \par \uc2 INTERVIEWER: Are you vaccinated?\par \par \uc2 DJOKOVIC: I am not vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2022/jan/10/i-am-not-vaccinated-novak-djokovics-interview-with-australian-border-force"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I am not vaccinated\u8217\'20\'19: Novak Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s interview with Australian Border Force }}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.02pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2  has condemned protesters on the French overseas territory of Saint Pierre and Miquelon who pelted an MP with seaweed and dirt during a protest against vaccine passes.\par \par \uc2 Video circulating on social media showed Stephane Claireaux, an MP from from Macron\u8217\'20\'19s ruling party, confronted on Sunday at his home on the windswept territory off the Canadian island of Newfoundland.\par \par \uc2 L'agression de St\u233\'00\'E9phane Claireaux \u224\'00\'E0 son propre domicile lors de la manifestation contre le passe sanitaire est absolument inacceptable. Les images sont profond\u233\'00\'E9ment choquantes. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/S4ttQF2WTG"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/S4ttQF2WTG}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Annick Girardin\uc2  (@AnnickGirardin) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/AnnickGirardin/status/1480270903736942596?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 Tensions over health measures have risen in France since Macron last week said he planned to \u8220\'20\'1Cpiss off\u8221\'20\'1D the unvaccinated until they accepted shots.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, during a visit to Nice, in the south of France, he said the \u8220\'20\'1Cintolerable\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cunacceptable\u8221\'20\'1D incident was part of \u8220\'20\'1Can intensification of violence\u8221\'20\'1D against elected officials, according to the French news agency AFP.\par \par \uc2 The \u8220\'20\'1Cpasse sanitaire\u8221\'20\'1D (health pass) is due to be introduced in Saint Pierre and Miquelon shortly. It requires people to present proof of vaccination or a negative coronavirus test to enter public spaces like bars.\par \par \uc2 Claireaux had earlier told the broadcaster France Info that he had been waiting for the demonstrators outside his home \u8220\'20\'1Cso as to talk to them\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a car loaded with seaweed \u8230\'20\'26 and people started chucking it at me. It was like being stoned. My wife came out to join me on the front step. I dodged a rock that missed our faces by fivecm,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Claireaux added that he would be filing criminal charges against those responsible.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are free to think that we\u8217\'20\'19re not making the right decisions. We\u8217\'20\'19re all getting death threats by email. At some point this has to stop,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Christophe Castaner\uc2 , leader of Macron\u8217\'20\'19s party in parliament, told France Inter radio there had been 322 threats against MPs in 2021 \u8211\'20\'13 two-thirds of them against the ruling party.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Moderna has announced it expects record sales of about $18.5bn (\u163\'00\'A313.6bn) in contracts for its Covid vaccine in 2022. The US vaccine maker said it could also make about $3.5bn from potential additional purchases, including booster candidates updated for variants.\par \par \uc2 The company in November said its sales could be in the range of $17bn to $22bn in 2022, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Moderna said it was in active discussions for more Covid-19 vaccine contracts this year and that it was developing a booster vaccine candidate, called mRNA-1273.529, to target Omicron variant\par \par \uc2 Booster doses of the company\u8217\'20\'19s current Covid-19 vaccine, mRNA-1273, increased neutralising antibody levels against Omicron using doses at 50 micrograms and 100 micrograms, Moderna said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 1.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Indian states have reserved more than double the Covid hospital beds for children than recommended by federal experts out of fear of being under-prepared, government data shows.\par \par \uc2 However, doctors say not many youngsters have needed critical care yet.\par \par \uc2 The country\u8217\'20\'19s health ministry also said on Monday that only 5% to 10% of all those people infected in India have sought hospitalisation, compared with 20% to 23% during the last major wave.\par \par \uc2 Authorities say most people have shown no or mild symptoms and have recovered quickly at home.\par \par \uc2 India reported 179,723 new cases on Monday, taking the total to 35.7 million, the highest in the world after the United States. Deaths rose by 146 to 483,936, only behind the tally of the United States and Brazil.\par \par \uc2 12.40pm GMT\par \par \uc2 A Covid spike has disrupted businesses in the Philippines, with banks, malls and airlines reducing operations and some schools suspending online classes due to staff sickness.\par \par \uc2 The Philippines reported a record 33,169 new coronavirus infections on Monday, bringing its overall tally close to three million as the Omicron variant takes its toll, with the overpopulated capital Manila and surrounding provinces worst hit.\par \par \uc2 Commercial airlines have cancelled more than a hundred domestic and international flights as cases surge, with airlines reporting infections among staff and lower demand due to uncertainty among travellers.\par \par \uc2 The Southeast Asian nation was gradually easing restrictions late last year as vaccination rates rose and infection rates fell, but authorities were last week forced to tighten mobility curbs to contain a rapid spread.\par \par \uc2 12.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  has said getting vaccinated against coronavirus is a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral obligation\u8221\'20\'1D and denounced how naysayers had been swayed by \u8220\'20\'1Cbaseless information\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press reported:\par \par \uc2 Francis used some of his strongest words yet calling for people to get vaccinated in a speech to ambassadors accredited to the Holy See, an annual event in which he takes stock of the world and sets out the Vatican\u8217\'20\'19s foreign policy goals for the year.\par \par \uc2 Francis, 85, has generally shied away from speaking about vaccination as a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral obligation\u8221\'20\'1D, though his Covid advisory body has referred to it as a \u8220\'20\'1Cmoral responsibility\u8221\'20\'1D. Rather, Francis has termed vaccination as \u8220\'20\'1Can act of love\u8221\'20\'1D and that refusing to get inoculated was \u8220\'20\'1Csuicidal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, he went a step further, saying that individuals had a responsibility to care for themselves \u8220\'20\'1Cand this translates into respect for the health of those around us. Health care is a moral obligation\u8221\'20\'1D, he asserted.\par \par \uc2 He lamented that, increasingly, ideological divides were discouraging people from getting vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 12.00pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Sweden to introduce stricter restrictions including work from home mandate\par \par \uc2 Stricter pandemic measures are to be introduced in Sweden in response to a rising number of Covid cases and pressure on hospitals, the prime minister has said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe situation has deteriorated, without doubt. The level of infections in Sweden is at a historically high level,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Magdalena Andersson\uc2  told a news conference, according to Reuters.\par \par \uc2 The new measures include a work from home mandate where possible and a cap on the number of people allowed at public events. Restaurants will have to close at 11pm and guests will have to be seated and in groups no bigger than eight people. Adults are also being asked to limit social contacts indoors.\par \par \uc2 The measures will be evaluated after two weeks, but are expected to be in place for at least four weeks.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 11.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 The prime minister of Spain, Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez, has called for European officials to consider ditching detailed pandemic tracking for Covid in favour of a flu-like monitoring system.\par \par \uc2 The change would mean treating Covid-19 as an \u8220\'20\'1Cendemic illness\u8221\'20\'1D rather than a pandemic, S\u225\'00\'E1nchez said on Monday. He pointed out that deaths as a proportion of recorded cases has fallen since the initial onset of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The Associated Press quoted S\u225\'00\'E1nchez telling Spanish radio: \u8220\'20\'1CI believe that we have the conditions for, with precaution, slowly, opening the debate at the technical level and at the level of health professionals, but also at the European level, to start evaluating the evolution of this disease with different parameters than we have until now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 S\u225\'00\'E1nchez also announced that Spain is purchasing this month 344,000 pills of a Covid-19 antiviral drug developed by the US pharmaceutical firm \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Despite one of Europe\u8217\'20\'19s most successful vaccination rollouts, with 81% of the country having received a full course of Covid vaccinations, Spain is grappling with an unprecedented surge of coronavirus infections.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 11.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 As schools reopen in one part of the world, they are closing in another. Schools across Nepal will close for nearly three weeks after a spike in coronavirus cases, a government spokesman said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Nepal reported 841 new cases on Sunday, the biggest single-day jump since September last year, taking its total to 832,589 since the pandemic began. Its death toll from the coronavirus is 11,604.\par \par \uc2 Deepak Sharma, spokesperson for the education ministry, said schools would remain closed until 29 January, although a campaign to vaccinate children aged 12 to 17 at their schools would go ahead.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSchools must notify students about the time and date when they need to go to schools and receive the shots,\u8221\'20\'1D Sharma told Reuters.\par \par \uc2 Nepal has provided two shots of Covid-19 vaccines to 36.7% of its population of 30 million since an inoculation drive began a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.04pm GMT\par \par \uc2 11.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 Children in Uganda have finally begun returning to school, after nearly two years off. On Monday, the country ended 83 weeks of full or partial school closures, the longest anywhere in the world.\par \par \uc2 Uganda first shut its schools in March 2020, after the first coronavirus case was recorded in Africa. Some classes were reopened to students in February 2021, but a total lockdown was imposed again in June.\par \par \uc2 The shutdown affected more than 10 million students.\par \par \uc2 Welcoming the reopening of Uganda\u8217\'20\'19s schools, \uc2 Save the Children\uc2  warned that \u8220\'20\'1Clost learning may lead to high dropout rates in the coming weeks without urgent action\u8221\'20\'1D, including what it described as catch-up clubs.\par \par \uc2 The aid group warned in a statement Monday of a wave of dropouts \u8220\'20\'1Cas returning students who have fallen behind in their learning fear they have no chance of catching up\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 According to the Associated Press, the school reopenings on Monday caused traffic congestion in some areas of the capital, Kampala. Students walked through the city\u8217\'20\'19s streets carrying their mattresses on their backs, a back-to-boarding school phenomenon not witnessed here for nearly two years.\par \par \uc2 But schools may yet close again, and soon, with infection numbers on the rise again in Uganda in recent days. Museveni has warned of a possible new lockdown if intensive care units reach 50% occupancy.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.05pm GMT\par \par \uc2 10.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 Summary\par \par \uc2 Here is a brief round-up of the day\u8217\'20\'19s top Covid news stories so far:\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Dr David Nabarro\uc2 , the World Health Organization\u8217\'20\'19s special envoy on Covid, said the virus is going to pose a very difficult situation for the next three months \u8220\'20\'1Cat least\u8221\'20\'1D but \u8220\'20\'1Cwe can see the end in sight\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * High levels of T-cells from common cold coronaviruses can provide protection against Covid, an \uc2 Imperial College London\uc2  study published on Monday has found, which could inform approaches for second-generation vaccines.\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 UK government\uc2  is warning that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/uk-government-urges-all-pregnant-women-to-get-immediate-covid-jab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost all pregnant women admitted to hospital with Covid symptoms were unvaccinated }}}\uc2  in one analysis over several months last year, as it kicks off an advertising campaign encouraging expectant mothers to get boosted.\par \par \uc2 * Novak Djokovic has won a battle with the Australian government after a court quashed the decision to cancel his visa \u8211\'20\'13 but he may still yet lose the war. No sooner had federal circuit judge Anthony Kelly revealed the home affairs minister had agreed to settle the case, than the Australian government\u8217\'20\'19s counsel warned the immigration minister could still decide to use a personal power to cancel Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s visa anew.\par \par \uc2 * In the UK, hospitals will be able to use spare capacity in the private sector under a deal struck with the \uc2 NHS\uc2 . Under the three-month agreement, private healthcare staff and facilities will be put on standby to support the \uc2 NHS\uc2  should hospital admissions or staff absences due to Covid threaten the provision of urgent care.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 India\u8217\'20\'19s health ministry\uc2  has said that only between five and 10% of Covid patients have needed hospitalisation this time around compared with 20-23% in the previous wave that peaked in May.\par \par \uc2 * China is battling to stamp out its first outbreak of the Omicron variant, only weeks before the Chinese new year and the Beijing Winter Olympics, with cases recorded in at least two distant provinces.\par \par \uc2 * The Spanish government is working on rules to limit the retail price of Covid antigen tests, prime minister Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez said on Monday, after shortages were reported in many pharmacies across the country last month.\par \par \uc2 * People self-testing for Covid should swab their throat as well as their nose when using rapid antigen kits to increase the chances of detecting the Omicron variant, a top Israeli health official said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 * Indonesian authorities have granted emergency authorisation to five different Covid vaccines as booster shots that will prioritise vulnerable groups.\par \par \uc2 * Two of New Zealand \u8217\'20\'19s most prominent Covid-19 experts have warned that the country is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/10/new-zealand-not-prepared-for-omicron-outbreak-expected-in-matter-of-weeks-experts-warn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unprepared to prevent the health system}}}\uc2  from being overloaded by an Omicron outbreak.\par \par \uc2 * US Democratic congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez has announced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/09/alexandria-ocasio-cortez-positive-covid-test-omicron-new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she tested positive for Covid-19.}}}\par \par \uc2 * Hungary\u8217\'20\'19s government is considering the option of a fourth Covid-19 vaccine booster shot, Reuters is reporting.\par \par \uc2 * Israel could have up to nearly 40% of its population infected by Covid during the current wave, prime minister Naftali Bennett has said.\par \par \uc2 I am now handing over the global coronavirus blog to my colleague Damien Gayle, who will be along shortly. He will be bringing you the latest Covid news from around the world for the next hour.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 10.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 China is battling to stamp out its first outbreak of the Omicron variant, just weeks before the Chinese new year and the Beijing Winter Olympics, with cases recorded in at least two distant provinces.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, health authorities reported 97 new locally transmitted cases for the preceding 24 hours, across several cities. At least 30 cases were in Henan province, while 21 new cases were reported in the Tianjin district of Jinnan, including 15 children aged five to 15.\par \par \uc2 At least two of the cases detected in Jinnan were reported as Omicron, the latest variant of Covid-19, which is many times more transmissible and is tearing through other countries around the world. In Anyang, Henan province, two Omicron cases were traced to a student who had arrived from Tianjin more than 300 miles (500km) away, officials said. There were 15 cases reported in Anyang for Monday but the breakdown of variant type was not disclosed.\par \par \uc2 State media have described the outbreak as China\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cfirst real battle against Omicron\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/10/china-battles-omicron-outbreak-weeks-before-winter-olympics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China battles Omicron outbreak weeks before Winter Olympics}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 12.06pm GMT\par \par \uc2 10.14am GMT\par \par \uc2 T-cells from common colds can provide Covid protection\par \par \uc2 High levels of T-cells from common cold coronaviruses can provide protection against Covid, an \uc2 Imperial College London\uc2  study published on Monday has found, which could inform approaches for second-generation vaccines.\par \par \uc2 Reuters reported:\par \par \uc2 Immunity against Covid is a complex picture, and while there is evidence of waning antibody levels six months after vaccination, T-cells are also believed to play a vital role in providing protection.\par \par \uc2 The study, which began in September 2020, looked at levels of cross-reactive T-cells generated by previous common colds in 52 household contacts of positive Covid cases shortly after exposure, to see if they went on to develop infection.\par \par \uc2 It found that the 26 who did not develop infection had significantly higher levels of those T-cells than people who did get infected. Imperial did not say how long protection from the T-cells would last.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe found that high levels of pre-existing T-cells, created by the body when infected with other human coronaviruses like the common cold, can protect against Covid-19 infection,\u8221\'20\'1D study author Dr Rhia Kundu said.\par \par \uc2 The authors of the study, published in Nature Communications, said that the internal proteins of the Sars-CoV-2 virus which are targeted by the T-cells could offer an alternative target for vaccine makers.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 10.06am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Spanish government is working on rules to limit the retail price of Covid antigen tests, prime minister Pedro S\u225\'00\'E1nchez said on Monday, after shortages were reported in many pharmacies across the country last month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe debate we had before and during the Christmas season was the supply of tests, there was a bottleneck,\u8221\'20\'1D S\u225\'00\'E1nchez said in an interview with Cadena SER radio station. \u8220\'20\'1CNow, we will get into the control of the prices of tests.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The higher price of antigen tests in Spain during the surge of the Omicron coronavirus variant and the scarcity of tests in pharmacies have raised protests from opposition politicians and consumer groups, many of whom are calling for their sale to be allowed in supermarkets.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 9.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 India\u8217\'20\'19s health ministry\uc2  has said that only between five and 10% of Covid patients have needed hospitalisation this time around compared with 20-23% in the previous wave that peaked in May.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe situation is dynamic and evolving, therefore, the need for hospitalisation may also change rapidly,\u8221\'20\'1D health secretary \uc2 Rajesh Bhushan\uc2  wrote in a letter to state authorities asking them to regularly review their requirements of healthcare workers.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 9.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 Novak Djokovic has won a battle with the Australian government after a court quashed the decision to cancel his visa \u8211\'20\'13 but he may still yet lose the war.\par \par \uc2 No sooner had federal circuit judge Anthony Kelly revealed the home affairs minister had agreed to settle the case, than the Australian government\u8217\'20\'19s counsel warned the immigration minister could still decide to use a personal power to cancel Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s visa anew.\par \par \uc2 That means that \uc2 Alex Hawke\uc2 , one of the closest political allies of prime minister Scott Morrison, now has a momentous political decision to make: let Djokovic stay and play for a record 21st grand slam singles title; or deport him, which comes with a hefty three-year ban from re-entering Australia.\par \par \uc2 The harsh border policies that have allowed Australian governments to detain asylum seekers indefinitely are now being directed at one unvaccinated man, who happens to be the world\u8217\'20\'19s No 1 ranked male tennis player.\par \par \uc2 Djokovic obtained an exemption on the basis he had recently contracted Covid-19, which the judge noted had satisfied a qualified physician and an independent panel set up by the Victorian government.\par \par \uc2 But a delegate of the home affairs minister cancelled his visa on the basis the tennis star might pose a risk to public health. The government argues recently having Covid by itself does not justify an exemption, because Djokovic has recovered.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2022/jan/10/djokovic-wins-in-court-but-australias-government-can-still-use-its-nuclear-option-to-deport-him"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Djokovic wins in court, but Australia\u8217\'20\'19s government can still use its nuclear option to deport him}}}\par \par \uc2 9.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 NHS\uc2  likely to be under 'real pressure' for 'next two or three weeks'\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 NHS\uc2  is likely to be under real pressure for \u8220\'20\'1Cthe next two or three weeks, perhaps longer\u8221\'20\'1D, \uc2 Michael Gove\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 The cabinet minister told BBC Radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme:\par \par \uc2 Our first responsibility at the moment must be to support the \uc2 NHS\uc2 , but you quite rightly legitimately ask if we get through \u8211\'20\'13 and at the moment I hope and pray that we will get through this difficult period \u8211\'20\'13 then there will be better times ahead.\par \par \uc2 And I think one of the things that we do need to think about is how we live with Covid, how we live with this particular type of coronavirus. There are other coronaviruses which are endemic and with which we live, viruses tend to develop in a way whereby they become less harmful but more widespread.\par \par \uc2 So, guided by the science, we can look to the progressive lifting of restrictions, and I think for all of us the sooner the better. But we\u8217\'20\'19ve got to keep the \uc2 NHS\uc2  safe.\par \par \uc2 Gove said the prime minister was right in his decision not to introduce extra restrictions. He said he had been at the \u8220\'20\'1Cmore cautious end\u8221\'20\'1D in the discussions.\par \par \uc2 But he added that \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  had \u8220\'20\'1Cargued publicly that we would be able to get through this with the booster campaign, so if more were required then we would be ready to put in additional measures\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe always keep that under review but his judgment has been vindicated,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 10.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 9.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 People self-testing for Covid should swab their throat as well as their nose when using rapid antigen kits to increase the chances of detecting the Omicron variant, a top Israeli health official said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The recommendation goes against the advice of the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration\uc2 , which has said manufacturers\u8217\'20\'19 instructions should still be followed and that any incorrect use of throat swabs could pose a safety risk.\par \par \uc2 On Israeli Army Radio, Sharon Alroy-Preis, Israel\u8217\'20\'19s public health chief, said antigen tests, used widely in the country, are less sensitive than PCR tests in detecting illness.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn order to increase their sensitivity we will from now on recommend swabbing the throat and the nose. It\u8217\'20\'19s not what the manufacturer instructs but we are instructing this,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 9.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 WHO says end of pandemic is 'in sight, but we\u8217\'20\'19re not there'\par \par \uc2 Dr David Nabarro\uc2 , the World Health Organization\u8217\'20\'19s special envoy on Covid, said the virus is going to pose a very difficult situation for the next three months \u8220\'20\'1Cat least\u8221\'20\'1D but \u8220\'20\'1Cwe can see the end in sight\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He told Sky News:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m afraid we are moving through the marathon but there\u8217\'20\'19s no actual way to say that we\u8217\'20\'19re at the end \u8211\'20\'13 we can see the end in sight, but we\u8217\'20\'19re not there. And there\u8217\'20\'19s going to be some bumps before we get there.\par \par \uc2 And I can\u8217\'20\'19t tell you how bad they\u8217\'20\'19re going to be, but I can at least tell you what I\u8217\'20\'19m expecting. First of all, this virus is continuing to evolve \u8211\'20\'13 we have Omicron but we\u8217\'20\'19ll get more variants.\par \par \uc2 Secondly, it really is affecting the whole world. And, whilst health services in western Europe are just about coping, in many other parts of the world, they are completely overwhelmed.\par \par \uc2 And thirdly, it\u8217\'20\'19s really clear that there\u8217\'20\'19s no scope for major restrictions in any country, particularly poor countries. People have just got to keep working and so there are some very tough choices for politicians right now. It\u8217\'20\'19s going to be difficult for the next three months at least.\par \par \uc2 Asked about a suggestion that there could be coronavirus surges two or three times a year, he added:\par \par \uc2 The way this virus is behaving, and has behaved really since we first met it, is that it builds up and then surges quite dramatically, and then it comes down again, and then surges again about every three or four months.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s difficult to use past behaviour to predict the future. And I don\u8217\'20\'19t like doing that too much. But I would agree that the pattern, I think, that is going to happen with this virus is continued surges, and living with Covid means being able to prepare for these surges and to react and really quickly when they occur.\par \par \uc2 Life can go on, we can get the economy going again in many countries, but we just have to be really respectful of the virus and that means having really good plans in place for dealing with the surges.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 11.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.44am GMT\par \par \uc2 While all the focus in Australia has been on whether defending champion Novak Djokovic will compete at the Australian Open next week, another major name in the men\u8217\'20\'19s draw is now in doubt.\par \par \uc2 Nick Kyrgios is a huge doubt after contracting Covid just a week out from the season-opening major.\par \par \uc2 Kyrgios pulled out of the Sydney Tennis Classic on Monday hours before his match against the Italian firebrand Fabio Fognini. The 26-year-old withdrew from the Melbourne Summer Set last week with a mystery illness that sparked his asthma.\par \par \uc2 The world No 114 was cleared of Covid at the time, but on Monday confirmed he had contracted the virus. The Australian Open begins on 17 January, giving him a week to recover and be cleared to return.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2022/jan/10/nick-kyrgios-in-doubt-for-australian-open-after-contracting-covid-19"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Nick Kyrgios in doubt for Australian Open after contracting Covid-19 }}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK today, Graham Medley, professor of infectious disease modelling at the \uc2 London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine\uc2 , has been asked whether the Omicron wave is over in London but not elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 He told BBC Radio 4\u8217\'20\'19s Today programme:\par \par \uc2 I think that at the moment the testing capacity issues, and Christmas and new year, mean that we can\u8217\'20\'19t really rely on cases to tell us what\u8217\'20\'19s going to happen exactly.\par \par \uc2 At the moment we are seeing a relatively high number of admissions. How long that continues, whether that goes up or goes down, I think is unknown at the moment.\par \par \uc2 He said the Omicron virus itself is \u8220\'20\'1Cless severe\u8221\'20\'1D than Delta but it is \u8220\'20\'1Cjust as threatening\u8221\'20\'1D due to its transmissibility.\par \par \uc2 Pressed on whether the nation was moving away from a situation where Covid was an \u8220\'20\'1Cemergency\u8221\'20\'1D, Prof Medley added:\par \par \uc2 I think that that transition is absolutely true. It can\u8217\'20\'19t be an emergency forever.\par \par \uc2 So at some point it will have to stop being an emergency but that is likely to be a phase out rather than an active point in time where somebody can declare the epidemic over.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s going to fade out and disappear much more slowly than that, I think.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 9.00am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 Indonesian authorities have granted emergency authorisation to five different Covid vaccines as booster shots that will prioritise vulnerable groups.\par \par \uc2 Penny Lukito, head of Indonesia\u8217\'20\'19s Food and Drug Monitoring Agency, said Sinovac, \uc2 Pfizer\uc2 , \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 , Moderna and Zifivax vaccines would be distributed as a third vaccine shot in the country.\par \par \uc2 Lukito added there are several additional vaccines that are currently in the process of clinical trials to obtain emergency use permits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to give the booster vaccine to maintain vaccine efficacy against Covid-19 infection,\u8221\'20\'1D Lukito said at a news conference on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The health ministry-run booster programme will begin on Wednesday and prioritise elderly and groups of immunocompromised individuals, according to a ministry statement. The government has distributed booster jabs to medical workers since July last year.\par \par \uc2 8.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 Access to vaccines, masks and tests will help us make the awkward transition from pandemic to endemic, argue scientists Erin Mordecai and Mallory Harris.\par \par \uc2 Read their full opinion piece here.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2022/jan/10/herd-immunity-threshold-covid-new-variants"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Will we ever reach herd immunity to Covid? | Erin Mordecai and Mallory Harris}}}\par \par \uc2 8.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Philippines \u8217\'20\'19 health ministry has reported 33,169 new coronavirus cases, marking the third straight day of record-high infections.\par \par \uc2 In a bulletin, it said total cases had reached almost 3 million, while deaths were at 52,293, after confirming a further 145 deaths on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The rising number of infections risked overwhelming the healthcare system, health undersecretary Maria Rosiario Vergeire told the ANC news channel, calling on symptomatic people to immediately isolate and get tested.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 China locks down major port city of Tianjin\par \par \uc2 The major port of Tianjin may be facing China\u8217\'20\'19s first local outbreak of the Omicron variant of coronavirus, less than a month before the winter Olympics open in nearby Beijing.\par \par \uc2 State broadcaster CCTV said the government has divided Tianjin and its 14 million residents into three levels of restrictions, starting with lockdown areas where people are not allowed to leave their homes at all.\par \par \uc2 In control areas, each household is allowed to have one family member leave to buy groceries every other day, while in prevention areas, people must remain inside their immediate neighbourhoods.\par \par \uc2 Buses and trains from Tianjin to Beijing have been suspended and people are being told not to leave the city unless they have urgent business, the Associated Press reported.\par \par \uc2 The city began mass testing of all its residents on Sunday after a cluster of 20 children and adults tested positive for Covid, including at least two with the Omicron variant. A further 20 people tested positive on Sunday, bringing the total to 40. Officials said earlier that the virus has been circulating so the number of cases could rise.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 8.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 Healthcare and frontline workers, along with people aged 60 and above with health problems, queued today at vaccination centres across India to receive a third Covid jab.\par \par \uc2 The doses, which India is calling a \u8220\'20\'1Cprecautionary\u8221\'20\'1D shot instead of a booster, were given as new confirmed coronavirus infections rocketed to over 179,000 on Monday, nearly an eightfold increase in a week.\par \par \uc2 Hospitalisations, while still relatively low, are also beginning to rise in large, crowded cities such as New Delhi, Mumbai and Kolkata, according to the Associated Press. It reported:\par \par \uc2 India is better prepared now than it was last year when the delta variant overwhelmed hospitals. When cases spiked in March last year, not even 1% of its population of nearly 1.4 billion was fully vaccinated. India\u8217\'20\'19s creaky medical infrastructure meant millions likely died.\par \par \uc2 Since then, the government has bolstered healthcare, built oxygen plants and added beds to hospitals. About 47% of the population is now fully vaccinated and many have antibodies from previous infections. This may provide \u8220\'20\'1Chybrid immunity\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a combination of immunity from previous infections and vaccines \u8211\'20\'13 comparable to boosters, said Dr Chandrakant Lahariya, an Indian epidemiologist.\par \par \uc2 Although the Omicron variant seems to cause less severe illness than the Delta variant, India\u8217\'20\'19s massive population, crowded cities, and understaffed hospitals mean that health systems may still become strained. Elections may further spread the more infectious variant, allowing it to infect vulnerable people that previous variants didn\u8217\'20\'19t reach.\par \par \uc2 But the biggest fear is that hospitals will be overwhelmed because of sick medical personnel, said Dr Vineeta Bal, an immunologist at the \uc2 Indian Institute of Science Education and Research\uc2  in the city of Pune.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere would be beds [in hospitals] but no people to take care of individuals,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 8.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 Lateral flow tests in England will be free for \u8220\'20\'1Cas long as we need\u8221\'20\'1D, \uc2 Michael Gove\uc2  has confirmed.\par \par \uc2 The levelling up secretary told Sky News it was \u8220\'20\'1Cimpossible to predict\u8221\'20\'1D how long that would be. But he said:\par \par \uc2 But it is the case that in this country lateral flow tests are free, unlike in many other jurisdictions. They\u8217\'20\'19re a vital tool in making sure that we can curb the spread of the infection and also that people who are needed to isolate do so.\par \par \uc2 We are moving to a situation \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19re not there yet \u8211\'20\'13 but we are moving to a situation where it is possible to say that we can live with Covid and that the pressure on the \uc2 NHS\uc2  and on vital public services is abating.\par \par \uc2 But it\u8217\'20\'19s absolutely vital to recognise that we are not there yet and as the health secretary has reminded us, there will be some difficult weeks ahead and that is why we all need to continue to test, continue \u8211\'20\'13 if we are positive \u8211\'20\'13 to isolate and continue broadly to support the \uc2 NHS\uc2  as it goes through a challenging period. But one in which the frontline professionals are doing an amazing job.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.35am GMT\par \par \uc2 There is still pressure on British hospitals and the country is not yet in a position to say it can live with Covid, senior minister \uc2 Michael Gove\uc2  said today.\par \par \uc2 Asked how long rapid tests would be provided for free, Gove, who is housing minister, that they were a vital tool in curbing a pandemic that was not yet over.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are moving to a situation where it is possible to say that we can live with Covid and that the pressure on the \uc2 NHS\uc2  and on vital public services is abating,\u8221\'20\'1D he told Sky News.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut it\u8217\'20\'19s absolutely vital to recognise that we are not there yet \u8230\'20\'26 there will be some difficult weeks ahead.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 7.15am GMT\par \par \uc2 Hello. Tom Ambrose here and I will be bringing you all the latest Covid news from around the world.\par \par \uc2 We start with the news that, in the UK, hospitals will be able to use spare capacity in the private sector under a deal struck with the \uc2 NHS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Under the three-month agreement, private healthcare staff and facilities will be put on standby to support the \uc2 NHS\uc2  should hospital admissions or staff absences due to Covid threaten the provision of urgent care. Patients that can be referred include some of those waiting for cancer surgery.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NHS\uc2  has also been asked to look at using spare capacity in gyms and education centres to create \u8220\'20\'1Csuper-surge\u8221\'20\'1D wards on top of their usual surge capacity.\par \par \uc2 Nightingale hubs are already being created in the grounds of some hospitals as part of a move to create up to 4,000 extra beds.\par \par \uc2 Health and social care secretary \uc2 Sajid Javid\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 This agreement demonstrates the collaboration across our healthcare services to create an additional safeguard that ensures people can continue to get the care they need from our world-leading \uc2 NHS\uc2 , whenever they need it.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 6.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 Summary of key developments\par \par \uc2 * The \uc2 UK government\uc2  is warning that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/uk-government-urges-all-pregnant-women-to-get-immediate-covid-jab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost all pregnant women admitted to hospital with Covid symptoms were unvaccinated }}}\uc2  in one analysis over several months last year, as it kicks off an advertising campaign encouraging expectant mothers to get boosted.\par \par \uc2 * An Australian court has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2022/jan/10/novak-djokovic-appeal-federal-circuit-court-cancellation-australian-visa-covid-vaccination-live-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quashed the decision to cancel Novak Djokovic \u8217\'20\'19s visa}}}\uc2  last week, meaning the visa he came to Australia on stands, and he will be released from detention and get his passport and other personal effects back.\par \par \uc2 * China has detected more Omicron cases as cities tighten restrictions.\par \par \uc2 * Some of Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s tourism hotspots will reopen to fully vaccinated international tourists from Tuesday, as the country tries to balance the risks posed by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/omicron"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Omicron variant}}}\uc2  with the need to boost the travel sector.\par \par \uc2 * Two of New Zealand \u8217\'20\'19s most prominent Covid-19 experts have warned that the country is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/10/new-zealand-not-prepared-for-omicron-outbreak-expected-in-matter-of-weeks-experts-warn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unprepared to prevent the health system}}}\uc2  from being overloaded by an Omicron outbreak.\par \par \uc2 * US Democratic congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez has announced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/09/alexandria-ocasio-cortez-positive-covid-test-omicron-new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she tested positive for Covid-19.}}}\par \par \uc2 * Germany will study how reliable rapid antigen tests are in detecting the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/omicron"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Omicron variant}}}\uc2  of Covid-19, health minister \uc2 Karl Lauterbach\uc2  said on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 * Israel could have up to nearly 40% of its population infected by Covid during the current wave, prime minister Naftali Bennett has said.\par \par \uc2 * Downing Street is facing calls to ensure that \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  will be personally interviewed by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/05/new-head-of-no-10-parties-investigation-unlikely-to-start-from-scratch"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sue Gray inquiry}}}\uc2  about alleged No 10 gatherings during the first lockdown, after it emerged he may have been present at a \u8220\'20\'1Cbring your own booze\u8221\'20\'1D party.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 7.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 6.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 Hungary\u8217\'20\'19s government is considering the option of a fourth Covid-19 vaccine booster shot, Reuters is reporting.\par \par \uc2 The country\u8217\'20\'19s daily tally of new Covid-19 infections could exceed 13,000 in the new wave of the pandemic, the minister for human resources reportedly told Inforadio on Monday morning.\par \par \uc2 6.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 Court quashes Novak Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s visa decision\par \par \uc2 Judge Anthony Kelly has just read out a minute agreed to by both the government and Djokovic, where he quashes the decision to cancel Novak Djokovic \u8217\'20\'19s visa, orders government to pay costs, and for Djokovic to be released from detention in 30 minutes with his passport and personal effects released to him.\par \par \uc2 The minute notes Djokovic was allowed until 8.30am when he was originally detained to respond to the notification to cancel his visa, but the decision was made at 7.42am.\par \par \uc2 It also found if the player had had more time he could have consulted wider and responded further than he was able to.\par \par \uc2 Follow our live blog on the verdict here.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2022/jan/10/novak-djokovic-appeal-federal-circuit-court-cancellation-australian-visa-covid-vaccination-live-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Novak Djokovic wins court appealt against cancellation of Australian visa \u8211\'20\'13 live updates}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 6.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 Some lively scenes have emerged from Melbourne, Australia where Novak Djokovic is appealing in court against the cancellation of his Australian visa.\par \par \uc2 Supporters continue to hold placards outside the immigration detention hotel where the tennis star is staying.\par \par \uc2 Others have rallied outside the federal court of Australia where the athlete is in the midst of a legal battle over his visa to enter the country and play in the Australian Open later this month.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 6.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 6.03am GMT\par \par \uc2 More on the Novak Djokovic court appeal as the tennis star makes a bid to reinstate his Australian visa.\par \par \uc2 The hearing is back underway in the federal circuit court after a long adjournment earlier today.\par \par \uc2 About one hour ago a white van left the Park Hotel after Djokovic was allowed to move to another, unknown address, so he can watch the court proceedings remotely.\par \par \uc2 Christopher Tran, counsel for the federal government, flagged that the injunction keeping Novak Djokovic in Australia is due to expire on Monday afternoon and Judge Anthony Kelly extended it to 8pm which means the hearing will be hopefully finalised today.\par \par \uc2 The expedited hearing comes ahead of a looming {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/tennis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennis}}}\uc2  Australia deadline of Tuesday for Djokovic to be included in the draw to play the Australian Open.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a useful rundown of the first half of the hearing below.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2022/jan/10/novak-djokovic-judge-agitated-by-issue-of-why-australian-government-did-not-accept-tennis-players-medical-exemption"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Novak Djokovic: judge asks what more tennis star could have done to prove vaccination exemption }}}\par \par \uc2 5.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 China detects more Omicron cases as cities tighten restrictions\par \par \uc2 China has reported more cases of the Omicron variant on Monday, with authorities on high alert over flare-ups in major cities just weeks ahead of the Beijing Winter Olympics.\par \par \uc2 Authorities are battling several outbreaks - including in Xi\u8217\'20\'19an where 13 million city residents are currently in their third week of lockdown.\par \par \uc2 Fears have also grown about a cluster of infections in the northern city of Tianjin, which was linked to two Omicron cases reported on Monday in the city of Anyang around 400 kilometres (250 miles) away, Agence France-Press reports.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe general public should not leave Tianjin unless essential to do so,\u8221\'20\'1D city officials said in a statement Sunday.\par \par \uc2 Those who need to leave must obtain official permission and test negative for the virus within 48 hours of their departure, it added.\par \par \uc2 Schools and university campuses have been closed, and trains into Beijing from Tianjin have been cancelled.\par \par \uc2 Road checkpoints have been set up for vehicles entering the capital.\par \par \uc2 Tianjin - just 150 kilometres from Beijing - has already ordered the testing of all 14 million residents.\par \par \uc2 Another 21 cases were reported in the city Monday, although the variant of the virus wasn\u8217\'20\'19t confirmed.\par \par \uc2 Authorities in the city of Anyang in central Henan province also announced the testing of all residents - more than five million people - over the weekend. The city on Monday reported two Omicron cases linked to the Tianjin cluster.\par \par \uc2 Henan reported 60 new cases Monday but did not break down the tally by variant. The provincial capital Zhengzhou has closed schools and kindergartens, and stopped restaurants from accepting dine-in customers.\par \par \uc2 4.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 India reported 179,723 new Covid-19 infections on Monday, the highest figure since late May, health ministry {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.mohfw.gov.in/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data}}}\uc2  showed.\par \par \uc2 The Asian nation also recorded another 146 deaths taking the death toll to 483,936 although many believe the actual number to be much higher.\par \par \uc2 4.10am GMT\par \par \uc2 Some of Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s tourism hotspots will reopen to fully vaccinated international tourists from Tuesday, as the country tries to balance the risks posed by the Omicron variant with the need to boost the travel sector.\par \par \uc2 Tourists will be able to enter Krabi, Phang-Nga, Ko Samui, Ko Pha-ngan and Ko Tao, provided they stay at an approved hotel for at least seven days. Phuket reopened in July last year under a similar arrangement, which also requires tourists to be tested prior to and during their stay.\par \par \uc2 Thailand\u8217\'20\'19s tourism industry has been devastated by the pandemic. Almost 40 million foreigners arrived in Thailand in 2019, but visitor numbers fell to just 6.7 million in 2020 as Covid-19 halted international travel. The government previously eased entry requirements across the country last year in an attempt to boost the economy, but then suspended arrivals late in December in response to the emergence of the Omicron variant.\par \par \uc2 To reduce infections, the government has also encouraged people to work from home, and has banned the consumption of alcohol in restaurants after 9pm in high risk areas, including the capital Bangkok.\par \par \uc2 Thailand has vaccinated roughly 70% of the population. On Monday, 7,926 new cases and 13 deaths were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Covid19Thailand/status/1480343234786193408"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 3.52am GMT\par \par \uc2 South Korea is reporting a daily rise of 3,007 new confirmed coronavirus cases and 34 deaths, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KoreaDCA/status/1480339836636917762"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data}}}\uc2  recently published by the Korea Centres for Disease Control and Prevention.\par \par \uc2 A total of 86.4 % of the population have received at least one dose of a Covid vaccine and 83.9 % have received at least two doses.\par \par \uc2 An alert reader has also shared their experience of living in South Korea throughout the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve had minimal disruption to our daily lives (bar wanting to travel abroad during vacation) the last two years, and still have low numbers,\u8221\'20\'1D English teacher Dominic Evans told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CEverybody wears masks in public. Showing proof of vaccination is compulsory now for most places and vaccine uptake is shooting along.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Korean situation shows you can diligently \u8216\'20\'18live with Covid\u8217\'20\'19, control the numbers, still have a normal daily life, and still have public services and day to day services running smoothly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 3.41am GMT\par \par \uc2 Germany is reporting a daily rise of 25,255 new confirmed coronavirus cases and 52 deaths, according to the \uc2 Robert Koch Institute\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 3.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 New Zealand not prepared for Omicron outbreak, experts warn\par \par \uc2 Two of New Zealand \u8217\'20\'19s most prominent Covid-19 experts have warned that the country is unprepared to prevent the health system from being overloaded by an Omicron outbreak, with likely fatal consequences.\par \par \uc2 Otago University\u8217\'20\'19s Dr Nick Wilson and Dr Michael Baker also said it was only a \u8220\'20\'1Cmatter of weeks\u8221\'20\'1D before the highly transmissible variant seeped into the community due to border failures.\par \par \uc2 Wilson said that despite New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s high vaccination rates, the number of adults who had received a booster dose of the vaccine \u8211\'20\'13 essential for minimising the effects of Omicron \u8211\'20\'13 remains dangerously low, and noted that the vaccine rollout for children between five and 11 still had not begun.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/10/new-zealand-not-prepared-for-omicron-outbreak-expected-in-matter-of-weeks-experts-warn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/10/new-zealand-not-prepared-for-omicron-outbreak-expected-in-matter-of-weeks-experts-warn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New Zealand not prepared for Omicron outbreak expected in \u8216\'20\'18matter of weeks\u8217\'20\'19, experts warn}}}\par \par \uc2 3.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 China has reported 157 new confirmed coronavirus cases for 9 January, down slightly from 165 a day earlier, its health authority said on Monday.\par \par \uc2 Of the new infections, 97 were locally transmitted, according to a statement by the National Health Commission, compared with 92 a day earlier. Most of the new local cases were in Henan, Tianjin and Shaanxi.\par \par \uc2 According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-01-09/china-reports-nation-s-first-community-spread-of-omicron-variant?srnd=premium-asia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg}}}\uc2 , China saw its first community spread of the Omicron variant after two cases were detected in the port city of Tianjin and later confirmed by the Chinese Center for Disease Control and Prevention (CCDCP).\par \par \uc2 The news publication said the cases were known after a local branch of the CCDCP completed genome sequencing, citing a CCTV report.\par \par \uc2 The infections were reported to be from the same transmission chain but officials have yet to establish if the strain is the same as imported Omicron cases reported earlier in Tianjin, according to the report.\par \par \uc2 China also reported 42 new asymptomatic cases, which it classifies separately from confirmed cases, down from 46 a day earlier.\par \par \uc2 There were no new deaths, leaving the death toll at 4,636. Mainland China had 103,776 confirmed cases as of 9 January.\par \par \uc2 3.01am GMT\par \par \uc2 Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s counsel Nicholas Wood has said the delegate for the minister making the decision to cancel his visa has not provided evidence that previous infection from Covid-19 is not a valid reason for medical exemption from being vaccinated against Covid-19 prior to entering Australia.\par \par \uc2 And in that case, the only evidence that can be relied upon is the Atagi guidelines, which did say vaccination can be deferred for up to six months from infection as a valid reason for a medical exemption.\par \par \uc2 Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s visa was cancelled at 7.29am, and the tennis player was informed at 7.42am. He had been asking for until 8.30am to respond to the cancellation notice, and needed to speak to people. The government made the decision on the basis of what he had provided up to that point.\par \par \uc2 The government suggested \u8220\'20\'1Ca response\u8221\'20\'1D made from Djokovic counts as responding to the notice to cancel the visa, while Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s counsel argues that reading would change it from someone having a right to a response, to the delegate for the minister having discretion over how much or little of a response from someone counts in responding to the notice.\par \par \uc2 In submissions, the government argued that to allow such extensions of time would amount to filibustering.\par \par \uc2 Follow all the live updates {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2022/jan/10/novak-djokovic-appeal-federal-circuit-court-cancellation-australian-visa-covid-vaccination-live-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/live/2022/jan/10/novak-djokovic-appeal-federal-circuit-court-cancellation-australian-visa-covid-vaccination-live-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Novak Djokovic appeals in court against cancellation of Australian visa \u8211\'20\'13 live updates}}}\par \par \uc2 Updated at 3.26am GMT\par \par \uc2 2.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 Some more updates from Guardian reporter Josh Taylor who is currently watching Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s court appeal unfold.\par \par \uc2 Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s counsel, Nicholas Wood, went through the process medical practitioners go through to register someone on the Australian Immunisation Register for people who register as not being able to get vaccinated against Covid-19 on medical grounds.\par \par \uc2 Under \u8220\'20\'1Ctemporary vaccine exemption\u8221\'20\'1D, it allows people to tick a box for \u8220\'20\'1Cacute major medical illness\u8221\'20\'1D, and then the guidelines refer to the Atagi advice document on major medical illnesses that mention having a PCR-confirmed Covid-19 infection in the past six months.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the critical Atagi document... it is an eminently clear expression by Atagi... that a valid reason for a temporary exemption for an acute major medical condition... [includes a] PCR confirmed infection,\u8221\'20\'1D Wood said.\par \par \uc2 He says this advice is unremarkable; someone who has recently had Covid-19 has some level of natural immunity from having had Covid-19. He said vaccination \u8220\'20\'1Cis not advisable\u8221\'20\'1D and would have limited utility for someone who has recently had Covid-19, combined with the risk of an adverse effect. Those are his words.\par \par \uc2 2.28am GMT\par \par \uc2 Tennis number one Novak Djokovic is currently appealing in court against the cancellation of his Australian visa ahead of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/australian-open"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australian Open}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The hearing kicked off at 10am AEST in Melbourne, which was 11pm GMT or 6pm EST. The 34-year-old is being held at a quarantine hotel in Melbourne.\par \par \uc2 Judge Anthony Kelly has noted that the ONLY ground the delegate relied on when deciding to cancel Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s visa is that: \u8220\'20\'1C the presence of its holder in Australia is or may be, or would or might be, a risk to: (i) the health, safety or good order of the Australian community or a segment of the Australian community\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s counsel Nicholas Wood notes in the request for declaration of vaccination, Djokovic claimed he cannot be vaccinated on medical grounds.\par \par \uc2 When prompted to provide proof, Djokovic uploaded the medical exemption document from the CMO at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/sport/tennis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennis}}}\uc2  Australia.\par \par \uc2 Kelly said a \u8220\'20\'1Cmatter that has really preoccupied me\u8221\'20\'1D is the status attached to the \uc2 Tennis Australia\uc2  document, and questions why it was not accepted by the delegate making the decision on the visa:\par \par \uc2 Here, a professor and an eminently qualified physician have produced and provided to the applicant a medical exemption. Further to that, that medical exemption and the basis on which it was given was separately given by a further independent expert specialist panel established by the Victorian state government and that document was in the hands of the delegate.\par \par \uc2 The point I am agitated about is \u8216\'20\'18what more could this man have done?\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Wood also accused the Australian government\u8217\'20\'19s delegate of an \u8220\'20\'1Cutter paucity of evidence\u8221\'20\'1D in the section of the notice in which they were required to explain why grounds to cancel the visa exist.\par \par \uc2 Wood argued it is \u8220\'20\'1Cmanifestly incorrect\u8221\'20\'1D that the biosecurity determination requires both a declaration of an exemption and evidence of the exemption - only the former is required, Djokovic\u8217\'20\'19s team argues.\par \par \uc2 He also said the Department of Home Affairs, before [Djokovic] left, said Djokovic met the requirements for quarantine-free travel.\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s made the declaration, provided the evidence even though it was not required, and then he received [notice] from the Department of Home Affairs... saying the application has been assessed and meets the required for quarantine-free travel.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Wood says any reasonable person would have considered he ticked every box.\par \par \uc2 Updated at 2.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 1.57am GMT\par \par \uc2 US Democratic congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez announced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/jan/09/alexandria-ocasio-cortez-positive-covid-test-omicron-new-york"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 she tested positive for Covid-19.}}}\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/RepAOC/status/1480315667802959873/photo/1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  on Sunday evening, the office of the New York progressive said she was \u8220\'20\'1Cexperiencing symptoms and recovering at home\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe congresswoman received her booster shot this fall and encourages everyone to get their booster and follow all \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  guidance\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 1.46am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK government\uc2  urges all pregnant women to get immediate Covid jab\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UK government\uc2  is warning that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/uk-government-urges-all-pregnant-women-to-get-immediate-covid-jab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost all pregnant women admitted to hospital with Covid symptoms were unvaccinated }}}\uc2  in one analysis over several months last year, as it kicks off an advertising campaign encouraging expectant mothers to get boosted.\par \par \uc2 The campaign is calling on pregnant women not to wait to get either their first, second or booster jab. It will highlight the risks of Covid-19 to mothers and babies, with testimonies of pregnant women who have had the vaccine to be broadcast on radio and social media.\par \par \uc2 The government says it has been clear, along with medical experts and institutions, that Covid-19 vaccines are safe for pregnant women and have no impact on fertility.\par \par \uc2 The Department of Health and Social Care cited statistics from the UK Obstetric Surveillance System which showed 96.3% of pregnant women admitted to hospital with Covid-19 symptoms between May and October were unvaccinated, a third of whom required respiratory support.\par \par \uc2 About 20% of women admitted to hospital with the virus need to be delivered pre-term to help them recover, and 20% of their babies need care in the neonatal unit, the Department of Health and Social Care said.\par \par \uc2 Read the full story {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/uk-government-urges-all-pregnant-women-to-get-immediate-covid-jab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/10/uk-government-urges-all-pregnant-women-to-get-immediate-covid-jab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK government urges all pregnant women to get immediate Covid jab }}}\par \par \uc2 default\par \par \uc2 1.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Germany to study rapid antigen-test reliability for Omicron\par \par \uc2 Germany will study how reliable rapid antigen tests are in detecting the Omicron variant of Covid-19, health minister \uc2 Karl Lauterbach\uc2  said on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe do not know exactly how well these tests work for Omicron,\u8221\'20\'1D Lauterbach said on public broadcasting channel ARD, adding the results of the assessment would become available within the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 It was clear, however, that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe alternative not to test at all... would be far too dangerous,\u8221\'20\'1D said Lauterbach, a scientist and physician.\par \par \uc2 Omicron now accounts for 44% of coronavirus infections in Germany, the \uc2 Robert Koch Institute\uc2  (RKI) for infectious disease said. On Sunday, RKI registered 36,552 newly reported Covid infections within 24 hours, three times the number a week earlier.\par \par \uc2 Late last month, the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) \uc2 also released a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/coronavirus-covid-19-and-medical-devices/sars-cov-2-viral-mutations-impact-covid-19-tests#omicronvariantimpact"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  saying rapid antigen tests appear less sensitive to Omicron than previous variants.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEarly data suggests that antigen tests do detect the Omicron variant but may have reduced sensitivity,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 FDA\uc2  said at the time.\par \par \uc2 1.11am GMT\par \par \uc2 40% of Israel could be infected in current wave, PM says\par \par \uc2 Israel could see up to nearly 40% of its population infected by Covid during the current wave, prime minister Naftali Bennett has said.\par \par \uc2 Bennett described the Omicron variant as a \u8220\'20\'1Cstorm that is sweeping the world\u8221\'20\'1D in a series of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/naftalibennett/status/1480221702210654213"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweets}}}\uc2  on Sunday:\par \par \uc2 Information presented at the cabinet meeting indicates that here, in Israel, between two to four million citizens in total will be infected during this current wave.\par \par \uc2 When standing in line it is difficult to see the successes and evaluation steps we have made, extremely important steps that will save lives. I understand the frustration, but ask you to be aware of that as well.\par \par \uc2 A country of just 9.4 million, Israel has seen infections nearly quadruple over the past week compared to the previous week. The health ministry reported 17,518 new infections on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 ?????? ?????,\par \par \uc2 ???? ??, ??? ???? ?? ?????? ????? ????.\par \par \uc2 ?? ?? ?? ????? ?????? ??????, ??? ????? ????? ????? ???? ?????? ???.\par \par \uc2 ??? ???? ?????? ??? ??? ?????, ????? ??? ???? ???? ???? ?? ??????.\par \par \uc2 ?????, ????????? ?? ???? ????? ??? ???? ?????. ??? ???? ??????? ??? ?????. >>\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Naftali Bennett ??? (@naftalibennett) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/naftalibennett/status/1480221700105060356?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 January 9, 2022}}}\par \par \uc2 12.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 Hello it\u8217\'20\'19s Samantha Lock back with you on the blog, ready to take you through all the Covid news this Monday.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s dive right in with the news that Israel could see up to nearly 40% of its population infected by Covid during the current wave, according to the latest data.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInformation presented at the cabinet meeting indicates that here, in Israel, between two to four million citizens in total will be infected during this current wave,\u8221\'20\'1D prime minister Naftali Bennett said on Sunday in a series of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/naftalibennett/status/1480221702210654213"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 tweets}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A country of just 9.4 million, Israel has seen infections nearly quadruple over the past week compared to the previous week. The health ministry reported 17,518 new infections on Saturday.\par \par \uc2 Germany has said it will launch a study into how reliable rapid antigen tests are in detecting the Omicron variant, health minister \uc2 Karl Lauterbach\uc2  announced on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe do not know exactly how well these tests work for Omicron,\u8221\'20\'1D Lauterbach said in an interview with public broadcasting channel ARD, adding the results of the study would become available within the next few weeks.\par \par \uc2 Late last month, the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration (FDA) \uc2 also released a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/coronavirus-covid-19-and-medical-devices/sars-cov-2-viral-mutations-impact-covid-19-tests#omicronvariantimpact"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement}}}\uc2  saying rapid antigen tests appear less sensitive to Omicron than previous variants.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEarly data suggests that antigen tests do detect the Omicron variant but may have reduced sensitivity,\u8221\'20\'1D the \uc2 FDA\uc2  said at the time.\par \par \uc2 Here is a recap of some of the main developments so far today.\par \par \uc2 Europe:\par \par \uc2 * Greece sets 1 February deadline for booster jabs. Those who have not received their coronavirus booster jabs will be barred from most indoor venues.\par \par \uc2 * France should not impose mandatory vaccination and would not be the most efficient way to encourage people to get vaccinated, a government spokesperson said. More than 105,000 people took to the streets on Saturday in protest.\par \par \uc2 * Downing Street is facing calls to ensure that \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  will be personally interviewed by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/2022/jan/05/new-head-of-no-10-parties-investigation-unlikely-to-start-from-scratch"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Sue Gray inquiry}}}\uc2  about alleged No 10 gatherings during the first lockdown, after it emerged he may have been present at a \u8220\'20\'1Cbring your own booze\u8221\'20\'1D party that month.\par \par \uc2 * \uc2 Boris Johnson\uc2  has been warned by a Conservative rebel ringleader he faces a massive revolt from his own MPs if he does not end all coronavirus restrictions this month.\par \par \uc2 * Germany will study how reliable rapid antigen tests are in detecting the fast-spreading Omicron variant, the health minister, \uc2 Karl Lauterbach\uc2 , said on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 * More than 150,000 people have died in the UK from coronavirus since the start of the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 * Covid should be treated as an endemic virus similar to flu, and ministers should {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2022/jan/08/end-mass-jabs-and-live-with-covid-says-ex-head-of-vaccine-taskforce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 end mass vaccination after the booster campaign}}}\uc2 , the former chairman of the UK \u8217\'20\'19s vaccine taskforce has said.\par \par \uc2 * Lateral flow tests in the UK will remain free, education secretary \uc2 Nadhim Zahawi\uc2  insisted amid reports they could be scaled back despite soaring Covid cases.\par \par \uc2 * London \u8217\'20\'19s public health chief said the Omicron variant \u8220\'20\'1Cmay have passed its peak\u8221\'20\'1D in the UK capital.\par \par \uc2 * The boss of one of London \u8217\'20\'19s busiest hospitals has said he is worried about losing staff when new rules come in requiring them to be vaccinated, \uc2 BBC News\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Asia:\par \par \uc2 * The US and Japan have reached an agreement to keep American troops within their bases amid concerns over a surge in Covid cases that has been linked to US military bases.\par \par \uc2 * Three people were arrested for breaking India\u8217\'20\'19 s Covid measures after police raided a dog\u8217\'20\'19s lavish birthday party in Gujarat\u8217\'20\'19s Ahmedabad city.\par \par \uc2 * The Philippines reported a record number of daily infections with 28,707 new cases, up from 26,458 cases the previous day.\par \par \uc2 * Tianjin, a major Chinese port city near the capital Beijing, has begun mass-testing its 14 million residents after a cluster of 20 children and adults tested positive for Covid-19, including at least two with the Omicron variant.\par \par \uc2 Africa:\par \par \uc2 * Africa has registered a total of more than 10m cases since the start of the pandemic, according to figures from the Africa Centres for Disease Control.\par \par \uc2 * South Africa, Morocco, Tunisia, Ethiopia and Libya are among the countries with the highest number of cases on the continent.\par \par \uc2 Middle East:\par \par \uc2 * The dissident Iran ian poet and filmmaker Baktash Abtin, 48, died after contracting Covid-19 in a hospital in Tehran after being released on a furlough from prison. Abtin was serving a six-year sentence for \u8220\'20\'1Canti-government propaganda\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Cactions against national security\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 * Kuwait and Qatar reported record daily Covid cases over the weekend. Kuwait reported 2,999 new cases on Sunday and Qatar on Saturday reported 3,487 new cases.\par \par \uc2 Americas :\par \par \uc2 * Brazil\u8217\'20\'19s health regulator \uc2 Anvisa\uc2  asked president \uc2 Jair Bolsonaro\uc2  to retract statements he made criticising the Covid vaccination for children.\par \par \uc2 If you have anything you would like to add please get in touch on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Samantha__Lock"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Twitter}}}\uc2  or by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "mailto:samantha.lock@theguardian.com"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 email}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 2022-01-10T00:53:03Z2022-01-11T10:50:36Z{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.guim.co.uk/8a8309384e77ae0b4372e2047918b8743f43971b/0_49_4788_2873/500.jpgA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://media.guim.co.uk/8a8309384e77ae0b4372e2047918b8743f43971b/0_49_4788_2873/500.jpgA}}}\uc2  medical worker in PPE with on a patient who has Covid-19 in Worcester, Massachusetts.French prime minister \uc2 Jean Castex\uc2  said that more than 10,000 classes - 2% of the total - had to be cancelled because of coronavirus outbreaks.Yair Lapid at the Knesset, Israel\u8217\'20\'19s parliament, in Jerusalem on 31 May, 2021.A row of ambulances is seen outside a hospital in Montreal, on Monday, 10 January, 2022.People wait to receive a test for Covid-19 on Capitol Hill on 10 January, 2022, in Washington, DC.A nurse prepares to inject a dose of Covid vaccine in Srinagar, the summer capital of Indian Kashmir.Pope Francis meets diplomats accredited to the Holy See.A health worker collects a swab sample for a coronavirus disease (COVID-19) test from a man with a COVID-19 positive household member, as part of the government\u8217\'20\'19s contact tracing program, in Quezon City, Metro Manila.Nurses work inside one of the 12 mobile units that Castille and Leon\u8217\'20\'19s regional government has contracted to test for antigens to the population with symptoms of coronavirus throughout January, in Valladolid, central Spain.Health workers carry a Covid-19 patient to be admitted at Civil hospital in Ahmedabad, India, on Saturday.Dr David Nabarro.A resident undergoes a nucleic acid test for Covid-19 in north China\u8217\'20\'19s Tianjin on 10 January.A health worker inoculates a man with a third \u8216\'20\'18booster\u8217\'20\'19 dose of Covaxin vaccine in Chennai on 10 January.Michael Gove leaves Downing Street in central London last week.Supporters of Serbian tennis player Novak Djokovic rally outside the federal court of Australia on 10 January.A supporter of Djokovic holds placards outside an immigration detention hotel where the athlete is staying in Melbourne, Australia.Media surround a vehicle at the facility where Djokovic has been held in Melbourne.People line up for Covid tests after local cases of the Omicron variant were detected in Tianjin, China.From Tuesday, tourists in Thailand will be able to enter Krabi, Phang-Nga, Ko Samui, Ko Pha-ngan and Ko Tao (pictured) provided they stay at an approved hotel for at least seven days.People wear masks to prevent contracting Covid-19 in downtown Seoul on 5 January.Residents line up to undergo a Covid-19 test in Tianjin, China, on 9 January.Supporters of Novak Djokovic gather outside the Federal Court building in Melbourne on 10 January where the tennis player is currently appealing in court against the cancellation of his Australian visa.US Democratic congresswoman Alexandria Ocasio-Cortez announced she tested positive for Covid-19 on Sunday.People with mandatory face masks line up to enter the Cathedral in Cologne as health minister \uc2 Karl Lauterbach\uc2  announces Germany will study how reliable rapid antigen tests are in detecting the Omicron variant of Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220110ei1a0005l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
