\par\par \uc2 Education\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Morale is low\u8217\'20\'19: US teachers push back on school reopenings amid Omicron wave\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,273 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 January 2022\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2022.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Educators concerned about staffing, working conditions and testing as schools grapple with Covid safety protocols\par \par \uc2 Many teachers across the US are resisting immediate school reopenings and asking for improved safety measures this month as a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2022-01-04/u-s-sets-new-global-daily-record-of-over-1-million-virus-cases?utm_campaign=socialflow-organic&utm_source=twitter&utm_content=politics&cmpid%3D=socialflow-twitter-politics&utm_medium=social"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 record number }}}\uc2  of Covid-19 cases have been recorded as a result of the spread of the Omicron variant.\par \par \uc2 A few school districts have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.edweek.org/leadership/what-the-return-from-winter-break-looks-like-for-schools-across-the-country/2022/01"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  opted }}}\uc2  to restart school after the winter break remotely for the first week or two, while most others are grappling with tightening or reinstituting Covid-19 safety protocols.\par \par \uc2 Several teachers who spoke to the Guardian expressed concerns over severe staffing shortages, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.change.org/p/sfusd-sick-out-for-covid-safety-january-6th-2022?utm_content=cl_sharecopy_31875053_en-US%3A3&recruiter=1244071250&recruited_by_id=c5815840-6a7c-11ec-a318-a5ef95177dcd&utm_source=share_petition&utm_medium=copylink&utm_campaign=psf_combo_share_initial&share_bandit_exp=initial-31875053-en-US&fbclid=IwAR3r-kAXfPirJTvsFdwaxYTU3IeAt5dA7ls383jaOervemIvrh5lQZC4FQ4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a lack of adequate and promised}}}\uc2  personal protective equipment, difficulties in accessing Covid testing and worsening overall working conditions.\par \par \uc2 Rori Abernethy, a teacher in San Francisco, California, said teachers are being faced with severe staffing shortages that are forcing teachers to serve as substitutes during preparation time and are increasing their classroom sizes due to teachers and other staff retiring, resigning, or calling out sick.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is the hardest teaching year I\u8217\'20\'19ve had in 20 years,\u8221\'20\'1D said Abernethy. \u8220\'20\'1CMorale is low amongst teachers and it feels like the whole city is fighting over politics and no one cares about what teachers are going through in the classroom. If we do complain, people are cruel and tell us to quit or that we don\u8217\'20\'19t care about kids.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She also noted staff and students haven\u8217\'20\'19t received enough personal protective equipment such as proper masks, that Covid testing is difficult for teachers given stringent drop-off times and is not mandated or easily available for students, and that Covid sick leave for teachers expired at the end of 2021. According to Abernethy, four teachers at her school quit before winter break, and 25 staff were absent on the first day of school after the break, on 3 January, though student attendance was normal.\par \par \uc2 There were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/teacher-labor-shortage-outlast-pandemic-d0953fec-115a-4d19-82a9-6fed552f29f9.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 575,000 fewer}}}\uc2  local and state education employees in October 2021 than in February 2020. Before the pandemic, teacher shortages were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/the-teacher-shortage-is-real-large-and-growing-and-worse-than-we-thought-the-first-report-in-the-perfect-storm-in-the-teacher-labor-market-series/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 widespread and growing}}}\uc2  throughout the US, while many public school buildings were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/education/2019/03/05/too-many-americas-public-schools-are-crumbling-literally-heres-one-plan-fix-them/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crumbling}}}\uc2 , in dire need of repairs, proper upkeep or replacement.\par \par \uc2 Retha Roblero, an elementary school teacher in Columbus, Ohio, participated in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/columbusea/status/1478033915571015683"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 safety strike}}}\uc2  on 3 January with other teachers who were working in their school building while students were remote, as part of the building had no working heat. Less than an hour after the strike began, teachers were permitted by the district to work remotely.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s no good answer,\u8221\'20\'1D said Roblero, about the choice between in-person or remote learning in response to Covid surges. \u8220\'20\'1CI want to be in person teaching every day. But I also don\u8217\'20\'19t want to carry something or somebody else to carry something.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Alison Ross, a teacher for about 20 years based in the Atlanta, Georgia, area, resigned from her school district position in fall 2020 over a disregard for Covid protections as she is immunocompromised. She has been tutoring and teaching remotely part-time, while relying on savings, and is dismayed at the prospect of returning to a full-time position as the pandemic continues to spread in the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like my livelihood was stolen from me,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ross. \u8220\'20\'1CWe could afford N95 masks for everybody, we could afford good ventilation, we can afford paying teachers more and having smaller classes. These are all choices that as a society, our government has made. People just think that\u8217\'20\'19s just how it has to be and that\u8217\'20\'19s not how it has to be.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 If we do complain, people are cruel and tell us to quit or that we don\u8217\'20\'19t care about kids\par \par \uc2 Rori Abernethy, a teacher in San Francisco\par \par \uc2 A teacher in Crested Butte, Colorado, who requested to remain anonymous for fear of retaliation described a lack of testing options for staff and students, with long wait lines at testing sites and no at-home tests available. The teacher says staff have been reporting to work after testing positive and there\u8217\'20\'19s a lack of transparency in contact tracing compared with previous periods during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have been told that they simply do not have the staff or time to deal with it, so contact tracing is out the window,\u8221\'20\'1D the teacher said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe morale is quite low because staff feel uncared for. I know several teachers with the mindset that we will all get it eventually so let\u8217\'20\'19s just get it over with. As teachers, we\u8217\'20\'19re the ones that take on the brunt of it to protect the kids.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Staffing shortages in their school district have also extended to a lack of school bus drivers, substitute teachers, cafeteria workers and a decline in student attendance. They already used up their Covid sick leave earlier this school year when they tested positive after their son caught it, despite being fully vaccinated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo the general public and administration, it just feels that staff and student health doesn\u8217\'20\'19t matter. We should have tested to return. No one wants to wear masks or go to school virtually, however now we may be forced to do so without a plan in place,\u8221\'20\'1D the teacher added. \u8220\'20\'1CI have been searching for jobs that are safer and value my family\u8217\'20\'19s health and safety more. We feel so undervalued.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MOREcaucusUFT/status/1478129650031382533"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 New York City}}}\uc2 , teachers held a protest during the first school week in January over school reopenings and a lack of Covid safety measures for students and staff.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re concerned about not having a negative Covid result as a requirement for returning like other school districts and private schools had,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ronnie Almonte, a teacher at Bard High School Early College in Manhattan, who noted Bard College requires a negative test to return to the campus this spring. He also expressed concerns with the new Covid isolation guidelines, which decreased isolation days from 10 to five days.\par \par \uc2 Teachers in Chicago {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ctulocal1.org/posts/dont-lock-educators-out-for-demanding-safe-in-person-learning-in-face-of-largest-surge-of-pandemic/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 voted}}}\uc2  to return to remote learning over lack of Covid safety measures, though Chicago public schools {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wbez.org/stories/cps-cancels-classes-wednesday-after-73-of-teachers-reject-in-person-learning/e3076169-00f4-40b0-8657-7daf4e76a014"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 cancelled school and locked out}}}\uc2  teachers from electronic accounts in retaliation.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Caswell, a science teacher in Philadelphia, argued the push to continue in-person school during a pandemic hasn\u8217\'20\'19t accounted for the problems facing public education systems before the pandemic began, from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://patch.com/michigan/dearborn/overcrowding-schools-why-it-huge-issue"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 overcrowding}}}\uc2  to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/education/study-says-standardized-testing-is-overwhelming-nations-public-schools/2015/10/24/8a22092c-79ae-11e5-a958-d889faf561dc_story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 excessive standardized testing}}}\uc2  and socio-economic disparities, with Covid further exposing those problems.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMorale is the absolute lowest that I\u8217\'20\'19ve ever seen it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Caswell. \u8220\'20\'1CThis goes from the top all the way down through the building administration. They expect that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to treat families and students with grace and understand that everybody\u8217\'20\'19s living through these crazy times and be supportive and encouraging, which is absolutely what we have to do and should be doing right now. But they don\u8217\'20\'19t show the same grace with us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In Philadelphia, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.inquirer.com/news/snow-covid-school-closures-philadelphia-pennsylvania-nj-20220103.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 81 schools switched }}}\uc2  to remote learning with less than 24 hours notice, and Caswell noted that numerous staff at her school tested positive during the first day of school.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s this constant anxiety and stress,\u8221\'20\'1D added Caswell. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s just a lot of pressure from a lot of different directions and not a whole lot of compassion and grace. My belief is the academics will work their way out. Kids are resilient when you provide them with the right support, and instead we\u8217\'20\'19re saying that we\u8217\'20\'19re going to go back to what we\u8217\'20\'19ve always done. That didn\u8217\'20\'19t work before the pandemic, so why does it work now?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020220108ei1800107\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
