\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 How electric vehicles have helped labor and climate groups team up\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel M Cohen \par \uc2 1,345 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2021\par 20:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Revitalized alliance could help strengthen both the fight for reducing emissions and for bolstering worker rights\par \par \uc2 As the Biden administration attempts to increase incentives for the production of electric vehicles, labor unions and climate groups have teamed up to push for better wages and working conditions for autoworkers \u8211\'20\'13 and leave the door open for the nascent EV industry to unionize.\par \par \uc2 Last month, leaders from some of the largest environmental groups in the country sent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://americanprogress.org/wp-content/uploads/2021/11/Letter_to_Toyota-1.pdf?_ga=2.47506917.334051170.1639314160-633263304.1631789064"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a letter}}}\uc2  to top executives at Toyota, lambasting the automaker for appearing to support the electric vehicle revolution all while lobbying against a proposed tax credit for union-made electric cars.\par \par \uc2 The tax credit would give $4,500 to consumers who buy electric from unionized US plants \u8211\'20\'13 currently just \uc2 Ford\uc2 , \uc2 General Motors\uc2  and \uc2 Stellantis NV\uc2  would qualify \u8211\'20\'13 and has been hotly opposed by companies like Tesla, \uc2 Honda\uc2 , Hyundai and \uc2 Nissan\uc2 . But Toyota went a step further and began {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.detroitnews.com/story/business/autos/2021/11/02/toyota-hits-union-made-electric-vehicle-credit-proposal-new-ad/6244628001/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 funding ads}}}\uc2  in papers like the \uc2 New York Times\uc2 , Wall Street Journal and trade press claiming the federal bonus would hurt non-union autoworkers. (All electric vehicles, union or not, would remain eligible for an existing $7,500 tax credit.)\par \par \uc2 Representatives from 12 groups \u8211\'20\'13 including \uc2 the Sierra Club\uc2 , the \uc2 League of Conservation Voters\uc2  and Evergreen Action \u8211\'20\'13 blasted Toyota for \u8220\'20\'1Cgreenwash[ing] its image\u8221\'20\'1D and called its lobbying \u8220\'20\'1Cextensive and unacceptable\u8221\'20\'1D. The organizations rejected Toyota\u8217\'20\'19s claim that incentivizing electric vehicles with union labor would hurt the nation\u8217\'20\'19s climate targets, and said its \u8220\'20\'1Cmanipulation of the political system and undermining action on climate is not limited to the United States\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 This came on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.sierraclub.org/press-releases/2021/11/ten-leading-environmental-advocacy-groups-tell-ev-start-engage-unions-build"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 heels of another letter}}}\uc2  from prominent climate groups, urging electric vehicle start-up \uc2 Rivian\uc2  to respect workers\u8217\'20\'19 wishes should they decide to unionize, and permit what\u8217\'20\'19s known as a card check process. Rivan, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnn.com/2021/11/12/cars/rivian-electric-vehicles/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went public last month}}}\uc2  in the biggest US IPO since \uc2 Facebook\uc2 , is poised to ramp up vehicle production in the next year.\par \par \uc2 Ten climate groups, including Friends of the Earth, Greenpeace USA, Sunrise Movement and \uc2 350.org\uc2 , signed on to the letter \u8211\'20\'13 and said therein that they had reached out to \uc2 Rivian\uc2  privately in August. After two months with no response, the groups decided to take their demand for labor peace public. The president of the United Auto Workers announced in the spring that his union {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2021/04/26/with-biden-as-an-ally-uaw-union-prepares-to-battle-for-ev-jobs.html?__source=sharebar%7Ctwitter&par=sharebar"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was planning to organize EV workers}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As lawmakers in Congress continue to hash out a wide-ranging social spending bill, labor unions, climate groups and prominent liberal thinktanks have joined forces to make the case that reducing emissions while creating unionized manufacturing jobs in the US is not only possible \u8211\'20\'13 but better for consumers and manufacturers alike.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 This isn\u8217\'20\'19t the first time that labor and climate groups have found common ground \u8211\'20\'13 but it represents a revitalized alliance that could help strengthen both the fight for reducing emissions and for bolstering worker rights.\par \par \uc2 Transportation is the largest source of pollution in the country, and the growth of non-union auto work has been accompanied by a nearly 20 percent decline in wages in the auto industry since 1990. \u8220\'20\'1CThe fight for environmental and economic justice \u8211\'20\'13the right for every American worker to not just survive but to thrive \u8211\'20\'13 are inextricably linked,\u8221\'20\'1D wrote UAW president \uc2 Ray Curry\uc2  and \uc2 Center for American Progress\uc2  founder \uc2 John Podesta\uc2  in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newsweek.com/green-cars-future-must-made-workers-union-jobs-opinion-1638635"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 joint-op-ed}}}\uc2  this summer.\par \par \uc2 Rivian\uc2  is not the only vehicle manufacturer that green groups have been targeting, according to Katherine Garc\u237\'00\'EDa, who leads the Transportation for All campaign at \uc2 the Sierra Club\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CBehind the scenes there are other vehicle manufacturers we\u8217\'20\'19re working with on labor peace,\u8221\'20\'1D she told \uc2 the Guardian\uc2 , adding that they\u8217\'20\'19ve been holding one-on-one meetings to make their case.\par \par \uc2 While environment and labor groups have not always seen eye-to-eye, the two have a history of shared interests. Josiah Rector, an urban historian at the University of Houston who has studied the 20th century environmental movement, noted that the \uc2 UAW\uc2  has previously fought against toxic chemicals and pollution in auto plants.\par \par \uc2 But in the 1970s, says Rector, automakers viewed the fight for cleaner air and other environmental standards as yet another threat to their bottom line, at a time when they were already hurting from two major oil crises, intensifying deindustrialization, and the closing of manufacturing plants. Automakers began to play \u8220\'20\'1Cenvironmental blackmail\u8221\'20\'1D against their workers, insisting that any further regulation from the government would force companies to cut even more jobs. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 UAW\uc2 , under threat of job blackmail, helped the companies lobby for a weaker Clean Air Act, which really strained tensions with the environmental movement,\u8221\'20\'1D said Rector.\par \par \uc2 Erik Loomis, a labor historian at the University of Rhode Island, also noted that the decline in industrial unions has especially hurt the unions that were more progressive to begin with \u8211\'20\'13 widening the gap between climate progressives and labor. \u8220\'20\'1CThat left you with the longtime more conservative unions, and as environmentalism has become more of a rich person\u8217\'20\'19s movement it also became disconnected from working-class culture,\u8221\'20\'1D Loomis said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think the reality is climate groups have now realized that\u8217\'20\'19s politically untenable and they need to build working-class coalitions if they want to get climate change legislation passed,\u8221\'20\'1D he adds. In other words, Loomis said, whether the modern-day solidarity is an alliance of deeply shared values or of convenience, the fact is that \u8220\'20\'1Cneither are particularly strong enough on their own to get laws passed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Jason Walsh, executive director of the BlueGreen Alliance, a coalition of labor unions and national environmental groups, attributes the work they\u8217\'20\'19ve been doing recently to many years of relationship building, but credits the Build Back Better Act for catalyzing their efforts in 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat has focused everyone\u8217\'20\'19s attention, and there\u8217\'20\'19s an understanding that we need to reverse this trend of offshoring and de-unionization that\u8217\'20\'19s hollowed out communities across the country,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 ***\par \par \uc2 The White House has set an ambitious goal of making {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.whitehouse.gov/briefing-room/statements-releases/2021/08/05/fact-sheet-president-biden-announces-steps-to-drive-american-leadership-forward-on-clean-cars-and-trucks/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 half of all new car sales}}}\uc2  electric by 2030. Even with the united front from labor and climate groups, and the strong backing of a unionized EV sector from \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , it\u8217\'20\'19s not clear the union-made tax credit will make it through the Senate. West Virginia senator Joe Manchin has voiced {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://news.bloombergtax.com/daily-tax-report/manchin-opposes-bonus-ev-tax-credit-for-union-made-cars"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 opposition to the subsidy}}}\uc2 , saying that taxpayer dollars shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be used as an incentive for consumers. Republican governors with non-union auto facilities have also urged Congress to reject the tax credit, and \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , the CEO of Tesla, called Biden a \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 UAW\uc2  \u8230\'20\'26 puppet\u8221\'20\'1D on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . (Brian Rothenberg, a spokesperson for UAW, declined an interview, saying their focus is on passing the Build Back Better Act.)\par \par \uc2 For now labor and climate groups are scrambling to get the bill over the finish line, and make the case that in order to compete effectively in the global electric vehicle revolution, the US needs to invest more in the workers who make the cars on American soil.\par \par \uc2 Sam Ricketts, co-director of Evergreen Action, said while there have been signs of solidarity between unions and environmental organizations in recent years, the steps being taken in the electric vehicle debates, like climate groups pressuring companies directly and publicly, represent something new and more concerted. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is new work happening in civil society, with groups recognizing that organizing can happen in the commercial sphere, not just the halls of Congress,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s leading-edge in that way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Rector, the University of Houston historian, said that if climate and labor groups want to surmount the problems that have plagued their solidarity efforts in the past, they\u8217\'20\'19ll need a plan to defeat arguments that climate goals are zero-sum for union workers. \u8220\'20\'1CTo solve the environmental blackmail problem, you need to have strong policies that undercut the argument coming from corporations and today from politicians like Joe Manchin,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd to do that, you need a strong coalition to push those policies through.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211228ehcs002jq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
