\par\par \uc2 Society\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Slap in the face\u8217\'20\'19: \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  workers describe exploitative work conditions\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,089 \uc2 words\par \uc2 28 December 2021\par 10:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Nearly minimum wage pay, chronic understaffing and proposed healthcare cuts leave employees disgruntled\par \par \uc2 Workers at the \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  charity are speaking out about what they say is low pay, chronic understaffing, poor working conditions throughout the pandemic and proposed cuts to their healthcare.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe morale is at an all time low in my 23 years of history here,\u8221\'20\'1D said Darryl Ford, a collection technician at American Red Cross in Warner Robins, Georgia, and president of local union branch USW L254.\par \par \uc2 He described Red Cross\u8217\'20\'19 response to the pandemic as business as usual, as the non-profit ramped up blood drives anywhere they were able to hold them, since many typical blood drive sites closed when the pandemic hit.\par \par \uc2 Ford\uc2  also criticized the \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  for doing little to nothing to clarify to the public that the blood donation drives were just testing for Covid-19 antibodies, not performing Covid-19 testing and providing inadequate PPE to workers.\par \par \uc2 Due to the low pay, scheduling problems and working conditions, \uc2 Ford\uc2  noted that \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  in his region experienced workers leaving throughout the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPeople are quitting,\u8221\'20\'1D added \uc2 Ford\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s a slap in the face to the employees for management to say \u8216\'20\'18we\u8217\'20\'19re going to cut your healthcare and not pay you anything, while we\u8217\'20\'19re going to work from home and be safe.\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Coalition of American Red Cross Unions, which represents about 4,900 workers from 11 international unions at \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  across the US, is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cwad2-13.org/news/red-cross-national-bargaining-december-update"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bargaining}}}\uc2  with the \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  over the national addendum to the unions\u8217\'20\'19 contracts, which expires on 31 March 2022.\par \par \uc2 The unions are pushing for wage increases, to preserve existing healthcare plans and for solutions to address chronic staffing shortages around the US.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  reported a revenue of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.redcross.org/content/dam/redcross/about-us/publications/2020-publications/FY2020_ARC_Form_990_FINAL.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $2.8bn }}}\uc2  in the 2020 fiscal year and the CEO of the non-profit received a salary of more than $700,000.\par \par \uc2 The majority of its revenue, approximately $1.73bn, went toward biomedical services, the part of the organization that collects blood donations and sells them to hospitals and healthcare providers. The \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  provides {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.redcrossblood.org/donate-blood/how-to-donate/how-blood-donations-help/blood-needs-blood-supply.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about 40%}}}\uc2  of the nation\u8217\'20\'19s blood supply.\par \par \uc2 Workers who collect and manage blood donations for the non-profit continued working throughout the Covid-19 pandemic as essential workers.\par \par \uc2 Alexis Zebrowski, a member of CWA Local 1118, worked as an aide specialist at the branch in Albany, New York for one year before quitting in November 2021 over the low pay around minimum wage, understaffing and working conditions through the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 The people they\u8217\'20\'19ve hired since November are significantly making more money than the staff who have been here for years\par \par \uc2 Bobbie Terrell\par \par \uc2 As an aide specialist, she collected platelet, plasma and blood donations, conducted medical physicals of donors and took patient histories and helped run blood donation drives, which she noted were consistently understaffed and resulted in work shifts that went at least one hour or longer past her scheduled ten-hour shift without being able to take breaks.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI can go work at any fast food restaurant and make more money,\u8221\'20\'1D said Zebrowski. \u8220\'20\'1CI was sick of the pay and all the constant bargaining and everything. The contracts still keep getting pushed out because they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to give us a raise or even acknowledge anything we do for the company. It\u8217\'20\'19s a slap in the face to work for the amount of money we are working for.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said the low pay and forced overtime led to worsening understaffing through the pandemic, with management rushing new hires through training and blaming existing staff when errors were made as a result.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen we\u8217\'20\'19re working, we\u8217\'20\'19re expected to work at 100%, but we never have 100% in return,\u8221\'20\'1D added Zebrowski. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re always forced to come into work no matter what, sick or not, we were expected to be there. Otherwise, we\u8217\'20\'19d be fearing for our job because we\u8217\'20\'19d be too worried about being written up for attendance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Bobbie Terrell, a collections technician and member of AFSCME Council 31 at a branch in Peoria, Illinois, criticized \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2  for responding to chronic staffing shortages in her area by offering $1,500 sign on bonuses for new hires and paying them higher wages, while the only appreciation or benefit that longtime employees have received is pizza.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe people they\u8217\'20\'19ve hired since November are significantly making more money than the staff who have been here for years,\u8221\'20\'1D said Terrell. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s a major slap in the face to the current staff that have worked through this whole pandemic and not been offered anything but pizza.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She also expressed frustration with \uc2 American Red Cross\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s proposal to cut the employees\u8217\'20\'19 health insurance benefits, saying she is concerned that cutting healthcare will worsen staffing problems. She also criticized the organization for not paying workers to quarantine after being exposed to donors or coworkers who test positive for Covid-19.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CStaff are being sent home for 10 to 14 days at a time, unpaid,\u8221\'20\'1D added Terrell. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re on the frontlines doing the job for them of collecting these life-saving products and basically they\u8217\'20\'19re telling us that it\u8217\'20\'19s at our own risk.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re always forced to come into work, sick or not, we were expected to be there. Otherwise, we\u8217\'20\'19d be fearing for our job\par \par \uc2 Alexis Zebrowski\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for American Red Cross claimed the non-profit plans to offer wage increases and retention bonuses to existing employees. But the spokesperson added that the group has not yet reached an agreement with unions on the terms and how the raises will be implemented, in addition to ongoing negotiations over healthcare.\par \par \uc2 They noted employees were provided with pay to quarantine in Spring 2020 and from 21 November 2020 to 2 April 2021.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAcross the country, many employers are facing staffing shortages as a result of pandemic employment trends and vaccination requirements, which have exacerbated the challenges to recruit and retain staff,\u8221\'20\'1D said the spokesperson. \u8220\'20\'1COver the past few months, the Red Cross actively recruited to fill vacancies on our biomedical services teams and have made significant investments in additional resources and tools to seek out and attract candidates, including new hire bonuses.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The spokesperson added: \u8220\'20\'1COur intention is to reach a fair and amicable agreement with the Coalition of Red Cross Unions and all of our local unions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211228ehcs0012y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
