\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18A lot of abuse for little pay\u8217\'20\'19: how US farming profits from exploitation and brutality\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 908 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 December 2021\par 18:29 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2021.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Two dozen conspirators forced workers to pay fees for travel and housing while forcing them to work for little to no pay\par \par \uc2 In June, a farm worker from Mexico, who requested to remain anonymous for fear of retaliation, was transported through a trafficking network from Monterey to work on farms in Georgia.\par \par \uc2 They paid the traffickers 20,000 pesos, about $950, loaned from their mother, taking frequent trips back and forth to Monterey, before being told it was safe to leave. Then they were finally transported across the border.\par \par \uc2 Initially, the worker was told they would be working on a blueberry farm, but was sent to a corn farming operation instead.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe arrived at the house where we would live, and had to clean the rooms ourselves. There were roaches, spiders, mosquitoes, and the mattresses were covered in lice,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe bathrooms and showers were dirty and clogged. The kitchen was horrible. We had no air conditioning in hot weather.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The worker began work daily at 3 or 4am and worked until 3 or 4pm with just one 15-minute lunch break, making just $225 for 15 days of work. They heard rumors that several workers had died. The worker claimed that Haitian immigrants were also brought into the same network.\par \par \uc2 After 20 days at the corn farm, the worker was sent to a cucumber warehouse where they weren\u8217\'20\'19t paid anything for their work, and then transferred to Texas before escaping the operation and returning to Mexico in July.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was a lot of abuse for little pay,\u8221\'20\'1D the worker added. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was a total fraud.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The contractor the worker said he worked under, JC Longoria Castro, was one of two dozen defendants {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdga/pr/human-smuggling-forced-labor-among-allegations-south-georgia-federal-indictment"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 indicted}}}\uc2  on federal conspiracy charges in October, based on findings from a multi-year investigation into a massive human smuggling and labor trafficking operation based in southern Georgia that extended to Florida and Texas.\par \par \uc2 The indictments characterized the operation as \u8220\'20\'1Cmodern-day slavery\u8221\'20\'1D, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ciw-online.org/slavery/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 longstanding problem}}}\uc2  in the US agricultural industry where workers were smuggled from Central American countries to the US and imprisoned as contracted farm workers.\par \par \uc2 Farmworkers in the US, especially immigrant workers, have few protections. They were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://therealnews.com/while-other-sectors-experience-strikes-farm-workers-are-still-fighting-for-basic-human-rights?fbclid=IwAR0NEuv_rYNLRx4Wdn5-V-xShHySlXxm1_nKF5fYGfQUo-_X3OmuxsmLoGg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  excluded }}}\uc2  from the National Labor Relations Act passed in 1935, and from the Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938. Workers in America\u8217\'20\'19s agricultural fields are regularly subjected to abuses ranging from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pbs.org/wgbh/frontline/film/rape-in-the-fields/transcript/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 high occurrences of sexual assault and harassment}}}\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/federal-labor-standards-enforcement-in-agriculture-data-reveal-the-biggest-violators-and-raise-new-questions-about-how-to-improve-and-target-efforts-to-protect-farmworkers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wage theft}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.osha.gov/agricultural-operations#:~:text=Farmworkers%20are%20at%20high%20risk,use%20and%20prolonged%20sun%20exposure."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 safety issues}}}\uc2  including injuries, fatalities on the job and exposure to hazardous chemicals.\par \par \uc2 The investigation, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/business-georgia-slavery-forced-labor-migrant-workers-0e0d7235e79a4e216307e007a7aa716b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Operation Blooming Onion}}}\uc2 , found the conspirators forced workers to pay fees for transportation to the US, food, and housing through the H2-A work visa program, while withholding their travel and identification documents and forcing them to work for little to no pay in inhumane living conditions.\par \par \uc2 The two dozen conspirators made $200m from their operation, laundering the money through land, houses, more than a dozen vehicles, the purchase of a restaurant and nightclub, and through a casino, according to the investigation. More than 100 workers were freed from the operation.\par \par \uc2 The H2-A visa program is an often-used avenue for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cdmigrante.org/new-report-ripe-for-reform-abuse-of-agricultural-workers-in-the-h-2a-visa-program/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 exploitation }}}\uc2  of migrant workers in the US, as it ties immigration status to employment on a temporary basis with no pathways to permanent citizenship. Many of these workers are forced to take on debt to recruiters to enter the H2-A visa program, with {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.farmworkerjustice.org/wp-content/uploads/2012/05/7.2.a.6-No-Way-To-Treat-A-Guest-H-2A-Report.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 several cases}}}\uc2  of debt peonage, forced labor and human trafficking reported through the program.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really the structure of the program that facilitates this kind of stuff happening, often with impunity,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Daniel Costa\uc2 , director of immigration law and policy research at the Economic Policy Institute.\par \par \uc2 He cited a severe lack of labor law enforcement in the agricultural industry as a driving factor in widespread abuses of workers, and the lack of regulation of recruiters outside the US who connect migrant workers with temporary jobs. Inspections conducted by the wage and hour division of the US Department of Labor {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epi.org/publication/federal-labor-standards-enforcement-in-agriculture-data-reveal-the-biggest-violators-and-raise-new-questions-about-how-to-improve-and-target-efforts-to-protect-farmworkers/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 declined}}}\uc2  significantly over the past few decades due to underfunding, and the low number of inspectors responsible for overseeing a vast number of employers.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf you\u8217\'20\'19re an agricultural employer, there\u8217\'20\'19s only around a 1% chance that you\u8217\'20\'19ll be investigated for anything in any given year, so they can pretty much get away with not treating your workers the way they should,\u8221\'20\'1D added Costa.\par \par \uc2 The Georgia workers were threatened with deportation or violence if they did not comply with the conspirators. The indictment includes allegations of \u8220\'20\'1Craping, kidnapping and threatening or attempting to kill some of the workers or their families, and in many cases sold or traded the workers to other conspirators\u8221\'20\'1D. At least two workers died as a result of the living and working conditions and another was repeatedly raped, the indictment said.\par \par \uc2 Some of the workers were promised up to $12 an hour in pay, but instead were ordered by armed overseers to dig up onions by hand for $0.20 per bucket.\par \par \uc2 A grand jury indicted the 24 conspirators in a federal court in Waycross, Georgia, on counts including forced labor, mail fraud, witness tampering and conspiracy to commit money laundering. Arraignments in the case were scheduled for 21 December and 6 January at the southern district of Georgia federal courthouse in Waycross, Georgia.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020211225ehcp000dy\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/10/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Source\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 The Guardian 